window.COMMERCE_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"commerce-occupations","old":{"title":"Commerce, trade and occupations","body":"Commerce consists of trade and the activities that assist trade. Trade is buying and selling goods and services. The aids to trade include banking, insurance, transport, warehousing, communication and advertising. Trade is the centre of commerce: the aids make exchange easier, safer and more regular. Commerce connects production with consumption; it does not mean manufacturing alone.\n\nAn occupation is a lawful activity through which a person earns a living. Industrial occupations obtain raw materials, change their form or construct things. Farming, mining, manufacturing and building are examples. Commercial occupations distribute goods and provide aids to trade. Retailers, wholesalers, bankers and transport operators perform commercial work. Service occupations provide personal or professional assistance. A teacher, doctor or lawyer gives a direct service to the person who needs it.\n\nDirect services satisfy wants directly. Commercial services assist the process of exchange. A lawyer advising a client is usually classified as giving a direct professional service in school Commerce. Do not classify every paid activity as trade merely because money changes hands. The question may be asking about the nature of the work, not its payment.\n\nCommerce developed as people produced surpluses and exchanged them. Barter is the direct exchange of goods for goods. It is limited by the need for a double coincidence of wants: each trader must want what the other offers. Money removes much of that difficulty. Better roads, communication, security, banking and storage support larger markets and greater specialization.\n\nWorked example: a farmer grows maize, a mill turns it into flour, and a shop sells the flour. Growing is primary production, milling is secondary production, and the shop performs trade. The bank financing the shop supplies an aid to trade. Together these activities help consumers obtain a useful product.\n\nPublic services such as policing and law-making benefit society collectively and are traditionally called indirect services. Private professional assistance to an individual is a direct service.\n\nAdvertising expands awareness and demand, supporting trade; sustainable growth also depends on production, distribution, finance and wider conditions.\n\nCommerce can generate government revenue through lawful taxes and charges, helping finance public services and development.\n\nTrade creates interdependence between nations and can encourage cooperation and friendly relationships. It does not guarantee that every international disagreement disappears.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Commerce is wider than trade but is not the same as all production.\n• Trade is the buying-and-selling part; manufacturing changes the form of goods.\n• Commercial activity includes services, not only physical goods.\n• The reward earned does not determine the occupational category. Examine what the worker actually does."},"new":{"title":"Commerce, trade and occupations","body":"COMMERCE, TRADE AND OCCUPATIONS — HOW GOODS REACH THE CONSUMER\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA farmer can produce a thousand bags of rice, yet families in another state may still be unable to buy them. Production alone does not guarantee that goods reach the people who need them. Commerce solves the problems of exchange, movement, storage, payment, information and risk. In JAMB, the key is to identify precisely what each business activity does.\n\n1. MEANING AND SCOPE OF COMMERCE\nCommerce is the branch of business activity concerned with the exchange and distribution of goods and services, together with the activities that facilitate exchange. Its two major divisions are TRADE and AIDS TO TRADE.\n\nTrade is buying and selling goods and services. A trader normally acquires goods for resale or connects buyers and sellers. A shopkeeper selling exercise books performs trade; the printer manufacturing them performs production.\n\nAids to trade are services that make trade possible, convenient or less risky:\n• Transport moves goods between places and creates place utility.\n• Warehousing stores goods until they are needed and creates time utility.\n• Banking provides payment services, savings facilities and credit.\n• Insurance provides financial protection against specified risks, subject to policy terms.\n• Advertising informs or persuades potential buyers.\n• Communication transfers orders, quotations, complaints and other commercial information.\n\nEXAMPLE — FROM KANO TO PORT HARCOURT\nA tomato farmer harvests tomatoes in Kano. A merchant buys them for resale (trade). A refrigerated vehicle carries them to Port Harcourt (transport). A bank transfers the purchase price (banking). A phone call confirms delivery arrangements (communication). An insurer may cover specified transit losses (insurance). A shop displays a price promotion (advertising). Each activity has a different function, even though they support the same transaction.\n\n2. COMMERCE, INDUSTRY AND BUSINESS: THE DIFFERENCE\nBusiness is the broad activity of producing or supplying goods and services, usually to earn income or meet an organisational objective. Industry is primarily concerned with extracting, processing, manufacturing or constructing goods. Commerce is concerned with exchange and distribution.\n\nA bakery making bread is engaged in secondary production. The same bakery selling bread directly to customers also undertakes trade. A business can therefore perform more than one function.\n\nProduction creates or increases usefulness (utility):\n• Form utility: changing raw materials into a more useful product, as when timber becomes furniture.\n• Place utility: making a product available where consumers want it, through transport.\n• Time utility: making a product available when needed, through storage.\n• Possession utility: transferring ownership or the right to use a product through exchange.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Do not say that commerce manufactures goods. Manufacturing is industrial production; commerce helps bring goods and services to the market.\n\n3. TRADE: HOME AND FOREIGN\nHome (domestic or internal) trade takes place within a country. It includes wholesale trade, which commonly involves purchasing in bulk and selling to retailers or other business users, and retail trade, which involves selling in smaller quantities to final consumers.\n\nForeign (international or external) trade crosses national borders:\n• Import trade: buying goods or services from abroad.\n• Export trade: selling goods or services to buyers abroad.\n• Entrepôt trade: importing goods and subsequently re-exporting them, usually with little or no substantial processing.\n\nEXAMPLE: A Nigerian dealer buying shoes from another Nigerian dealer conducts home trade. Buying shoes from Italy is import trade from Nigeria's perspective. Selling Nigerian cocoa to a buyer in the Netherlands is export trade.\n\nA wholesaler does not always sell only to retailers, and a retailer does not always operate from a physical shop. Classify the transaction by the function performed.\n\n4. OCCUPATION: MEANING AND MAJOR CLASSES\nAn occupation is a regular activity through which a person earns a livelihood. For school Commerce, occupations are commonly grouped into industrial, commercial and service occupations.\n\nINDUSTRIAL OCCUPATIONS\nThese involve obtaining materials from nature, transforming them or constructing structures.\n• Extractive or primary: farming, fishing, mining, forestry.\n• Manufacturing or processing: milling rice, making soap, producing cement.\n• Construction: building bridges, houses and roads.\n\nCOMMERCIAL OCCUPATIONS\nThese involve buying and selling or providing services that assist exchange: wholesalers, retailers, bankers, insurance workers, transporters, warehouse operators and advertising professionals.\n\nSERVICE OCCUPATIONS\nThese supply services rather than primarily producing or distributing physical goods. Direct or personal services are provided to identifiable clients, such as medical treatment, teaching, hairdressing or legal advice. Indirect or public services serve the wider community, such as public security and certain government administrative functions.\n\nIMPORTANT DISTINCTION: Banking and transport are services in the broad economic sense, but school Commerce often groups them specifically under commercial occupations because they are aids to trade. A doctor treating a patient is ordinarily classified as providing a direct service, not as a trader merely because the patient pays.\n\n5. DIVISION OF LABOUR AND INTERDEPENDENCE\nSpecialisation means concentrating on a particular task or economic activity. As workers and firms specialise, they depend on others for goods and services they do not produce themselves.\n\nConsider a school uniform: a cotton grower provides raw material, a textile factory makes cloth, a tailor sews the uniform, a distributor transports it, and a shop sells it. No single occupation necessarily performs every step.\n\nSpecialisation can increase productivity and skill, but it also creates interdependence. If transport stops, a retailer may run out of stock even when the factory is still producing. Commerce connects specialised producers to consumers.\n\n6. HOW COMMERCE DEVELOPED\nIn subsistence economies, families produced much of what they consumed. As specialisation and surplus production increased, people exchanged goods.\n\nBARTER is the direct exchange of goods or services for other goods or services without money. Its difficulties include:\n• Double coincidence of wants: each party must want what the other offers.\n• Lack of a common measure of value: comparing unlike goods is difficult.\n• Indivisibility: a cow cannot always be divided conveniently to pay for a small item.\n• Difficulty storing some goods as wealth: perishable goods spoil.\n• Difficulty arranging deferred payments: agreeing what to repay later can be problematic.\n\nMoney developed as a generally accepted medium of exchange. Its principal functions are medium of exchange, unit of account, store of value and standard of deferred payment. Money makes exchange easier but does not eliminate scarcity or guarantee stable purchasing power.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO\nAda has a bag of beans and wants shoes. Tunde sells shoes but wants a bicycle, not beans. They cannot barter directly because their wants do not coincide. If Ada sells the beans for money, she can use the proceeds to buy shoes without needing the shoe seller to want beans.\n\n7. IMPORTANCE OF COMMERCE TO AN ECONOMY\nCommerce:\n• Connects producers with customers and widens markets.\n• Makes specialised production more practical.\n• Creates jobs in distribution, banking, insurance and related services.\n• Reduces some exchange risks and transaction difficulties.\n• Makes goods available at appropriate places and times.\n• Encourages investment and can increase business income.\n• Facilitates domestic and international trade.\n• Generates tax and other lawful government revenue.\n• May strengthen economic links between regions and countries.\n\nThese are potential contributions, not guarantees. Poor roads, insecurity, high transaction costs or weak institutions can prevent commerce from functioning effectively.\n\n8. COMPARISON GUIDE\nTrade versus commerce: trade is buying and selling; commerce includes trade plus aids to trade.\nIndustry versus commerce: industry obtains or transforms goods; commerce facilitates their exchange and distribution.\nWholesaler versus retailer: wholesalers generally sell in bulk to resellers or organisations; retailers generally sell to final consumers.\nDirect versus indirect services: direct services benefit identifiable clients; indirect/public services benefit the community collectively.\nBarter versus monetary exchange: barter swaps items directly; monetary exchange uses money as the intermediary.\n\n9. JAMB-STYLE APPLICATIONS\nQ1. A firm keeps bags of rice after harvest so they can be sold during the dry season. Which aid to trade is involved?\nAnswer: Warehousing. The main benefit is time utility.\n\nQ2. A furniture factory changes timber into tables. Is this commerce?\nAnswer: The transformation is secondary production. Selling the finished tables is trade.\n\nQ3. A lawyer advises a customer about a tenancy agreement. Which occupation is illustrated?\nAnswer: Direct professional service, even though the lawyer receives payment.\n\nQ4. A Nigerian company buys goods abroad and sells them to a third country without substantial processing. What is the trade called?\nAnswer: Entrepôt or re-export trade.\n\nQ5. Why does barter require a double coincidence of wants?\nAnswer: Each participant must simultaneously desire the item the other participant offers.\n\nQ6. What is the difference between place and time utility?\nAnswer: Transport creates place utility by changing location; warehousing creates time utility by changing when goods are available.\n\n10. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Commerce is broader than trade, but it is not synonymous with every kind of business activity.\n• Manufacturing is not an aid to trade; transport and banking are.\n• A paid occupation is not automatically trade.\n• A service can belong to a commercial occupation when its role is to assist trade.\n• Foreign trade is classified from the viewpoint of the country concerned: one country's export is another country's import.\n• Entrepôt trade involves re-export, not ordinary local retailing.\n• Warehousing does not create form utility; manufacturing commonly does.\n• Money reduces barter difficulties but does not remove the need for resources.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nCOMMERCE = TRADE + AIDS TO TRADE.\nAsk three questions about any activity: Is something being MADE, EXCHANGED, or HELPING EXCHANGE? That usually identifies industry, trade, or an aid to trade."}},{"id":"production-factors","old":{"title":"Production, factors and specialization","body":"Production is the creation of goods and services that satisfy human wants. It also creates or adds utility, meaning usefulness. A product is not fully useful to its consumer merely because it has left a factory. Transport creates place utility; warehousing creates time utility by keeping goods until they are needed.\n\nPrimary production obtains products from nature. Farming, fishing, forestry and mining are examples. Secondary production changes raw materials into more useful forms or constructs fixed structures. Manufacturing turns cotton into cloth; construction provides roads and buildings. Manufacturing can assemble factory components; construction assembles manufactured materials into usable works. A stem saying only “assembling products” needs context to distinguish these categories. Tertiary production provides services. Trade, banking, transport and direct professional services fall within the service sector.\n\nThe factors of production are land, labour, capital and enterprise. Land includes natural resources, not just soil. Its supply is relatively fixed from the economy's viewpoint, although particular land can be improved or reclaimed. Its reward is rent. Labour is human physical and mental effort used in production; its reward is wages or salaries. Workers can move between places or occupations, although training and family ties may restrict mobility.\n\nCapital means human-made resources used for further production. Machines, tools and factory buildings are capital goods. Money finances their purchase, but money is not itself a productive machine. The reward for using borrowed capital is interest. The entrepreneur organizes the other factors, makes business decisions and bears business risk. Profit is the reward for successful enterprise, while losses are possible.\n\nDivision of labour breaks production into separate tasks performed by different workers. Specialization means concentrating on a particular activity, product or area of work. Division of labour is one form of specialization. It can increase skill, save time and permit the use of machinery. However, repetitive work can cause boredom, and a failure at one stage can delay the whole process.\n\nWorked example: one worker cuts material, another sews and another checks finished school uniforms. This is division of labour. The machines are capital, the workers supply labour, and the owner who organizes production acts as entrepreneur.\n\nCapital goods are human-made production aids; intermediate goods are inputs used up or transformed in making other goods. In the economic long run, all factors can vary. Aptitude means natural ability for a particular occupation.\n\nFabrication forms or assembles materials into products; conversion changes their form. Use the particular transformation described when both terms appear among options.\n\nSpecialization can reduce craftsmanship.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Secondary production includes construction as well as manufacturing.\n• Capital goods assist further production; consumer goods satisfy wants directly.\n• The entrepreneur's main role is coordination and risk-bearing, not supplying every factor personally."},"new":{"title":"Production, factors and specialization","body":"PRODUCTION, FACTORS AND SPECIALISATION — HOW BUSINESSES CREATE VALUE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nBefore goods can be bought and sold, somebody must make them available. Production, factors of production and division of labour explain how that happens. JAMB often presents a practical situation—a quarry, a tailoring workshop or a transport firm—and asks students to classify its activities, identify the relevant factor or explain the benefit or limitation of specialisation.\n\n1. MEANING OF PRODUCTION\nProduction is the creation or addition of utility through goods and services that satisfy human wants. Utility means usefulness or want-satisfying power. Production is not restricted to factory work: farming produces crops, a teacher provides education, and a transporter makes goods available in another place.\n\nFour useful forms of utility:\n• Form utility: processing or transforming materials, such as turning cassava into garri.\n• Place utility: moving goods to where they are wanted, such as transporting tomatoes from Jos to Lagos.\n• Time utility: storing goods until they are needed, such as keeping grain after harvest.\n• Possession utility: making ownership or the right to use goods available through exchange, such as selling a bicycle to a customer.\n\nBe careful: these describe different ways usefulness is added. A retailer may contribute possession utility without changing a product's physical form.\n\n2. STAGES OF PRODUCTION\nPRIMARY PRODUCTION obtains resources from nature. Examples include crop farming, fishing, forestry, mining and quarrying. Its output often supplies raw materials to other industries.\n\nSECONDARY PRODUCTION transforms raw materials or creates structures.\n• Manufacturing: cotton into cloth; cocoa beans into chocolate; metal into cooking pots.\n• Construction: building roads, bridges, houses and dams.\nManufacturing may include assembly. Construction is distinguished by producing structures or works, often fixed at their locations.\n\nTERTIARY PRODUCTION supplies services. It includes commercial services such as banking, transport, insurance, retailing and communication, as well as direct services such as medical treatment and education.\n\nPRODUCTION CHAIN — FOLLOW A BAG OF RICE\nFARMER (primary: grows paddy)\n  ↓\nRICE MILL (secondary: removes husk and processes rice)\n  ↓\nTRANSPORTER (tertiary: moves bags to market)\n  ↓\nWHOLESALER → RETAILER (tertiary trade)\n  ↓\nHOUSEHOLD (final consumer)\n\nThis is a simplified flow diagram, not a rule that every product must pass through every intermediary. A farmer can sell directly to consumers.\n\nJAMB APPLICATION: A firm that mines limestone performs primary production. A cement factory processing limestone performs secondary production. A lorry carrying cement to a dealer provides tertiary services.\n\n3. FACTORS OF PRODUCTION: THE FOUR INPUTS\nFactors of production are resources used to produce goods and services. The conventional school-Commerce classification is LAND, LABOUR, CAPITAL and ENTERPRISE.\n\nLAND\nLand means all natural resources used in production, not merely the ground beneath a building. It includes farmland, rivers, minerals, forests and natural fishing grounds.\nCharacteristics:\n• It is a gift of nature rather than a human-made capital good.\n• Its total natural supply is relatively fixed, though usable land can be improved, reclaimed or reallocated.\n• Its quality and location differ.\n• It is geographically immobile, although its use may change.\nReward: RENT, particularly payment for the use of land or natural resources.\n\nLABOUR\nLabour is human physical or mental effort used in producing goods and services. A mason, accountant, teacher and machine operator all supply labour.\nCharacteristics:\n• Labour is inseparable from the worker.\n• Skill, training, health and experience affect productivity.\n• Labour may move between occupations or places, but mobility is restricted by qualifications, language, family circumstances and other barriers.\n• Labour is perishable in the sense that an unused hour of labour cannot be stored for later.\nReward: WAGES or SALARIES.\n\nCAPITAL\nCapital consists of human-made resources used to produce other goods and services: machines, tools, production buildings, vehicles and equipment.\n• Fixed capital: durable productive assets used repeatedly, such as factory machinery.\n• Working or circulating capital: resources used up or regularly replaced in the production cycle, such as raw materials and supplies. In business finance, working capital also commonly means current assets minus current liabilities; do not confuse the two contexts.\n• Physical capital: productive equipment and structures.\n• Financial capital: funds available to acquire productive assets and finance operations.\n\nReward: INTEREST is conventionally associated with capital in factor-reward questions, especially lending. An owner may also earn other returns depending on how capital is employed.\n\nMoney is not itself a machine. It enables a business to buy machines and materials. Likewise, a bag of cement used to build a house is an intermediate input, while a cement mixer repeatedly used on projects is a capital good.\n\nENTERPRISE OR ENTREPRENEURSHIP\nEnterprise is the function of organising land, labour and capital, making decisions, innovating and bearing business risk.\nAn entrepreneur may:\n• Identify a market opportunity.\n• Combine resources and arrange finance.\n• Choose products, prices and production methods.\n• Hire workers and supervise operations.\n• Bear uncertainty about sales, costs and competition.\nReward: PROFIT when the venture succeeds; LOSS is possible when it fails.\n\nFACTORS AND REWARDS — MEMORY TABLE\nLand → Rent\nLabour → Wages or salaries\nCapital → Interest\nEnterprise → Profit (or possible loss)\n\nThese are conventional examination associations. In actual businesses, an individual may supply several factors and receive different types of income.\n\n4. FIXED CAPITAL, CONSUMER GOODS AND INTERMEDIATE GOODS\nA good's classification depends on HOW IT IS USED.\n• Consumer good: bought for direct satisfaction of wants, such as a family's refrigerator.\n• Capital good: used to produce or distribute goods and services, such as a refrigerator in a restaurant kitchen.\n• Intermediate good: used up or transformed in making another product, such as flour used by a bakery.\n\nA vehicle used for commercial deliveries is a capital good for the transport business; a family car used privately is primarily a consumer durable. The same physical object can therefore have different economic classifications.\n\n5. SPECIALISATION AND DIVISION OF LABOUR\nSpecialisation means concentrating on a particular job, skill, product or branch of production. It can occur among individuals, firms, regions and countries.\n\nDivision of labour means splitting a production process into distinct tasks assigned to different workers or groups. It is a form of specialisation, but not all specialisation is division of labour. A country specialising in cocoa exports does not necessarily divide a single factory process among workers.\n\nTAILORING WORKSHOP — TASK FLOW DIAGRAM\nMEASURE CUSTOMER\n  ↓\nCUT FABRIC\n  ↓\nSEW GARMENT\n  ↓\nATTACH BUTTONS / ZIP\n  ↓\nPRESS AND INSPECT\n  ↓\nDELIVER TO CUSTOMER\n\nIf different workers specialise in these stages, the workshop practises division of labour. If one tailor completes every stage, the process is not divided among workers, even if that tailor specialises in making uniforms.\n\n6. ADVANTAGES OF DIVISION OF LABOUR\n• Repetition can improve skill and speed.\n• Workers save time otherwise spent switching between tasks.\n• Specialised machines may be used efficiently.\n• Training for a narrow task may be faster.\n• Large-scale production can become easier.\n• Output per worker may rise, lowering unit cost.\n• Standardisation and quality checks can improve consistency.\n\nThese gains depend on good coordination, reliable materials and sufficient demand. Division of labour does not automatically increase quality or wages.\n\n7. DISADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS\n• Repetitive tasks may cause boredom and reduce motivation.\n• Workers may lose the ability to make an entire product.\n• A stoppage in one stage can interrupt the whole production line.\n• Highly specialised workers may struggle to change jobs.\n• Excessive dependence on machines or other workers can create vulnerability.\n• Work may become monotonous or reduce individual craftsmanship.\n\nSpecialisation is more effective when the market is large enough to support sustained demand, capital and equipment are available, workers can be trained, and transport and communication are reliable.\n\nEXAMPLE: A bakery with 30 workers can assign teams to mixing, baking, packaging and sales. A village bakery with only one worker and very few customers may find such a division impractical.\n\n8. SCALE OF PRODUCTION\nSmall-scale production generally uses fewer resources and serves a limited market. It can be flexible and close to customers but may face higher unit costs and limited finance.\n\nLarge-scale production uses greater productive capacity and may achieve economies of scale: reductions in average cost as output increases.\nInternal economies arise within a growing firm, such as bulk purchasing, specialised machinery or management expertise.\nExternal economies arise from advantages available to firms in an industry or location, such as better shared infrastructure or a pool of skilled workers.\n\nGrowth can also create DISECONOMIES OF SCALE, including coordination difficulties, communication delays and bureaucratic waste.\n\nWORKED COST EXAMPLE\nA workshop spends ₦120,000 producing 100 units.\nAverage cost = ₦120,000 ÷ 100 = ₦1,200 per unit.\nAfter better organisation, it spends ₦200,000 producing 200 units.\nAverage cost = ₦200,000 ÷ 200 = ₦1,000 per unit.\nAverage cost falls by ₦200 per unit. This illustrates a possible economy of scale, though lower average cost alone does not identify its precise cause.\n\n9. PRODUCTIVITY AND EFFICIENCY\nProductivity measures output relative to an input, often labour time or number of workers.\n\nLabour productivity = Total output ÷ Labour input.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA workshop produces 480 school bags with 12 workers over a comparable period.\nOutput per worker = 480 ÷ 12 = 40 bags.\nAfter training and improved tools, 12 workers produce 600 bags.\nNew output per worker = 600 ÷ 12 = 50 bags.\nIncrease = 10 bags per worker, or (10 ÷ 40) × 100 = 25%.\n\nProductivity is not the same as profitability. A factory can produce more units per worker but still lose money if costs rise sharply or customers refuse to buy its products.\n\n10. FACTOR MOBILITY AND LOCATION\nOccupational mobility is the ability of a factor—especially labour—to move from one job or use to another. Geographical mobility is movement between places. Training can improve occupational mobility; housing costs and family responsibilities can limit geographical mobility.\n\nLand cannot physically move from Enugu to Abuja, but land use can change. Capital equipment can often be relocated, although moving a large installed factory may be expensive. Entrepreneurs can transfer their skills and investment across activities.\n\n11. EXAMINATION DISTINCTIONS\nProduction versus manufacturing: production includes goods and services; manufacturing is one branch of secondary production.\nPrimary versus secondary: primary obtains resources from nature; secondary transforms materials or constructs works.\nCapital versus money: physical capital helps produce; money finances acquisition and payments.\nCapital good versus intermediate good: capital goods serve repeatedly; intermediate goods are used up or transformed.\nSpecialisation versus division of labour: specialisation is concentration; division of labour separates stages of one process.\nProductivity versus profit: productivity compares output to inputs; profit compares revenue to costs.\n\n12. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE WITH EXPLANATIONS\nQ1. A firm extracts granite from a quarry. What stage of production is this?\nAnswer: Primary production. It obtains a natural resource.\n\nQ2. A company builds a bridge. What stage of production?\nAnswer: Secondary production, specifically construction.\n\nQ3. Which factor of production receives profit as its conventional reward?\nAnswer: Enterprise. The entrepreneur organises resources and bears risk.\n\nQ4. A business buys a delivery van to distribute products. How is the van classified?\nAnswer: A capital good, because it supports ongoing business operations.\n\nQ5. A bakery uses flour to make bread. Is the flour a capital good or intermediate good?\nAnswer: Intermediate good, because it is transformed into the final product.\n\nQ6. A factory assigns cutting, stitching and inspection to different workers. What arrangement is shown?\nAnswer: Division of labour.\n\nQ7. Why can division of labour reduce unit costs?\nAnswer: Specialisation may improve speed, reduce switching time and permit efficient use of equipment.\n\nQ8. What is a major risk of extreme division of labour?\nAnswer: A breakdown at one stage can interrupt other stages; repetitive work can also cause boredom.\n\nQ9. Twelve workers produce 360 units in a day. What is output per worker?\nAnswer: 360 ÷ 12 = 30 units per worker per day.\n\nQ10. A machine is used for five years in production. Is it normally fixed or circulating capital?\nAnswer: Fixed capital, because it is a durable productive asset.\n\n13. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Tertiary production includes trade and professional services, not only transportation.\n• Secondary production includes construction as well as factory manufacturing.\n• The reward of land is rent; do not confuse it with the purchase price of land.\n• Money is financial capital in business usage but is not itself physical productive machinery.\n• The entrepreneur need not personally own every factor of production.\n• Division of labour is a form of specialisation, not an exact synonym for all specialisation.\n• Higher output does not necessarily mean higher productivity if inputs increased even faster.\n• Higher productivity does not guarantee profit.\n• An item may be a consumer good in one use and a capital good in another.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nPRODUCTION: Primary → Secondary → Tertiary.\nFACTORS: Land → Rent; Labour → Wages; Capital → Interest; Enterprise → Profit.\nDIVISION OF LABOUR: Different people perform different stages of one process."}},{"id":"enterprise-location","old":{"title":"Starting, locating and expanding a business","body":"A business supplies goods or services with stated objectives, commonly including profit, survival, growth and customer satisfaction. Before starting, an entrepreneur should understand the proposed activity, identify customers, estimate costs and obtain suitable finance. Knowledge and feasibility come before assuming that registration alone guarantees success.\n\nLocation is the geographical place where a business operates. A factory may need access to raw materials, power, water, labour and transport. A retail shop often needs convenient access to customers. The weight, perishability and cost of moving goods matter. Cement production usually benefits from proximity to bulky mineral materials; a neighbourhood bakery benefits from closeness to buyers and regular supplies.\n\nOther location factors include land costs, security, government rules, waste disposal, reliable electricity and communication. No factor is always the most important for every enterprise. For an examination question, identify the type of business and the specific constraint mentioned. A firm should also consider competition, the size of the market and the possibility of future expansion.\n\nBusinesses can grow internally by opening branches, increasing capacity or introducing products. External growth involves combining with other firms. A horizontal merger joins businesses at the same stage in the same industry. A vertical merger joins firms at different stages of producing or distributing a related product. A conglomerate merger combines businesses in unrelated activities. A holding company controls another company through ownership of sufficient voting interests.\n\nA joint venture is a business arrangement for a particular project or purpose, often for a limited period. Each participant may contribute finance, technology, distribution or expertise. A consortium is an association of organizations cooperating on an undertaking too large for one alone. Neither term automatically means that all participating businesses cease to exist separately.\n\nWorked example: a cloth manufacturer and a clothing retailer combining are linked vertically. Two competing flour mills combining form a horizontal merger. A flour mill joining an unrelated insurance business is a conglomerate combination.\n\nRetrenchment reduces activities when resources or demand are inadequate. Consolidating operations can concentrate work in fewer locations and reduce duplication. These responses differ from diversifying into additional products or industries.\n\nMerger consideration can include shares, cash or both. A premium may attract acceptance, but neither a premium nor shares are guaranteed in every real transaction.\n\nA cartel coordinates otherwise separate producers, commonly concerning output or prices. OPEC illustrates coordination among oil-producing countries.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Location is not determined by raw materials in every industry.\n• A joint venture concerns a specific undertaking; an ordinary partnership can continue indefinitely.\n• A market opportunity is not enough without finance, relevant knowledge and operating resources.\n• Historical questions may use older labels for forms of business combinations; compare their actual activities."},"new":{"title":"Starting, locating and expanding a business","body":"A business supplies goods or services with stated objectives, commonly including profit, survival, growth and customer satisfaction. Before starting, an entrepreneur should understand the proposed activity, identify customers, estimate costs and obtain suitable finance. Knowledge and feasibility come before assuming that registration alone guarantees success.\n\nLocation is the geographical place where a business operates. A factory may need access to raw materials, power, water, labour and transport. A retail shop often needs convenient access to customers. The weight, perishability and cost of moving goods matter. Cement production usually benefits from proximity to bulky mineral materials; a neighbourhood bakery benefits from closeness to buyers and regular supplies.\n\nOther location factors include land costs, security, government rules, waste disposal, reliable electricity and communication. No factor is always the most important for every enterprise. For an examination question, identify the type of business and the specific constraint mentioned. A firm should also consider competition, the size of the market and the possibility of future expansion.\n\nBusinesses can grow internally by opening branches, increasing capacity or introducing products. External growth involves combining with other firms. A horizontal merger joins businesses at the same stage in the same industry. A vertical merger joins firms at different stages of producing or distributing a related product. A conglomerate merger combines businesses in unrelated activities. A holding company controls another company through ownership of sufficient voting interests.\n\nA joint venture is a business arrangement for a particular project or purpose, often for a limited period. Each participant may contribute finance, technology, distribution or expertise. A consortium is an association of organizations cooperating on an undertaking too large for one alone. Neither term automatically means that all participating businesses cease to exist separately.\n\nWorked example: a cloth manufacturer and a clothing retailer combining are linked vertically. Two competing flour mills combining form a horizontal merger. A flour mill joining an unrelated insurance business is a conglomerate combination.\n\nRetrenchment reduces activities when resources or demand are inadequate. Consolidating operations can concentrate work in fewer locations and reduce duplication. These responses differ from diversifying into additional products or industries.\n\nMerger consideration can include shares, cash or both. A premium may attract acceptance, but neither a premium nor shares are guaranteed in every real transaction.\n\nA cartel coordinates otherwise separate producers, commonly concerning output or prices. OPEC illustrates coordination among oil-producing countries.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Location is not determined by raw materials in every industry.\n• A joint venture concerns a specific undertaking; an ordinary partnership can continue indefinitely.\n• A market opportunity is not enough without finance, relevant knowledge and operating resources.\n• Historical questions may use older labels for forms of business combinations; compare their actual activities.\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB frequently tests the difference between a sensible location factor and an absolute rule, and between horizontal, vertical and conglomerate combinations.\n\n1. STARTING A BUSINESS\nIdentify demand, test feasibility, calculate expected costs and revenues, secure skills and finance, comply with registration and permits, then plan operations. Registration alone does not establish commercial viability.\n\n2. LOCATION IS INDUSTRY-SPECIFIC\nA heavy cement works may locate near limestone to cut bulky-input transport costs. A neighbourhood pharmacy prioritizes customers, accessibility and licensing. A data-service firm may prioritize electricity, connectivity and skills. Compare input weight, market proximity, infrastructure, labour, security, environmental rules and future expansion. No single factor always wins.\n\n3. BUSINESS GROWTH\nInternal growth expands existing operations. Horizontal integration combines firms at the same production/distribution stage (two competing supermarkets). Vertical integration joins linked stages (flour mill and bakery). Conglomerate integration joins unrelated industries (bakery and insurance company).\n\n4. COOPERATION IS NOT ALWAYS A MERGER\nJoint venture: parties collaborate on a specific venture, often sharing risk and resources. Consortium: organisations cooperate on a large undertaking while typically retaining separate identities. Cartel: independent competitors coordinate competition-sensitive matters such as prices, output or markets. Mere cooperation does not by itself prove a cartel; the anti-competitive coordination matters.\n\n5. HOLDING COMPANY AND RETRENCHMENT\nA holding company controls another through sufficient voting power; it need not own every share. Retrenchment cuts activities or costs, while diversification enters new lines. Consolidation brings operations together; it is not automatically diversification.\n\n6. EXAM SCENARIOS\nTwo competing banks combine → horizontal. A cocoa processor acquires a cocoa farm → vertical. A bank buys a furniture company → conglomerate. Several builders bid together for one airport project → consortium or joint venture, depending on their agreement; not automatically cartel.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\n• 'Near raw materials' is not universally the best location.\n• Vertical = different linked stages; horizontal = same stage.\n• A consortium is not defined merely as any voluntary association.\n• A joint venture does not require permanent merger.\n• A cartel needs evidence of coordination that restricts competition."}},{"id":"sole-trade-partnership","old":{"title":"Sole traders, partnerships and agency relationships","body":"A sole proprietorship is a business owned and controlled by one person. The owner supplies or arranges capital, makes decisions, receives the profit and bears losses. The business is usually easy to establish and decisions can be quick. Its limitations include restricted capital, heavy responsibility on the owner and unlimited liability: personal assets may be used to meet business debts.\n\nA partnership is a relationship between persons carrying on business together with a view to profit. Partners combine resources and skills. An ordinary partnership is generally not a separate incorporated legal person like a limited company. Do not assume that every partner's liability is limited. Liability depends on the type of partnership and applicable law.\n\nAn active partner takes part in management. A sleeping or dormant partner contributes capital and shares profit but does not participate in day-to-day management. Such inactivity does not by itself remove liability. A nominal partner lends a name or reputation to the firm rather than genuinely contributing capital and sharing its profits as an ordinary partner. School texts sometimes use passive and sleeping similarly; read the options carefully when both are offered.\n\nA partnership deed is the written agreement setting out the partners' rights and duties. It can state capital contributions, profit-and-loss sharing, drawings, interest arrangements, management responsibilities, admission of new partners and procedures for retirement or dissolution. It is not a prospectus, which invites investment in a company, or an invoice for goods sold.\n\nAdvantages include more capital than one owner may provide, shared work and complementary skills. Disadvantages include possible disagreements, difficulties transferring an interest and uncertainty of continuity when a partner leaves. A disagreement can be reduced by clear terms and proper accounts, but a deed does not guarantee that partners will never quarrel.\n\nWorked example: Ada contributes money and handles purchases, while Bayo contributes technical knowledge and manages repairs. Their partnership combines complementary strengths. If Chika only provides capital and stays out of management, Chika is a sleeping partner, not automatically a nominal partner.\n\nAdmission of a new partner can increase partnership capital. Bankruptcy can lead to dissolution under the relevant agreement and law; a single ordinary trading loss need not automatically dissolve the business.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sleeping does not mean having no financial interest.\n• A nominal partner is not defined simply as someone absent from work.\n• The deed records an agreement; it does not turn an ordinary partnership into a limited company."},"new":{"title":"Sole traders, partnerships and agency relationships","body":"A sole proprietorship is a business owned and controlled by one person. The owner supplies or arranges capital, makes decisions, receives the profit and bears losses. The business is usually easy to establish and decisions can be quick. Its limitations include restricted capital, heavy responsibility on the owner and unlimited liability: personal assets may be used to meet business debts.\n\nA partnership is a relationship between persons carrying on business together with a view to profit. Partners combine resources and skills. An ordinary partnership is generally not a separate incorporated legal person like a limited company. Do not assume that every partner's liability is limited. Liability depends on the type of partnership and applicable law.\n\nAn active partner takes part in management. A sleeping or dormant partner contributes capital and shares profit but does not participate in day-to-day management. Such inactivity does not by itself remove liability. A nominal partner lends a name or reputation to the firm rather than genuinely contributing capital and sharing its profits as an ordinary partner. School texts sometimes use passive and sleeping similarly; read the options carefully when both are offered.\n\nA partnership deed is the written agreement setting out the partners' rights and duties. It can state capital contributions, profit-and-loss sharing, drawings, interest arrangements, management responsibilities, admission of new partners and procedures for retirement or dissolution. It is not a prospectus, which invites investment in a company, or an invoice for goods sold.\n\nAdvantages include more capital than one owner may provide, shared work and complementary skills. Disadvantages include possible disagreements, difficulties transferring an interest and uncertainty of continuity when a partner leaves. A disagreement can be reduced by clear terms and proper accounts, but a deed does not guarantee that partners will never quarrel.\n\nWorked example: Ada contributes money and handles purchases, while Bayo contributes technical knowledge and manages repairs. Their partnership combines complementary strengths. If Chika only provides capital and stays out of management, Chika is a sleeping partner, not automatically a nominal partner.\n\nAdmission of a new partner can increase partnership capital. Bankruptcy can lead to dissolution under the relevant agreement and law; a single ordinary trading loss need not automatically dissolve the business.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sleeping does not mean having no financial interest.\n• A nominal partner is not defined simply as someone absent from work.\n• The deed records an agreement; it does not turn an ordinary partnership into a limited company.\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nExams contrast ownership, management, liability and the legal documents used by small businesses. Keep those four questions separate.\n\n1. SOLE PROPRIETORSHIP\nOne owner controls the business, takes residual profit and normally bears unlimited personal liability. Advantages: easy formation, quick decisions, confidentiality and direct incentive. Disadvantages: limited finance, heavy workload, exposure to loss and continuity risk.\n\n2. PARTNERSHIP BASICS\nPartners carry on a business in common with a view to profit. They may combine skills and capital. In an ordinary general partnership, partners may have extensive personal liability and mutual agency; a partner can bind the firm in the ordinary course of its business, subject to applicable law and circumstances. Limited partnership arrangements differ.\n\n3. TYPES OF PARTNER\nActive partner participates in management. Sleeping/dormant partner contributes but stays out of daily management; this does not automatically erase liability. Nominal partner permits their name to be associated with the firm, typically without the ordinary financial participation of a full partner; liability to third parties can arise from holding out. A minor's position depends on the governing law—never assume a minor can freely contract as a full adult partner.\n\n4. PARTNERSHIP DEED\nThe deed records agreed capital contributions, profit sharing, drawings, duties, dispute handling and admission or retirement. It is an agreement between partners, not a company prospectus or share certificate. Absence of a written deed does not always mean no partnership exists.\n\n5. WORKED EXAMPLE\nAmaka runs the shop daily; Bello invests and does not manage. Amaka is active, Bello is sleeping. If Bello's name appears on the door, that fact alone does not make him nominal: his actual investment and rights still matter.\n\n6. QUICK CHECK\nQ: Who usually makes decisions alone in a sole trade? A: The sole proprietor.\nQ: Does a sleeping partner necessarily have limited liability? A: No.\nQ: Which document states agreed profit-sharing ratios? A: Partnership deed.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\n• One owner ≠ one employee: a sole trader may employ many people.\n• Sleeping ≠ nominal; management inactivity does not define nominal status.\n• A partnership deed does not incorporate the firm.\n• Sharing profits is relevant evidence but does not alone settle every legal question about partnership."}},{"id":"companies-liquidation","old":{"title":"Companies, ownership, shares and liquidation","body":"A company is an incorporated business organization with a legal identity separate from its members. It can own property, enter contracts and continue despite changes in ownership. Limited liability means that members' exposure is generally limited to unpaid amounts on their shares or their agreed guarantee, according to the company type. It does not mean that a company can ignore its debts.\n\nA private company restricts public invitations to subscribe for its securities and has restrictions appropriate to its legal form. A public company can invite public investment subject to the law. In the standard examination distinction, it has no fixed maximum number of shareholders. Registration with the Corporate Affairs Commission is incorporation; admission to a securities exchange is listing. A public company is not automatically listed, and an unlisted company is not necessarily a sole trader.\n\nOrdinary shareholders are owners who usually receive variable dividends and bear the residual business risk. Preference shareholders have stated priority over ordinary shareholders, commonly concerning dividends and repayment of capital. Debenture holders are creditors, not owners merely because they provide long-term funds. Interest is a borrowing cost; dividends are distributions to shareholders.\n\nA memorandum traditionally states basic constitutional matters, including the company's objects, while articles regulate internal management. Historical questions may describe an act outside the objects as ultra vires, meaning beyond its powers. Modern company legislation has changed the effect of some constitutional restrictions. Explain the rule appropriate to the question's date rather than presenting every historical consequence as current law.\n\nLiquidation is winding up a company, realizing its assets and settling claims before any balance is distributed. A liquidator carries out that process. An auditor examines accounts; a broker arranges transactions; neither is the same as a liquidator. Insolvency means inability to meet financial obligations, whereas liquidation is a process and can occur in circumstances other than hopeless insolvency.\n\nWorked example: if assets must be sold, creditors' valid claims normally take priority over members' residual capital. Secured-creditor rights, liquidation expenses and statutory preferential claims affect the exact order. Do not teach that ordinary shareholders are paid before creditors.\n\nA realization account records disposal of assets and settlement items during winding up; liquidation expenses, including the liquidator’s fee, are charged there in standard partnership-style realization accounts.\n\nA company’s board directs overall policy and planning; shareholders exercise their ownership rights through the relevant company procedures.\n\nVoluntary winding up involves members’ or creditors’ appointment procedures; not every liquidator is appointed by directors.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Incorporation and stock-exchange listing are different.\n• PLC identifies a public limited company, not a government-owned corporation.\n• Debenture interest is not an ordinary dividend.\n• A simplified examination priority must not erase secured or statutory claims."},"new":{"title":"Companies, ownership, shares and liquidation","body":"A company is an incorporated business organization with a legal identity separate from its members. It can own property, enter contracts and continue despite changes in ownership. Limited liability means that members' exposure is generally limited to unpaid amounts on their shares or their agreed guarantee, according to the company type. It does not mean that a company can ignore its debts.\n\nA private company restricts public invitations to subscribe for its securities and has restrictions appropriate to its legal form. A public company can invite public investment subject to the law. In the standard examination distinction, it has no fixed maximum number of shareholders. Registration with the Corporate Affairs Commission is incorporation; admission to a securities exchange is listing. A public company is not automatically listed, and an unlisted company is not necessarily a sole trader.\n\nOrdinary shareholders are owners who usually receive variable dividends and bear the residual business risk. Preference shareholders have stated priority over ordinary shareholders, commonly concerning dividends and repayment of capital. Debenture holders are creditors, not owners merely because they provide long-term funds. Interest is a borrowing cost; dividends are distributions to shareholders.\n\nA memorandum traditionally states basic constitutional matters, including the company's objects, while articles regulate internal management. Historical questions may describe an act outside the objects as ultra vires, meaning beyond its powers. Modern company legislation has changed the effect of some constitutional restrictions. Explain the rule appropriate to the question's date rather than presenting every historical consequence as current law.\n\nLiquidation is winding up a company, realizing its assets and settling claims before any balance is distributed. A liquidator carries out that process. An auditor examines accounts; a broker arranges transactions; neither is the same as a liquidator. Insolvency means inability to meet financial obligations, whereas liquidation is a process and can occur in circumstances other than hopeless insolvency.\n\nWorked example: if assets must be sold, creditors' valid claims normally take priority over members' residual capital. Secured-creditor rights, liquidation expenses and statutory preferential claims affect the exact order. Do not teach that ordinary shareholders are paid before creditors.\n\nA realization account records disposal of assets and settlement items during winding up; liquidation expenses, including the liquidator’s fee, are charged there in standard partnership-style realization accounts.\n\nA company’s board directs overall policy and planning; shareholders exercise their ownership rights through the relevant company procedures.\n\nVoluntary winding up involves members’ or creditors’ appointment procedures; not every liquidator is appointed by directors.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Incorporation and stock-exchange listing are different.\n• PLC identifies a public limited company, not a government-owned corporation.\n• Debenture interest is not an ordinary dividend.\n• A simplified examination priority must not erase secured or statutory claims.\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nThis topic combines company identity, capital terminology, shareholders versus creditors and liquidation priorities. Precise vocabulary matters more than memorizing a single list.\n\n1. INCORPORATION AND COMPANY TYPES\nIncorporation creates a legal person distinct from its members. A private company and a public company differ in how they may offer securities and in legal requirements. Being public does not automatically mean stock-exchange listing. The Corporate Affairs Commission handles incorporation; an exchange handles listing under its rules.\n\n2. OWNERS AND CREDITORS\nOrdinary shareholders hold residual ownership interests and usually receive variable dividends when declared. Preference shares may carry dividend or capital priorities, subject to their terms. Debenture holders are creditors entitled to contractual repayment/interest; they do not become shareholders merely by lending.\n\n3. CAPITAL LANGUAGE — DO NOT SUBSTITUTE TERMS\nAuthorised/nominal capital is the traditional maximum share capital stated in the constitution in older textbooks; modern Nigerian company law has changed this framework. Issued capital represents shares actually issued. Unissued capital is the portion not issued. Called-up capital is the amount requested on issued shares; paid-up capital is what shareholders have actually paid. Reserve capital traditionally refers to a portion of uncalled capital set aside for winding up, NOT simply the unissued portion.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA historical exam says 5 million shares of 50 kobo each are available, and 80% are issued. Traditional nominal value total = ₦2.5 million; issued nominal capital = ₦2 million; unissued nominal portion = ₦500,000. Calling ₦500,000 'reserve capital' without an additional reserve resolution is technically inaccurate.\n\n4. MEMORANDUM, ARTICLES AND ULTRA VIRES\nThe memorandum traditionally sets constitutional fundamentals; articles govern internal rules. Ultra vires historically meant beyond the company's stated powers. Modern legal effects differ from older textbook statements, so use the paper's historical context and avoid treating every older doctrine as current Nigerian law.\n\n5. WINDING UP\nLiquidation realizes assets, settles valid claims and distributes any remainder. A liquidator administers the process; an auditor examines financial statements. Liquidation does not always mean fraud or that a business never had value. Creditors and statutory priorities generally precede ordinary shareholders; secured interests and liquidation expenses affect the order.\n\n6. QUICK CHECK\nQ: Is a debenture holder a shareholder? A: No, a creditor.\nQ: Does a PLC automatically trade on an exchange? A: No.\nQ: Is unissued capital automatically reserve capital? A: No.\nQ: Who administers winding up? A: Liquidator.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\n• Incorporation ≠ listing.\n• Limited liability protects members within its legal scope; it does not cancel company debts.\n• Debenture interest ≠ share dividend.\n• Unissued, uncalled, called-up and paid-up capital are different.\n• Liquidator ≠ auditor; shareholders are not first in line for assets."}},{"id":"cooperatives-public-enterprises","old":{"title":"Cooperatives, public enterprises and privatization","body":"A cooperative society is a voluntary association formed to meet members' common economic needs through a jointly owned undertaking. Typical features include democratic control and service to members. Voting commonly follows one member, one vote rather than the number of shares held. A cooperative can earn a surplus, but member service is central to its purpose.\n\nA consumer or retail cooperative buys goods for members and reduces the margin otherwise earned by outside middlemen. A producers' cooperative helps members produce or market their output. A credit and thrift society encourages saving and provides loans to members under agreed rules. Credit is funded by members' savings and other permitted resources; it is not free money without repayment obligations.\n\nCooperatives can provide access to supplies, credit and a stronger bargaining position. Problems may include limited capital, weak management, unpaid loans and low participation. Equal voting is a control principle, not proof that all members make identical purchases or receive an identical patronage refund.\n\nA public enterprise is owned or controlled by government. It may provide essential services, infrastructure, strategic goods or employment, and can pursue social objectives alongside financial ones. Its problems can include political interference, weak accountability and dependence on subsidies. Public ownership does not by itself establish that an enterprise must operate inefficiently.\n\nPrivatization transfers government ownership or equity interests to private investors. Commercialization reorganizes a public enterprise to operate on commercial principles and improve financial self-sufficiency, without necessarily transferring government ownership. Deregulation reduces direct restrictions or controls and permits greater market competition. These are related policies but not identical actions.\n\nIn Nigerian historical questions, the Bureau of Public Enterprises implements privatization and commercialization programmes, while the National Council on Privatization provides high-level policy oversight. Do not confuse the implementing agency with the highest policy-making body. Earlier papers may mention predecessor arrangements, so the question's date matters.\n\nA core investor combines a substantial investment with the managerial or technical capacity expected for the enterprise; money alone is not the whole idea.\n\nWorked example: if government retains ownership but requires an enterprise to cover costs through revenue, that is commercialization. If it sells its shares to private investors, that is privatization. Removing entry restrictions on competitors is deregulation.\n\nA cooperative patronage refund commonly follows the volume of members’ purchases or dealings, not equal amounts for every voter. Thrift concerns regular saving.\n\nNITEL’s public ownership supports a public-enterprise classification; “Limited” and “public corporation” still require care about its actual legal form.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Privatization concerns ownership, not simply raising prices.\n• Commercialization does not automatically mean a sale.\n• Cooperative voting and company voting should not be assumed to follow the same rule."},"new":{"title":"Cooperatives, public enterprises and privatization","body":"COOPERATIVES, PUBLIC ENTERPRISES AND PRIVATISATION — WHO OWNS AND CONTROLS THE BUSINESS?\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA group of farmers may pool money to buy fertiliser, while government may establish an organisation to provide public infrastructure. Both are business organisations, but their ownership, objectives, control and sources of finance differ. JAMB tests these distinctions and often asks students to identify a cooperative type or distinguish privatisation from commercialisation.\n\n1. COOPERATIVE SOCIETY: MEANING\nA cooperative is a voluntary association of persons who join together to meet common economic or social needs through an enterprise they jointly own and democratically control. The purpose is primarily to serve members, although a cooperative must still manage its finances responsibly.\n\nTypical cooperative principles include voluntary and open membership, democratic member control, member economic participation, autonomy, education and training, cooperation among cooperatives and concern for the community. In many primary cooperatives, the voting principle is ONE MEMBER, ONE VOTE rather than one vote per share.\n\nEXAMPLE: Twenty market women contribute savings to establish a bulk-purchasing society. It buys rice directly from suppliers and distributes it to members on agreed terms. The society exists chiefly to improve members' access to goods, not to maximise profits for outside shareholders.\n\n2. TYPES OF COOPERATIVE SOCIETIES\nCONSUMERS' COOPERATIVE: Members combine purchasing power to buy goods in bulk, reduce distribution costs and supply members at reasonable prices. Example: civil servants jointly operating a grocery shop.\n\nPRODUCERS' COOPERATIVE: Small producers share equipment, inputs or production facilities. Example: cassava processors buying and operating a shared grating machine.\n\nMARKETING COOPERATIVE: Members pool produce for grading, storage, transport and sale, improving bargaining power. Example: cocoa farmers marketing their harvest collectively.\n\nCREDIT AND THRIFT / SAVINGS COOPERATIVE: Members save regularly and obtain loans under agreed conditions. Loans are not gifts; borrowers must meet repayment rules.\n\nMULTIPURPOSE COOPERATIVE: Combines several activities such as savings, lending, purchasing and marketing.\n\nHOUSING COOPERATIVE: Members organise to acquire land, finance or develop housing.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A society that chiefly lends money from pooled member savings is a credit and thrift cooperative, not a consumers' cooperative merely because its members are consumers.\n\n3. HOW A COOPERATIVE IS ORGANISED\nThe general meeting of members normally exercises ultimate democratic control within the law and the society's rules. An elected management committee oversees activities, while appointed staff may handle daily operations. The society's constitution or bye-laws set membership conditions, contributions, elections, meetings, use of surplus and dispute procedures.\n\nSOURCES OF FINANCE\n• Members' shares and subscriptions.\n• Regular savings and contributions.\n• Retained surplus from operations.\n• Permitted loans and grants.\n• Other lawful financing arrangements.\n\nA cooperative surplus may be retained for development, used to support members or distributed according to applicable rules, often considering patronage. Equal voting rights do not mean that every member must receive the same amount of surplus.\n\n4. ADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS\nAdvantages:\n• Collective bargaining can secure better buying or selling terms.\n• Members may access affordable credit or shared equipment.\n• Democratic participation encourages member involvement.\n• Small producers can achieve some benefits of large-scale operation.\n• Shared risks and training may improve resilience.\n\nLimitations:\n• Capital may be insufficient for expansion.\n• Weak record-keeping or dishonest management can cause losses.\n• Loan defaults may undermine a credit society.\n• Disagreement among members can slow decisions.\n• Poor attendance and limited managerial expertise may reduce performance.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A cooperative buys 100 bags of fertiliser for ₦3,000,000. Average purchase cost is ₦30,000 per bag before transport and administration. If individual members would otherwise pay ₦34,000 each, the gross saving is ₦4,000 per bag, but the net benefit must allow for cooperative operating costs.\n\n5. PUBLIC ENTERPRISES\nA public enterprise is an organisation owned or controlled by government to provide goods or services, implement policy or operate in a strategic sector. It may be established under a specific law or organised as a government-controlled company, depending on the jurisdiction.\n\nPossible objectives include:\n• Providing essential infrastructure and services.\n• Serving areas or groups that private businesses might neglect.\n• Supporting economic development and employment.\n• Protecting strategic interests.\n• Generating revenue where commercially appropriate.\n\nPublic ownership does not automatically mean services are free, and a public enterprise does not necessarily operate at a loss.\n\n6. SOURCES OF FINANCE AND CONTROL OF PUBLIC ENTERPRISES\nPublic enterprises may receive government equity, budgetary allocations, internally generated revenue, loans, grants and other lawful funds. The exact arrangement depends on their legal form.\n\nGovernment may appoint governing boards, set policy objectives and require public reporting. Managers handle operations subject to the enterprise's governing rules.\n\nPotential strengths include large-scale financing, continuity of essential services and pursuit of public objectives. Common challenges include political interference, bureaucratic delays, weak incentives, poor maintenance, overstaffing and financial losses. These are possible problems, not inevitable features of every public organisation.\n\n7. PRIVATISATION, COMMERCIALISATION AND NATIONALISATION\nPRIVATISATION means transferring government ownership or controlling interest in an enterprise or asset to private owners. It may be full or partial.\n\nCOMMERCIALISATION means operating an enterprise on a more commercial basis, with greater emphasis on cost recovery, efficiency and revenue generation. Government ownership can remain unchanged.\n\nNATIONALISATION means transferring private ownership of an enterprise or industry to government.\n\nDEREGULATION means reducing or removing certain government restrictions on market entry, prices or operations. Deregulation is not identical to privatisation: private and public firms may operate in a deregulated market.\n\nCOMPARISON\nPrivatisation → change in ownership.\nCommercialisation → change in operating approach.\nNationalisation → ownership moves toward the state.\nDeregulation → change in regulatory restrictions.\n\n8. REASONS AND RISKS OF REFORM\nGovernments may privatise to raise proceeds, attract investment, reduce the burden of funding loss-making enterprises or encourage efficiency. Commercialisation may aim to improve service quality, accountability and financial sustainability.\n\nPossible risks include job losses, higher prices, reduced access for poorer consumers, private monopoly and inadequate oversight. Outcomes depend on competition, regulation, contracts and management.\n\nEXAMPLE: If government retains 100% ownership of a water company but directs it to cover more of its costs from customer payments, that is commercialisation rather than privatisation. If government sells a controlling shareholding to private investors, ownership has been privatised.\n\n9. COOPERATIVE VERSUS PUBLIC ENTERPRISE\nOwnership: cooperative → members; public enterprise → government.\nControl: cooperative → democratic member structures; public enterprise → public authorities and appointed governance bodies.\nMain focus: cooperative → member needs; public enterprise → public-policy and/or commercial objectives.\nFinance: cooperative → member contributions and permitted borrowing; public enterprise → public funds, operating revenue and permitted borrowing.\nSurplus: cooperative → retained or applied for members under rules; public enterprise → used according to its legal and financial arrangements.\n\n10. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. A society enables farmers to sell produce together at better prices. Which type? Answer: Marketing cooperative.\nQ2. Which principle commonly distinguishes a primary cooperative from a share company? Answer: One member, one vote.\nQ3. A group pools savings and grants repayable loans to members. Which cooperative? Answer: Credit and thrift.\nQ4. Government retains ownership but requires a public corporation to cover operating costs. What reform? Answer: Commercialisation.\nQ5. Government sells its controlling interest to private investors. What reform? Answer: Privatisation.\nQ6. Private firms are brought under state ownership. What is this called? Answer: Nationalisation.\nQ7. Why might a cooperative obtain cheaper supplies? Answer: Collective bulk purchasing and stronger bargaining power.\nQ8. Name one risk of poor cooperative management. Answer: Misuse of funds, weak records or loan defaults.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• A cooperative serves members but still needs sound financial management.\n• One member, one vote concerns governance, not equal financial contributions.\n• Credit and thrift loans must normally be repaid.\n• Public ownership does not mean every service is free.\n• Privatisation changes ownership; commercialisation need not.\n• Deregulation and privatisation are different policies.\n• Nationalisation moves ownership toward government, not away from it.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nCOOPERATIVE = MEMBERS OWN AND CONTROL.\nPUBLIC ENTERPRISE = GOVERNMENT OWNS OR CONTROLS.\nPRIVATISATION = SELL OWNERSHIP; COMMERCIALISATION = OPERATE COMMERCIALLY."}},{"id":"business-management","old":{"title":"Management functions and organizational structure","body":"Management is the process of planning, organizing, directing and controlling resources to achieve organizational objectives. Resources include people, money, materials, machines, information and business opportunities. Effective management achieves the intended objective; efficient management avoids unnecessary waste in doing so.\n\nPlanning decides what to achieve and how to achieve it. Organizing groups tasks, establishes departments and assigns authority. Staffing obtains, places and develops suitable workers. Directing guides people in carrying out their duties. Motivation encourages the willingness and effort needed for performance. Coordinating brings related activities into harmony. Controlling compares actual results with plans and arranges corrective action.\n\nAuthority is the right to give instructions and make decisions within an assigned role. Responsibility is the duty to carry out a task. Accountability is being answerable for performance. Delegation assigns duties and sufficient authority to a subordinate, but the manager remains answerable for the work of the unit. Delegation is not simply abandoning supervision.\n\nAn organizational chart shows positions and formal reporting relationships. A line structure has a direct chain of authority. A line-and-staff structure combines line managers with specialists who advise them. In a functional structure, specialist authority is organized around particular functions. A matrix structure combines functional and project relationships, which can create more than one reporting line.\n\nSpan of control is the number of subordinates directly supervised by a manager. A narrow span can permit close supervision but may increase the number of management levels. A wide span can reduce levels but may overload a supervisor. Unity of command means receiving instructions from one immediate superior for a particular responsibility; it should not be confused with having only one department.\n\nWorked example: the sales manager sets a monthly target, allocates territories and checks actual sales. Setting the target is planning; assigning territories is organizing; comparing sales with the target is controlling. Encouraging effort through recognition and suitable rewards concerns motivation.\n\nDelegation depends on a superior’s judgment about the task and subordinate, but accountability is not abandoned.\n\nA simple line structure can suit a small enterprise because direct authority makes decisions and responsibility clear. Suitability still depends on size, specialist needs and complexity; it is not a universal rule for every medium-sized firm.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Recruitment finds candidates; staffing is the wider personnel function.\n• Organizing is about jobs, departments and authority, not merely keeping an office tidy.\n• A staff adviser does not automatically possess every line manager's command authority.\n• Motivation affects willingness and intensity of effort; it is not identical to planning."},"new":{"title":"Management functions and organizational structure","body":"BUSINESS MANAGEMENT AND ORGANISATIONAL STRUCTURE — MAKING PEOPLE AND RESOURCES WORK TOGETHER\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA business may have workers, machines and money yet fail because tasks are poorly planned or nobody knows who makes decisions. Management coordinates these resources. JAMB tests the functions of management, authority and responsibility, organisational charts, delegation, leadership and motivation.\n\n1. MEANING OF MANAGEMENT\nManagement is the process of planning, organising, staffing, directing, coordinating and controlling people and other resources to achieve organisational goals.\n\nEFFECTIVENESS means achieving the intended result. EFFICIENCY means achieving results with minimal unnecessary waste of time, money and materials.\n\nEXAMPLE: A shop planned to deliver 100 orders by Friday. Delivering all 100 achieves the target (effectiveness). Doing so without avoidable overtime, damaged stock or unnecessary transport costs improves efficiency.\n\n2. FUNCTIONS OF MANAGEMENT\nPLANNING: Deciding objectives in advance and choosing how to achieve them. A school-uniform maker forecasts demand, budgets for cloth and sets a production schedule.\n\nORGANISING: Dividing work into tasks, grouping activities and allocating resources and authority. A firm establishes purchasing, production, sales and accounts units.\n\nSTAFFING: Recruiting, selecting, placing, training and developing suitable employees. A firm hires qualified machine operators.\n\nDIRECTING OR LEADING: Guiding, communicating with and supervising workers so they understand what to do.\n\nMOTIVATING: Encouraging effort through fair pay, recognition, growth opportunities and suitable working conditions.\n\nCOORDINATING: Ensuring that different units work together. Purchasing must supply cloth before production can sew uniforms.\n\nCONTROLLING: Setting standards, measuring actual performance, comparing results with targets and correcting deviations.\n\nMANAGEMENT CONTROL CYCLE\nSET TARGET → MEASURE RESULTS → COMPARE WITH TARGET → CORRECT PROBLEMS → REVIEW.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A bakery plans to produce 500 loaves daily but makes 400. Management checks whether the problem is labour absence, power supply, machine failure or poor planning before taking corrective action. Simply blaming staff is not effective control.\n\n3. LEVELS OF MANAGEMENT\nTOP MANAGEMENT sets broad policy and long-term direction. Examples: board and senior executives.\nMIDDLE MANAGEMENT translates policy into departmental plans and supervises units. Examples: production and sales managers.\nLOWER OR SUPERVISORY MANAGEMENT oversees day-to-day tasks. Examples: shift supervisors and team leaders.\n\nThe exact titles differ across businesses. The important distinction is the scope of decisions and responsibilities.\n\n4. AUTHORITY, RESPONSIBILITY AND ACCOUNTABILITY\nAUTHORITY is the legitimate right to make decisions, issue instructions and use resources within an assigned role.\nRESPONSIBILITY is the duty to perform assigned work.\nACCOUNTABILITY is the obligation to explain and answer for results.\n\nDELEGATION occurs when a manager assigns a task and appropriate authority to a subordinate. The subordinate becomes responsible for carrying it out, but the manager retains overall accountability for the unit's work.\n\nEXAMPLE: A branch manager authorises a cashier to approve refunds up to ₦10,000. The cashier has limited delegated authority; refunds beyond that limit require higher approval.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Delegation is not abdication. A manager does not cease to be accountable simply because a subordinate performs the task.\n\n5. ORGANISATIONAL STRUCTURE\nAn organisational structure shows how duties, reporting relationships and decision-making powers are arranged.\n\nA simple line structure follows a direct chain of command:\nMANAGING DIRECTOR\n  ↓\nBRANCH MANAGER\n  ↓\nSUPERVISOR\n  ↓\nSALES STAFF\n\nA functional structure groups specialists into departments such as finance, operations, human resources and marketing.\n\nLINE AUTHORITY is the direct authority of a manager over subordinates in the chain of command.\nSTAFF AUTHORITY is specialist advice or support, such as legal or human-resource advice. Staff specialists do not automatically command all line departments.\n\nSPAN OF CONTROL is the number of subordinates directly reporting to a manager. A wide span means many direct reports; a narrow span means fewer.\n\nCHAIN OF COMMAND is the formal route through which instructions and reports pass.\nUNITY OF COMMAND means that, under the classical principle, a worker should receive orders from one immediate superior to avoid conflicting instructions.\n\n6. CENTRALISATION AND DECENTRALISATION\nCENTRALISATION concentrates important decisions at higher levels of management.\nDECENTRALISATION distributes decision-making authority to lower levels or branches.\n\nCentralisation can support consistency and control but may delay local decisions. Decentralisation can speed responses and encourage initiative but requires effective supervision and coordination.\n\nEXAMPLE: If all discounts require approval from headquarters, pricing decisions are centralised. If branch managers may set discounts within limits, some authority is decentralised.\n\n7. LEADERSHIP STYLES\nAUTOCRATIC: The leader makes decisions with little subordinate participation. It can be fast in emergencies but may reduce employee involvement.\nDEMOCRATIC: The leader consults staff and encourages participation. It can improve commitment but may take longer.\nLAISSEZ-FAIRE: Workers receive considerable freedom. It may suit experienced specialists but can lead to weak coordination when roles are unclear.\n\nNo style is always best. Effectiveness depends on urgency, staff competence and the task.\n\n8. MOTIVATION AND COMMUNICATION\nMotivation is the willingness to exert effort toward organisational goals. Financial incentives include wages, bonuses and allowances. Non-financial incentives include recognition, promotion opportunities, training, job security and good working conditions.\n\nCOMMUNICATION is the exchange of information and understanding.\n• Downward communication: manager to subordinate.\n• Upward communication: subordinate to manager.\n• Horizontal communication: between colleagues or units at similar levels.\n• Formal communication: follows official channels.\n• Informal communication: occurs through unofficial social networks.\n\nBarriers include noise, unclear language, poor listening, delayed feedback and information overload.\n\n9. COORDINATION, SUPERVISION AND CONTROL\nCoordination links related tasks; supervision guides day-to-day work; control measures performance against standards.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO: A retail chain advertises a product without informing its warehouse. Customers arrive, but the product is unavailable. The immediate problem is poor coordination between marketing and stock control. The remedy includes shared inventory information and approval procedures.\n\n10. BUSINESS ETHICS AND MANAGEMENT\nManagers should consider honest accounting, customer safety, fair treatment of employees, compliance with law and responsible use of company assets. A profitable decision is not necessarily ethical or lawful.\n\n11. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which function compares actual performance with planned targets? Answer: Controlling.\nQ2. A manager assigns a task but remains answerable for the result. What process? Answer: Delegation.\nQ3. What is span of control? Answer: Number of direct subordinates reporting to a manager.\nQ4. A worker receives conflicting instructions from two supervisors. Which classical principle is affected? Answer: Unity of command.\nQ5. Branch managers may approve local purchases. What management arrangement? Answer: Decentralisation.\nQ6. A manager invites staff contributions before deciding. What leadership style? Answer: Democratic.\nQ7. What communication direction describes a worker reporting a fault to the manager? Answer: Upward.\nQ8. A firm meets sales targets but wastes excessive materials. Is it necessarily efficient? Answer: No. It may be effective but inefficient.\nQ9. Who normally develops broad long-term strategy? Answer: Top management.\nQ10. What function brings departments into harmony? Answer: Coordinating.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Planning precedes action; controlling checks results and corrects deviations.\n• Authority is a right; responsibility is a duty; accountability means answerability.\n• Delegation does not erase a manager's accountability.\n• Staff specialists advise; they do not automatically possess line command.\n• Span of control concerns direct reports, not total company employees.\n• Centralisation is about where decisions are made, not the number of offices.\n• Motivation includes non-financial incentives.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nPLAN → ORGANISE → STAFF → DIRECT → COORDINATE → CONTROL.\nAUTHORITY = RIGHT; RESPONSIBILITY = DUTY; ACCOUNTABILITY = ANSWERABILITY."}},{"id":"business-finance","old":{"title":"Sources of finance and forms of capital","body":"Business finance is money obtained and used to establish, operate or expand a business. A suitable source depends on the purpose, amount, cost, repayment period, security required and effect on control. Long-term assets should not normally depend entirely on a loan that must be repaid immediately.\n\nInternal finance is generated within an existing business, such as retained profit and proceeds from selling unwanted business assets. External finance comes from outside the business, including new share subscriptions, loans, debentures and trade credit. Personal savings can finance a sole trader, but a shareholder's private savings are not retained earnings generated by an incorporated company.\n\nShort-term sources include trade credit, bank overdrafts and accrued tax payable. Tax accruals temporarily retain cash until lawful settlement; tax itself is not income or permission to pay late. An overdraft permits withdrawals beyond an agreed current-account balance, subject to the bank's limit and terms. A bank loan provides a specified sum with repayment conditions. Leasing obtains the use of an asset in return for rental payments without necessarily buying it. Retained earnings are internal, long-term funds, though they can finance working capital.\n\nFixed capital finances durable assets used repeatedly, such as buildings and machinery. Working capital is current assets minus current liabilities. Current assets include stock, debtors, bank and cash when realizable within the operating cycle. Current liabilities are obligations due within that cycle or the relevant short-term period. Owners' equity is assets less liabilities; for a company it includes paid-up share capital and accumulated reserves, subject to other equity items.\n\nHistorical school questions distinguish authorized or nominal capital, issued capital, called-up capital and paid-up capital. Authorized capital was the maximum permitted share capital under the older constitutional framework. Issued capital is the amount offered and allotted; called-up capital is the amount demanded on allotted shares; paid-up capital is the amount actually paid. Modern Nigerian company law uses an issued-share-capital framework, so do not treat an old authorized-capital rule as unchanged present law.\n\nWorked example: current assets of ₦300,000 and current liabilities of ₦180,000 give working capital of ₦120,000. A shareholder loan is a liability, not automatically equity merely because its lender owns shares.\n\nCapital employed is commonly calculated as total assets less current liabilities, or fixed assets plus working capital. Distinguish it from owners’ equity and check whether the question includes long-term borrowing.\n\nA mortgage loan or bond is secured against property. Lenders commonly require security worth more than the loan to allow for recovery risk.\n\nEquivalently, add owners’ equity and long-term debt.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Bank borrowing is not retained profit.\n• Working capital is not all assets or total cash alone.\n• A debenture holder is a lender; a shareholder is an owner."},"new":{"title":"Sources of finance and forms of capital","body":"BUSINESS FINANCE AND CAPITAL — CHOOSING THE RIGHT MONEY FOR THE RIGHT PURPOSE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA profitable business can fail because it lacks cash when bills fall due. A growing business can also become overburdened by borrowing. JAMB examines sources of finance, types of capital, time periods, ownership and borrowing, and the difference between liquidity and profitability.\n\n1. MEANING OF BUSINESS FINANCE\nBusiness finance concerns obtaining, managing and using funds to start, operate and expand an enterprise. Businesses need finance for premises, machinery, raw materials, wages, electricity, stock, advertising and emergencies.\n\nThe appropriate source depends on the amount required, purpose, repayment period, cost, available security, risk and whether owners want to retain control.\n\nMATCH THE TERM TO THE NEED\nShort-term need: paying for stock that will sell next month.\nMedium-term need: acquiring a delivery van.\nLong-term need: building a new factory.\n\nUsing an overdraft repayable on demand to fund a factory that will generate returns over ten years creates a maturity mismatch and may cause financial distress.\n\n2. INTERNAL AND EXTERNAL SOURCES\nINTERNAL FINANCE originates from resources already within the business:\n• Retained earnings: profit kept in the business rather than distributed.\n• Sale of surplus or unwanted assets.\n• Cash released through better inventory or receivables management.\n\nEXTERNAL FINANCE comes from outside:\n• Additional owner contributions or share issues.\n• Bank loans and overdrafts.\n• Trade credit from suppliers.\n• Debentures or other debt securities.\n• Leasing and hire purchase arrangements.\n• Permitted grants or investment from outside financiers.\n\nA sole trader's personal savings may finance the business, but those savings are not retained earnings earned by an existing company.\n\n3. FINANCE BY DURATION\nSHORT-TERM FINANCE is generally repayable within about one year.\nExamples: trade credit, bank overdraft, short-term loan and some accrued expenses.\n\nMEDIUM-TERM FINANCE commonly covers more than one year up to several years.\nExamples: term loans, leasing and hire purchase, depending on contract length.\n\nLONG-TERM FINANCE is intended for major lasting investment.\nExamples: equity shares, long-term debentures, long-term loans and retained earnings.\n\nThese are useful classroom categories; exact year boundaries may vary by textbook and financing agreement.\n\n4. OWNER'S CAPITAL VERSUS BORROWED CAPITAL\nEQUITY or OWNERSHIP FINANCE comes from owners who bear business risk and generally share in residual profits. A company can raise equity by issuing shares. Ordinary shareholders usually have voting rights subject to company law and share class.\n\nDEBT FINANCE creates a contractual obligation to repay principal and usually pay interest. Examples include loans and debentures. Lenders are creditors, not owners merely because they provide money.\n\nCOMPARISON\nEquity: ownership interest; dividends are not automatically guaranteed; no ordinary fixed maturity for share capital; may dilute existing owners' control.\nDebt: creditor relationship; interest and repayment obligations according to terms; may require security; excessive borrowing increases financial risk.\n\n5. BANK OVERDRAFT AND BANK LOAN\nOVERDRAFT: A bank allows a current-account holder to withdraw beyond the account balance up to an agreed limit. It is usually suited to short-term fluctuating cash needs, and charges apply under the agreement.\n\nBANK LOAN: A specified sum is advanced for an agreed period with repayment terms. Loans may be secured or unsecured.\n\nEXAMPLE: A retailer expecting customer payments in two weeks may use a short-term overdraft for stock. A transport business buying a vehicle may prefer a term loan with repayments spread over the asset's useful period.\n\n6. TRADE CREDIT, HIRE PURCHASE AND LEASING\nTRADE CREDIT: A supplier allows the buyer to receive goods now and pay later. It supports working capital but late payment can damage supplier relationships or attract penalties.\n\nHIRE PURCHASE: A customer obtains possession of goods and pays by instalments; ownership usually transfers after the required conditions and final payment are satisfied. The exact legal position depends on the agreement.\n\nLEASING: A business pays for the right to use an asset for an agreed period without necessarily acquiring ownership. Leasing can reduce the immediate cash needed to use equipment.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Possession is not the same as ownership. In a typical hire-purchase arrangement, the hirer uses the item before title passes.\n\n7. TYPES OF CAPITAL\nFIXED CAPITAL: Long-lived productive assets such as factory buildings, machines and commercial vehicles.\n\nWORKING CAPITAL: In financial accounting, current assets minus current liabilities. It helps assess resources available for day-to-day operations.\n\nCURRENT ASSETS may include cash, inventories and trade receivables.\nCURRENT LIABILITIES may include trade payables and short-term debts.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nCash ₦80,000 + Inventory ₦220,000 + Receivables ₦100,000 = Current assets ₦400,000.\nTrade payables ₦150,000 + short-term loan ₦50,000 = Current liabilities ₦200,000.\nWorking capital = ₦400,000 − ₦200,000 = ₦200,000.\n\nIn production economics, circulating capital may refer to raw materials and inputs consumed in the production process. Do not confuse this usage with the accounting formula for net working capital.\n\n8. AUTHORISED, ISSUED, CALLED-UP AND PAID-UP SHARE CAPITAL\nAUTHORISED SHARE CAPITAL is a traditional textbook term for the maximum nominal share capital a company was permitted to issue under its constitutional documents. Modern Nigerian company law has moved away from the old authorised-share-capital framework toward minimum issued share capital; examination questions may still use the traditional vocabulary.\n\nISSUED SHARE CAPITAL: Shares allotted or issued to shareholders.\nCALLED-UP CAPITAL: The amount the company has required shareholders to pay on issued shares.\nPAID-UP CAPITAL: The amount actually paid by shareholders.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA company issues 10,000 shares with nominal value ₦2 each.\nNominal issued capital = 10,000 × ₦2 = ₦20,000.\nIf the company calls ₦1.50 per share, called-up capital = ₦15,000.\nIf shareholders have paid ₦1.20 per share, paid-up capital = ₦12,000.\nUnpaid calls = ₦15,000 − ₦12,000 = ₦3,000.\n\n9. LIQUIDITY, SOLVENCY AND PROFITABILITY\nLIQUIDITY means ability to meet short-term obligations when due.\nSOLVENCY concerns ability to meet overall obligations over the longer term.\nPROFITABILITY measures earnings relative to sales, investment or resources.\n\nA business may report profit while waiting months for customers to pay. It can then struggle to pay suppliers despite being profitable on paper.\n\nCURRENT RATIO = Current assets ÷ Current liabilities.\nUsing the earlier figures: ₦400,000 ÷ ₦200,000 = 2:1.\n\nA current ratio of 2:1 is not universally ideal; the appropriate level depends on the industry and quality of assets.\n\n10. COST OF FINANCE AND FINANCIAL RISK\nBorrowing can allow expansion without surrendering ownership, but interest must usually be paid even when sales disappoint. Equity may avoid fixed interest obligations but can dilute control and require sharing future returns.\n\nA prudent manager considers:\n• Effective borrowing cost and fees.\n• Timing of repayments.\n• Expected cash inflows.\n• Asset life.\n• Security or collateral requirements.\n• Sensitivity to changing sales and interest rates.\n\n11. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Profit retained for business expansion is what source? Answer: Internal finance, specifically retained earnings.\nQ2. A bank permits withdrawals above an agreed current-account balance. What facility? Answer: Overdraft.\nQ3. Goods are supplied now with payment due in 30 days. What source? Answer: Trade credit.\nQ4. What is the principal difference between shareholders and debenture holders? Answer: Owners versus creditors.\nQ5. Current assets ₦600,000 and current liabilities ₦250,000. Working capital? Answer: ₦350,000.\nQ6. Using those figures, current ratio? Answer: 600,000 ÷ 250,000 = 2.4:1.\nQ7. Which arrangement usually gives possession before eventual ownership on completion of instalments? Answer: Hire purchase.\nQ8. Is an accounting profit the same as cash available in the bank? Answer: No.\nQ9. Which source may dilute existing shareholders' ownership? Answer: New equity shares.\nQ10. What financing risk arises when a long-term asset is funded entirely by an overdraft? Answer: Short-term repayment may fall due before the asset generates sufficient cash.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Working capital = current assets MINUS current liabilities, not total fixed assets.\n• An overdraft differs from a term loan.\n• Interest belongs to borrowing; dividends are returns on shares when declared.\n• Hire purchase possession does not necessarily mean immediate ownership.\n• Profit and liquidity are different.\n• Old authorised-share-capital terminology must be distinguished from current Nigerian legal requirements.\n• Retained earnings belong to an existing business; personal savings are a separate source.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nSHORT-TERM NEED → SHORT-TERM FINANCE.\nLONG-TERM ASSET → SUITABLE LONG-TERM FUNDING.\nWORKING CAPITAL = CURRENT ASSETS − CURRENT LIABILITIES."}},{"id":"trade-retailing-wholesaling","old":{"title":"Home trade, retailers and wholesalers","body":"Home trade is buying and selling within a country. It includes wholesale and retail trade. Wholesale trade normally involves buying in large quantities and selling to retailers or other business users. Retail trade sells goods to final consumers, usually in smaller quantities. The customer's purpose matters as well as the size of the purchase.\n\nWholesalers break bulk, store goods, provide market information, bear some trading risks and may give credit to retailers. They help producers reach numerous buyers without managing every small transaction. Retailers assemble goods conveniently for consumers, explain products, provide local access and may offer credit or after-sales service. Both wholesalers and retailers can break bulk at different stages.\n\nA merchant wholesaler takes title to the goods traded and bears the risks of ownership. An agent generally arranges sales for another person and earns commission without necessarily owning the goods. A broker brings parties together. A factor may sell goods entrusted to the factor and often has possession of them. Do not assume that every intermediary is both owner and agent.\n\nSmall retail outlets include market stalls, kiosks, hawkers and unit shops. A multiple or chain store has branches under common ownership and control. A department store has several departments under one roof, each dealing in a product range. A supermarket emphasizes self-service, while a shopping centre can contain separately owned businesses. Mail-order and online retailing permit purchases without visiting a conventional shop.\n\nMiddlemen may be bypassed when large retailers buy directly, producers sell online or goods are too perishable to pass through many stages. However, eliminating a wholesaler does not eliminate storage, transport, financing or distribution. Someone else must perform the necessary functions and bear their costs.\n\nWorked example: a drinks manufacturer supplies a wholesaler with 500 crates; the wholesaler supplies ten shops with smaller lots; each shop sells bottles to families. This is producer–wholesaler–retailer–consumer distribution. A supermarket buying directly removes one intermediary but still needs delivery and stock management.\n\nHire purchase lets a consumer use goods while paying instalments, with ownership passing according to the agreement, usually after the final payment. This is different from ordinary cash buying; failure to meet terms can lead to repossession under the applicable rules.\n\nManufacturers determine actual product characteristics through materials and production control. Sales regulations impose quality requirements; they do not manufacture the goods.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A chain store is not simply one shop with many departments.\n• A merchant wholesaler owns goods; an agent is not defined by ownership.\n• Raising prices artificially is not a productive service justifying a wholesaler.\n• A shorter channel is not always cheaper once all necessary functions are considered."},"new":{"title":"Home trade, retailers and wholesalers","body":"HOME TRADE, WHOLESALERS AND RETAILERS — HOW GOODS MOVE THROUGH THE MARKET\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA bag of flour can travel from a manufacturer to a wholesaler, then a retailer and finally a household. Sometimes the manufacturer sells directly online instead. JAMB tests the functions of intermediaries, channels of distribution, types of retailing and circumstances in which middlemen can be eliminated.\n\n1. HOME TRADE\nHome or internal trade is buying and selling within the boundaries of a country. It is commonly divided into wholesale and retail trade.\n\nWHOLESALE TRADE generally involves buying in large quantities from producers or other suppliers and selling to retailers, institutions or other business buyers.\n\nRETAIL TRADE generally involves selling goods or services to final consumers for personal or household use, usually in smaller quantities.\n\nThe classification depends largely on the customer's role and the purpose of the transaction, not only on how many items are bought.\n\nEXAMPLE: A restaurant purchasing 50 bags of rice for its kitchen may buy through wholesale channels. A household buying one bag from a neighbourhood shop is engaging in retail trade.\n\n2. CHANNELS OF DISTRIBUTION\nA channel of distribution is the route through which goods pass from producers to users.\n\nCOMMON CHANNELS\nProducer → Consumer.\nProducer → Retailer → Consumer.\nProducer → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer.\nProducer → Agent → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer.\n\nA longer channel is not automatically worse. It may be useful when producers need wide geographic coverage or when many small retailers require regular supply.\n\nDIRECT DISTRIBUTION occurs when producers sell straight to consumers. INDIRECT DISTRIBUTION involves one or more intermediaries.\n\n3. FUNCTIONS OF A WHOLESALER\nA wholesaler may:\n• Buy goods in bulk from manufacturers.\n• Break bulk into quantities suitable for retailers.\n• Store goods and smooth seasonal supply.\n• Finance some distribution by buying ahead of retail sales.\n• Provide trade credit to retailers.\n• Bear ownership and storage risks where goods are purchased outright.\n• Supply market information to producers.\n• Sort, grade, package or assemble goods from different sources.\n• Arrange transportation and delivery.\n\nEXAMPLE: A beverage wholesaler buys 2,000 cartons from a factory and supplies 20 shops with 100 cartons each. The wholesaler breaks bulk and saves the manufacturer from handling many small orders.\n\n4. FUNCTIONS OF A RETAILER\nRetailers:\n• Make goods accessible near consumers.\n• Sell in convenient quantities.\n• Assemble assortments from different producers.\n• Explain products and advise buyers.\n• Display goods and advertise locally.\n• Offer selected credit, delivery or after-sales service.\n• Provide consumer feedback to suppliers.\n• Hold stock for immediate purchase.\n\nA retailer may also break bulk. It is incorrect to say that only wholesalers do so.\n\n5. TYPES OF WHOLESALERS AND INTERMEDIARIES\nMERCHANT WHOLESALER: Buys goods, takes ownership and resells at a margin.\n\nAGENT: Acts on behalf of a principal and may receive commission. An agent does not necessarily take ownership of the goods.\n\nBROKER: Brings buyers and sellers together and helps arrange a transaction, often for commission.\n\nFACTOR: Traditionally a commercial agent entrusted with possession of goods for sale and sometimes authorised to sell in the agent's own name. The exact powers depend on the arrangement.\n\nMANUFACTURERS' AGENT: Represents producers in securing sales.\n\nJAMB TRAP: An agent arranging a sale is not necessarily the owner of the goods. Ownership and agency are separate questions.\n\n6. TYPES OF RETAIL OUTLETS\nSMALL-SCALE RETAILING:\n• Market stall or kiosk.\n• Hawking or mobile vending.\n• Neighbourhood shop.\n• Speciality shop serving a narrow product category.\n\nLARGE-SCALE OR ORGANISED RETAILING:\n• Supermarket: self-service store, often with food and household items.\n• Department store: different departments selling varied product groups.\n• Chain or multiple shops: branches under common ownership.\n• Shopping centre: a complex housing different independent or chain retailers.\n• Hypermarket: very large store combining supermarket and general merchandise features.\n\nNON-STORE RETAILING:\n• Online and mobile commerce.\n• Mail-order selling.\n• Vending machines.\n• Door-to-door or direct selling.\n\nA shopping centre is not itself necessarily a department store: one contains several businesses; the other is usually one retailer divided into departments.\n\n7. ELIMINATION OF THE MIDDLEMAN\nDisintermediation means removing one or more intermediaries from a distribution channel.\n\nWhy it may happen:\n• Producers operate their own stores or websites.\n• Large retailers buy directly from factories.\n• Digital platforms connect producers and consumers.\n• Goods are perishable and need rapid delivery.\n• Buyers place sufficiently large orders.\n\nPossible advantages: fewer margins, closer customer contact and faster feedback.\nPossible disadvantages: producers must handle storage, transport, customer service and small transactions themselves.\n\nEXAMPLE: A poultry farm sells eggs directly to households using its own delivery van. It eliminates a wholesaler, but now bears delivery and order-processing costs.\n\n8. FACTORS AFFECTING CHANNEL CHOICE\n• Nature of goods: perishability, bulk and fragility.\n• Market size and geographic spread.\n• Availability and cost of intermediaries.\n• Producer's financial and storage capacity.\n• Desired control over pricing and customer experience.\n• Frequency and size of orders.\n• Competition and delivery speed.\n\nPerishable vegetables may favour shorter, faster channels. Durable packaged goods may pass through multiple intermediaries.\n\n9. PRICING AND TRADE MARGINS\nA trade margin is the difference between selling price and purchase cost before accounting for other operating expenses.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA retailer buys a carton for ₦18,000 and sells it for ₦22,000.\nGross margin amount = ₦22,000 − ₦18,000 = ₦4,000.\nGross margin on selling price = ₦4,000 ÷ ₦22,000 × 100 ≈ 18.18%.\nMarkup on cost = ₦4,000 ÷ ₦18,000 × 100 ≈ 22.22%.\n\nMargin and markup are not identical because they use different denominators. The ₦4,000 is not necessarily net profit; rent, transport and other expenses may remain.\n\n10. MODERN RETAIL AND CONSUMER PROTECTION\nRetailers increasingly combine physical shops, online ordering, electronic payments and delivery services. Regardless of the channel, honest descriptions, accurate pricing, safe products and appropriate handling of customer complaints matter.\n\n11. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Who usually buys in bulk and sells to retailers? Answer: Wholesaler.\nQ2. Who commonly sells to final consumers? Answer: Retailer.\nQ3. What does breaking bulk mean? Answer: Dividing large quantities into smaller saleable lots.\nQ4. A person introduces a buyer to a seller for commission without taking ownership. What role? Answer: Broker or agent, depending on the stated authority.\nQ5. A producer sells directly through its website. What happens to the channel? Answer: It becomes more direct; an intermediary may be eliminated.\nQ6. What retail outlet typically uses self-service for groceries and household goods? Answer: Supermarket.\nQ7. A single company owns 30 similar stores. What arrangement? Answer: Chain or multiple shops.\nQ8. A trader buys for ₦8,000 and sells for ₦10,000. Gross margin amount? Answer: ₦2,000.\nQ9. Is that ₦2,000 automatically net profit? Answer: No, operating expenses must still be considered.\nQ10. Why may a producer retain wholesalers? Answer: Bulk purchasing, storage, wider market coverage and reduced handling of small orders.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Wholesale and retail are classified by their commercial function, not solely quantity.\n• Both wholesalers and retailers can break bulk.\n• Agents need not own the goods they sell for principals.\n• A shopping centre differs from a department store.\n• Removing intermediaries does not remove distribution costs.\n• Markup uses cost as denominator; gross margin percentage uses selling price.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nWHOLESALER → BULK, STORAGE, RETAILER SUPPLY.\nRETAILER → FINAL CONSUMER, CONVENIENCE, ASSORTMENT.\nCHANNEL = ROUTE FROM PRODUCER TO USER."}},{"id":"trade-documents-payments","old":{"title":"Buying procedures, trade documents and credit instruments","body":"A normal buying procedure begins with an enquiry about goods and terms. The seller may reply with a quotation, price list or catalogue. The buyer sends an order specifying goods required. An invoice states the goods supplied, quantities, prices, discounts and amount payable. A delivery note accompanies the goods and helps the receiver check delivery; it is not proof that the invoice has been paid.\n\nA pro forma invoice gives details and prices before a completed sale, often to support an advance payment or an import arrangement. It is not an ordinary demand for payment for goods already delivered. A statement of account summarizes transactions and the balance owing over a period. A receipt acknowledges payment. An internal requisition requests goods or purchasing action within an organization.\n\nA credit note reduces the buyer's debt, for example when goods are returned or an overcharge is corrected. A debit note can request or record an additional charge, according to the transaction. Always check whose books and whose document the question describes. E and OE means errors and omissions excepted: accidental mistakes may be corrected; it is not a licence for fraud.\n\nA cheque is an order by an account holder directing a bank to pay a stated sum. A bill of exchange is an unconditional written order directing another person to pay a specified amount at an agreed time. A promissory note is a written promise by its maker to pay. Acceptance indicates the drawee's agreement to pay a bill. Discounting a bill means obtaining money before maturity, less a charge, from a willing financial institution.\n\nCredit permits goods or funds to be obtained now with payment later. It can increase sales and enable production before cash is received. However, it creates collection costs, default risk and possible overborrowing. Cash transactions avoid some credit risk but may restrict purchasing power.\n\nWorked example: a shop receives an invoice for ₦80,000, returns damaged goods worth ₦5,000 and receives a credit note. The invoice balance becomes ₦75,000 before any other payment or adjustment.\n\nA negotiable instrument can transfer payment rights by the legally required delivery or endorsement, subject to its form and rules. Cash with order, CWO, requires payment when ordering; cash on delivery requires it when goods arrive.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An invoice records an amount charged; a receipt records payment.\n• An order requests goods; a requisition is generally an internal request.\n• A promise to pay and an order to pay are different credit instruments."},"new":{"title":"Buying procedures, trade documents and credit instruments","body":"TRADE DOCUMENTS, BUYING PROCEDURES AND PAYMENT INSTRUMENTS — FOLLOW THE PAPER TRAIL\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB frequently asks students to identify the document used at a particular stage of a sale. An invoice, quotation, receipt and statement of account may contain similar details, but they serve different purposes. The safest method is to follow the transaction in chronological order and ask what each document proves.\n\n1. THE NORMAL BUYING AND SELLING PROCEDURE\nA typical transaction may proceed as follows:\nENQUIRY → QUOTATION → ORDER → DELIVERY → INVOICE → PAYMENT → RECEIPT.\n\nThis is a teaching sequence, not a rigid legal requirement. Some sellers issue invoices before delivery, request advance payment or use electronic order confirmations.\n\nENQUIRY: Buyer asks about price, availability, quality or delivery terms.\nQUOTATION: Seller communicates a proposed price and conditions for specified goods or services.\nORDER: Buyer specifies the goods or services requested, quantity and terms.\nDELIVERY NOTE: Accompanies delivered goods and helps the buyer check what arrived.\nINVOICE: Seller's bill showing goods supplied, quantities, prices, discounts, charges and amount payable.\nRECEIPT: Acknowledges that payment has been received.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO: A school asks a stationery supplier for the price of 100 exercise books (enquiry). The supplier responds with prices and delivery terms (quotation). The school confirms its purchase (order). The books arrive with a delivery note. The supplier issues an invoice. After payment, the supplier issues a receipt.\n\n2. PRE-SALE DOCUMENTS\nLETTER OF ENQUIRY: Formal request for information about products and terms.\n\nPRICE LIST: General list of products and prices; unlike a tailored quotation, it may not address one buyer's specific request.\n\nCATALOGUE: Describes available products, often with specifications and illustrations.\n\nQUOTATION: Proposed price and terms for a particular supply. Its legal effect depends on wording and circumstances; a quotation is not always a binding contract by itself.\n\nTENDER: A formal offer submitted in response to an invitation to bid, commonly for substantial supplies or projects.\n\nPRO FORMA INVOICE: A preliminary document resembling an invoice, issued before a completed sale or shipment to indicate expected goods, prices and terms. It may support advance payment, import procedures or financing.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A pro forma invoice is not ordinarily proof that goods have already been supplied.\n\n3. DOCUMENTS DURING AND AFTER DELIVERY\nPURCHASE ORDER: Buyer's formal instruction to supply specified goods.\n\nDELIVERY NOTE: Details goods delivered; signed acknowledgement may confirm receipt of items, but does not itself prove payment.\n\nINVOICE: Records the seller's claim for the price of goods or services supplied under the transaction.\n\nSTATEMENT OF ACCOUNT: Periodic summary of transactions, payments, credits and outstanding balance between seller and buyer.\n\nRECEIPT: Evidence that payment has been received.\n\nGOODS RECEIVED NOTE: Internal record confirming goods received and checked by a business.\n\nREQUISITION: Internal request for stock or purchasing action; it is not the same as an order sent to an outside supplier.\n\n4. CREDIT NOTE AND DEBIT NOTE\nCREDIT NOTE: Commonly issued by a seller to reduce the amount owed by a buyer, such as when goods are returned, a price is reduced or an overcharge is corrected.\n\nDEBIT NOTE: Can notify a party of an additional charge or adjustment, depending on which party issues it and the accounting context. Some textbook examples use a debit note from a buyer to notify a supplier of returned goods. Always read who issued the document and why.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nInvoice total = ₦75,000.\nBuyer returns damaged goods valued at ₦10,000.\nSeller issues credit note = ₦10,000.\nAdjusted amount due = ₦75,000 − ₦10,000 = ₦65,000.\n\nThe credit note is not the same as a receipt. It reduces a debt; a receipt acknowledges payment.\n\n5. E. & O.E. AND TERMS OF SALE\nE. & O.E. stands for Errors and Omissions Excepted. It indicates that accidental errors or omissions in a document may be corrected. It is not permission to deceive buyers or ignore contractual obligations.\n\nCASH SALE: Payment occurs at or around purchase.\nCREDIT SALE: Buyer is allowed to pay later under agreed terms.\nC.O.D.: Cash on delivery, meaning payment is made when goods are delivered.\nC.I.F.: Cost, Insurance and Freight, a trade term commonly used in international shipping; responsibilities are governed by the applicable Incoterms rules and agreement.\nF.O.B.: Free on Board, another international trade term specifying responsibilities at the named port under applicable rules.\n\nDo not confuse a document's purpose with the timing of payment.\n\n6. TRADE DISCOUNTS AND CASH DISCOUNTS\nTRADE DISCOUNT is a reduction from a listed price, commonly offered to traders or for quantity purchases. It is usually deducted in calculating the invoice price.\n\nCASH DISCOUNT encourages prompt payment under specified terms.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nList price = ₦100,000.\nTrade discount 10% = ₦10,000.\nNet invoice price = ₦90,000.\nIf a further 5% cash discount is allowed for prompt payment, it is calculated on ₦90,000:\nCash discount = ₦4,500.\nAmount paid = ₦85,500.\n\nDo not add 10% and 5% and simply deduct 15% from the original price: successive discounts use different bases.\n\n7. PAYMENT INSTRUMENTS\nCASH: Notes and coins accepted for immediate payment.\n\nCHEQUE: A written order directing a bank to pay a stated sum from the drawer's account to the payee or bearer, subject to applicable rules.\n\nBANK TRANSFER: Electronic movement of funds between accounts.\n\nBANK DRAFT: A payment instrument issued by a bank, generally involving the bank's undertaking under its terms.\n\nDIRECT DEBIT: Authorised collection of payment from a payer's account, often for recurring bills.\n\nSTANDING ORDER: Instruction from an account holder to the bank to make regular payments of a specified amount or under specified arrangements.\n\nDEBIT CARD: Payment generally draws on available funds in the linked account, subject to account arrangements.\nCREDIT CARD: Payment is made using a credit facility and repaid under agreed terms.\n\n8. PARTIES TO A CHEQUE\nDRAWER: Person who writes and signs the cheque.\nDRAWEE: Bank directed to pay.\nPAYEE: Person entitled to receive payment.\n\nEXAMPLE: Ada writes a cheque from her account at Bank A to pay Chidi. Ada is the drawer; Bank A is the drawee; Chidi is the payee.\n\n9. CROSSING AND ENDORSEMENT OF CHEQUES\nA crossed cheque has markings that generally direct payment through a bank account rather than cashing over the counter, subject to applicable rules.\n\nA general crossing commonly uses two parallel lines; a special crossing names a bank. Words such as 'account payee' further restrict how the cheque should be collected.\n\nEndorsement is signing or otherwise properly transferring certain negotiable instruments. Not every crossed or account-payee cheque can be freely transferred.\n\n10. BILLS OF EXCHANGE AND PROMISSORY NOTES\nBILL OF EXCHANGE: A written unconditional order by one party directing another to pay a certain sum to a specified person or bearer on demand or at a determinable future time, subject to legal requirements.\n\nPROMISSORY NOTE: A written unconditional promise by the maker to pay a certain sum to a specified person or bearer under the relevant law.\n\nKEY DISTINCTION: A bill orders someone else to pay; a promissory note contains the maker's promise to pay.\n\nParties commonly described:\nBill: drawer, drawee, payee; the drawee may become acceptor.\nPromissory note: maker and payee.\n\n11. TRADE CREDIT AND PAYMENT RISK\nCredit sales can increase sales but create risk of late payment or default. Businesses may check customer creditworthiness, set credit limits, agree due dates, issue statements and follow up overdue accounts.\n\nElectronic payments reduce the need for cash handling but require accurate account details, transaction verification and fraud controls.\n\n12. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which document is a preliminary statement of expected charges before a completed sale? Answer: Pro forma invoice.\nQ2. Which document acknowledges payment? Answer: Receipt.\nQ3. Which document normally reduces the amount a buyer owes after returning goods? Answer: Credit note from the seller.\nQ4. Which document summarises account transactions over a period? Answer: Statement of account.\nQ5. Which document usually accompanies goods for checking at delivery? Answer: Delivery note.\nQ6. Who is the drawee of a cheque? Answer: The bank directed to pay.\nQ7. What does E. & O.E. mean? Answer: Errors and Omissions Excepted.\nQ8. What is the key difference between a bill of exchange and promissory note? Answer: An order to pay versus a promise to pay.\nQ9. A seller quotes ₦40,000 less 10% trade discount. Net price? Answer: ₦36,000.\nQ10. Does an invoice prove the buyer has paid? Answer: No. A receipt or verified payment record provides evidence of payment.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Quotation comes before purchase; invoice records a bill for a transaction.\n• Pro forma invoice is preliminary, not an ordinary post-delivery invoice.\n• Delivery note proves neither full payment nor ownership by itself.\n• Credit note reduces debt; receipt acknowledges payment.\n• Statement of account is a summary, not a single-transaction invoice.\n• Drawer writes the cheque; drawee is the bank; payee receives payment.\n• Bill of exchange = order; promissory note = promise.\n• Successive discounts are calculated in sequence.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nASK → QUOTE → ORDER → DELIVER → BILL → PAY → RECEIPT.\nCREDIT NOTE = REDUCE THE BILL.\nRECEIPT = PAYMENT ACKNOWLEDGED."}},{"id":"discounts-profit-ratios","old":{"title":"Discounts, profit, working capital and ratios","body":"A trade discount is a reduction from a list price, commonly allowed because of a buyer's trade status or distribution role. A quantity discount encourages a sufficiently large purchase. A cash discount rewards prompt payment. These discounts have different purposes and must not automatically be combined into one percentage.\n\nApply successive discounts to the balance remaining after the previous discount unless the question expressly states another basis. A 10% trade discount followed by a 5% cash discount is not normally a 15% reduction from the original amount. Terms such as 2/10, net 30 mean a 2% cash discount for payment within 10 days; otherwise the net invoice amount falls due within 30 days. Explain an unclear printed term rather than inventing missing conditions.\n\nWorked example: goods have a list value of ₦100,000. A 10% trade discount is ₦10,000, leaving ₦90,000. A 5% prompt-payment discount on ₦90,000 is ₦4,500. The buyer pays ₦85,500. The total reduction is ₦14,500, not ₦15,000. If a quantity discount is also allowed, identify exactly when and on what amount it applies.\n\nGross profit is sales less cost of goods sold. Cost of goods sold is opening stock plus net purchases and relevant carriage inwards, less closing stock. Net profit is gross profit plus other income, less operating expenses. Turnover usually means total sales over a period in school Commerce; do not confuse it with profit or the stock-turnover ratio.\n\nMark-up expresses gross profit as a percentage of cost. Margin expresses gross profit as a percentage of selling price. If goods cost ₦80 and sell for ₦100, gross profit is ₦20, mark-up is 25% and margin is 20%. The same profit therefore produces different percentages because the denominators differ.\n\nThe current ratio is current assets divided by current liabilities. The acid-test ratio usually excludes stock from current assets before division. Working capital is the difference between current assets and current liabilities, not a ratio. Owners' equity equals total assets less external liabilities.\n\nThe stock-turnover rate is cost of goods sold divided by average stock. Average stock is opening stock plus closing stock, divided by two. A conflicting printed gross-profit figure should be disclosed rather than silently substituted.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Add and classify balance-sheet figures before calculating a ratio.\n• A percentage discount amount is not the final amount paid.\n• Trade discount and cash discount are not interchangeable.\n• Show the calculation if a booklet answer conflicts with the stated numbers."},"new":{"title":"Discounts, profit, working capital and ratios","body":"DISCOUNTS, PROFIT, WORKING CAPITAL AND RATIOS — CALCULATE, THEN INTERPRET\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB Commerce calculations often look simple but test whether you choose the correct base. A discount is not always calculated on the original price, markup is not the same as margin, and a profitable business can still lack cash. Work carefully through each stage.\n\n1. TYPES OF DISCOUNT\nA trade discount reduces a list price, commonly for a trade buyer. A quantity discount rewards larger purchases. A cash discount encourages payment within an agreed time. A seasonal discount encourages buying outside peak periods. A discount is a price reduction; its purpose determines its name.\n\nEXAMPLE: A wholesaler lists goods at ₦80,000 and allows 15% trade discount. Discount = 0.15 × ₦80,000 = ₦12,000. Net invoice price = ₦68,000.\n\n2. SUCCESSIVE DISCOUNTS\nDiscounts applied one after another use the remaining balance, unless the question states otherwise.\nA list price of ₦100,000 attracts 20% trade discount and then 5% cash discount.\nAfter trade discount: ₦100,000 × 0.80 = ₦80,000.\nCash discount: ₦80,000 × 0.05 = ₦4,000.\nFinal payment: ₦76,000.\nTotal reduction = ₦24,000, or 24% of the original—not 25%.\n\nTerms '2/10, net 30' mean the buyer may deduct 2% if payment is made within 10 days; otherwise the full net amount is due within 30 days. Always identify when the discount period starts under the terms given.\n\n3. COST PRICE, SELLING PRICE AND GROSS PROFIT\nCost of goods sold (COGS) is the cost of goods actually sold, not necessarily all goods purchased.\nGross profit = Net sales − Cost of goods sold.\nNet sales = Sales − Sales returns.\nCOGS = Opening inventory + Net purchases + Carriage inward − Closing inventory, where relevant.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nOpening stock ₦30,000; purchases ₦150,000; purchases returns ₦10,000; carriage inward ₦5,000; closing stock ₦25,000.\nNet purchases = ₦140,000.\nCOGS = ₦30,000 + ₦140,000 + ₦5,000 − ₦25,000 = ₦150,000.\nIf net sales are ₦210,000, gross profit = ₦60,000.\n\n4. NET PROFIT AND OPERATING EXPENSES\nNet profit is the amount remaining after relevant expenses and other income or charges have been accounted for.\nFor a simple trading business:\nNet profit = Gross profit + Other income − Operating expenses.\nIf gross profit is ₦60,000, rent ₦15,000, salaries ₦20,000 and other expenses ₦5,000, net profit = ₦20,000.\n\nGross profit is not net profit. Selling goods above their purchase cost does not guarantee overall profit.\n\n5. MARKUP VERSUS MARGIN\nMarkup percentage = Gross profit on an item ÷ Cost price × 100.\nGross margin percentage = Gross profit ÷ Selling price × 100.\n\nEXAMPLE: Buy for ₦8,000; sell for ₦10,000. Profit = ₦2,000.\nMarkup = 2,000 ÷ 8,000 × 100 = 25%.\nGross margin = 2,000 ÷ 10,000 × 100 = 20%.\nThe same transaction gives different percentages because the denominators differ.\n\n6. PROFITABILITY RATIOS\nGross profit margin = Gross profit ÷ Net sales × 100.\nNet profit margin = Net profit ÷ Net sales × 100.\nReturn on capital employed (ROCE) is commonly operating profit ÷ capital employed × 100; textbooks may specify slightly different measures of profit and capital, so follow the question's definitions.\n\nEXAMPLE: Net profit ₦45,000 on net sales ₦300,000 gives a net profit margin of 15%.\n\n7. WORKING CAPITAL AND LIQUIDITY\nWorking capital = Current assets − Current liabilities.\nCurrent assets include cash, inventory and receivables. Current liabilities include payables and short-term debts.\nCurrent ratio = Current assets ÷ Current liabilities.\nQuick or acid-test ratio = (Current assets − Inventory) ÷ Current liabilities, in the usual simplified textbook formula.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nCurrent assets ₦500,000 including inventory ₦200,000; current liabilities ₦250,000.\nWorking capital = ₦250,000.\nCurrent ratio = 500,000 ÷ 250,000 = 2:1.\nQuick ratio = (500,000 − 200,000) ÷ 250,000 = 1.2:1.\n\nA high ratio does not automatically mean excellent liquidity if much of the current assets consist of slow-moving inventory or uncollectible receivables.\n\n8. STOCK TURNOVER\nInventory turnover measures how often stock is sold and replaced over a period.\nInventory turnover = Cost of goods sold ÷ Average inventory.\nAverage inventory = (Opening inventory + Closing inventory) ÷ 2.\n\nEXAMPLE: COGS ₦600,000; opening inventory ₦80,000; closing inventory ₦120,000.\nAverage inventory = ₦100,000.\nTurnover = 600,000 ÷ 100,000 = 6 times.\n\nHigher turnover may indicate efficient stock movement, but extremely low inventory can also lead to stockouts. Compare similar businesses and periods.\n\n9. CASH FLOW VERSUS PROFIT\nProfit is measured under accounting rules and may include credit sales. Cash flow records actual receipts and payments.\nA business sells ₦200,000 of goods on credit and records profit but may have no immediate cash from the sale. It still needs money to pay wages and suppliers.\n\n10. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. List price ₦50,000 less 10% trade discount. Net price? ₦45,000.\nQ2. A further 10% discount on ₦45,000 gives what payment? ₦40,500.\nQ3. Goods cost ₦4,000 and sell for ₦5,000. Markup? 25%.\nQ4. For the same goods, gross margin? 20%.\nQ5. Gross profit ₦70,000; expenses ₦45,000. Net profit? ₦25,000, assuming no other items.\nQ6. Current assets ₦90,000; liabilities ₦60,000. Working capital? ₦30,000.\nQ7. Current assets ₦90,000; liabilities ₦60,000. Current ratio? 1.5:1.\nQ8. Which discount rewards prompt payment? Cash discount.\nQ9. Is sales revenue identical to profit? No; costs and expenses must be deducted.\nQ10. Why can a profitable trader run short of cash? Credit sales, delayed collections or immediate payment obligations.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Calculate successive discounts sequentially.\n• Markup uses cost; margin uses selling price.\n• Gross profit excludes many operating expenses.\n• Inventory turnover uses cost of goods sold, not sales revenue, in the standard formula.\n• Current ratio and working capital use the same balance-sheet categories but give different measures.\n• Profit does not equal cash in hand.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nPROFIT = REVENUE MINUS RELEVANT COSTS.\nMARKUP → COST BASE. MARGIN → SALES BASE.\nWORKING CAPITAL = CURRENT ASSETS − CURRENT LIABILITIES."}},{"id":"banking-money","old":{"title":"Money, banking and bank accounts","body":"Money is anything generally accepted as a means of payment for goods and services and settlement of debts. Its main functions are medium of exchange, measure of value or unit of account, store of value and standard of deferred payment. Money facilitates specialization because a seller need not find someone who both wants the seller's product and offers the exact product desired.\n\nGood money should be generally acceptable, portable, durable, divisible, recognizable and reasonably stable in value. Scarcity relative to demand helps protect value, but scarcity alone does not make an object money. Legal tender is money a creditor is required by law to accept in the relevant circumstances; not every useful payment instrument is itself legal tender.\n\nCommercial banks accept deposits, provide payment services and lend to customers. A current account supports frequent business transactions and cheque or equivalent payment facilities, subject to the bank's arrangements. A savings account encourages saving and commonly earns interest. A fixed-deposit account places money for an agreed period, normally earning an agreed return subject to withdrawal conditions.\n\nA central bank manages currency and monetary policy, serves as banker to government and banks, holds relevant reserves and acts as lender of last resort under its legal framework. It is not an ordinary retail shop offering every member of the public a current account. Public-debt management and government financing arrangements must be read in their institutional and historical context.\n\nSpecialized institutions include development banks, mortgage institutions and microfinance banks. Development finance supports investment and economic development; mortgage finance concerns property-related borrowing. A bureau de change conducts permitted foreign-exchange transactions. Its name does not make it the authority that creates a country's monetary policy.\n\nElectronic banking includes transfers, cards, automated teller machines and online account services. A standing order instructs a bank to make specified recurring payments. A bank giro or credit transfer moves funds from one account to another. Security requires protecting passwords, verifying recipients and recognizing fraudulent messages.\n\nWorked example: a trader paid by bank transfer receives a bank-account credit. This is a payment mechanism, not proof that the trader has received a new bank loan.\n\nBanking supplies intangible financial services rather than manufacturing physical merchandise. Financial instruments can be traded, but they represent claims, obligations or payment instructions.\n\nA cashier’s cheque is drawn by a bank on itself. A bank draft can be drawn on another bank; terminology varies. Current-account fees include bank charges or historical commission on turnover.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An overdraft is borrowing, not the account holder's savings.\n• Bank rate terminology in historical papers concerns central-bank lending terms.\n• Money's exchange function differs from simply measuring a product's value."},"new":{"title":"Money, banking and bank accounts","body":"MONEY, BANKING AND BANK ACCOUNTS — HOW PAYMENTS AND CREDIT WORK\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nCommerce depends on reliable payment and financial services. JAMB asks about the functions and qualities of money, the central bank versus commercial banks, bank accounts, payment instruments and the creation of credit. Do not confuse a bank's power to lend with the central bank's authority over currency and monetary policy.\n\n1. WHAT IS MONEY?\nMoney is anything generally accepted as payment for goods and services and settlement of debts. Its acceptance allows exchange without barter's double coincidence of wants.\n\nFUNCTIONS OF MONEY\n• Medium of exchange: used to buy and sell.\n• Unit of account: common measure for recording and comparing prices.\n• Store of value: preserves purchasing power for later use, though inflation may reduce it.\n• Standard of deferred payment: expresses debts payable in the future.\n\nEXAMPLE: A carpenter earns ₦20,000 and uses part to buy food. Money separates the sale of carpentry services from the purchase of food.\n\n2. QUALITIES OF GOOD MONEY\nGeneral acceptability, portability, durability, divisibility, recognisability, relative scarcity and reasonable stability in value are desirable.\nA sack of tomatoes is poor money because it spoils, is bulky and has variable quality. A banknote is more portable and recognisable, although inflation can reduce its value.\n\n3. FORMS OF MONEY\nCommodity money has value as a commodity, such as salt or precious metals in some historical systems.\nFiat money is issued and accepted under a monetary system rather than being redeemable for a fixed commodity quantity.\nBank deposits are balances that can be used for payments through transfers or other authorised methods.\nElectronic money and digital payment balances have rules that depend on their legal and institutional structure.\n\nLEGAL TENDER refers to money that has legally prescribed status for settling monetary obligations under applicable conditions. A debit card is a payment instrument, not itself a banknote or coin.\n\n4. CENTRAL BANK VERSUS COMMERCIAL BANK\nA central bank is the monetary authority responsible for currency issuance under law, monetary policy, financial-system oversight and other statutory functions. In Nigeria, the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) performs central-banking functions.\n\nCommercial banks accept eligible deposits, operate accounts, provide payment services, make loans and offer other financial services under regulation.\n\nCENTRAL BANK FUNCTIONS\n• Issues and manages currency under statutory authority.\n• Conducts monetary policy to influence money and credit conditions.\n• Serves as banker and financial adviser to government.\n• Holds and manages foreign reserves as authorised.\n• Acts as banker to banks and may provide lender-of-last-resort facilities under conditions.\n• Oversees aspects of banking and payments regulation.\n\nCOMMERCIAL BANK FUNCTIONS\n• Accepts deposits.\n• Provides loans and overdrafts.\n• Facilitates payments and transfers.\n• Provides account statements and banking information.\n• Offers trade finance, guarantees and other permitted services.\n\nJAMB TRAP: The central bank does not ordinarily open everyday savings accounts for the general public like a commercial retail bank.\n\n5. TYPES OF BANK ACCOUNTS\nSAVINGS ACCOUNT: Intended mainly for saving, with interest and withdrawal conditions determined by the bank and product.\nCURRENT ACCOUNT: Used for frequent business and personal transactions; may support cheques and agreed overdrafts.\nFIXED OR TERM DEPOSIT: Funds placed for an agreed period, usually with specified interest and early-withdrawal conditions.\nDOMICILIARY ACCOUNT: Account denominated in a foreign currency, subject to applicable bank and regulatory rules.\n\nAccount terms change between banks; identify the purpose and contractual conditions rather than assuming all products are identical.\n\n6. CHEQUES AND PAYMENT INSTRUMENTS\nCheque: Written order by a drawer directing a bank (drawee) to pay a payee.\nBank transfer: Electronic movement of funds.\nStanding order: Account holder's instruction to make recurring payments.\nDirect debit: Authorised collection initiated by a payee under agreed mandate.\nDebit card: Usually draws from linked available funds.\nCredit card: Uses an agreed credit facility.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Chika instructs her bank to pay ₦15,000 to her landlord every month. That is typically a standing order. If the utility company collects varying authorised monthly bills under a mandate, that is typically a direct debit.\n\n7. BANK LENDING AND CREDIT CREATION\nBanks may expand deposit money through lending, subject to capital, liquidity, regulatory, borrower-demand and risk constraints. A simplified textbook explanation is that a loan credited to a customer's account creates a deposit while the bank records a loan asset.\n\nBanks cannot lend without limit. Loan defaults, liquidity requirements, funding costs and regulation constrain lending.\n\nAn OVERDRAFT permits an account holder to withdraw above the available balance up to an agreed limit. A LOAN usually advances a specified amount with repayment terms.\n\n8. MONETARY POLICY\nMonetary policy is the use of central-bank instruments to influence monetary and financial conditions, often in pursuit of price stability and other statutory objectives.\n\nCommon instruments:\n• Policy interest rate: influences borrowing and deposit rates through the financial system.\n• Open market operations: purchase or sale of eligible securities to affect liquidity.\n• Reserve requirements: rules about funds banks must hold in reserve.\n• Other liquidity and prudential measures as applicable.\n\nCONTRACTIONARY POLICY generally seeks to reduce excessive monetary expansion or inflationary pressure; EXPANSIONARY POLICY generally seeks to ease monetary conditions. The effects are not instantaneous or guaranteed.\n\n9. BANKING SAFETY AND FINANCIAL INCLUSION\nBanks verify customer identity, monitor fraud and secure payment systems. Customers should protect PINs, passwords and one-time codes. Financial inclusion seeks to make suitable financial services available and usable by more people.\n\n10. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which function of money allows comparison of prices? Unit of account.\nQ2. Which bank issues currency under law in Nigeria? Central Bank of Nigeria.\nQ3. Which institution typically accepts everyday retail deposits? Commercial bank.\nQ4. Which account is most associated with frequent business cheques? Current account.\nQ5. Which account generally holds funds for a fixed agreed term? Fixed deposit.\nQ6. A customer withdraws beyond an agreed current-account balance. What facility? Overdraft.\nQ7. What does lender of last resort mean? Conditional central-bank support to eligible financial institutions facing liquidity stress.\nQ8. What monetary instrument involves buying or selling securities? Open market operations.\nQ9. Is a debit card itself legal-tender currency? No; it is a payment instrument.\nQ10. Does inflation preserve money's purchasing power? No; it generally reduces purchasing power.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Money is more than notes and coins; payment balances can perform money functions.\n• Legal tender and electronic payment instruments are not synonyms.\n• Central banks set monetary conditions; commercial banks serve everyday customers.\n• Savings, current and fixed-deposit accounts have different purposes.\n• Banks can create deposit money through lending but not without constraints.\n• A standing order differs from a direct debit.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nMONEY: EXCHANGE, MEASURE, STORE, DEFER.\nCENTRAL BANK: CURRENCY AND MONETARY POLICY.\nCOMMERCIAL BANK: DEPOSITS, PAYMENTS AND CREDIT."}},{"id":"capital-market","old":{"title":"Capital markets, securities and stock exchange","body":"The capital market provides medium- and long-term finance through instruments such as shares, bonds and debentures. The money market deals mainly in short-term instruments. A stock exchange is an organized market with rules for trading admitted securities. It does not create every company's capital or guarantee that every investor will make a profit.\n\nThe primary market concerns newly issued securities. Methods can include a public offer, rights issue or private placement. A prospectus supplies information for a public investment invitation. The secondary market permits investors to trade existing securities. The company does not normally receive the purchase price each time one investor sells an existing share to another.\n\nA share represents an ownership interest in a company. A bond or debenture represents borrowing on stated terms. Some debts are secured against assets; others are unsecured. Convertible debt may be exchanged for shares under its terms. Bearer securities are associated with possession of the instrument, while registered holdings identify the owner in a register. Historical texts may describe inscribed holdings recorded without individual paper certificates.\n\nA stockbroker acts for clients in buying or selling securities and earns fees or commission. A dealer or jobber trades on the dealer's own account. A bull expects prices to rise; a bear expects prices to fall. These describe market expectations or strategies, not companies permanently assigned to one category. Speculation can increase liquidity but does not guarantee price stability.\n\nIn Nigerian historical questions, the Nigerian Stock Exchange provided the organized securities market, while the Securities and Exchange Commission regulated capital-market activities and securities offers. The exchange was reorganized into the Nigerian Exchange structure in 2021. Use the name applicable to the paper's date. Incorporation by the Corporate Affairs Commission is separate from exchange listing.\n\nWorked example: a company sells new shares to raise money for a factory: primary-market finance. Later, Musa sells those shares to Ngozi through a broker: a secondary-market transaction. Both support investment, but only the new issue directly raises fresh funds for the company.\n\nFounders’ or deferred shares traditionally receive their dividend only after specified prior claims or conditions are met. The exact rights depend on their issue terms. A company group can consist of a holding company and its subsidiaries; incorporation alone does not establish exchange listing.\n\nExchange lists report prices and trades. Dividends and appreciation support share value; profitable sales realize capital gains. The historical Second-tier Securities Market helped smaller companies raise capital and attracted investment.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A shareholder is an owner; a bondholder is a creditor.\n• A prospectus is not a bill of exchange.\n• The regulator and the exchange have different functions.\n• Securities may be held electronically; older certificate terminology needs its historical context."},"new":{"title":"Capital markets, securities and stock exchange","body":"CAPITAL MARKETS, SECURITIES AND STOCK EXCHANGE — HOW INVESTORS FUND BUSINESSES\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nBusinesses need long-term funds, while investors seek returns. Capital markets connect them. JAMB tests the difference between primary and secondary markets, money and capital markets, shares and debentures, and the functions of a stock exchange.\n\n1. WHAT IS THE CAPITAL MARKET?\nThe capital market is the segment of the financial system where medium- and long-term funds are raised and securities are issued or traded. Instruments include shares, bonds and debentures.\n\nThe MONEY MARKET deals mainly in short-term borrowing and lending instruments, such as treasury bills and other short-maturity instruments.\nThe CAPITAL MARKET deals mainly in longer-term finance and investment securities.\n\nA stock exchange is an organised marketplace where admitted securities are bought and sold under established rules. It is one part of the wider capital-market system.\n\n2. PRIMARY AND SECONDARY MARKETS\nPRIMARY MARKET: New securities are issued to raise funds for the issuer. Example: a company offers newly issued shares to investors and receives the subscription proceeds.\n\nSECONDARY MARKET: Investors buy and sell existing securities. Example: Ada sells shares she already owns to Musa through an exchange. The company normally does not receive the purchase price from this resale.\n\nWHY THE SECONDARY MARKET MATTERS\nIt provides liquidity: investors can potentially sell investments before the underlying company winds up. Greater liquidity may make investors more willing to buy newly issued securities.\n\n3. TYPES OF SECURITIES\nORDINARY SHARES represent an ownership interest. Ordinary shareholders may receive dividends when declared and may vote subject to applicable rights. They generally bear residual risk: creditors rank ahead of shareholders on winding up.\n\nPREFERENCE SHARES have preferential rights specified by their terms, often relating to dividends and repayment of capital. They are still shares, not automatically the same as a bank loan.\n\nDEBENTURES AND BONDS are debt instruments under which an issuer borrows from investors subject to agreed interest and repayment terms. Holders are creditors rather than owners solely because they hold the debt.\n\nCOMPARISON\nOrdinary share → ownership; dividend not guaranteed; capital value may rise or fall.\nDebenture → debt; interest under contractual terms; repayment obligations according to instrument.\nPreference share → ownership with specified preferences; rights depend on terms.\n\n4. METHODS OF RAISING CAPITAL\nPUBLIC OFFER: Securities are offered to eligible investors under applicable securities rules.\nRIGHTS ISSUE: Existing shareholders are offered the opportunity to subscribe for additional shares, usually in proportion to their holdings.\nPRIVATE PLACEMENT: Securities are offered to a selected group of investors under applicable rules.\nBONUS ISSUE: Additional shares are allotted to existing shareholders from eligible reserves; it does not by itself bring fresh cash into the company.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A company has 100,000 shares and announces a rights issue of one new share for every four held. A shareholder with 800 shares is entitled to subscribe for 800 ÷ 4 = 200 new shares under the stated ratio, subject to the offer terms.\n\n5. STOCK EXCHANGE FUNCTIONS\n• Provides an organised market for trading admitted securities.\n• Helps investors buy and sell through authorised participants.\n• Supports price discovery through trading.\n• Requires disclosures and market conduct under relevant rules.\n• Can improve access to capital and investment opportunities.\n• Provides information about traded securities.\n\nA stock exchange does not guarantee profit or prevent all losses. Share prices can rise or fall with company results, economic conditions and investor expectations.\n\n6. STOCKBROKERS AND MARKET PARTICIPANTS\nA stockbroker is a licensed intermediary who executes securities transactions for clients under relevant rules. Dealers may trade for their own account. Registrars maintain specified ownership records and handle related corporate actions. Custodians safeguard financial assets and administer them for clients.\n\nThe exact duties depend on market arrangements and regulation.\n\n7. DIVIDENDS, INTEREST AND CAPITAL GAINS\nDIVIDEND: Distribution to shareholders when properly declared.\nINTEREST: Contractual return commonly associated with debt instruments.\nCAPITAL GAIN: Increase realised when an asset is sold above its acquisition cost, ignoring fees and taxes in a simplified question.\n\nEXAMPLE: Buy shares for ₦40,000; sell for ₦46,000. Simplified capital gain = ₦6,000, or 15% of purchase cost before expenses. If the price falls, the investor may incur a capital loss.\n\n8. RISKS OF SECURITIES INVESTMENT\n• Market risk: prices change.\n• Credit risk: a debt issuer may fail to pay.\n• Liquidity risk: an investment may be difficult to sell promptly at a fair price.\n• Inflation risk: returns may not keep up with rising prices.\n• Business risk: poor company performance can reduce returns.\n• Interest-rate risk: changes in market rates can affect bond prices.\n\nDiversification can reduce some company-specific risk but does not eliminate market-wide risk.\n\n9. NIGERIAN MARKET CONTEXT\nThe Nigerian Exchange (NGX) operates a securities exchange, while the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) is a principal securities-market regulator. A regulator is not the same thing as the exchange, and neither is a commercial bank.\n\n10. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. A company sells newly issued shares to raise funds. Which market? Primary market.\nQ2. An investor sells existing shares to another investor. Which market? Secondary market.\nQ3. Who owns a portion of a company? Shareholder.\nQ4. Who lends to an issuer through a debenture? Debenture holder or creditor.\nQ5. Which market mainly handles short-term instruments? Money market.\nQ6. Which issue offers existing shareholders additional shares proportionately? Rights issue.\nQ7. Does a bonus issue automatically raise fresh cash? No.\nQ8. What is the principal purpose of an organised stock exchange? Facilitate trading in admitted securities.\nQ9. A share bought for ₦5,000 sells for ₦5,750. Simplified gain percentage? 750 ÷ 5,000 × 100 = 15%.\nQ10. Does exchange listing guarantee dividends? No.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Primary = new issue; secondary = resale of existing securities.\n• Shareholders are owners; bond and debenture holders are creditors.\n• A dividend is not the same as interest.\n• A rights issue can raise new cash; a bonus issue generally capitalises reserves.\n• The exchange facilitates trading; the regulator oversees market conduct.\n• Capital market investments carry risk.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nPRIMARY → ISSUER GETS NEW FUNDS.\nSECONDARY → INVESTORS TRADE EXISTING SECURITIES.\nSHARES → OWNERSHIP; DEBENTURES → DEBT."}},{"id":"insurance-principles","old":{"title":"Insurance, assurance and risk principles","body":"Insurance is an arrangement in which an insurer undertakes, in return for a premium, to provide agreed compensation or benefits if a specified event occurs. A premium is the amount paid for cover. Pooling means combining many contributions in a common fund from which covered claims are met. The insurer assesses risk rather than promising payment for every imaginable loss.\n\nInsurable interest is a legally recognized financial or other relevant relationship to the subject insured. Utmost good faith requires honest disclosure of material facts under the applicable rules. Indemnity aims to restore the insured's financial position after a covered loss, not permit profit from that loss. Contribution concerns sharing a covered loss between insurers where overlapping indemnity cover applies. Subrogation permits the insurer, after paying, to exercise relevant recovery rights. Proximate cause identifies the effective cause of a loss for deciding whether it falls within cover.\n\nLife assurance pays a stated benefit on death or specified survival, not ordinary property indemnity. Whole-life cover concerns death. An endowment pays on survival to maturity or earlier death under its terms. Surrender value is the amount available on permitted early termination, not necessarily the maturity benefit.\n\nProperty and liability covers include fire, motor, burglary, marine and fidelity insurance. Fidelity cover protects against specified employee dishonesty. Marine hull insurance concerns the vessel; cargo insurance concerns goods; freight insurance concerns freight earnings or charges at risk. Policy terms determine the precise cover. A cover note gives temporary evidence of cover while formal documents are prepared.\n\nPure risks involve loss or no loss; speculative risks can produce gain as well as loss. Gambling and ordinary business speculation are generally not insured like accidental property loss. Reinsurance is insurance obtained by an insurer to spread its own exposure.\n\nWorked example: a warehouse insured for a covered fire loss of ₦400,000 does not automatically receive ₦1 million merely because that is the policy limit. Indemnity, limits, excesses and other terms affect payment.\n\nGeneral average involves an intentional, reasonable sacrifice or expense for the common safety of a marine adventure. Particular average concerns an accidental partial loss borne under the relevant cover, without that shared sacrifice. Do not confuse either with a time or voyage policy.\n\nA floating marine policy covers a series of shipments under agreed declarations. An ex gratia payment is made without admitting a legal obligation. Property type and construction affect fire risk and premiums.\n\nDeath is certain, its timing uncertain; life-policyholders are assured.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Insurance does not prevent every accident.\n• A premium is not compensation paid to the claimant.\n• Assurance and insurance distinctions depend on the type of benefit, not a claim that human life has a fixed resale value."},"new":{"title":"Insurance, assurance and risk principles","body":"INSURANCE, ASSURANCE AND RISK — WHY PEOPLE PAY PREMIUMS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nBusinesses face risks from fire, theft, accidents and other events. Insurance helps manage specified financial losses. JAMB tests technical principles—indemnity, insurable interest, utmost good faith, subrogation, contribution and proximate cause—through everyday examples.\n\n1. MEANING OF INSURANCE\nInsurance is a contract in which an insurer undertakes, in return for a premium, to provide specified benefits or compensation if a covered event occurs, subject to policy terms.\n\nINSURER: Company providing cover.\nINSURED OR POLICYHOLDER: Person or organisation covered or holding the policy, as appropriate.\nPREMIUM: Price paid for insurance.\nPOLICY: Contract setting out coverage, exclusions and obligations.\nCLAIM: Request for payment or benefits following a covered event.\n\nInsurance works partly by pooling premiums from many policyholders to meet claims arising from covered losses. It does not prevent every loss or pay for every possible event.\n\n2. RISK AND INSURABILITY\nRisk concerns uncertainty about outcomes or losses. PURE RISK involves the possibility of loss or no loss, such as a warehouse fire. SPECULATIVE RISK includes the possibility of gain or loss, such as a commercial investment.\n\nInsurance commonly focuses on insurable pure risks. An insurer assesses probability, severity, exposure and policy conditions. Not every uncertain event is insurable.\n\n3. PRINCIPLE OF INSURABLE INTEREST\nThe insured must have a legally recognised interest in the subject matter so that the insured would suffer a relevant loss or has another legally sufficient relationship if the insured event occurs.\n\nEXAMPLE: A shop owner has an insurable interest in stock owned by the shop. A stranger ordinarily cannot buy property insurance on that stock merely to profit if it burns.\n\nThe timing and form of insurable interest differ by insurance class and law; use the particular question's context.\n\n4. UTMOST GOOD FAITH\nInsurance depends on honest disclosure of material facts under applicable law and policy rules. Material information may affect an insurer's decision to accept the risk or determine terms.\n\nEXAMPLE: A business seeking fire cover should answer relevant questions honestly about hazardous materials stored on the premises. Concealing important facts may affect coverage or remedies under applicable rules.\n\n5. INDEMNITY\nIndemnity aims to place the insured, financially, in approximately the position occupied immediately before a covered loss, without allowing profit from that loss. It applies to many property and liability insurance contracts, subject to policy terms.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A shop's insured stock suffers a covered loss valued at ₦300,000. Under a straightforward indemnity policy with sufficient cover and no deductible, compensation is generally based on the covered loss, not an arbitrary larger windfall.\n\nLife assurance is generally not a strict indemnity contract: human life is not valued by replacing a specific damaged asset.\n\n6. SUBROGATION\nAfter paying an insured claim, an insurer may acquire the insured's rights against a responsible third party to the extent permitted by law and the policy. This helps prevent double recovery.\n\nEXAMPLE: A transport insurer pays for covered cargo damage caused by a negligent carrier. The insurer may pursue the carrier under subrogation rights.\n\n7. CONTRIBUTION\nWhere more than one valid policy covers the same interest, subject matter and risk, insurers may share the covered loss according to applicable contribution rules. The insured cannot normally collect the full loss independently from each insurer and make a profit.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Covered loss ₦120,000; two policies contribute proportionately under an assumed 1:2 ratio. Insurer A pays ₦40,000; insurer B pays ₦80,000. Actual contribution depends on the policies and legal rules.\n\n8. PROXIMATE CAUSE\nProximate cause is the effective or dominant cause of a loss for insurance purposes, rather than simply the event closest in time. Coverage depends on whether that cause is insured and on policy exclusions.\n\nEXAMPLE: A storm damages a warehouse roof, allowing rain to damage stored goods. A claim may require determining whether the covered storm is the proximate cause under the policy.\n\n9. MITIGATION OF LOSS\nAn insured should take reasonable steps required by law and policy to prevent or minimise loss. A shop owner should not deliberately allow a small fire to spread in order to claim a larger payment.\n\n10. INSURANCE VERSUS ASSURANCE\nIn traditional school Commerce terminology, INSURANCE often refers to protection against uncertain events such as fire or theft, while ASSURANCE often refers to life contracts where death is certain but its timing is uncertain. In modern usage, 'insurance' may be used broadly, including life insurance.\n\n11. TYPES OF INSURANCE\nFIRE INSURANCE: Covers specified fire-related losses.\nMARINE INSURANCE: Covers agreed risks affecting ships, cargo or marine interests.\nMOTOR INSURANCE: Covers specified vehicle risks and liabilities.\nBURGLARY/THEFT INSURANCE: Covers specified theft-related risks.\nLIFE ASSURANCE: Provides benefits under life-policy terms.\nHEALTH INSURANCE: Covers agreed healthcare expenses or benefits.\nEMPLOYERS' LIABILITY OR OTHER LIABILITY COVER: Addresses specified legal liabilities.\n\nEach policy has exclusions, limits and conditions. Naming the type does not prove every loss is covered.\n\n12. REINSURANCE\nReinsurance is insurance purchased by an insurer to transfer part of its own risk to another insurer or reinsurer. It helps insurers manage large exposures. The original policyholder's contractual relationship ordinarily remains with the original insurer.\n\n13. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What is the payment made for cover called? Premium.\nQ2. What principle requires a legally recognised interest in the insured subject? Insurable interest.\nQ3. What principle aims to prevent profit from a property loss? Indemnity.\nQ4. Which principle allows an insurer that paid a claim to pursue a responsible third party? Subrogation.\nQ5. Which principle shares a covered loss among relevant insurers? Contribution.\nQ6. What principle identifies the effective cause of loss? Proximate cause.\nQ7. Does insurance eliminate the possibility of a fire? No.\nQ8. Why is life assurance not ordinarily a strict indemnity contract? Human life is not compensated as an ordinary replacement asset.\nQ9. What is reinsurance? An insurer transferring some of its own risk to another insurer.\nQ10. Why should applicants disclose material facts? To comply with applicable disclosure duties and allow proper risk assessment.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Premium is payment for cover; claim is a request for benefit.\n• Indemnity does not generally permit profit from a covered property loss.\n• Subrogation concerns third-party recovery; contribution concerns multiple insurers.\n• Proximate cause is the effective cause, not necessarily the last event.\n• Reinsurance is purchased by insurers, not simply any second policy bought by a customer.\n• Policy exclusions and limits matter.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nINTEREST → GOOD FAITH → INDEMNITY → SUBROGATION → CONTRIBUTION → PROXIMATE CAUSE."}},{"id":"transport-logistics","old":{"title":"Transport, ports and distribution logistics","body":"Transport is the movement of people and goods from one place to another. It creates place utility, widens markets and connects producers with suppliers and customers. A suitable mode depends on cost, speed, distance, reliability, safety, accessibility, the nature of the goods and the quantity involved. The fastest mode is not automatically the cheapest or most suitable.\n\nRoad transport is flexible and can provide door-to-door service. It is useful for short journeys and local distribution but can be affected by traffic, accidents and poor roads. Rail can carry bulky loads over land economically where routes exist, but it lacks road transport's route flexibility. Air is fast and useful for urgent, valuable or perishable cargo, though it is relatively costly and subject to capacity limits.\n\nWater transport is important for bulky international cargo. Ocean liners follow regular routes and schedules; tramp shipping operates according to available cargo and charter arrangements rather than a fixed advertised timetable. Ferries generally provide shorter passenger or vehicle crossings. Pipelines continuously carry suitable liquids or gases and can efficiently move crude oil, but they require substantial fixed investment and do not carry general merchandise.\n\nA seaport provides facilities such as berths, loading and unloading arrangements, storage and access for ships and cargo. A ports authority administers port facilities and operations within its mandate. Customs administers duties and related border controls; the two bodies are not interchangeable. Clearing agents assist customs clearance, while forwarding agents arrange movement and documentation.\n\nA consignment note records goods entrusted to a carrier. A manifest lists cargo carried. A bill of lading acknowledges receipt or shipment, evidences the carriage contract and, in appropriate negotiable form, can act as a document of title. Do not assume that every transport document transfers ownership.\n\nWorked example: a Lagos importer may use a ship for a container of machines, a port warehouse while clearance is arranged and a truck for final delivery. These stages complement rather than replace one another.\n\nA carrier undertakes carriage of goods or people; the consignor sends the consignment. Airport and seaport facilities can both include cargo handling, storage and security.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A scheduled ocean service is a liner, not a tramp.\n• A port's loading function does not mean the ports authority is the customs authority.\n• Water transport is not always appropriate for an urgent medical consignment.\n• A pipeline transports particular fluids, not every industrial product."},"new":{"title":"Transport, ports and distribution logistics","body":"TRANSPORT, PORTS AND LOGISTICS — CHOOSING THE RIGHT WAY TO MOVE GOODS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA manufacturer may produce goods cheaply but lose customers because delivery is slow, unsafe or expensive. Transport creates place utility and connects production with consumption. JAMB tests transport modes, advantages and limitations, ports, shipping documents and the difference between transport and wider logistics.\n\n1. MEANING AND IMPORTANCE\nTransport is the movement of people and goods from one location to another. It creates PLACE UTILITY by making goods available where they are wanted.\n\nTransport:\n• Links producers to raw materials and customers.\n• Widens domestic and international markets.\n• Supports specialisation and large-scale production.\n• Enables regional trade and employment.\n• Helps move agricultural goods to consuming areas.\n• Supports emergency services and mobility.\n\nTransport costs influence final selling prices. A cheap product can become expensive if moving it to the buyer costs too much.\n\n2. ROAD TRANSPORT\nExamples: lorries, vans, buses and motorcycles.\nAdvantages:\n• Flexible routes and schedules.\n• Often provides door-to-door delivery.\n• Useful for short and medium distances.\n• Suitable for local distribution and small consignments.\nLimitations:\n• Congestion, accidents and road damage.\n• Higher costs for some very bulky long-distance loads.\n• Exposure to weather, insecurity and vehicle breakdown.\n\nEXAMPLE: A supermarket receives daily bread deliveries by van because shops need frequent door-to-door service.\n\n3. RAIL TRANSPORT\nAdvantages:\n• Can carry heavy and bulky loads over long distances.\n• Potentially economical per unit for large consignments.\n• Can reduce road congestion.\nLimitations:\n• Fixed routes and terminal dependence.\n• High infrastructure investment.\n• Goods may require road transport for final delivery.\n\nEXAMPLE: A mining company may prefer rail for large volumes of minerals when suitable rail infrastructure exists.\n\n4. WATER TRANSPORT\nInland waterways use rivers and canals; sea transport uses coastal and ocean routes.\n\nAdvantages:\n• Suitable for heavy, bulky and high-volume international cargo.\n• Often lower unit freight cost than air for large loads.\nLimitations:\n• Slower transit times.\n• Requires ports, navigable routes and handling equipment.\n• Weather, congestion and customs processes may cause delays.\n\nShips may carry containers, bulk commodities, vehicles or specialised cargo. A tanker carries liquid bulk such as petroleum; a bulk carrier carries unpackaged dry bulk such as grain or ore; container ships carry standardised containers.\n\n5. AIR TRANSPORT\nAdvantages:\n• Fast over long distances.\n• Useful for urgent, high-value or time-sensitive cargo.\n• Can connect distant international markets quickly.\nLimitations:\n• High cost relative to sea for many goods.\n• Capacity and weight constraints.\n• Weather and airport infrastructure dependence.\n\nEXAMPLE: Urgent medical supplies may justify air transport. Large quantities of cement generally do not.\n\n6. PIPELINE TRANSPORT\nPipelines move suitable liquids or gases, such as crude oil, refined products or natural gas.\nAdvantages: continuous movement, high capacity for suitable commodities, reduced repeated loading.\nLimitations: high installation cost, fixed routes, leaks, vandalism and limited commodity suitability.\n\n7. FACTORS IN CHOOSING TRANSPORT\nConsider:\n• Cost per unit.\n• Speed and delivery deadline.\n• Distance and route availability.\n• Weight, bulk and value of goods.\n• Perishability and fragility.\n• Security and reliability.\n• Handling and insurance costs.\n• Environmental and regulatory requirements.\n\nWORKED COMPARISON\nA florist shipping delicate flowers for an urgent overseas event may choose air despite high cost. A steel importer shipping thousands of tonnes normally prefers sea because bulk capacity and unit cost matter more than speed.\n\n8. CONTAINERISATION AND MULTIMODAL TRANSPORT\nContainerisation places goods in standardised containers for transfer between ships, trains and trucks. It can reduce handling, damage and theft while improving efficiency.\n\nMULTIMODAL TRANSPORT uses two or more modes in a journey. A container might travel by sea to Lagos, by rail to an inland terminal and by truck to a warehouse.\n\nTransport is the physical movement of goods. LOGISTICS is broader: planning and coordinating transport, storage, inventory, order fulfilment and related information.\n\n9. PORTS AND SHIPPING TERMS\nA PORT is a location equipped for ships to load, unload and handle cargo. A harbour is a sheltered area for vessels; a port typically includes commercial facilities and operations.\n\nBILL OF LADING: A transport document commonly issued by a carrier acknowledging receipt of cargo, evidencing the carriage contract and, in appropriate form, functioning as a document of title.\nAIR WAYBILL: Air-cargo document evidencing shipment and carriage arrangements; generally not a negotiable document of title in the same way as an order bill of lading.\nMANIFEST: List of cargo carried by a vessel or other transport unit.\nDOCK WARRANT OR WAREHOUSE WARRANT: Documents associated with stored goods under particular arrangements.\n\nCustoms authorities administer import and export procedures under law. A port authority's infrastructure role differs from customs revenue and border-control functions.\n\n10. FREIGHT, DEMURRAGE AND INSURANCE\nFREIGHT is the charge for transporting cargo.\nDEMURRAGE generally refers to charges when agreed free time for using transport equipment or port-related facilities is exceeded, depending on contract and context.\nTRANSIT INSURANCE covers specified risks during transportation under policy terms.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Freight is the normal transport charge; demurrage arises from delay beyond agreed free time.\n\n11. ECONOMIC AND ENVIRONMENTAL CONSIDERATIONS\nTransport supports growth but can cause congestion, pollution, noise and accidents. Efficient route planning, maintenance and suitable mode selection can reduce waste and costs.\n\n12. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which utility does transport chiefly create? Place utility.\nQ2. Which mode commonly offers door-to-door flexibility? Road.\nQ3. Which mode is often economical for very bulky overseas cargo? Sea.\nQ4. Which mode is usually fastest for urgent long-distance cargo? Air.\nQ5. Which mode is especially suited to oil and gas movement? Pipeline.\nQ6. What is containerisation? Using standard containers to move goods across handling and transport stages.\nQ7. What is multimodal transport? Use of two or more transport modes in a journey.\nQ8. What is freight? Charge for transporting cargo.\nQ9. What does a bill of lading commonly evidence? Receipt of cargo and contract of carriage, with possible title function depending on its form.\nQ10. Why might rail be chosen for minerals? Ability to move large heavy loads where routes exist.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Fastest is not necessarily cheapest.\n• Road is flexible but not automatically best for heavy intercontinental cargo.\n• Sea is economical for bulk but generally slower than air.\n• Pipelines carry suitable fluids, not every type of cargo.\n• A harbour is not identical to the full commercial operations of a port.\n• Logistics includes more than transportation.\n• Freight and demurrage are different charges.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nROAD → FLEXIBLE.\nRAIL → BULKY LAND LOADS.\nSEA → BULKY INTERNATIONAL CARGO.\nAIR → SPEED.\nPIPELINE → LIQUIDS AND GASES."}},{"id":"warehousing","old":{"title":"Warehousing, stockholding and time utility","body":"Warehousing is the storage and protection of goods until they are required for sale or use. It bridges the time gap between production and consumption and therefore creates time utility. A warehouse does not necessarily increase the physical quantity or improve the original quality of goods; its value includes preserving availability and condition.\n\nStorage permits seasonal products to be supplied beyond the harvest period. It helps businesses maintain regular production, assemble goods for distribution and meet unexpected demand. Warehousing can also allow grading, packing and preparation for sale. Holding stock ties up finance and creates risks such as theft, fire, damage, obsolescence and deterioration, so more stock is not always better.\n\nA private warehouse is operated for its owner's goods. A public warehouse offers storage services to different users for charges. A bonded warehouse holds goods under customs control, commonly before relevant import duties are paid. Duties are not abolished simply because goods enter a bonded warehouse. A refrigerated or cold-storage facility keeps temperature-sensitive products under suitable conditions.\n\nLocation depends on the goods and the business purpose. A distribution warehouse often benefits from nearness to customers and reliable roads. Storage for imported goods may be near a port; a factory store may be near production. Safety, space, transport access, utilities, land costs, labour and the nature of surrounding activities also matter. No single location rule fits every warehouse.\n\nStock records show quantities received, issued and remaining. Regular stocktaking checks physical stock against records. Reorder arrangements help prevent shortages, but businesses must consider delivery time and changing demand. FIFO describes issuing or accounting for earlier receipts first; it is not proof that goods can never expire or suffer damage.\n\nWorked example: rice bought after harvest is stored safely and sold throughout the year. The storage service makes the rice available when consumers need it. If demand rises unexpectedly, existing stock can reduce temporary shortages, but a warehouse cannot create unlimited supplies.\n\nA state warehouse is government-owned or controlled. Public warehousing describes storage available to customers, not necessarily state ownership. Manufacturers and wholesalers can keep private warehouses for their own stocks.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Warehousing is a productive service because it creates utility.\n• Bonded storage postpones or controls duty treatment; it does not automatically cancel duty.\n• A warehouse near raw materials is not necessarily ideal for serving final consumers.\n• Price stabilization is a possible result of regular supply, not a guarantee that prices never change."},"new":{"title":"Warehousing, stockholding and time utility","body":"WAREHOUSING, STOCKHOLDING AND TIME UTILITY — KEEPING GOODS AVAILABLE WHEN NEEDED\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nProduction and consumption often occur at different times. Farmers harvest crops seasonally, while households need food throughout the year. Warehousing bridges that gap. JAMB tests the functions, types, documents and costs of storage, and distinguishes warehousing from transportation and inventory control.\n\n1. MEANING AND UTILITY\nWarehousing is the receiving, storing, protecting and releasing of goods until they are required. It creates TIME UTILITY by making goods available at the right time. Transport creates place utility; manufacturing usually creates form utility.\n\nEXAMPLE: A rice dealer stores bags after harvest and releases them gradually to retailers. Storage makes rice available months after harvest, although it does not create additional bags of rice.\n\n2. FUNCTIONS OF A WAREHOUSE\n• Receive and inspect deliveries against orders and delivery notes.\n• Protect goods from theft, weather, pests, deterioration and damage.\n• Maintain records of stock quantities and locations.\n• Preserve goods using suitable temperatures, ventilation and handling.\n• Break bulk, assemble orders, grade or repackage where appropriate.\n• Dispatch goods to wholesalers, retailers or final customers.\n• Support regular supply despite seasonal production or demand.\n• Provide space for goods awaiting customs clearance or onward transport.\n\nStorage can also assist price stability by smoothing supply, but it does not guarantee low prices. Holding too much stock can increase costs.\n\n3. TYPES OF WAREHOUSES\nPRIVATE WAREHOUSE: Owned or operated by a business mainly for its own goods. A large supermarket chain may run a distribution warehouse.\n\nPUBLIC WAREHOUSE: Offers storage services to different customers for payment. It is not necessarily government-owned; 'public' refers to availability to customers.\n\nBONDED WAREHOUSE: An authorised facility where imported goods may be stored under customs control, generally with applicable import duties and taxes deferred until goods are released for domestic use under the relevant rules.\n\nCOLD STORAGE: Temperature-controlled facility for perishable goods such as fish, meat, vaccines or fresh produce.\n\nDISTRIBUTION CENTRE: Emphasises rapid receiving, sorting and dispatch rather than prolonged storage.\n\nSILO OR BULK STORAGE: Designed for commodities such as grain, often with specialised loading and preservation systems.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A bonded warehouse is distinguished by customs control and duty arrangements, not simply by being strongly built or secure.\n\n4. STOCKHOLDING AND INVENTORY\nInventory means goods and materials held for use or sale. It may include:\n• Raw materials awaiting production.\n• Work in progress undergoing processing.\n• Finished goods ready for sale.\n• Consumables and spare parts used in operations.\n\nInventory control aims to maintain enough stock to meet demand without tying up unnecessary cash. It requires records, reorder decisions, stock counts and protection against losses.\n\n5. REORDER LEVEL AND BUFFER STOCK\nREORDER LEVEL is the stock quantity at which a new order should be placed.\nLEAD TIME is the period between ordering and receiving replacement goods.\nSAFETY OR BUFFER STOCK is extra stock held against uncertainty in demand or delivery.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA shop sells 10 cartons daily. Supplier delivery normally takes 5 days, and management wants 20 cartons as safety stock.\nExpected demand during lead time = 10 × 5 = 50 cartons.\nSimplified reorder level = 50 + 20 = 70 cartons.\nThe shop should reorder when available stock reaches about 70 cartons, assuming demand and lead time behave as stated.\n\n6. ECONOMIC ORDER QUANTITY\nEconomic order quantity (EOQ) is a stock-control concept that balances ordering costs against holding costs to find a cost-efficient order size under stated assumptions. Frequent tiny orders raise ordering costs; very large orders raise storage costs.\n\nJAMB may ask the purpose rather than a calculation: EOQ seeks to minimise relevant total inventory costs, not maximise the number of orders.\n\n7. STOCK ROTATION\nFIFO (first in, first out) means older stock is issued or sold before newer stock. It is especially useful where goods can deteriorate.\n\nFEFO (first expiry, first out) prioritises goods with the earliest expiry dates, regardless of arrival order. It is important for medicines and many packaged foods.\n\nEXAMPLE: A pharmacy receives two batches of medicine. A later-delivered batch expires earlier. FEFO directs staff to use or sell the earlier-expiring batch first.\n\n8. WAREHOUSE DOCUMENTS\nGOODS RECEIVED NOTE: Records receipt and checking of goods.\nBIN CARD: Tracks quantities received, issued and remaining for an item at a storage location.\nSTOCK RECORD: Provides information on inventory movements and balances.\nWAREHOUSE RECEIPT OR WARRANT: May acknowledge stored goods and, depending on its legal form, support transfer or financing arrangements.\nDELIVERY OR DISPATCH NOTE: Accompanies goods leaving storage.\n\nDo not confuse a warehouse receipt with a sales receipt: one concerns goods held in storage, the other acknowledges payment.\n\n9. COSTS AND RISKS OF WAREHOUSING\nCosts include rent, insurance, wages, security, electricity, refrigeration, handling and interest on money tied up in stock.\n\nRisks include fire, theft, flooding, infestation, expiry, obsolescence and inaccurate records. Appropriate controls include inspections, access restrictions, insurance, fire prevention, stock counts and suitable environmental conditions.\n\n10. WAREHOUSING VERSUS TRANSPORT\nWarehousing changes WHEN goods are available. Transport changes WHERE goods are available. Logistics coordinates both, together with ordering, stock management and delivery information.\n\n11. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What utility does warehousing chiefly create? Time utility.\nQ2. Which facility stores imported goods under customs control pending relevant duty arrangements? Bonded warehouse.\nQ3. Is a public warehouse necessarily owned by government? No.\nQ4. Which warehouse is suitable for frozen fish? Cold storage.\nQ5. What is lead time? Time between placing an order and receiving it.\nQ6. Daily demand 8 units; lead time 4 days; safety stock 10. Reorder level? (8 × 4) + 10 = 42 units.\nQ7. What does FIFO mean? First in, first out.\nQ8. What does FEFO prioritise? Earliest expiry date.\nQ9. What is the main purpose of EOQ? Balancing ordering and holding costs.\nQ10. Which document records quantities at a storage location? Bin card.\n\nCOMMON EXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Warehousing creates time utility, not place utility.\n• Bonded storage concerns customs procedures, not ordinary security alone.\n• Public warehouses may be privately owned.\n• Stockholding can prevent shortages but creates costs.\n• FIFO and FEFO are not always identical.\n• Goods received notes and payment receipts have different purposes.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nWAREHOUSING = RIGHT GOODS AT THE RIGHT TIME.\nREORDER LEVEL = EXPECTED LEAD-TIME DEMAND + SAFETY STOCK."}},{"id":"communication-ict","old":{"title":"Business communication and computer fundamentals","body":"Communication is the transmission of information and meaning from a sender to a receiver. A simple process involves a sender, encoding, a message, a channel, a receiver, decoding and feedback. Noise is anything that interferes with understanding, including a poor connection, unclear language or distraction. Sending a message is not proof that its intended meaning has been understood.\n\nOral communication allows quick explanation and feedback. Written communication provides a record but may take longer to prepare or interpret. Visual communication uses pictures, charts or signs; non-verbal communication includes gestures and expression. The best channel depends on urgency, confidentiality, accuracy, cost, distance and the need for a record.\n\nPostal and courier services move letters and parcels. A teleprinter was used for telex communication, while a fax transmitted an image of a document. These appear in historical papers even though email and online messaging now perform much routine communication. A registered item may require a collection notice and proof of identity; it is not collected merely because someone knows the sender.\n\nA computer accepts data, processes it under instructions, stores it and produces information. Hardware is the physical equipment; software is the set of programs. System software includes operating-system functions, while application software performs user tasks. The processor interprets and executes instructions. Input devices supply data; output devices present results. RAM is temporary working memory, unlike permanent file storage.\n\nComputers can improve speed, consistency, storage and information retrieval. They still depend on suitable data and instructions: incorrect input can produce incorrect output. Security, reliable electricity, training, maintenance and backup are important. An internet connection links external networks; an intranet is a private internal network using related technologies. Email is a service, not the entire internet.\n\nHistorical language questions may associate FORTRAN with scientific computation and COBOL with business data processing. Treat this as a traditional language classification, not a claim that no modern language can perform either task.\n\nWorked example: a shop enters sales with a keyboard, the program calculates totals, data is stored, and a printer produces receipts. These are input, processing, storage and output functions.\n\nTraditional size classifications include microcomputers, minicomputers, mainframes and supercomputers; a desktop is a common microcomputer. Analogue, digital and hybrid refer to data-handling methods. Mixing a size category with a method is not a consistent classification. A workstation is designed for an individual user’s demanding tasks.\n\nRegistered or recorded-delivery services provide particular tracking or receipt evidence under their terms; a certificate of posting alone is not proof that the addressee received the item.\n\nExpress prioritizes speed.\n\nCommon traps:\n• RAM is not the same as a hard drive.\n• Software is not the physical processor.\n• Communication moves information; transport moves people and goods."},"new":{"title":"Business communication and computer fundamentals","body":"BUSINESS COMMUNICATION AND COMPUTER FUNDAMENTALS — MOVING INFORMATION ACCURATELY\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nOrders, payments and customer complaints depend on clear information. A message may be sent without being understood. JAMB tests the communication process, channels, barriers, computer hardware and software, networks and safe use of digital tools in business.\n\n1. MEANING OF COMMUNICATION\nCommunication is the process of transmitting and understanding information, ideas or instructions between parties. Effective communication occurs when the receiver interprets the intended meaning sufficiently accurately.\n\nCommunication is not merely speaking or sending an email. It includes listening, feedback and clarification.\n\n2. ELEMENTS OF THE COMMUNICATION PROCESS\nSENDER: Originates the information.\nENCODING: Converts an idea into words, symbols or other signals.\nMESSAGE: Information being conveyed.\nCHANNEL: Means of transmission, such as a letter, phone or email.\nRECEIVER: Intended recipient.\nDECODING: Interpretation of the message.\nFEEDBACK: Receiver's response showing understanding or requesting clarification.\nNOISE: Anything that disrupts transmission or interpretation.\n\nEXAMPLE: A purchasing manager emails 'Deliver 20 cartons on Friday.' The supplier replies 'Confirmed, 20 cartons by 10 a.m. Friday.' The reply provides feedback. If the manager actually meant 20 packs, the mismatch should be corrected before delivery.\n\n3. TYPES AND DIRECTIONS OF COMMUNICATION\nORAL: Spoken conversations, meetings and telephone calls. Quick feedback is possible, but details can be forgotten.\nWRITTEN: Letters, memos, reports, emails and messages. Provides a record but may take longer to prepare.\nNON-VERBAL: Gestures, facial expressions and body language. Meaning depends on context.\n\nFORMAL COMMUNICATION follows approved organisational channels. INFORMAL COMMUNICATION takes place outside official reporting structures.\n\nDOWNWARD: Senior manager to subordinate.\nUPWARD: Subordinate to manager.\nHORIZONTAL: Between people or units at similar levels.\nDIAGONAL: Across departments and levels without following a simple vertical chain.\n\n4. BUSINESS COMMUNICATION DOCUMENTS\nMEMORANDUM: Common internal written communication.\nBUSINESS LETTER: Formal correspondence with customers, suppliers or other organisations.\nCIRCULAR: Similar information sent to several recipients.\nREPORT: Organised findings, analysis or recommendations.\nMINUTES: Official record of meeting proceedings and decisions.\nAGENDA: List of matters planned for discussion at a meeting.\nNOTICE: Announcement of an event, instruction or requirement.\n\nJAMB TRAP: An agenda lists what will be discussed; minutes record what happened and was decided.\n\n5. BARRIERS TO COMMUNICATION\n• Physical noise or poor network connections.\n• Ambiguous words or unfamiliar technical language.\n• Language differences.\n• Emotional tension or assumptions.\n• Poor listening and distractions.\n• Excessive messages and information overload.\n• Incorrect recipient or outdated contact details.\n• Lack of feedback.\n\nREMEDIES: Use clear language, appropriate channels, confirmation, accurate records, active listening and follow-up.\n\n6. HARDWARE AND SOFTWARE\nHARDWARE is the physical equipment of a computer system: keyboard, monitor, processor, printer and storage devices.\nSOFTWARE consists of programs and instructions used by computers.\n\nSYSTEM SOFTWARE includes operating systems and utilities that manage computer resources.\nAPPLICATION SOFTWARE helps users perform tasks such as word processing, accounting, spreadsheets and inventory management.\n\nA spreadsheet application is software; a laptop is hardware. A keyboard is an input device; a monitor is an output device.\n\n7. DATA, INFORMATION AND PROCESSING\nDATA are raw facts such as individual sales amounts. INFORMATION is processed or organised data that is meaningful for decisions.\n\nEXAMPLE: Daily sales of ₦10,000, ₦12,000 and ₦8,000 are data. Their total, ₦30,000, and an analysis of sales trends are information useful to management.\n\nA simplified information-processing cycle is INPUT → PROCESSING → OUTPUT → STORAGE/FEEDBACK.\n\n8. NETWORKS AND THE INTERNET\nA COMPUTER NETWORK links devices to share information and resources.\nLAN (local area network) commonly covers a limited area such as an office.\nWAN (wide area network) connects devices over broader geographic areas.\nThe INTERNET is a global network of interconnected networks.\nAn INTRANET is a restricted internal network using internet-related technologies.\nAn EXTRANET permits controlled access to selected external partners.\n\nEMAIL is a communication service; the internet is the underlying wider network infrastructure used by many services.\n\n9. E-COMMERCE AND DIGITAL PAYMENTS\nE-commerce is buying and selling through electronic systems, particularly online platforms. It may include product listings, ordering, payments, fulfilment and customer support.\n\nB2B: Business-to-business, such as a wholesaler supplying a retailer.\nB2C: Business-to-consumer, such as an online store selling shoes to a household.\nC2C: Consumer-to-consumer, such as individuals trading through a marketplace.\n\nElectronic payments can improve convenience and record-keeping but introduce risks such as fraud, failed transactions and unauthorised access.\n\n10. CYBERSECURITY AND PRIVACY\nBusinesses should protect customer and financial information through access controls, strong authentication, backups, staff training and careful verification of payment instructions.\n\nPHISHING uses deceptive messages to trick people into revealing credentials or sending money.\nMALWARE is malicious software.\nBACKUP is a separate copy of important data used for recovery.\nENCRYPTION helps protect information by converting it into a form readable only with appropriate access.\n\nNever share passwords or one-time verification codes with someone claiming to be customer support.\n\n11. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Who originates a message? Sender.\nQ2. What shows whether a message has been understood? Feedback.\nQ3. What is interference in communication called? Noise.\nQ4. Which document records meeting decisions? Minutes.\nQ5. Which document lists items for a forthcoming meeting? Agenda.\nQ6. Is a printer hardware or software? Hardware.\nQ7. Which software category includes spreadsheets? Application software.\nQ8. What is a LAN? A local area network.\nQ9. A manufacturer supplies a retailer through an online portal. Which model? B2B.\nQ10. What is phishing? Deceptive attempts to obtain sensitive information or money.\n\nCOMMON EXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Sending is not proof of understanding.\n• Feedback completes or improves the communication process.\n• Agenda is prepared before a meeting; minutes document the meeting.\n• Hardware is physical; software consists of programs.\n• Data and processed information are related but different.\n• Internet and intranet are not synonyms.\n• Digital transactions still require security controls.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nSENDER → MESSAGE/CHANNEL → RECEIVER → FEEDBACK.\nINPUT → PROCESS → OUTPUT.\nHARDWARE = EQUIPMENT; SOFTWARE = PROGRAMS."}},{"id":"marketing-advertising","old":{"title":"Marketing, promotion and customer satisfaction","body":"Marketing identifies customers' needs and organizes activities that satisfy them through a suitable offer. It includes product planning, pricing, distribution, promotion and customer service. Selling is the part concerned with persuading a customer and completing a transaction. Marketing begins before a finished product is offered for sale and continues after delivery.\n\nThe marketing concept emphasizes customer orientation, coordinated business effort and achieving objectives through customer satisfaction. The production concept emphasizes availability and efficient output; the product concept emphasizes quality or features; the selling concept emphasizes strong sales effort. A firm practising marketing does not assume that customers must buy whatever it has already produced.\n\nThe marketing mix is commonly summarized as product, price, place and promotion. Product includes design, quality, branding, packaging and related service. Price concerns the amount and terms charged. Place concerns distribution and access. Promotion communicates the offer and can include advertising, personal selling, sales promotion and public relations.\n\nMarket segmentation divides a broad market into groups with relevant shared characteristics. A business can select a target segment and position its product as offering a distinctive benefit. An underserved group can provide an opportunity, but selecting a segment and establishing a position are not identical to physically moving a shop.\n\nAdvertising is paid, non-personal communication by an identified sponsor through media such as radio, television, print or online channels. Informative advertising explains a product; persuasive advertising encourages preference or purchase. Sales promotion offers shorter-term incentives such as samples, demonstrations, discounts or competitions. Personal selling uses direct interaction with a buyer. Public relations develops understanding and goodwill between an organization and its publics.\n\nWorked example: a detergent maker develops small affordable packs for students, sets a suitable price, supplies nearby shops and advertises on local radio. The pack is product, the charge is price, the shops are place and the advertisement is promotion. Giving trial samples is sales promotion rather than a new distribution channel.\n\nMarket research studies a particular market; marketing research covers wider marketing decisions. A market structure describes the participants and competitive relationships. Pricing must consider demand, cost, competition and objectives; one factor is not universally decisive.\n\nCompetitive advertising stresses a brand against rival brands. Useful advice and free installation can build goodwill; installation after purchase is also an after-sales service. These categories can overlap without making all publicity the same as advertising.\n\nCommon traps:\n• The customer, not the firm's production convenience, is central to marketing orientation.\n• Advertising informs and persuades; transporting stock is distribution.\n• A promotion objective can differ from the medium used.\n• The product element includes planning and development, not only the selling price."},"new":{"title":"Marketing, promotion and customer satisfaction","body":"MARKETING, PROMOTION AND CUSTOMER SATISFACTION — START WITH THE CUSTOMER\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nSelling is only one part of marketing. A company can advertise heavily yet fail if its product is unsuitable, overpriced or unavailable. JAMB tests the marketing concept, the four Ps, promotion methods, market research, segmentation, branding and consumer satisfaction.\n\n1. MEANING OF MARKETING\nMarketing involves identifying customer needs and creating, pricing, communicating and distributing offerings that satisfy those needs while meeting organisational objectives.\n\nSELLING focuses on persuading buyers to complete transactions. MARKETING is broader: it begins with researching customer needs and continues through product development, delivery and after-sales service.\n\nEXAMPLE: A school-bag manufacturer interviews parents about durability and price, designs bags, selects retailers, advertises and handles complaints. All these activities are marketing, not merely the final sale.\n\n2. THE MARKETING CONCEPT\nThe marketing concept holds that an organisation should understand and satisfy target customers more effectively than competitors while pursuing its objectives.\n\nA production orientation stresses efficient production. A product orientation stresses product quality or features. A selling orientation emphasises aggressive sales. A marketing orientation begins with customer needs.\n\nNo orientation guarantees success; however, ignoring customer demand creates obvious commercial risk.\n\n3. THE MARKETING MIX: FOUR Ps\nPRODUCT: The good or service, including quality, design, packaging, brand and after-sales support.\nPRICE: What customers pay, including discounts, credit terms and perceived value.\nPLACE: Distribution channels and availability.\nPROMOTION: Communication activities used to inform, persuade or remind customers.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO\nA bakery introduces affordable whole-wheat bread (product), sells it at ₦1,200 (price), supplies neighbourhood shops (place), and uses local radio and social media (promotion).\n\nJAMB TRAP: 'Place' means distribution and accessibility, not merely the street address of the producer.\n\n4. MARKET RESEARCH\nMarket research systematically gathers and analyses information about buyers, competitors and market conditions.\n\nPRIMARY DATA are collected directly for a particular study through surveys, interviews, observation or experiments.\nSECONDARY DATA already exist in sources such as published reports, industry records and government statistics.\n\nEXAMPLE: Interviewing 100 customers about preferred package sizes is primary research. Using an existing industry report is secondary research.\n\nAdvantages include better demand forecasts, reduced uncertainty and improved product decisions. Limitations include cost, sampling errors and inaccurate responses.\n\n5. MARKET SEGMENTATION AND TARGETING\nMarket segmentation divides a broad market into groups with similar needs or characteristics.\nDEMOGRAPHIC: Age, income or occupation.\nGEOGRAPHIC: Location or climate.\nPSYCHOGRAPHIC: Lifestyle or interests.\nBEHAVIOURAL: Usage rate, loyalty or purchasing habits.\n\nTARGET MARKET is the group the business chooses to serve.\nPOSITIONING is how the business seeks to be perceived relative to alternatives.\n\nEXAMPLE: A company targets students who need durable, affordable backpacks. Its positioning may emphasise long-lasting quality at a student-friendly price.\n\n6. PRODUCT, BRANDING AND PACKAGING\nA BRAND is a name, symbol, design or combination identifying a seller's offering and distinguishing it from competitors.\nBRANDING supports recognition, loyalty and differentiation.\nPACKAGING protects goods, provides information and can help promotion.\nLABELLING communicates details such as ingredients, quantity, usage instructions or safety information where applicable.\n\nA trademark is a legally protected sign under relevant law; not every brand element is automatically registered as a trademark.\n\n7. PRODUCT LIFE CYCLE\nA simplified product life cycle has four stages:\nINTRODUCTION: New product, low awareness, promotion costs may be high.\nGROWTH: Sales expand, competition may increase.\nMATURITY: Sales growth slows; differentiation and retention matter.\nDECLINE: Sales may fall as needs or technology change.\n\nThe model is a guide, not a fixed timetable. Products can be repositioned or improved and may not follow every stage neatly.\n\n8. PRICING METHODS\nCOST-PLUS PRICING: Add a markup to cost.\nCOMPETITION-BASED PRICING: Consider rival prices.\nVALUE-BASED PRICING: Reflect customers' perceived value.\nPENETRATION PRICING: Relatively low introductory price to attract buyers.\nPRICE SKIMMING: Relatively high introductory price, often reduced later as the market develops.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA product costs ₦4,000 per unit and the seller adds a 25% markup on cost.\nMarkup = ₦1,000.\nSelling price = ₦5,000.\nThis is not a 25% profit margin on selling price; the margin is ₦1,000 ÷ ₦5,000 = 20%.\n\n9. PROMOTIONAL MIX\nADVERTISING: Paid, usually non-personal communication through media such as radio, posters and online placements.\nPERSONAL SELLING: Direct interaction with potential customers to explain and persuade.\nSALES PROMOTION: Short-term incentives such as coupons, samples or temporary discounts.\nPUBLIC RELATIONS: Activities to build understanding, reputation and goodwill.\nDIRECT MARKETING: Direct contact with selected customers through suitable channels.\n\nPUBLICITY refers to media attention or exposure; it may be unpaid and is not always under the firm's control.\n\n10. ADVERTISING MEDIA: STRENGTHS AND LIMITATIONS\nRadio can reach broad audiences and use local languages but has no visual display.\nTelevision combines sound and pictures but may be expensive.\nOutdoor posters offer repeated local exposure but limited detail.\nDigital advertising can target specific audiences and measure responses but depends on connectivity and raises privacy concerns.\nNewspapers and magazines can provide detailed information but may have limited reach among some audiences.\n\nChoose a medium based on audience, budget, product and objective.\n\n11. CUSTOMER SATISFACTION AND AFTER-SALES SERVICE\nCustomer satisfaction depends on whether experience meets or exceeds expectations. Useful after-sales activities include warranties, repair support, returns procedures and complaint handling.\n\nA business should avoid misleading advertisements, hidden charges and unsafe products. Repeat purchases often depend on trust and consistent quality.\n\n12. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which marketing-mix element concerns distribution? Place.\nQ2. Which element concerns discounts and payment terms? Price.\nQ3. Is selling broader than marketing? No.\nQ4. A firm interviews customers directly. Primary or secondary data? Primary.\nQ5. Dividing customers by income is what segmentation? Demographic.\nQ6. What distinguishes a brand from an unmarked product? Identifying and differentiating features.\nQ7. What promotional method involves direct salesperson interaction? Personal selling.\nQ8. What pricing approach uses a low introductory price to build market share? Penetration pricing.\nQ9. Product cost ₦2,000, markup 20% on cost. Selling price? ₦2,400.\nQ10. Why is after-sales service important? It supports satisfaction, trust and repeat business.\n\nCOMMON EXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Marketing includes research, product planning, distribution and service.\n• 'Place' is distribution, not only a physical location.\n• Primary research is newly collected; secondary uses existing information.\n• Advertising and personal selling are different promotion methods.\n• Markup percentage and profit margin percentage differ.\n• Publicity is not always paid advertising.\n• A brand name is not necessarily a registered trademark.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nFOUR Ps = PRODUCT, PRICE, PLACE, PROMOTION.\nRESEARCH → SEGMENT → TARGET → POSITION → SERVE."}},{"id":"foreign-trade-documents","old":{"title":"Foreign trade, payment documents and trade balances","body":"Foreign trade is buying and selling between countries. Imports are goods or services bought from abroad; exports are sold abroad. Entrepôt trade involves importing goods and re-exporting them. Differences in resources, climate, skills, technology and production costs encourage international exchange. Countries do not need identical resources or costs before they can trade.\n\nVisible trade concerns physical goods. Invisible trade concerns services and related non-merchandise transactions, such as transport and tourism receipts. Balance of trade is the difference between the value of merchandise exports and imports over a period. The balance of payments is the systematic record of economic transactions between residents and non-residents over a period; it is wider than imports and exports of goods alone.\n\nA buyer may send an indent to an overseas supplier or agent specifying goods required. A commercial invoice states the goods and price. A certificate of origin identifies the goods' country of origin. An insurance certificate evidences relevant cover. A bill of lading concerns receipt and carriage of sea cargo and may have a title function. A consular invoice involves certification under the importing country's requirements where applicable.\n\nDocumentary credit, commonly a letter-of-credit arrangement, involves a bank undertaking payment subject to compliant documents and stated terms. A documentary bill is presented with specified shipping documents. Neither term means that the bank guarantees the physical quality of the goods merely because documents are supplied. A letter of hypothecation gives relevant rights over goods used as security under its arrangement.\n\nFOB and CIF are historical examination terms as well as modern trade-rule labels. CIF includes cost, insurance and freight in the seller's quoted responsibilities. FOB concerns delivery on board under the applicable rule. Who pays a charge and when risk transfers must be distinguished; modern Incoterms do not make them the same event in every case.\n\nWorked example: merchandise exports of ₦90 million and imports of ₦110 million produce an adverse merchandise trade balance of ₦20 million. Tourism receipts could improve the wider external position but do not turn into merchandise exports.\n\nCommodity markets trade primary products and other standardized goods. They can link exporters and importers, but not every commodity transaction crosses a national border.\n\nForeign-exchange markets trade currencies; instruments such as travellers’ cheques can represent foreign-currency claims, unlike ordinary domestic shares.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A trade deficit is not automatically the whole balance-of-payments position.\n• A manifest lists cargo; an indent orders goods.\n• Trade barriers include tariffs, quotas, exchange restrictions and burdensome procedures, not only long distances."},"new":{"title":"Foreign trade, payment documents and trade balances","body":"FOREIGN TRADE, DOCUMENTS AND BALANCES — COMMERCE ACROSS BORDERS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nAn importer may buy machinery abroad while an exporter sells cocoa to another country. International transactions require transport, foreign-currency payments, customs procedures and documentation. JAMB tests imports versus exports, visible and invisible trade, trade documents, tariffs, exchange rates and trade balances.\n\n1. MEANING AND TYPES OF FOREIGN TRADE\nForeign or international trade is buying and selling goods and services between countries.\nIMPORT: Goods or services purchased from abroad.\nEXPORT: Goods or services sold to buyers abroad.\nENTREPÔT TRADE: Importing goods and subsequently re-exporting them, often with limited processing, subject to the circumstances.\n\nEXAMPLE: A Nigerian firm buys machines from Germany: import into Nigeria. A Nigerian cocoa producer sells beans to a buyer in another country: export from Nigeria.\n\n2. WHY COUNTRIES TRADE\nCountries differ in natural resources, climate, technology, labour skills, capital and production costs. Specialisation and exchange can expand the variety of goods available and may reduce costs.\n\nCOMPARATIVE ADVANTAGE means a country can produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country. It is different from ABSOLUTE ADVANTAGE, which means producing more output with the same resources or the same output with fewer resources.\n\nEven a country that is absolutely more productive in several goods may benefit from specialisation according to relative opportunity costs.\n\n3. VISIBLE AND INVISIBLE TRADE\nVISIBLE TRADE involves physical goods, such as vehicles, textiles and crude oil.\nINVISIBLE TRADE involves services, such as tourism, shipping, insurance, professional services and some digital services.\n\nBALANCE OF VISIBLE TRADE = Value of merchandise exports − Value of merchandise imports.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nGoods exports = ₦900 billion.\nGoods imports = ₦1,050 billion.\nVisible trade balance = ₦900 billion − ₦1,050 billion = −₦150 billion.\nThis is a visible trade deficit of ₦150 billion.\n\nA surplus occurs when exports exceed imports; a deficit when imports exceed exports.\n\n4. BALANCE OF PAYMENTS\nThe balance of payments (BOP) is a systematic record of economic transactions between residents of a country and the rest of the world during a period.\n\nThe CURRENT ACCOUNT includes trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transfers under standard international accounting classifications.\nThe CAPITAL ACCOUNT records specified capital transfers and non-produced non-financial assets.\nThe FINANCIAL ACCOUNT records cross-border investment and financing transactions.\n\nDo not equate the balance of visible trade with the entire balance of payments. A goods deficit may coexist with surpluses in services or other entries.\n\n5. FOREIGN EXCHANGE AND EXCHANGE RATES\nForeign exchange refers to currencies and claims used in international payments. An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nIf $1 = ₦1,500, a $200 import costs ₦300,000 before freight, duties and other charges.\nIf the rate changes to $1 = ₦1,600, the same $200 costs ₦320,000.\nThe naira has depreciated against the dollar in this example, making that dollar-priced import more expensive in naira terms.\n\nAPPRECIATION: A currency rises in value relative to another.\nDEPRECIATION: It falls in value under a market exchange-rate system.\nDEVALUATION: Official downward adjustment of a currency's value under an administered or fixed-rate arrangement.\n\n6. RESTRICTIONS ON FOREIGN TRADE\nTARIFF: Tax imposed on specified imports or exports, commonly imports.\nQUOTA: Quantitative limit on permitted imports or exports.\nEMBARGO: Official prohibition on specified trade.\nIMPORT LICENCE: Official authorisation where required.\nEXCHANGE CONTROL: Rules restricting or regulating access to foreign exchange or certain cross-border payments.\n\nReasons for restrictions may include protecting domestic industries, revenue collection, safety, national security or balance-of-payments management. Costs may include higher consumer prices, reduced choice and retaliation.\n\n7. FOREIGN TRADE DOCUMENTS\nCOMMERCIAL INVOICE: Seller's bill stating goods, prices and terms.\nPACKING LIST: Details the contents and packaging of consignments.\nBILL OF LADING: Sea-carriage document evidencing receipt and carriage arrangements; certain forms can function as documents of title.\nAIR WAYBILL: Air-carriage document, generally not a negotiable document of title.\nCERTIFICATE OF ORIGIN: Identifies the declared country of origin under applicable rules.\nINSURANCE CERTIFICATE: Evidence of insurance arrangements.\nCUSTOMS DECLARATION: Information supplied to customs for clearance.\nLETTER OF CREDIT: Bank undertaking to pay a beneficiary when stipulated documentary conditions are met, subject to its terms and applicable rules.\n\nA letter of credit is not itself proof that the physical goods are perfect. Banks typically examine documents under the credit's terms.\n\n8. TERMS OF DELIVERY\nFOB (Free on Board) and CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight) are recognised trade terms used in relevant sea or inland-waterway transactions under applicable Incoterms rules.\n\nCIF includes specified cost, insurance and freight obligations up to the named destination port, but risk allocation is governed by the chosen Incoterms rule. Do not assume that paying freight always means bearing all risks until arrival.\n\n9. BENEFITS AND PROBLEMS OF FOREIGN TRADE\nBenefits:\n• Access to goods not produced locally.\n• Wider markets for exporters.\n• Specialisation and possible efficiency gains.\n• Technology and knowledge transfer.\n• Foreign-exchange earnings from exports.\n\nProblems:\n• Exchange-rate fluctuations.\n• Transport and insurance costs.\n• Customs delays and documentation requirements.\n• Political or trade-policy changes.\n• Exposure to overseas competition.\n• Risk of non-payment or shipment damage.\n\n10. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Nigeria buys cars from Japan. Import or export? Import into Nigeria.\nQ2. What is visible trade? Trade in physical goods.\nQ3. Give one invisible export. Tourism or professional services sold to non-residents.\nQ4. Goods exports ₦400m, imports ₦500m. Trade balance? ₦100m deficit.\nQ5. Which document states country of origin? Certificate of origin.\nQ6. Which document usually accompanies air cargo? Air waybill.\nQ7. What is a tariff? A tax on specified cross-border trade, commonly imports.\nQ8. What is a quota? A quantitative restriction.\nQ9. If more naira are needed for one dollar, has the naira appreciated against the dollar? No, it has depreciated.\nQ10. Is visible trade balance the whole BOP? No.\n\nCOMMON EXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Imports are purchases from abroad; exports are sales abroad.\n• Visible trade covers goods; invisible trade covers services.\n• A trade deficit is not identical to a balance-of-payments deficit.\n• Tariffs are taxes; quotas are quantity restrictions.\n• Depreciation and devaluation are not interchangeable.\n• A letter of credit is a documentary bank undertaking, not a quality guarantee.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nEXPORTS − IMPORTS = VISIBLE TRADE BALANCE.\nGOODS = VISIBLE; SERVICES = INVISIBLE.\nTARIFF = TAX; QUOTA = QUANTITY LIMIT."}},{"id":"economic-integration","old":{"title":"Economic cooperation and regional organizations","body":"Economic integration is cooperation among economies to reduce barriers and coordinate selected policies. A free-trade area removes agreed trade barriers among members while allowing separate external trade policies. A customs union adds a common external tariff. A common market permits freer movement of factors such as labour and capital as well as goods. A monetary union goes further in monetary arrangements; these stages should not be treated as identical.\n\nRegional cooperation can enlarge markets, permit specialization, support shared infrastructure and strengthen bargaining power. Difficulties include different development levels, overlapping memberships, political disagreements, weak transport links and obstacles to implementing agreements. Signing a treaty does not prove that every barrier immediately disappears.\n\nECOWAS was established to promote cooperation and integration among West African states. Historical questions should be answered with membership and institutional arrangements appropriate to their date. Membership has changed, including withdrawals that took effect in 2025, so an old list should not be presented as permanently current. Sovereign states and admission under the treaty framework are relevant; population or landmass alone does not define membership.\n\nRiver-basin organizations coordinate shared water resources and related development. The Lake Chad Basin Commission's founding members were Cameroon, Chad, Niger and Nigeria. Later membership expanded, so a question asking about founders differs from one asking about members generally. The Niger Basin Authority, historically described as the Niger Basin Commission, involves countries sharing interests in the Niger basin. Ghana and the Gambia should not be assumed to belong merely because they are West African countries.\n\nThe Mano River Union began as a subregional cooperation arrangement and later expanded. The European Union is a wider political and economic integration project with its own membership rules; not every European country is an EU member. Regional financial institutions and commercial banks can support trade without being the same thing as an intergovernmental river-basin organization.\n\nWorked example: two neighbouring states removing tariffs between themselves establish an element of free trade. If they also apply the same tariff to non-members, they are moving toward a customs union. Allowing workers to move freely adds a different level of integration.\n\nEcobank is a regional commercial banking initiative associated with promoting African trade. A commercial bank’s regional purpose does not make it identical to a government-owned river-basin commission.\n\nThe Lake Chad Basin Commission was established in 1964 and has its headquarters in N’Djamena, Chad.\n\nECOWAS free-movement arrangements facilitate eligible citizens’ movement, residence and establishment; implementation and current membership require date-specific qualifications.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Geographical proximity alone does not prove membership.\n• Check whether the paper asks for founders, members or present membership.\n• Do not substitute today's political changes silently into an older examination question."},"new":{"title":"Economic cooperation and regional organizations","body":"ECONOMIC COOPERATION AND REGIONAL ORGANISATIONS — HOW COUNTRIES TRADE TOGETHER\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nCountries may reduce trade barriers, coordinate policies or share institutions to improve economic cooperation. JAMB tests the stages of integration and the roles of ECOWAS, the African Union and international trade bodies. The key is to distinguish levels of cooperation rather than assuming every organisation operates a full common market.\n\n1. MEANING OF ECONOMIC INTEGRATION\nEconomic integration is cooperation among economies to reduce barriers to exchange and coordinate selected economic policies. The depth of integration varies: some arrangements focus on trade preferences, while others address movement of people, capital or monetary policy.\n\nIntegration can expand markets and improve efficiency but may expose less competitive firms to stronger rivals.\n\n2. PREFERENTIAL TRADE ARRANGEMENT\nMembers grant selected trade advantages, such as reduced tariffs on specified goods, without necessarily removing most trade barriers between them.\n\nThis is generally a limited form of trade cooperation rather than a full free-trade area.\n\n3. FREE-TRADE AREA\nMembers remove agreed tariffs or other barriers on qualifying trade among themselves, while retaining separate external trade policies toward non-members.\n\nRULES OF ORIGIN help determine whether goods qualify for preferential treatment, preventing simple transshipment of goods from outsiders to avoid tariffs.\n\n4. CUSTOMS UNION\nA customs union combines internal trade liberalisation with a COMMON EXTERNAL TARIFF or coordinated external tariff policy toward non-members.\n\nKEY DISTINCTION: Free-trade area = members may retain different external tariffs. Customs union = common external tariff arrangement.\n\n5. COMMON MARKET\nA common market goes beyond a customs union by facilitating movement of factors of production, especially labour and capital, alongside goods and services.\n\nRemoving tariffs alone does not establish unrestricted movement of workers. Implementation may be partial and subject to agreed rules.\n\n6. ECONOMIC UNION AND MONETARY UNION\nAn ECONOMIC UNION involves deeper coordination or harmonisation of economic policies and institutions.\nA MONETARY UNION involves a shared currency or closely integrated monetary arrangements, commonly with a common monetary authority.\n\nThese concepts overlap in practice but are not identical. A country may participate in regional trade cooperation without adopting a shared currency.\n\nINTEGRATION LADDER\nPreferential arrangement → Free-trade area → Customs union → Common market → Economic union.\nA monetary union is a further monetary-policy arrangement and should not be assumed to follow automatically in every region.\n\n7. TRADE CREATION AND TRADE DIVERSION\nTRADE CREATION occurs when integration shifts purchases toward more efficient production within the bloc, replacing higher-cost domestic production.\n\nTRADE DIVERSION occurs when preferential tariffs shift purchases away from a more efficient outside supplier toward a less efficient supplier within the bloc.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO\nBefore integration, a buyer purchases from a low-cost outside supplier. After tariffs are removed only for members, a higher-cost member supplier becomes cheaper after tax. The switch may represent trade diversion.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Not every increase in trade within a bloc is automatically an efficiency gain.\n\n8. ECOWAS\nThe Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) was established to promote cooperation and integration among participating West African countries.\n\nCommonly examined objectives include:\n• Facilitating regional trade and economic cooperation.\n• Promoting movement of people and economic activity under applicable protocols.\n• Supporting infrastructure and development.\n• Encouraging regional coordination and stability.\n\nMembership and implementation can change over time. For a current-affairs question, verify the membership and legal status relevant to the examination date rather than relying on an old memorised country count.\n\n9. AFRICAN UNION AND AfCFTA\nThe AFRICAN UNION (AU) promotes continental cooperation across political, economic, social and other fields.\n\nThe AFRICAN CONTINENTAL FREE TRADE AREA (AfCFTA) is a framework for increasing intra-African trade through agreed liberalisation and related rules. It is not the same institution as the AU, although the initiative is associated with the continental integration agenda.\n\nImplementation involves schedules, rules of origin and national commitments; it does not mean every African good immediately crosses every border without conditions.\n\n10. WORLD TRADE ORGANIZATION\nThe WORLD TRADE ORGANIZATION (WTO) provides a framework for international trade rules, negotiations, transparency and dispute settlement arrangements under its agreements.\n\nIt does not act as a worldwide central bank, nor does it guarantee that every member has no tariffs.\n\n11. IMF AND WORLD BANK\nThe INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND (IMF) supports international monetary cooperation and provides financial assistance and policy engagement under its mandate.\n\nThe WORLD BANK GROUP includes institutions concerned with development finance, poverty reduction and related development objectives.\n\nJAMB TRAP: The IMF is not the same as the World Bank, and neither is a regional free-trade area.\n\n12. BENEFITS OF REGIONAL COOPERATION\n• Larger markets for businesses.\n• Greater scope for specialisation.\n• Possible economies of scale.\n• More competition and consumer choice.\n• Coordinated infrastructure and investment.\n• Stronger bargaining power in some negotiations.\n\n13. CHALLENGES\n• Unequal benefits among member countries.\n• Loss of some tariff revenue.\n• Pressure on less competitive local industries.\n• Differences in regulations and infrastructure.\n• Political disagreements and implementation delays.\n• Trade diversion and administrative costs.\n\n14. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which arrangement allows different external tariffs? Free-trade area.\nQ2. Which adds a common external tariff? Customs union.\nQ3. Which adds freer movement of labour and capital? Common market.\nQ4. What is a monetary union? Shared currency or integrated monetary arrangement.\nQ5. What is trade creation? Replacement of higher-cost domestic production by more efficient member production.\nQ6. What is trade diversion? Switching from a more efficient outside supplier to a less efficient member due to preferences.\nQ7. What does ECOWAS stand for? Economic Community of West African States.\nQ8. Which continental framework aims to expand intra-African trade? AfCFTA.\nQ9. Is the WTO a world central bank? No.\nQ10. Is the IMF identical to the World Bank? No.\n\nCOMMON EXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Free-trade area and customs union differ in external tariff policy.\n• A common market includes factor mobility, not only goods.\n• Monetary union is not automatic for all trade blocs.\n• Trade creation and trade diversion have different efficiency implications.\n• ECOWAS, AU, AfCFTA and WTO are distinct arrangements.\n• Regional membership and policy implementation can change.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nFTA → INTERNAL TRADE FREEDOM.\nCUSTOMS UNION → COMMON EXTERNAL TARIFF.\nCOMMON MARKET → FACTOR MOBILITY.\nECONOMIC UNION → DEEPER POLICY COORDINATION."}},{"id":"law-contract-agency","old":{"title":"Contracts, sale of goods and agency","body":"A contract is an agreement enforceable by law. Its usual requirements include offer, acceptance, consideration where required, intention to create legal relations, capacity and a lawful purpose. An agreement alone is not automatically enforceable. Consideration is something of value exchanged for a promise; it is not simply careful thinking before signing.\n\nAn offer states terms on which the maker is willing to be bound if accepted. Acceptance must correspond to the offer and meet the applicable communication rules. An invitation to treat invites offers rather than necessarily being an offer itself. A sale of goods transfers or agrees to transfer ownership of goods for a money price. Barter exchanges goods without the money-price element of a conventional sale.\n\nExisting goods are already owned or possessed by the seller. Future goods are to be manufactured or acquired after the contract. Specific goods are identified and agreed upon when the contract is made. These labels concern different characteristics; future does not mean that goods have already been specifically delivered.\n\nContracts may end through performance, agreement, breach, operation of law or frustration. Frustration involves an unforeseen event beyond the parties' control that makes the agreed performance impossible or fundamentally different under the applicable law. A higher cost or mere disappointment does not automatically establish frustration. Fraud concerns deception and should not be assumed whenever goods are lost.\n\nAgency is a relationship in which an agent is authorized to act for a principal in dealings with another party. It can arise by express agreement, implication, ratification, necessity or other recognized rules. Ratification is later approval of an act originally performed without authority, where legal conditions are satisfied. Agency by necessity requires a genuine emergency and circumstances justifying action to protect the principal's interests.\n\nWorked example: a carrier with perishable goods faces an emergency, cannot reasonably obtain instructions and sells them to prevent a greater loss. This may illustrate agency by necessity, not automatic authority to sell every consignment for convenience. An agent who handles goods must still account properly to the principal.\n\nA del credere agent receives extra commission for guaranteeing payment by buyers under the agency agreement. A voidable contract remains effective unless the entitled party rescinds it; an unrelated stranger cannot normally cancel it at will. A credit sale normally transfers ownership according to the sale agreement before all instalments are paid; hire purchase commonly postpones title.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Title means ownership, not merely physical possession.\n• A prospectus is not a contract of partnership.\n• Theft or loss does not automatically prove fraud by the contracting parties.\n• Historical legal questions require their precise assumptions; do not turn a simplified example into universal legal advice."},"new":{"title":"Contracts, sale of goods and agency","body":"CONTRACTS, SALE OF GOODS AND AGENCY — KNOW WHEN A PROMISE BECOMES ENFORCEABLE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA shopkeeper displays a phone at a price, a customer offers to buy it, and the shopkeeper agrees. Which act is the offer? What happens if the phone is defective? Can an employee bind the shop? JAMB tests these situations through contract formation, remedies, sale of goods and agency.\n\n1. MEANING OF CONTRACT\nA contract is an agreement enforceable by law. Not every promise is a contract. A social promise to visit a friend is normally different from a commercial agreement to supply goods for payment.\n\nCommon requirements are offer, acceptance, consideration where required, intention to create legal relations, capacity, certainty and lawful purpose. The exact rules and exceptions depend on the applicable law.\n\n2. OFFER AND INVITATION TO TREAT\nAn OFFER is a definite proposal indicating willingness to be bound when accepted on its terms.\nAn INVITATION TO TREAT invites other people to make offers. Displaying goods on shop shelves or advertising a price is often an invitation to treat, although particular wording or circumstances can make an advertisement an offer.\n\nEXAMPLE: A shop displays a calculator marked ₦8,000. A buyer takes it to the cashier and offers to pay. The shop may accept the purchase subject to applicable consumer and advertising laws. Do not automatically assume every displayed price forms a completed contract.\n\nA COUNTER-OFFER changes the proposed terms and generally rejects the original offer. A request for information, however, is not necessarily a counter-offer.\n\n3. ACCEPTANCE\nAcceptance is unqualified agreement to an offer on its terms, communicated as required by law. Silence is not ordinarily acceptance merely because the offeror says it will be.\n\nEXAMPLE: A supplier offers 100 chairs at ₦12,000 each. The school replies, 'We accept 100 at ₦10,000 each.' This is a counter-offer, not acceptance of the original price.\n\n4. CONSIDERATION AND INTENTION\nCONSIDERATION is something of legal value exchanged for a promise, such as money, goods, services or another promise. It need not always be economically equal in value to what is received.\n\nINTENTION TO CREATE LEGAL RELATIONS concerns whether parties intend their arrangement to have legal consequences. Commercial agreements commonly indicate such intention; ordinary domestic arrangements may not.\n\nA GIFT may be validly transferred, but a bare promise to give a gift is not necessarily enforceable as an ordinary bargain.\n\n5. CAPACITY, LEGALITY AND CERTAINTY\nParties must possess relevant legal capacity. Rules concerning minors and persons lacking capacity are specialised and may depend on the type of transaction.\n\nAn agreement for an unlawful purpose is not made enforceable merely because both parties consent. Terms must also be sufficiently certain for a court to determine the parties' obligations.\n\n6. VITIATING FACTORS\nMISREPRESENTATION: A false statement that induces a contract under relevant legal rules.\nMISTAKE: Certain fundamental misunderstandings can affect validity.\nDURESS: Improper pressure that undermines genuine consent.\nUNDUE INFLUENCE: Improper use of a relationship of influence.\nFRAUD: Deliberate dishonesty meeting applicable legal requirements.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A bad bargain is not automatically fraud. The legal facts matter.\n\n7. TYPES AND DISCHARGE OF CONTRACT\nEXPRESS CONTRACT: Terms stated in words, spoken or written.\nIMPLIED CONTRACT: Terms or obligations inferred from conduct or law.\nBILATERAL CONTRACT: Exchange of promises.\nUNILATERAL CONTRACT: A promise accepted through performing a specified act, under relevant conditions.\n\nContracts may be discharged by performance, agreement, frustration in qualifying circumstances, breach with appropriate consequences or operation of law.\n\n8. BREACH AND REMEDIES\nBREACH occurs when a party fails to perform a contractual obligation without lawful justification.\n\nPossible remedies include DAMAGES (monetary compensation), SPECIFIC PERFORMANCE (court order requiring performance where appropriate), RESCISSION (setting aside in qualifying circumstances) and INJUNCTION (court order restraining certain conduct).\n\nEXAMPLE: A supplier fails to deliver paid-for goods. The buyer may seek an appropriate contractual remedy. Specific performance is not automatic for ordinary goods easily bought elsewhere.\n\n9. SALE OF GOODS\nA sale of goods contract concerns transfer or agreement to transfer ownership of goods for money consideration. DISTINGUISH:\nSALE: Ownership passes under the contract.\nAGREEMENT TO SELL: Ownership is to pass later or subject to conditions.\n\nOwnership (TITLE) is not identical to physical possession. A warehouse may possess goods belonging to someone else.\n\n10. CONDITIONS, WARRANTIES AND CONSUMER RIGHTS\nA CONDITION is traditionally a fundamental contractual term whose breach may allow rejection or termination in appropriate circumstances. A WARRANTY is traditionally a less central term whose breach commonly gives rise to damages. Modern statutory rights and remedies can modify these textbook distinctions.\n\nBuyers may have rights concerning description, quality, fitness, delivery and misleading conduct under applicable Nigerian law. A seller cannot assume a printed 'no refund' sign removes all statutory consumer protections.\n\n11. AGENCY: MEANING\nAn agent is a person authorised to act on behalf of a principal in dealings with third parties. The principal may be bound by acts within the agent's actual or apparent authority, depending on the circumstances.\n\nPRINCIPAL: Person represented.\nAGENT: Person acting on the principal's behalf.\nTHIRD PARTY: Outside person dealing with the agent.\n\nEXAMPLE: A shop owner authorises a purchasing officer to order stock up to ₦200,000. Orders within that authority may bind the owner under applicable rules.\n\n12. CREATION AND TYPES OF AUTHORITY\nAgency may arise by express agreement, implication from conduct, ratification of an otherwise unauthorised act where lawful, necessity in limited circumstances or operation of law.\n\nACTUAL AUTHORITY: Expressly or impliedly granted by the principal.\nAPPARENT OR OSTENSIBLE AUTHORITY: Authority a third party reasonably understands the agent to have because of the principal's representations.\nRATIFICATION: Principal later adopts an act done on the principal's behalf where legal conditions are met.\n\nJAMB TRAP: An agent need not own the goods being sold. An agent may earn commission while the principal remains owner.\n\n13. DUTIES OF AGENTS AND PRINCIPALS\nAgents generally owe duties to follow lawful instructions, exercise reasonable care, avoid unauthorised conflicts of interest, account for money and disclose relevant information. Principals may owe agreed remuneration and reimbursement for authorised expenses.\n\nAgency can end through agreement, completion of the task, expiry, revocation or other legally recognised events, subject to protections and third-party issues.\n\n14. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What distinguishes a contract from an ordinary promise? Legal enforceability.\nQ2. A buyer proposes a different price. What is this? Counter-offer.\nQ3. Is silence ordinarily acceptance? No.\nQ4. What is consideration? Something of legal value exchanged for a promise.\nQ5. What remedy generally involves monetary compensation? Damages.\nQ6. What is the difference between sale and agreement to sell? When ownership passes.\nQ7. Who is represented by an agent? Principal.\nQ8. Does an agent always own the goods? No.\nQ9. What is ratification? A principal's lawful adoption of a qualifying prior act.\nQ10. Can an illegal agreement become enforceable merely through mutual consent? No.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Displaying goods is often an invitation to treat.\n• Counter-offer differs from acceptance.\n• Possession and ownership are not identical.\n• Agency and employment can overlap but are not synonyms.\n• A principal may be bound by authorised agency acts.\n• A condition and warranty have different traditional consequences.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nCONTRACT = OFFER + ACCEPTANCE + OTHER LEGAL REQUIREMENTS.\nAGENCY = PRINCIPAL → AGENT → THIRD PARTY."}},{"id":"consumer-business-environment","old":{"title":"Consumer protection, business environment and responsibility","body":"The business environment consists of internal and external conditions that affect business operations. Internal factors include employees, finance, management and organizational culture. External factors include customers, competitors, economic conditions, technology, politics, law and social expectations. Some school questions use environment narrowly for external influences, so read their wording rather than assuming every list uses the same definition.\n\nEconomic influences include income, inflation, interest rates, market structure and access to finance. Political and legal influences concern government stability, policy and regulations. Technological change affects production, information and distribution. Social and cultural factors include beliefs, habits and changing consumer needs. Businesses can respond to these influences but cannot directly control all of them.\n\nConsumer protection comprises measures that safeguard buyers against unsafe goods, deception and unfair practices. Common consumer rights include safety, information, choice, being heard and seeking redress. Redress means a remedy for a justified complaint, such as repair, replacement or refund under the relevant rules. A consumer does not have an automatic right to free samples, company profits or unlimited credit.\n\nStandards bodies set or enforce relevant product requirements, while professional bodies regulate or promote standards within their professions. In Nigerian questions, distinguish the Standards Organisation of Nigeria from the Manufacturers Association of Nigeria, consumer associations and chambers of commerce. Modern competition and consumer-protection arrangements differ from some older institutional names, so explain the historical context of past papers.\n\nSocial responsibility means considering the effects of business decisions on workers, customers, communities and the environment. It includes safe products, fair dealing, responsible waste disposal and appropriate community support. Scholarships or support for community sport can illustrate social responsibility, even when they also improve goodwill. Pollution is not made acceptable merely because a business earns profit.\n\nWorked example: an oil spill contaminating farmland and a stream can cause both land and water pollution. Dumping waste carelessly can affect health, fishing and farming. Prevention and proper treatment are business responsibilities, not just advertising opportunities.\n\nMerchantable quality concerns suitability for the ordinary uses of goods sold under their description. Fitness for a buyer’s particular purpose is a related but distinct requirement. Indigenization increases indigenous ownership; it can reduce foreign ownership and associated outward capital remittances, but it is not naturalization of a person.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Consumer awareness is useful but does not replace actual product quality or safety.\n• A trade association protects members' interests; it is not automatically the statutory quality regulator.\n• Copyright protects original creative expression; patents concern qualifying inventions; trademarks identify commercial origin."},"new":{"title":"Consumer protection, business environment and responsibility","body":"CONSUMER PROTECTION, BUSINESS ENVIRONMENT AND SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA business does not operate in isolation. Prices, technology, laws, competitors and customer expectations affect its decisions. Consumers also need protection against unsafe products and misleading claims. JAMB tests internal versus external influences, consumer rights, regulatory agencies and ethical responsibilities.\n\n1. MEANING OF BUSINESS ENVIRONMENT\nThe business environment comprises conditions and forces that influence an enterprise's activities and results.\n\nINTERNAL FACTORS are mainly within the organisation: employees, management, culture, equipment, financial resources and operating policies.\nEXTERNAL FACTORS arise outside the organisation: customers, suppliers, competitors, economic conditions, law, politics, technology and social trends.\n\nManagers can often control internal factors more directly than external ones, but they must respond to both.\n\n2. MICRO AND MACRO ENVIRONMENTS\nMICRO ENVIRONMENT includes actors closely connected to daily operations: customers, suppliers, intermediaries, competitors and other stakeholders.\n\nMACRO ENVIRONMENT includes broad influences such as economic conditions, population changes, government policy, technological development, natural conditions and cultural expectations.\n\nEXAMPLE: A bakery's flour supplier is a micro-environment actor. A nationwide rise in inflation is a macroeconomic influence.\n\n3. PESTLE ANALYSIS\nPOLITICAL: Government stability, policy direction and public decisions.\nECONOMIC: Inflation, exchange rates, interest rates, income and unemployment.\nSOCIAL: Population, lifestyles, education and consumer preferences.\nTECHNOLOGICAL: Automation, digital payments, online commerce and innovation.\nLEGAL: Consumer, labour, tax, environmental and competition rules.\nENVIRONMENTAL: Weather, climate, pollution, energy and resource availability.\n\nPESTLE helps identify external influences. It does not automatically predict what will happen.\n\n4. SWOT ANALYSIS\nSTRENGTHS: Internal advantages, such as experienced staff.\nWEAKNESSES: Internal limitations, such as outdated machinery.\nOPPORTUNITIES: External favourable possibilities, such as a growing market.\nTHREATS: External dangers, such as new competition or rising input costs.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA neighbourhood pharmacy has skilled staff (strength), limited storage (weakness), growing local demand (opportunity) and a new large competitor (threat).\n\nJAMB TRAP: A competitor is normally an external threat, not an internal weakness. Lack of staff training is usually an internal weakness.\n\n5. MEANING OF CONSUMER PROTECTION\nConsumer protection consists of laws, institutions and practices designed to safeguard buyers against unfair, unsafe, deceptive or exploitative market conduct.\n\nConsumers may face defective goods, misleading advertisements, hidden charges, counterfeit products, unsafe food or unfair contract terms.\n\nProtection encourages confidence in markets and responsible competition.\n\n6. COMMON CONSUMER RIGHTS\nCommonly taught consumer rights include:\n• Right to safety.\n• Right to information.\n• Right to choose among lawful alternatives.\n• Right to be heard.\n• Right to seek redress.\n• Right to consumer education.\n• Right to a healthy environment.\n• Right to satisfaction of basic needs.\n\nThe precise legal remedies and scope of these rights depend on applicable laws and circumstances.\n\n7. CONSUMER RESPONSIBILITIES\nConsumers should read product instructions, compare prices and quality, keep transaction records, use goods responsibly, report genuine problems accurately and avoid fraudulent claims.\n\nEXAMPLE: A customer who buys an electrical appliance should retain the receipt, follow safety instructions and promptly report a defect through the seller's appropriate complaint process.\n\n8. NIGERIAN CONSUMER PROTECTION INSTITUTIONS\nThe FEDERAL COMPETITION AND CONSUMER PROTECTION COMMISSION (FCCPC) is a major Nigerian institution for competition and consumer protection under the applicable legal framework.\n\nNAFDAC regulates specified foods, drugs, cosmetics, medical devices and related products under its mandate.\nThe STANDARDS ORGANISATION OF NIGERIA (SON) addresses standards and conformity assessment within its legal functions.\nSector regulators may handle complaints concerning banking, telecommunications, electricity and other regulated services.\n\nJAMB TRAP: NAFDAC, SON and FCCPC do not perform identical functions. A complaint about an unsafe medicine differs from a dispute over an unfair commercial practice, although responsibilities can overlap.\n\n9. COMPLAINT AND REDRESS PROCEDURE\nA sensible consumer response may involve:\n1. Identify the defect or unfair practice.\n2. Gather receipt, warranty, photographs and relevant messages.\n3. Contact the seller or provider and state the requested remedy.\n4. Escalate through appropriate complaint channels or regulator where necessary.\n5. Seek lawful mediation, adjudication or court remedies as appropriate.\n\nNot every dissatisfaction automatically entitles a buyer to any remedy they choose. Facts, law and contract terms matter.\n\n10. BUSINESS ETHICS\nBusiness ethics concerns standards of honest and responsible commercial conduct.\n\nExamples:\n• Truthful advertising and accurate measurements.\n• Fair treatment of workers and customers.\n• Proper accounting and payment of lawful taxes.\n• Avoiding bribery and conflicts of interest.\n• Protecting confidential customer information.\n• Respecting safety and environmental requirements.\n\nAn action can be profitable in the short term but unethical or unlawful.\n\n11. CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY\nCorporate social responsibility (CSR) concerns how businesses account for their effects on employees, communities, customers and the environment.\n\nExamples include safe workplaces, waste reduction, fair supply-chain practices, community education and responsible resource use.\n\nCSR is not limited to donations. A company that gives scholarships but pollutes a community still has serious responsibility problems.\n\n12. SUSTAINABILITY AND STAKEHOLDERS\nSTAKEHOLDERS are groups affected by or able to affect a business: owners, workers, customers, suppliers, lenders, communities and government.\n\nSUSTAINABILITY involves meeting present needs while considering long-term environmental, social and economic consequences.\n\nEXAMPLE: A manufacturer installing wastewater treatment may incur immediate costs but reduce pollution, regulatory risk and community harm.\n\n13. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Is employee training an internal or external factor? Internal.\nQ2. Is inflation a micro or macro influence? Macro.\nQ3. What does the 'T' in PESTLE mean? Technological.\nQ4. In SWOT, a new competitor is generally what? Threat.\nQ5. In SWOT, strong management is what? Strength.\nQ6. Which Nigerian body is central to competition and consumer protection? FCCPC.\nQ7. Which agency is associated with regulation of specified foods and medicines? NAFDAC.\nQ8. What does the right to information oppose? Misleading or withheld material product information.\nQ9. Is CSR only charitable donations? No.\nQ10. Name two stakeholders besides shareholders. Workers and customers, for example.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Internal factors and external factors must be separated.\n• SWOT strengths and weaknesses are internal; opportunities and threats are external.\n• Consumer protection includes safety and fair conduct, not merely low prices.\n• Different regulators have different mandates.\n• CSR includes how the business operates, not only gifts to communities.\n• Ethics and legal compliance are related but not identical.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nPESTLE = BROAD EXTERNAL FORCES.\nSWOT = INTERNAL STRENGTHS/WEAKNESSES + EXTERNAL OPPORTUNITIES/THREATS."}},{"id":"tourism","old":{"title":"Tourism as an aid to trade","body":"Tourism involves travelling and staying outside one's usual environment for purposes such as leisure, business or other permitted short-term visits. It creates demand for accommodation, food, transport, entertainment and related services. Domestic tourism occurs within a person's country; international tourism crosses national borders. A business visitor can generate tourism expenditure even without taking a holiday.\n\nTourism assists commerce by bringing customers to service providers and creating markets for local goods. Hotels buy food and supplies; guides provide information; transport operators move visitors; craftspeople sell products. Foreign visitors' spending can bring foreign-exchange earnings, while residents travelling abroad create spending outside the domestic economy. These service transactions are commonly described as invisible trade in school Commerce.\n\nAttractions may be natural, historical, cultural or business-related. Beaches, landscapes, festivals, museums and conferences can draw visitors. However, an attraction alone does not guarantee a successful tourism industry. Safe access, reliable transport, accommodation, sanitation, communication, skilled workers and information are also needed. Conservation protects attractions from damage and helps communities benefit over time.\n\nGovernment and specialized agencies can develop standards, promote destinations, support infrastructure and provide relevant information. Private businesses supply many of the actual services. A tourism-promotion agency is not the same as a customs authority, airline or hotel. Institutional names and responsibilities can change, so a past-paper answer should be tied to the stated period rather than silently replaced with a new acronym.\n\nBenefits include employment, tax receipts, income for local businesses and incentives to preserve cultural or natural sites. Possible costs include seasonal jobs, congestion, pollution, pressure on local resources and leakage when substantial spending goes to foreign suppliers. Responsible planning should consider residents as well as visitors.\n\nWorked example: visitors attending a festival in Osun State pay local transport operators, buy meals and stay in guesthouses. Their spending supports several businesses, not only the festival organizer. If the visitors are Nigerian residents, the example is domestic tourism rather than an export receipt from foreign visitors.\n\nTourism planning begins by identifying and assessing attractions and resources. Historic buildings are cultural attractions. Cultural interaction can be a benefit, while communities may fear unwanted foreign influences. Social benefits differ from financial receipts.\n\nThe World Tourism Organization is headquartered in Madrid. Distinguish it from the World Trade Organization.\n\nInbound tourism concerns non-residents visiting the country considered; outbound tourism concerns residents visiting another country.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Tourism is a service activity, not merely the sale of souvenirs.\n• A foreign-exchange benefit applies particularly to international visitor spending.\n• Poor security, unreliable infrastructure and weak publicity are obstacles, not inherent advantages.\n• Tourism's economic contribution does not remove the need to protect local communities and the environment."},"new":{"title":"Tourism as an aid to trade","body":"TOURISM AS AN AID TO TRADE — VISITORS CREATE MARKETS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA visitor who travels to a city may spend on accommodation, transport, food, crafts and entertainment. Tourism therefore creates demand across many sectors. JAMB tests the meaning and types of tourism, its economic benefits and costs, tourism facilities and the difference between tourism receipts and spending abroad.\n\n1. MEANING OF TOURISM\nTourism involves travel and stays outside a person's usual environment for leisure, business or other permitted purposes within relevant time and activity definitions. A tourist is not simply anyone travelling to work each day.\n\nA business traveller may be included in tourism activity even without a holiday. Ordinary commuting within one's usual environment is generally not tourism.\n\n2. DOMESTIC, INBOUND AND OUTBOUND TOURISM\nDOMESTIC TOURISM: Residents travel within their own country for tourism purposes.\nINBOUND TOURISM: Non-residents visit a country.\nOUTBOUND TOURISM: Residents travel to other countries.\n\nEXAMPLE: A resident of Lagos visits Calabar for a holiday: domestic tourism. A visitor from Ghana travels to Nigeria for leisure: inbound tourism for Nigeria. A Nigerian resident holidays in Kenya: outbound tourism for Nigeria.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Inbound and outbound depend on the country whose perspective is being used.\n\n3. PURPOSES AND FORMS OF TOURISM\nLEISURE TOURISM: Holidays, recreation and sightseeing.\nBUSINESS TOURISM: Meetings, conferences, exhibitions and other qualifying business travel.\nCULTURAL OR HERITAGE TOURISM: Festivals, museums and historic sites.\nECOTOURISM: Responsible nature-based tourism emphasising conservation and local benefits.\nRELIGIOUS TOURISM: Visits for religious purposes.\nSPORTS TOURISM: Travel to participate in or attend sports events.\nEDUCATIONAL TOURISM: Qualifying study tours and educational visits.\n\nCategories can overlap: a visitor may attend a conference and visit cultural attractions.\n\n4. TOURISM AS AN AID TO TRADE\nTourism supports trade by creating demand for services and locally produced goods. Visitors may purchase food, accommodation, transport, souvenirs, guide services and event tickets.\n\nTOURISM VALUE CHAIN\nVisitor → Transport → Accommodation → Food/Attractions → Local Suppliers.\n\nEXAMPLE: A hotel buys vegetables from local farmers, linen from a textile trader and maintenance services from technicians. Tourist spending supports these suppliers indirectly as well as the hotel directly.\n\n5. ECONOMIC BENEFITS\n• Employment in hotels, transport, guiding and entertainment.\n• Revenue for small businesses and suppliers.\n• Foreign-exchange receipts from eligible international visitors.\n• Tax revenue under applicable arrangements.\n• Investment in infrastructure and facilities.\n• Opportunities for cultural industries and crafts.\n• Economic diversification in suitable destinations.\n\nA tourism job may be seasonal. Benefits depend on how much spending remains in the local economy.\n\n6. TOURISM MULTIPLIER AND LEAKAGES\nThe tourism multiplier describes how initial visitor spending can generate further rounds of income and expenditure.\n\nEXAMPLE: A tourist pays ₦50,000 to a local guesthouse. The guesthouse pays workers and buys food from local suppliers. Workers and suppliers may spend part of that income locally, supporting other businesses.\n\nLEAKAGE occurs when tourism spending leaves the local economy, such as payment for imported supplies or profits remitted abroad. The larger the leakage, other things equal, the smaller the local income effect.\n\nDo not assume that every naira of visitor spending becomes local profit.\n\n7. TOURISM INFRASTRUCTURE\nTourism depends on:\n• Roads, airports and suitable transport.\n• Accommodation and food services.\n• Reliable water, electricity and communications.\n• Health, safety and security services.\n• Attractions, museums and event facilities.\n• Trained guides and hospitality workers.\n• Accessible information and booking systems.\n\nGood attractions alone may not produce successful tourism without safety and access.\n\n8. TRAVEL AGENTS AND TOUR OPERATORS\nTRAVEL AGENT: Helps customers arrange transport, accommodation, tickets and other travel services, often acting as an intermediary.\nTOUR OPERATOR: Organises or packages combinations of travel services, such as transport, accommodation and activities, for sale.\n\nEXAMPLE: An operator designs a three-day cultural tour including hotel and transport. A travel agent may sell that package to a customer.\n\nThe roles can overlap in practice, but the textbook distinction is useful.\n\n9. POSITIVE SOCIAL AND CULTURAL EFFECTS\nTourism can encourage preservation of heritage sites, cultural exchange, support for crafts and community pride. It may increase demand for better public facilities that residents also use.\n\n10. NEGATIVE EFFECTS AND RISKS\n• Environmental damage, waste and pollution.\n• Overcrowding at popular attractions.\n• Rising local prices or pressure on housing.\n• Cultural commercialisation or loss of authenticity.\n• Seasonal and insecure employment.\n• Vulnerability to insecurity, disease outbreaks or economic downturns.\n• Overdependence on visitor spending.\n\nSustainable tourism seeks to manage environmental and social impacts while maintaining long-term economic benefits.\n\n11. TOURISM AND FOREIGN EXCHANGE\nWhen a non-resident purchases tourism services in Nigeria, the transaction can contribute to Nigeria's international services exports under relevant accounting definitions. When Nigerian residents spend on tourism abroad, that generally contributes to imported travel services.\n\nTourism is therefore commonly treated as an INVISIBLE TRADE activity because it principally involves services rather than merchandise exports.\n\n12. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. A Nigerian resident visits another Nigerian state for leisure. What type? Domestic tourism.\nQ2. A foreign resident visits Nigeria for a conference. Inbound or outbound for Nigeria? Inbound.\nQ3. A Nigerian resident holidays abroad. What type for Nigeria? Outbound.\nQ4. Is tourism generally visible or invisible trade? Invisible, mainly services.\nQ5. Name two direct tourism services. Accommodation and transport.\nQ6. What is a tourism multiplier? Further income and spending generated by initial visitor expenditure.\nQ7. What is tourism leakage? Spending that flows out of the local economy.\nQ8. Who commonly assembles a package holiday? Tour operator.\nQ9. Is daily commuting normally tourism? No.\nQ10. Name one possible negative impact. Pollution, overcrowding or seasonal employment.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Domestic and inbound tourism are different.\n• Business travel may count as tourism.\n• Tourism receipts are not identical to net profit.\n• A tour operator commonly packages services; an agent often arranges or sells them.\n• Foreign visitor spending may support services exports.\n• More visitors do not automatically mean sustainable development.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nDOMESTIC = WITHIN HOME COUNTRY.\nINBOUND = VISITORS COME IN.\nOUTBOUND = RESIDENTS GO OUT.\nTOURISM = SERVICES + LOCAL SUPPLY CHAINS."}},{"id":"trade-associations","old":{"title":"Trade associations, chambers and professional bodies","body":"A trade association is a voluntary organization of businesses or persons in the same trade formed to promote their common interests. Members may exchange information, improve standards, make representations to government and discuss common commercial problems. A trade association is not formed primarily to buy every member's goods or distribute profits like an ordinary trading company.\n\nA chamber of commerce brings together businesses from different trades within an area to promote commerce generally. It may provide business information, encourage trade contacts, organize fairs and exhibitions, represent business concerns and assist with permitted documentation. Its broader membership distinguishes it from an association limited to one trade. A chamber does not replace the courts, customs authority or central bank.\n\nA manufacturers' association represents manufacturing interests. It can advise government on industrial policies, research common problems and disseminate information through publications and meetings. An employers' association represents employers in matters such as employment relations and negotiations. A trade union represents workers. Improving workers' welfare can be a consequence of several organizations' activities, but that does not erase the distinction between employer and employee representation.\n\nA professional body concerns people practising a particular profession. It may establish qualifications, professional conduct and disciplinary arrangements under its governing framework. The Nigerian Bar Association concerns the legal profession, while a statutory regulator's powers must be distinguished from a voluntary association's advocacy. A question about service-rendering professions is different from one about associations of miners, farmers or manufacturers.\n\nConsumer associations represent buyers' interests, provide information and press for fair treatment. They may report harmful practices or advise consumers, but they do not automatically possess every enforcement power of a government agency. Collective advocacy and statutory regulation can support one another without being identical.\n\nWorked example: several garment producers form an association to discuss textile tariffs and training. That is a trade or manufacturers' interest organization. If banks, shops, manufacturers and transport firms in the same city cooperate to promote local commerce, a chamber of commerce is the more suitable description.\n\nNACCIMA is the Nigerian Association of Chambers of Commerce, Industry, Mines and Agriculture. It is an umbrella association for chambers; distinguish its exact name from plausible but incorrect alternatives.\n\nHistorical papers assign trade-association registration to the commerce ministry; check the institution’s period.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A trade association is not the same as a trade union.\n• Promoting members' interests does not mean guaranteeing every member a profit.\n• Organizing a trade fair can promote contact and publicity; it does not itself transfer title to every displayed product.\n• Match an organization's actual membership and functions to the question, not merely a familiar acronym."},"new":{"title":"Trade associations, chambers and professional bodies","body":"TRADE ASSOCIATIONS, CHAMBERS AND PROFESSIONAL BODIES — ORGANISING COMMON INTERESTS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nBusinesses often cooperate even while competing for customers. They may join associations to share information, set standards, train members or represent common concerns. JAMB tests the differences between trade associations, chambers of commerce, employers' associations, professional bodies and trade unions.\n\n1. MEANING OF TRADE ASSOCIATION\nA trade association is an organisation formed by businesses or persons engaged in the same or related trade to promote their common interests.\n\nExamples include associations of retailers, transport operators or manufacturers in a particular industry. Membership and governance depend on the organisation's constitution and applicable law.\n\nA trade association is not ordinarily created mainly to distribute trading profits to members like an ordinary company.\n\n2. FUNCTIONS OF TRADE ASSOCIATIONS\n• Represent members' common concerns to government and regulators.\n• Share industry news, market information and research.\n• Promote quality and ethical standards.\n• Organise training, conferences and exhibitions.\n• Encourage cooperation and dispute resolution.\n• Provide advice on legislation and business conditions.\n• Support members in addressing common technical challenges.\n• Promote the reputation of the trade.\n\nEXAMPLE: An association of furniture manufacturers organises training on safer machinery and submits recommendations on timber-supply challenges.\n\n3. ADVANTAGES TO MEMBERS\nSmall firms may gain a stronger collective voice, access to information and opportunities to network. Joint training can reduce individual costs, while shared standards may build consumer confidence.\n\nAssociations may also improve dialogue between business and public authorities.\n\n4. LIMITATIONS AND RISKS\n• Membership fees and administrative costs.\n• Disagreement between large and small members.\n• Weak leadership or poor representation.\n• Decisions that do not suit every member.\n• Risk of anti-competitive conduct if competitors coordinate prices or divide markets.\n\nIMPORTANT: An association must not assume that its collective status permits unlawful price-fixing or cartel arrangements. Legitimate advocacy and industry standards differ from collusion.\n\n5. CHAMBER OF COMMERCE\nA chamber of commerce is an organisation promoting trade, industry and business interests, often across several sectors in a particular locality, region or country.\n\nUnlike an industry-specific trade association, a chamber commonly includes members from many trades.\n\nFunctions may include business networking, trade fairs, investment promotion, information services, policy advocacy and support for commercial contacts.\n\nEXAMPLE: A city chamber may bring together banks, hotels, manufacturers and traders to discuss infrastructure needs.\n\n6. TRADE ASSOCIATION VERSUS CHAMBER\nTRADE ASSOCIATION: Usually represents one trade or related industry.\nCHAMBER OF COMMERCE: Usually represents businesses across multiple industries or within a geographic business community.\n\nBoth may organise exhibitions and speak to government. The key distinction is the breadth and basis of membership.\n\n7. PROFESSIONAL BODIES\nA professional body represents or regulates aspects of an occupation requiring specialised knowledge and standards. Depending on law and its status, it may set ethical rules, support qualifications, organise continuing education or discipline members.\n\nEXAMPLE: A professional accounting body focuses on competence and professional conduct rather than buying and reselling merchandise.\n\nA professional body is not automatically a trade union, although members may share occupational interests.\n\n8. TRADE UNIONS\nA trade union is an organisation of workers formed to protect and advance members' employment-related interests.\n\nFunctions may include collective bargaining, representation in workplace disputes, welfare support and advocacy concerning pay, safety and conditions.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A trade association typically represents businesses or traders; a trade union typically represents workers in employment relations.\n\n9. EMPLOYERS' ASSOCIATIONS\nEmployers' associations represent employers' shared interests, including labour relations, policy engagement and employment practices.\n\nEmployers' associations may participate in collective bargaining with unions. They are not identical to worker unions.\n\n10. COOPERATIVES COMPARED\nA cooperative is a member-owned and democratically controlled enterprise organised to meet members' needs, often through trading, credit or production.\n\nA trade association mainly coordinates and represents interests; it does not necessarily operate a joint member-owned trading enterprise.\n\nEXAMPLE: Farmers forming a cooperative to market produce jointly differs from farmers joining an association to advocate better road access, although an organisation may perform more than one function.\n\n11. TRADE FAIRS AND EXHIBITIONS\nA TRADE FAIR brings businesses and potential buyers together to display and promote goods and services, establish contacts and gather market information.\n\nAn EXHIBITION is a display of products or information, sometimes within a trade fair. These events can generate sales leads, but participation does not guarantee orders.\n\n12. BUSINESS ETHICS AND COMPETITION\nTrade bodies can promote product quality, safety, consumer education and professional training. They should avoid practices that restrict competition unlawfully or mislead customers.\n\nEXAMPLE: Agreeing on common safety training is different from agreeing that all member shops must charge the same inflated price.\n\n13. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which body typically represents firms in one industry? Trade association.\nQ2. Which body commonly includes businesses from many sectors in a locality? Chamber of commerce.\nQ3. Which organisation primarily represents workers' employment interests? Trade union.\nQ4. Which organisation commonly represents employers in labour relations? Employers' association.\nQ5. Which body may set professional ethical standards? Professional body.\nQ6. What is a trade fair? An event where businesses display and promote offerings.\nQ7. Is a cooperative identical to a trade association? No.\nQ8. Name one benefit of trade association membership. Information, training or collective representation.\nQ9. Can competitors lawfully fix prices simply because they belong to an association? No.\nQ10. What is a chamber's broad objective? Promoting commerce and business interests.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Trade associations and trade unions are not synonyms.\n• A chamber of commerce generally spans multiple trades.\n• A professional body focuses on occupational standards and competence.\n• A cooperative jointly serves members through an enterprise; an association may chiefly represent interests.\n• Trade fairs promote products and contacts but do not guarantee sales.\n• Industry cooperation does not legalise price-fixing.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nTRADE ASSOCIATION = SAME INDUSTRY.\nCHAMBER = MANY INDUSTRIES.\nTRADE UNION = WORKERS.\nPROFESSIONAL BODY = OCCUPATIONAL STANDARDS."}},{"id":"business-computing","old":{"title":"Computer types, processing, storage and business software","body":"A computer is an electronic device that accepts data, processes it according to instructions, stores it and produces information. Hardware is the physical equipment. Software consists of programs that tell the equipment what to do. The keyboard is an input device; a monitor and printer are output devices. Input, processing and output units are hardware, not three kinds of software.\n\nThe central processing unit, or CPU, controls processing. Its control unit fetches and interprets instructions and coordinates the movement of data. The arithmetic and logic unit performs calculations and logical comparisons. Registers hold small amounts of data needed immediately by the processor. Calling the whole CPU the coordinating section is less precise than naming its control unit.\n\nDigital computers process discrete data represented by digits. Analogue computers represent continuously varying quantities. Hybrid systems combine analogue and digital features. These describe methods of handling data. Supercomputers, mainframes, minicomputers and microcomputers are traditional capacity or size classes. A list mixing analogue and hybrid with mainframe and microcomputer is not a consistent list of digital size classes. Modern weather forecasting commonly uses powerful digital computers; older school examples may describe analogue measurements combined with digital calculations.\n\nPrimary memory supports current processing. RAM normally loses its contents when power is removed. Secondary storage keeps files for later use. DASD means Direct Access Storage Device: a record can be reached without first reading every preceding record. “Assess” is a spelling error for “Access,” not a different storage principle. Permanent storage does not mean that a file can never be erased.\n\nA spreadsheet such as Microsoft Excel performs calculations and presents financial tables or charts. A database stores related records and supports searches and reports. A word processor prepares text documents. Choose the application from the actual task: calculations suggest a spreadsheet, while searching a structured customer list suggests a database.\n\nWorked example: an Abuja shop stores customer records in a database, calculates monthly sales in a spreadsheet and prints letters using a word processor. These tasks may exchange data, but the applications have different main purposes.\n\nA cybercafé pays for electricity, connectivity, equipment, maintenance and staff. These operating costs can be a major constraint; the leading constraint varies by place and period, so a paper’s ranking is contextual.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Software is not a physical input device.\n• Digital classification and capacity classification answer different questions.\n• The control unit coordinates; the arithmetic and logic unit calculates and compares.\n• An application's familiar name does not establish that it best fits every business task."},"new":{"title":"Computer types, processing, storage and business software","body":"COMPUTER TYPES, PROCESSING, STORAGE AND BUSINESS SOFTWARE — FROM RAW DATA TO DECISIONS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nRetailers use point-of-sale systems, banks process transfers and warehouses track stock with computers. JAMB tests input and output devices, CPU functions, primary and secondary memory, types of computers, software and business applications. Understand what each component does instead of memorising abbreviations alone.\n\n1. MEANING OF COMPUTER\nA computer is an electronic programmable device that accepts data, processes it according to instructions, stores it and produces output.\n\nDATA are raw facts; INFORMATION is organised or processed data meaningful to a user.\n\nEXAMPLE: Individual sales entries of ₦2,000, ₦3,500 and ₦4,500 are data. A daily total of ₦10,000 is useful information.\n\n2. INFORMATION-PROCESSING CYCLE\nINPUT → PROCESSING → OUTPUT → STORAGE/FEEDBACK.\n\nINPUT: Entering data or instructions.\nPROCESSING: Performing operations on data.\nOUTPUT: Presenting results.\nSTORAGE: Keeping data and programs for future use.\n\nA cashier scans a barcode (input), the POS system calculates the amount due (processing), a screen displays the total (output) and the transaction is recorded (storage).\n\n3. INPUT DEVICES\nKEYBOARD: Enters letters, numbers and commands.\nMOUSE OR TOUCHPAD: Controls pointer and selection.\nSCANNER: Converts printed material into digital form.\nBARCODE READER: Reads encoded product identifiers.\nMICROPHONE: Captures sound.\nTOUCHSCREEN: Can accept touch input and display output.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A touchscreen can serve both input and output functions.\n\n4. OUTPUT DEVICES\nMONITOR: Displays text and graphics.\nPRINTER: Produces hard-copy documents.\nSPEAKER: Produces sound.\nPROJECTOR: Displays images on a larger surface.\n\nSOFT COPY is electronic output; HARD COPY is physical printed output.\n\n5. CENTRAL PROCESSING UNIT\nThe CPU executes instructions and coordinates processing. Two traditional textbook components are:\nCONTROL UNIT (CU): Directs operations and coordinates instruction execution.\nARITHMETIC AND LOGIC UNIT (ALU): Performs arithmetic and logical comparisons.\n\nREGISTERS are very fast storage locations used during processing. Modern CPUs also contain caches and other components.\n\nJAMB TRAP: The CPU is not the same as a monitor or the whole computer cabinet.\n\n6. PRIMARY MEMORY\nRAM (RANDOM ACCESS MEMORY): Working memory used by running programs and active data; ordinarily volatile, meaning contents are lost when power is removed.\nROM (READ-ONLY MEMORY): Traditionally stores firmware or instructions retained without power; modern implementations vary.\nCACHE: Very fast memory helping reduce processor access time to frequently needed data.\n\nPrimary memory is generally accessed directly by the processing system. Not all memory types have the same speed or purpose.\n\n7. SECONDARY STORAGE\nSecondary storage keeps programs and data for longer-term use.\nExamples: hard disk drives, solid-state drives, USB flash drives, memory cards and optical discs.\n\nSSD: Uses flash memory, typically fast and without moving mechanical parts.\nHDD: Uses magnetic rotating disks.\nCLOUD STORAGE: Data held on remote infrastructure accessed through a network, subject to service arrangements.\n\nRAM is not a substitute for a permanent backup. Saving work to a drive or remote service helps retain it after shutdown.\n\n8. STORAGE UNITS\nBIT: Binary digit, 0 or 1.\nBYTE: Commonly eight bits.\nKILOBYTE, MEGABYTE, GIGABYTE and TERABYTE are progressively larger units, with decimal or binary conventions depending on context.\n\nIn simplified school calculations, 1 KB may be treated as 1,024 bytes; in decimal SI usage, 1 kB is 1,000 bytes. Follow the convention stated in the question.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A file contains 2,048 bytes. Under the 1 KB = 1,024 bytes classroom convention, size = 2 KB.\n\n9. TYPES OF COMPUTERS\nMICROCOMPUTERS OR PERSONAL COMPUTERS: Desktops and laptops used by individuals and businesses.\nSERVERS: Systems providing resources or services to other devices.\nMAINFRAMES: Powerful systems designed for high-volume enterprise processing and reliability.\nSUPERCOMPUTERS: Specialised high-performance systems used for demanding scientific and technical calculations.\n\nA smartphone is also a computer. The categories reflect roles and capabilities, not simply the physical size of a device.\n\n10. ANALOG, DIGITAL AND HYBRID COMPUTERS\nANALOG COMPUTING handles continuously varying quantities in specialised applications.\nDIGITAL COMPUTING represents and processes discrete values, typically binary data.\nHYBRID SYSTEMS combine analog and digital processing where appropriate.\n\nModern commercial laptops and POS terminals are digital computers.\n\n11. SYSTEM AND APPLICATION SOFTWARE\nSYSTEM SOFTWARE manages hardware and basic computing operations. Examples: operating systems, device drivers and utilities.\n\nAPPLICATION SOFTWARE performs user tasks:\n• Word processor: letters and reports.\n• Spreadsheet: calculations, budgets and charts.\n• Database system: structured records and queries.\n• Accounting package: transactions and financial reports.\n• Inventory software: stock balances and reorder alerts.\n• Point-of-sale software: sales and payments.\n• Presentation software: slides.\n\n12. DATABASES AND SPREADSHEETS\nA DATABASE organises data for storage, retrieval and controlled updates. A customer table may include customer ID, name and contact information.\n\nA SPREADSHEET uses cells arranged in rows and columns, with formulas and functions.\nWORKED EXAMPLE: If cells B2:B4 contain 5, 8 and 7 units sold, =SUM(B2:B4) returns 20 in a spreadsheet supporting that formula.\n\nDatabases are often preferable for large structured records with multiple users; spreadsheets are useful for flexible analysis and calculation.\n\n13. NETWORKING AND BUSINESS SYSTEMS\nA LAN connects devices within a limited area; a WAN spans wider areas. Businesses may use networks to share printers, stock records, sales information and internet access.\n\nAn INTEGRATED SYSTEM can connect sales, inventory and accounting so that a sale updates stock and financial records, reducing repeated manual entry.\n\n14. SECURITY, BACKUP AND RELIABILITY\nBusinesses should use access controls, passwords or passkeys, appropriate updates, malware protection, backups and verification of financial instructions.\n\nBACKUP means retaining a recoverable copy of data. A backup should be tested; simply storing all copies on one failing device is risky.\n\n15. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What device enters printed product codes at checkout? Barcode reader.\nQ2. What unit performs arithmetic and logical comparisons? ALU.\nQ3. What CPU component coordinates instructions? Control unit.\nQ4. Is RAM ordinarily volatile? Yes.\nQ5. Name one secondary storage device. SSD or HDD.\nQ6. Is a printer an input or output device? Output.\nQ7. What is a spreadsheet used for? Tabular calculations and analysis.\nQ8. What is the difference between data and information? Raw facts versus meaningful processed results.\nQ9. Which software category includes operating systems? System software.\nQ10. Under the 1,024-byte convention, how many KB are 4,096 bytes? 4 KB.\n\nCOMMON JAMB TRAPS\n• Hardware is physical; software is instructions and programs.\n• Input and output devices serve different roles; some devices do both.\n• RAM is not permanent storage.\n• CPU, system unit and monitor are not synonyms.\n• Data are raw facts; information is interpreted or organised data.\n• Storage-unit conventions should follow the question.\n• A backup is different from an ordinary working copy.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nINPUT → PROCESS → OUTPUT → STORE.\nCPU = CU + ALU (SIMPLIFIED TEXTBOOK MODEL).\nRAM = WORKING MEMORY; SSD/HDD = LONGER-TERM STORAGE."}},{"id":"office-communication","old":{"title":"Office procedures and the communication process","body":"Communication is the transmission of information and meaning from a sender to a receiver. The sender forms an idea and encodes it into words, signs or another understandable form. The message travels through a channel or medium. The receiver decodes it and may provide feedback. Feedback tells the sender whether the message was received and understood; it is not the original message travelling forward.\n\nNoise is anything that interferes with communication. It includes loud sound, a weak telephone connection, unfamiliar words, unclear handwriting and distraction. Distance can increase communication difficulties, but suitable technology may overcome it. A communication diagram should be read by following the arrows, not by guessing a letter from its position on the page.\n\nDownward communication passes from a superior to a subordinate, such as a manager giving instructions. Upward communication passes from a subordinate to a superior, such as a sales report. Horizontal or lateral communication occurs between people at comparable organizational levels. A department sending information to another department does not always make it upward or downward.\n\nAn internal memorandum, commonly called a memo, carries official information within an organization. Circulars send the same information to several recipients. Telephones and face-to-face discussion permit quick oral exchange. Notice boards display information where intended readers can see it. Advertising normally addresses customers or the public, so it is not the ordinary channel for confidential departmental instructions.\n\nA procedure states the ordered steps for carrying out a task. A policy supplies a general guide to decisions. A programme coordinates activities and timing to achieve an objective. These are connected management tools, but a step-by-step office method is specifically a procedure.\n\nThe Internet connects networks worldwide. Email is one Internet service for exchanging electronic messages. GSM mobile communication allows users to communicate while moving between suitable network coverage areas. Historical questions may contrast telephone, radio, telegraph and television; identify whether the message is oral, written, visual, public or private before choosing.\n\nWorked example: a Kaduna manager sends branch supervisors a circular about stocktaking. Each supervisor explains the instructions to assistants and sends a completion report back. The instructions are downward; the report is upward; the reply also provides feedback.\n\nA telegram is a written message sent through telegraph services. Telex uses linked teleprinters to transmit typed messages.\n\nReliable communication can reduce travel costs and risks, connect participants and improve coordination of goods delivery.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sending a message does not prove understanding.\n• Feedback and noise are different parts of the process.\n• An advertisement is not normally an internal memo.\n• Choose a medium for the stated audience and purpose, not simply because it reaches many people."},"new":{"title":"Office procedures and the communication process","body":"OFFICE PROCEDURES AND COMMUNICATION — HOW INFORMATION BECOMES ACTION\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nOffices receive enquiries, process orders, keep records and communicate decisions. JAMB questions distinguish communication channels, office documents, filing systems, meetings and the role of feedback. Learn both the sequence and the purpose of each stage.\n\n1. FUNCTIONS OF AN OFFICE\nAn office is a centre for collecting, processing, storing and communicating information needed to run an organisation. Typical functions include correspondence, record-keeping, customer enquiries, appointment scheduling, document preparation, payment records and administrative coordination. An office need not be a separate physical building: some functions can be performed remotely through secure digital systems.\n\n2. THE COMMUNICATION PROCESS\nA SENDER develops an idea and ENCODES it into understandable words, symbols or signals. A MESSAGE travels through a CHANNEL to the RECEIVER, who DECODES it. FEEDBACK communicates the receiver's response. NOISE is anything that interferes with the message.\n\nEXAMPLE: A manager emails a supplier to deliver 40 cartons on Tuesday. The supplier replies confirming 40 cartons for Tuesday. That confirmation is feedback. If the supplier hears '14' rather than '40' on a noisy telephone call, the interference illustrates noise.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Feedback is not the same as the original message; it flows back from receiver to sender.\n\n3. CHANNELS OF COMMUNICATION\nORAL: Meetings, telephone calls and conversations. Advantage: immediate clarification; weakness: details may be forgotten.\nWRITTEN: Letters, memos, reports, emails and text messages. Advantage: records can be retained; weakness: preparation and responses may take time.\nVISUAL: Charts, signs, diagrams and notices. Advantage: can explain relationships quickly; weakness: ambiguous symbols can confuse.\nELECTRONIC: Email, video calls and business messaging. These are delivery technologies that may carry written, oral or visual communication.\n\nChoose channels based on urgency, confidentiality, complexity, cost, reach and need for an audit trail.\n\n4. DIRECTIONS OF ORGANISATIONAL COMMUNICATION\nDOWNWARD: Manager to subordinate, for instructions and policies.\nUPWARD: Employee to manager, for reports and suggestions.\nHORIZONTAL: Between colleagues or departments at comparable levels.\nDIAGONAL: Between different departments and organisational levels.\n\nEXAMPLE: A cashier reports a cash discrepancy to the branch manager: upward communication. Two departmental heads coordinate an event: horizontal communication.\n\n5. INTERNAL OFFICE DOCUMENTS\nMEMORANDUM (MEMO): Brief official internal communication.\nCIRCULAR: Similar information distributed to multiple people.\nNOTICE: Announcement about an event, rule or requirement.\nAGENDA: Topics scheduled for a meeting.\nMINUTES: Record of meeting discussions, resolutions and action points.\nREPORT: Structured findings, evidence and recommendations.\n\nEXAMINATION DISTINCTION: Agenda is prepared before a meeting; minutes document what occurred during it.\n\n6. INCOMING AND OUTGOING CORRESPONDENCE\nIncoming correspondence is received, dated where appropriate, sorted, registered or logged, directed to the responsible officer and filed or answered.\nOutgoing correspondence is drafted, checked for accuracy and authority, approved if required, dispatched and recorded.\n\nEXAMPLE: A customer's complaint letter arrives. The receptionist logs it, sends it to customer service, and retains the response record. Losing the original complaint would weaken accountability.\n\n7. FILING AND RECORD MANAGEMENT\nALPHABETICAL FILING: Arranges by names.\nNUMERICAL FILING: Arranges by assigned numbers.\nSUBJECT FILING: Groups documents by topic.\nCHRONOLOGICAL FILING: Arranges by date.\nGEOGRAPHICAL FILING: Groups records by location.\n\nAn effective system enables accurate retrieval, controlled access, secure retention and lawful disposal. Digital filing also requires backups and permissions.\n\n8. OFFICE EQUIPMENT\nPRINTER produces hard copies; SCANNER digitises paper; PHOTOCOPIER reproduces documents; SHREDDER destroys sensitive paper; COMPUTER processes and stores data; TELEPHONE enables spoken communication. A multifunction device may combine printing, scanning and copying.\n\nThe best equipment depends on volume, accuracy, security and cost—not simply modern appearance.\n\n9. RECEPTION AND CUSTOMER SERVICE\nReception staff welcome visitors, verify appointments, direct enquiries and protect confidential information. Good telephone etiquette includes identifying the organisation, listening carefully, confirming important details and transferring calls correctly.\n\nA receptionist should not disclose a customer's financial records merely because a caller sounds authoritative.\n\n10. MEETINGS AND DECISION RECORDS\nA meeting normally has a purpose, notice, agenda, chairperson and record of decisions. A QUORUM is the minimum attendance required for valid proceedings under applicable rules. A RESOLUTION is a formal decision. Minutes should identify decisions and assigned actions accurately.\n\n11. BARRIERS AND REMEDIES\nBarriers include physical noise, poor connectivity, unfamiliar jargon, ambiguous wording, information overload, language differences and poor listening. Remedies include simple language, suitable media, confirmation, translation where needed, clear subject lines and follow-up.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO: An office sends 'ship next week' to a supplier. The phrase is vague. 'Dispatch 60 cartons by Wednesday, 12 November, and send the tracking reference' is more precise.\n\n12. DIGITAL OFFICE SAFETY\nUse authorised access, strong authentication, secure backups, accurate data entry and careful handling of personal information. Phishing messages may impersonate managers or banks. Verify unusual payment instructions through an independent trusted channel.\n\n13. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What is the response from receiver to sender? Feedback.\nQ2. What is interference in message transmission? Noise.\nQ3. Which document lists items for discussion? Agenda.\nQ4. Which document records meeting decisions? Minutes.\nQ5. A manager instructs a clerk. Direction? Downward.\nQ6. Two unit heads coordinate. Direction? Horizontal.\nQ7. Which filing method groups records by date? Chronological.\nQ8. Which equipment converts paper to digital images? Scanner.\nQ9. What is a quorum? Minimum required attendance under meeting rules.\nQ10. Why log incoming correspondence? To track receipt, routing and action.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• The office is an information-processing centre, not merely a room.\n• Sending a message does not guarantee understanding.\n• Memo, circular, agenda, minutes and report have different purposes.\n• Confidentiality matters even when communication is fast.\n• A filing method should support retrieval, not merely storage.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nSENDER → ENCODE → CHANNEL → DECODE → RECEIVER → FEEDBACK.\nAGENDA BEFORE; MINUTES AFTER."}},{"id":"securities-capital-calculations","old":{"title":"Share capital, securities and market participants","body":"A share represents an ownership interest in a company. A debenture or bond records borrowing under stated terms. Ordinary shareholders generally bear greater business risk and receive dividends after prior claims. Debt normally requires interest and repayment according to its terms. Convertible debt combines borrowing with a right to convert into shares; not every ordinary debenture is automatically convertible.\n\nIn historical Nigerian Commerce terminology, authorized or nominal capital is the maximum share capital permitted by the company's constitutional documents. Issued capital is the nominal value of shares offered and allotted. Called-up capital is the amount shareholders have been required to pay. Paid-up capital is the amount actually paid. Unissued capital is the authorized portion not yet issued. Reserve capital traditionally means uncalled capital reserved for a winding-up call; it must not casually be equated with every unissued or unpaid amount. Modern company-law requirements differ from some historical terminology.\n\nA share premium is the amount received above a share's nominal value and is commonly classified as a capital reserve. Revenue reserves arise from retained trading profits. Owners' equity includes share capital and relevant reserves; it does not include an outside lender's loan merely because the money finances assets.\n\nWorked example: a company has 2,000,000 shares of 50 kobo nominal value and issues 1,000,000 at ₦2 each. Nominal issued capital is ₦500,000. Cash proceeds are ₦2,000,000. The ₦1,500,000 excess is share premium. A rights issue offers new shares to existing shareholders, usually proportionately. A bonus issue capitalizes reserves without fresh subscription cash.\n\nA bull expects prices to rise; a bear expects them to fall. A stag subscribes to new issues hoping to sell soon at a profit. A broker deals for clients. A jobber or dealer buys and sells on the dealer's own account; the spread between buying and selling prices is traditionally called the jobber's turn. Speculation means trading in anticipation of price movements.\n\nAn irredeemable bond has no fixed redemption date under its terms, although interest remains payable. Treasury bills are short-term government securities usually described as low-risk or risk-free in simplified domestic-currency comparisons. Shares can offer some inflation protection through business growth, but their prices and dividends are not guaranteed.\n\nHistorical Nigerian Second-tier Securities Market questions concern eligibility for smaller indigenous firms and a three-year trading record. These past listing rules are not automatically current exchange requirements.\n\nRetained profits avoid flotation costs but still carry an opportunity cost; they are not economically free.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Nominal capital is not always cash proceeds.\n• A premium is not an ordinary trading profit.\n• A stag concerns new issues; a bull concerns expected price rises.\n• “Risk-free” in a classroom comparison does not mean absence of every economic risk."},"new":{"title":"Share capital, securities and market participants","body":"SHARE CAPITAL, SECURITIES AND MARKET PARTICIPANTS — OWNERSHIP, DEBT AND CALCULATIONS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB frequently mixes share terminology with arithmetic. The key is to identify whether money is raised by a company, paid between investors, or owed to creditors. Distinguish nominal value, issue price, market price, dividends, interest and capital gains.\n\n1. OWNERSHIP VERSUS BORROWING\nA SHARE represents an ownership interest in a company, subject to its class rights. A DEBENTURE or BOND is generally a debt instrument: its holder is a creditor. Ordinary shareholders may receive dividends when declared, but dividends are not guaranteed. Debt instruments ordinarily specify interest and repayment obligations.\n\nEXAMPLE: Ada buys 500 ordinary shares. She becomes a shareholder, not automatically a lender. Musa buys a company bond. He becomes a creditor under the bond terms.\n\n2. CLASSES OF SHARES\nORDINARY SHARES commonly carry residual ownership rights and may carry voting rights.\nPREFERENCE SHARES carry specified preferences, often concerning dividends or repayment of capital. Rights vary by issue terms.\nREDEEMABLE SHARES may be repurchased or redeemed in accordance with applicable law and their terms.\n\nA preference share is still a share; it is not automatically a debenture.\n\n3. SHARE CAPITAL TERMINOLOGY\nAUTHORISED SHARE CAPITAL is a traditional textbook term for a ceiling on shares a company could issue under earlier legal frameworks. Modern Nigerian company law uses different capital terminology, including MINIMUM ISSUED SHARE CAPITAL. When interpreting older JAMB questions, distinguish historical textbook terminology from current law.\n\nISSUED SHARE CAPITAL: Shares actually allotted or issued, measured under the relevant question's convention.\nPAID-UP CAPITAL: Amount paid or credited as paid on issued shares.\nUNPAID AMOUNT: Balance due on shares where payment has not been completed.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A company issues 100,000 shares at ₦2 each. If fully paid, proceeds are ₦200,000 before issue expenses. If holders have paid ₦1.50 per share, amount paid is ₦150,000 and unpaid amount is ₦50,000.\n\n4. NOMINAL, ISSUE AND MARKET VALUES\nNOMINAL OR PAR VALUE is the stated face value where relevant.\nISSUE PRICE is what subscribers pay for newly issued shares.\nMARKET PRICE is what investors pay to buy existing shares in trading.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A share with ₦1 nominal value is issued for ₦1.50. The ₦0.50 difference is traditionally called SHARE PREMIUM under the applicable accounting/legal framework. Later trading at ₦2.20 does not mean the company receives another ₦0.70 from each secondary-market trade.\n\n5. PRIMARY VERSUS SECONDARY MARKET\nPRIMARY MARKET: New securities are issued; the issuer normally receives the issue proceeds.\nSECONDARY MARKET: Existing securities are resold between investors; the issuer normally does not receive the resale price.\n\nAn exchange provides an organised trading venue. A securities regulator oversees conduct under law. These roles are different.\n\n6. DIVIDEND CALCULATIONS\nDividend per share × Number of shares = Total dividend received, assuming eligibility.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A company declares ₦0.40 per ordinary share. A holder of 2,500 eligible shares receives 2,500 × ₦0.40 = ₦1,000 before any applicable deductions.\n\nDIVIDEND YIELD = Annual dividend per share ÷ Current market price per share × 100.\nIf annual dividend is ₦0.50 and market price is ₦10, yield = 5%. Dividend yield is not the same as a guaranteed return.\n\n7. CAPITAL GAIN AND LOSS\nCapital gain = Selling proceeds − Purchase cost, ignoring expenses in simplified questions.\n\nEXAMPLE: Buy 400 shares at ₦8 each: cost ₦3,200. Sell at ₦9.50 each: proceeds ₦3,800. Simplified capital gain = ₦600. Percentage gain = ₦600 ÷ ₦3,200 × 100 = 18.75%.\n\nBrokerage, taxes and other costs affect actual net returns.\n\n8. RIGHTS ISSUES\nA RIGHTS ISSUE offers existing shareholders the opportunity to buy additional shares, often proportionate to current holdings.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A 1-for-4 rights issue offers one new share for every four held. A holder of 1,200 shares may subscribe for 300 new shares under the terms. At ₦3 each, subscription cost = ₦900.\n\n9. BONUS ISSUES\nA BONUS ISSUE allocates additional shares to existing holders using eligible company reserves. It does not by itself bring new cash into the company.\n\nEXAMPLE: Under a 1-for-5 bonus issue, a holder of 1,000 shares receives 200 additional shares. The number of shares rises, but that does not automatically mean the investor's total wealth rises by 20%.\n\n10. DEBENTURES AND INTEREST\nDebt holders have contractual claims under instrument terms. Interest may be fixed or variable; security may be provided or absent.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A ₦50,000 bond pays 8% annual coupon on face value. Annual coupon = ₦4,000. If purchased on the market for ₦40,000, current yield based on coupon ÷ market price = 10%, before other considerations. Coupon rate and current yield are not the same.\n\n11. MARKET PARTICIPANTS\nISSUER: Organisation raising funds.\nINVESTOR: Purchases securities.\nSTOCKBROKER: Licensed intermediary executing client transactions.\nREGISTRAR: Maintains specified ownership and corporate-action records.\nCUSTODIAN: Holds and administers assets for clients.\nSTOCK EXCHANGE: Organised securities trading platform.\nREGULATOR: Supervises market activities under law.\n\n12. RISKS\nShare prices can fall. Issuers may fail to pay debts. Securities can be difficult to sell promptly. Inflation can reduce real returns. Diversification can reduce some firm-specific risk but does not eliminate all losses.\n\n13. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Who owns part of a company? Shareholder.\nQ2. Who is a creditor? Debenture holder.\nQ3. A company issues 5,000 shares at ₦4. Proceeds before costs? ₦20,000.\nQ4. A holder owns 600 shares; dividend is ₦0.25 each. Dividend? ₦150.\nQ5. Dividend ₦1; market price ₦20. Dividend yield? 5%.\nQ6. A 1-for-3 rights issue on 900 shares offers how many? 300.\nQ7. A 1-for-4 bonus issue on 800 shares gives how many? 200.\nQ8. Does a secondary-market resale normally raise fresh company capital? No.\nQ9. A ₦10,000 bond has 6% annual coupon. Interest? ₦600.\nQ10. What is share premium in a traditional face-value question? Issue price above nominal value.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Dividends are not contractual interest.\n• Nominal value, issue price and market price differ.\n• Rights issues can raise cash; bonus issues do not by themselves.\n• Coupon rate is calculated on stated face value, not automatically on purchase price.\n• Older capital terminology may not reflect current Nigerian company law.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nSHARES = OWNERSHIP; BONDS = DEBT.\nPRIMARY = NEW ISSUE; SECONDARY = RESALE.\nDIVIDEND YIELD = DIVIDEND ÷ MARKET PRICE × 100."}},{"id":"banking-policy-payments","old":{"title":"Monetary control, bank facilities and cheque payments","body":"A commercial bank accepts deposits, provides payment services and lends under agreed conditions. A central bank manages currency and monetary policy and serves as banker to government and banks. Development finance institutions support particular development needs; a mortgage institution concentrates on property finance. Historical institutional names should be read in the period of the paper.\n\nThe central bank can influence the quantity of money and bank credit. A lower cash reserve requirement can leave banks with more funds available for lending; a higher requirement tends to restrict that capacity. In open-market operations, buying securities injects money into the banking system, while selling securities withdraws it. Actual lending also depends on demand, risk and other policy conditions. Moral suasion persuades banks to follow guidance rather than directly imposing a numerical tax.\n\nA current account supports frequent business receipts and payments, including cheque facilities under bank rules. A domiciliary account holds permitted foreign currency. A savings account encourages saving and commonly pays interest. A line of credit allows borrowing up to an agreed limit. Discounting a bill means paying its holder before maturity after deducting the agreed discount. Trade credit comes from a supplier allowing later payment, not directly from a bank.\n\nA bank draft is a payment instrument drawn by a bank rather than an ordinary customer's personal cheque. A crossed cheque is directed through a bank account rather than ordinary over-the-counter cash payment. A general crossing alone does not necessarily mean that only the named original payee can obtain title. Account-payee instructions are more specific. “Not negotiable” means a transferee cannot obtain a better title than the transferor; it does not simply prohibit every transfer.\n\nWorked example: a trader receives a crossed cheque from a customer and lodges it with the bank for collection. This provides an account record instead of obtaining cash directly at the drawee bank's counter. If the trader needs regular payments to a supplier, a standing order may suit the recurring instruction.\n\nPaper currency issued under law is legal tender. Cheques and drafts are useful payment instruments, but a creditor is not generally required to accept them as legal tender merely because banks process them.\n\nHistorical school rules commonly treat a cheque unpresented for six months as stale. Bank interest on lending is income; the loan principal is not itself earned revenue.\n\nA bearer cheque is payable to the bearer under its terms; a crossing separately affects the collection route.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Buying and selling securities have opposite monetary effects.\n• Cash reserve ratio is not an ordinary customer's interest rate.\n• A current account is not identical to an overdraft.\n• Read the exact crossing before asserting who can receive payment."},"new":{"title":"Monetary control, bank facilities and cheque payments","body":"MONETARY CONTROL, BANK FACILITIES AND CHEQUE PAYMENTS — FOLLOW THE MONEY\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB banking questions distinguish the central bank from commercial banks, monetary instruments from customer services, and different payment documents. Cheque questions are particularly tricky because drawer, drawee and payee sound similar.\n\n1. CENTRAL BANK VERSUS COMMERCIAL BANK\nThe CENTRAL BANK OF NIGERIA (CBN) conducts monetary policy and performs statutory currency, reserve, banking-system and payment-system functions.\nCOMMERCIAL BANKS accept eligible deposits, provide payment services and extend credit under regulation.\n\nEXAMPLE: A trader deposits sales proceeds in a commercial bank. The CBN does not ordinarily run a retail current account for that trader.\n\n2. MONETARY POLICY OBJECTIVES\nMonetary policy influences money, credit and financial conditions, commonly to support price stability and other statutory objectives. Inflation, borrowing costs and economic activity are related, but policy effects take time and are uncertain.\n\nEXPANSIONARY POLICY generally seeks easier monetary conditions.\nCONTRACTIONARY POLICY generally seeks tighter monetary conditions to restrain excessive inflationary pressure.\n\n3. MONETARY POLICY INSTRUMENTS\nPOLICY INTEREST RATE: Influences rates across the financial system.\nOPEN MARKET OPERATIONS: Purchase or sale of eligible securities to affect liquidity.\nRESERVE REQUIREMENTS: Specify reserves banks must maintain.\nOTHER LIQUIDITY FACILITIES: Can supply or absorb liquidity under relevant frameworks.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO: A central bank sells eligible securities to banks. Payment for those securities can absorb liquidity from the banking system. In a simplified textbook model, this is contractionary.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Raising reserve requirements generally constrains the funds available for lending in the simplified model; reducing them generally eases the constraint.\n\n4. BANK CREDIT CREATION\nWhen a bank grants a loan and credits a customer's deposit account, it can create a corresponding deposit liability. Lending is constrained by capital, liquidity, regulation, creditworthiness and risk.\n\nTEXTBOOK ILLUSTRATION: If an initial ₦100,000 deposit supports a simplified multiple-deposit expansion with a 20% reserve ratio and all restrictive assumptions hold, theoretical maximum total deposits = ₦100,000 ÷ 0.20 = ₦500,000. This is a simplified classroom model, not a prediction of actual Nigerian banking behaviour.\n\n5. LOANS AND OVERDRAFTS\nLOAN: Advance with agreed principal, interest and repayment conditions.\nOVERDRAFT: Permission to draw beyond an account's available balance up to an agreed limit.\nDISCOUNTING BILLS: Bank provides funds before a qualifying bill's maturity, less charges.\nBANK GUARANTEE: Bank undertakes a specified obligation if contractual conditions are met.\nLETTER OF CREDIT: Documentary payment undertaking used particularly in trade.\n\nA guarantee does not mean a bank has paid the supplier immediately. Its obligations depend on the instrument.\n\n6. CHEQUES: THE THREE PARTIES\nDRAWER: Account holder who writes and signs the cheque.\nDRAWEE: Bank directed to pay.\nPAYEE: Person or organisation named to receive payment.\n\nEXAMPLE: Uche writes a cheque on her bank account payable to Musa. Uche = drawer; her bank = drawee; Musa = payee.\n\n7. TYPES OF CHEQUES\nBEARER CHEQUE: Payable to bearer under applicable terms and rules.\nORDER CHEQUE: Payable to a named person or their order, subject to endorsement rules.\nCROSSED CHEQUE: Marked to direct payment through a bank account or collecting bank according to the crossing.\nOPEN CHEQUE: Not crossed, potentially cashable over the counter subject to rules.\nPOST-DATED CHEQUE: Bears a future date.\nSTALE CHEQUE: Presented after the period accepted under relevant banking practice.\nDISHONOURED CHEQUE: Bank refuses payment for a valid reason, such as insufficient funds or irregular signature.\n\nCrossing is primarily a payment-routing safeguard; it does not guarantee sufficient funds.\n\n8. CHEQUE ENDORSEMENT\nAn endorsement is a signature or instruction on an instrument that can affect transfer or collection under applicable rules. Not every cheque can be freely negotiated, and restrictive markings matter.\n\nA bank may dishonour a cheque if funds are insufficient, the signature differs, the cheque is materially altered or other valid issues exist.\n\n9. OTHER PAYMENT METHODS\nBANK TRANSFER: Electronic movement of funds.\nSTANDING ORDER: Payer's instruction for recurring payments.\nDIRECT DEBIT: Authorised payee-initiated collection under a mandate.\nDEBIT CARD: Usually accesses available linked funds.\nCREDIT CARD: Uses an agreed credit facility.\nBANK DRAFT: Bank-issued payment instrument under applicable terms.\n\nEXAMPLE: A customer tells the bank to pay ₦30,000 rent on the first day of each month. This is typically a standing order. An electricity provider collecting varying bills under a mandate commonly uses direct debit.\n\n10. ACCOUNT TYPES\nSAVINGS: Commonly for saving, subject to product terms.\nCURRENT: Frequent transactions and possible cheque facilities.\nFIXED DEPOSIT: Money placed for an agreed period.\nDOMICILIARY: Account denominated in foreign currency.\n\nDo not assume every account offers the same interest rate, overdraft or withdrawal conditions.\n\n11. DEVELOPMENT AND SPECIALISED FINANCE\nDevelopment finance institutions support defined sectors or development goals. Mortgage institutions focus on housing finance. Microfinance institutions offer eligible financial services to smaller customers and enterprises under their regulatory frameworks.\n\nInstitutional names and mandates can change. For historical JAMB questions, read them in the period being examined.\n\n12. BANKING RISKS AND CUSTOMER PROTECTION\nBanks face credit, liquidity, operational and fraud risks. Customers should verify payment instructions, protect authentication codes and retain transaction records. A transfer receipt alone may not prove that a disputed recipient account was correctly credited.\n\n13. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Who issues a cheque? Drawer.\nQ2. Who is directed to pay? Drawee bank.\nQ3. Who receives the payment? Payee.\nQ4. What is a crossed cheque mainly intended to do? Direct collection through banking channels.\nQ5. What facility allows an agreed negative current-account balance? Overdraft.\nQ6. What instrument involves buying or selling securities to affect liquidity? Open market operations.\nQ7. In the simplified model, what does increasing reserve requirements tend to do? Restrict lending capacity.\nQ8. Which payment instruction is commonly initiated by the payer at fixed intervals? Standing order.\nQ9. Which arrangement authorises a biller to collect payments? Direct debit.\nQ10. Does cheque crossing guarantee payment? No.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Drawer, drawee and payee are three distinct roles.\n• A crossed cheque is not automatically a dishonoured cheque.\n• A post-dated cheque is not necessarily fraudulent.\n• Loan, overdraft, guarantee and letter of credit differ.\n• Monetary policy is conducted by the central bank, not individual retail banks.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nDRAWER DRAWS → DRAWEE BANK PAYS → PAYEE RECEIVES.\nCENTRAL BANK = POLICY; COMMERCIAL BANK = CUSTOMER SERVICES."}},{"id":"external-trade-terms","old":{"title":"Foreign-trade prices, documents and external accounts","body":"Foreign trade exchanges goods and services between residents of different countries. Visible exports and imports concern physical goods. Invisible transactions include services such as shipping, insurance, consultancy and tourism. An imported service does not become visible trade merely because the customer pays money or receives a printed receipt.\n\nThe balance of trade compares merchandise exports and imports. The balance of payments records the wider set of transactions between residents and non-residents. Historical school accounts commonly distinguish current and capital items; modern presentations also identify a financial account. Selling foreign assets or using reserves can finance an external deficit, but it is not the same as increasing merchandise exports or permanently removing its underlying causes.\n\nA consular invoice is certified under the importing country's requirements where applicable and may help customs verify goods' values for assessing duty. An indent orders goods. A commercial invoice states goods and prices. A pro forma invoice is a preliminary invoice or quotation supplied before a final sale. A price list simply lists prices. Do not substitute a shipping note for every document with an invoice function.\n\nFOB means free on board under the applicable shipping rule. In the usual school example, the buyer arranges the main carriage and insurance after the seller's on-board delivery responsibility. C and F, traditionally cost and freight, includes freight in the quoted selling price but not the insurance obligation included in CIF. CIF means cost, insurance and freight. Payment of charges, risk and legal ownership are separate questions. Incoterms allocate delivery responsibilities and risk; they do not themselves decide transfer of property in every sale.\n\nA charter party is a contract for hiring a ship or its carrying capacity. A bill of lading acknowledges shipment, evidences the carriage contract and may function as a document of title. Documentary CIF sales commonly involve payment against shipping documents, but the parties' contract and applicable law determine precisely when title passes.\n\nWorked example: a Lagos importer compares an FOB quotation with a CIF quotation for the same goods. The FOB figure may look lower because main freight and insurance must still be arranged or paid separately. Compare the included obligations before deciding which offer is cheaper.\n\nCountertrade exchanges goods or services under an arrangement that substitutes partly or wholly for payment in foreign currency. Globalization increases international business links, but tariffs, sanctions and other barriers still exist.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Cost, risk and ownership do not always transfer together.\n• Customs duties and ports-authority charges are different.\n• Invisible imports are services, not imported goods that happen to be difficult to see.\n• Financing a payments deficit differs from correcting every cause of it."},"new":{"title":"Foreign-trade prices, documents and external accounts","body":"FOREIGN-TRADE PRICES, DOCUMENTS AND EXTERNAL ACCOUNTS — FOLLOW COST, RISK AND PAYMENT\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nInternational trade questions combine goods, transport, currency and documents. JAMB may ask who pays freight, what a bill of lading proves, whether a trade deficit means every external account is negative, or how an exchange-rate change affects import cost.\n\n1. IMPORTS AND EXPORTS\nAn IMPORT is a good or service purchased from a non-resident. An EXPORT is a good or service sold to a non-resident under the relevant accounting and trade context.\n\nVISIBLE TRADE concerns merchandise goods. INVISIBLE TRADE concerns services such as shipping, tourism, insurance and consultancy.\n\nEXAMPLE: A Nigerian exporter sells cocoa beans abroad: visible export. A Nigerian consultant provides paid services to a foreign customer: services export.\n\n2. BALANCE OF VISIBLE TRADE\nVisible trade balance = Merchandise exports − Merchandise imports.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Goods exports = ₦750 billion; goods imports = ₦920 billion. Balance = −₦170 billion: visible trade deficit.\n\nA deficit means imports exceeded exports in that category and period; it does not mean the country made no exports.\n\n3. BALANCE OF PAYMENTS\nThe balance of payments records transactions between residents and non-residents over a period. It includes a CURRENT ACCOUNT, CAPITAL ACCOUNT and FINANCIAL ACCOUNT under standard classifications.\n\nCurrent account covers goods, services, primary income and secondary income. Financial account records investment and financing transactions. A goods trade deficit is only one part of the broader external accounts.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Balance of trade is not identical to balance of payments.\n\n4. FOREIGN EXCHANGE\nAn exchange rate is the price of one currency in terms of another.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A $500 machine costs ₦750,000 at ₦1,500 per dollar. If the rate becomes ₦1,650 per dollar, the naira cost becomes ₦825,000 before other charges. The naira has depreciated against the dollar in this example.\n\nAPPRECIATION: Currency strengthens relative to another.\nDEPRECIATION: Currency weakens under market exchange arrangements.\nDEVALUATION: Official downward adjustment in an administered or fixed-rate system.\n\n5. QUOTATIONS AND PRO FORMA INVOICES\nA QUOTATION states proposed prices and terms, subject to its wording.\nA PRO FORMA INVOICE gives advance details of goods, prices and terms; it is often used before shipment or payment arrangements.\nA COMMERCIAL INVOICE is the seller's actual billing document describing goods, prices and transaction terms.\n\nA pro forma invoice is not automatically proof that goods were delivered or payment made.\n\n6. SHIPPING AND CUSTOMS DOCUMENTS\nBILL OF LADING: Carrier document acknowledging receipt or shipment of cargo, evidencing carriage arrangements and potentially functioning as a document of title depending on its form.\nAIR WAYBILL: Air transport document; generally not a negotiable document of title in the same way as an order bill of lading.\nPACKING LIST: Itemises packages and contents.\nCERTIFICATE OF ORIGIN: Records origin for applicable customs or trade purposes.\nINSURANCE CERTIFICATE: Evidence of insurance arrangements.\nCUSTOMS DECLARATION: Information submitted for clearance.\nIMPORT OR EXPORT LICENCE: Official authorisation where required.\n\nEXAMPLE: A customs officer needs information on the number and contents of cartons: packing list. A buyer wants evidence of a sea carrier's receipt of cargo: bill of lading.\n\n7. LETTER OF CREDIT\nA letter of credit is a bank undertaking to pay a beneficiary against presentation of documents complying with the credit's terms, subject to applicable rules.\n\nSIMPLIFIED FLOW\nImporter requests credit → Issuing bank opens credit → Exporter ships goods → Exporter presents required documents → Banks examine documents → Payment occurs according to terms.\n\nA bank examines stipulated documents, not the physical quality of every item inside a container.\n\n8. DELIVERY TERMS: FOB AND CIF\nFOB (FREE ON BOARD): Under the relevant Incoterms rule for sea or inland-waterway transport, the seller delivers goods on board the vessel at the named shipment port, with responsibilities and risk transfer defined by the rule.\n\nCIF (COST, INSURANCE AND FREIGHT): Seller arranges and pays specified freight and insurance to the named destination port, while risk transfers according to the CIF rule at shipment rather than necessarily at arrival.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Paying for freight to a destination is not the same as bearing all transit risk until destination. The chosen trade rule governs the allocation.\n\n9. LANDED COST\nA simplified LANDED COST adds purchase price, freight, insurance, duties and relevant charges incurred to bring imported goods to their destination.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nGoods price ₦2,000,000.\nFreight ₦200,000.\nInsurance ₦40,000.\nImport duty ₦300,000.\nOther landing charges ₦60,000.\nSimplified landed cost = ₦2,600,000.\n\nDo not include or exclude a charge without checking the question's definition and whether it is already included in a quoted CIF price.\n\n10. TARIFFS, QUOTAS AND EMBARGOES\nTARIFF: Tax on specified cross-border trade, usually imports.\nQUOTA: Quantitative restriction.\nEMBARGO: Official prohibition on specified trade.\nEXCHANGE CONTROL: Regulation of foreign exchange access or transactions.\n\nTariffs can raise government revenue or protect domestic producers but may increase consumer costs. Quotas limit quantity rather than directly setting a tax rate.\n\n11. TRADE FINANCE AND RISK\nExporters face non-payment, currency, transport and political risks. Importers face shipment delays, quality disputes and changing landed costs. Parties may use documentary credits, insurance, inspection arrangements and clear contracts to manage risks.\n\n12. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What is visible trade? Trade in physical goods.\nQ2. Exports ₦300m; imports ₦420m. Trade balance? ₦120m deficit.\nQ3. Which document lists package contents? Packing list.\nQ4. Which document may evidence receipt by a sea carrier? Bill of lading.\nQ5. What is the seller's billing document? Commercial invoice.\nQ6. Does a letter of credit guarantee product quality? No.\nQ7. What is a tariff? Tax on specified cross-border trade.\nQ8. What is a quota? Quantitative limit.\nQ9. $100 at ₦1,600/$ costs how much? ₦160,000 before other charges.\nQ10. Is CIF risk transfer necessarily at arrival? No.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Invisible trade refers to services, not hidden or illegal goods.\n• The goods trade balance is only part of the balance of payments.\n• Pro forma and commercial invoices serve different stages.\n• Bill of lading and air waybill are not identical.\n• FOB/CIF freight obligations must be distinguished from risk transfer.\n• Landed-cost questions require careful checking for double counting.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nTRADE BALANCE = GOODS EXPORTS − GOODS IMPORTS.\nCIF: SELLER ARRANGES COST, INSURANCE AND FREIGHT; RISK RULE STILL MATTERS."}},{"id":"business-legal-protection","old":{"title":"Business rights, intellectual property and sale obligations","body":"A contract is an agreement enforceable by law. Its requirements commonly include offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity, lawful purpose and intention to create legal relations. An offer announces willingness to be bound on stated terms when accepted. A condition is an important contractual term; breach may justify rejecting goods and other remedies. A warranty is a subsidiary term whose breach normally gives a damages remedy without the same rejection right. Actual remedies depend on the applicable law and facts.\n\nHistorical sale-of-goods rules imply obligations concerning matters such as title, description, quality and fitness in relevant circumstances. Merchantable quality concerns suitability for ordinary uses, while fitness for a particular purpose depends on the relevant conditions. A buyer should not treat every disappointment as proof that every implied condition was broken. Nor does a seller escape all responsibility simply by asking the buyer to inspect.\n\nAn employee performs duties under the employment relationship. The employer's responsibilities include agreed remuneration, appropriate working arrangements and duties concerning safety. An employee properly incurring authorized expenses for the employer may be entitled to reimbursement or indemnification. Annual leave, job security and working facilities are important, but the best answer to a question depends on the particular right being tested.\n\nA trademark distinguishes one trader's goods or services from another's. A patent protects a qualifying invention under its legal conditions. Industrial design protection concerns the appearance of an article. Copyright protects qualifying original expression, not every unrecorded idea. Historical Nigerian trademark questions may use an initial seven-year registration term followed by renewal periods; do not apply that number indiscriminately to patents, designs or every jurisdiction.\n\nA certificate of incorporation establishes the company's incorporation and legal personality. Older questions may distinguish it from a certificate permitting commencement of certain business. A memorandum concerns the company's constitutional framework; a prospectus invites investment. The corporate name must meet registration requirements and must not improperly conflict with an existing protected name. A company is legally distinct from its shareholders.\n\nWorked example: an employee buys authorized delivery fuel while carrying out the employer's instructions and submits the receipt. Repaying that expense concerns indemnification; it is not the employee's dividend or a new ownership interest in the firm.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A patent, trademark and copyright protect different interests.\n• Incorporation and an invitation to buy shares are different events.\n• An employee's expense reimbursement is not profit sharing.\n• Historical legal terminology should not be taught as a complete statement of today's law."},"new":{"title":"Business rights, intellectual property and sale obligations","body":"BUSINESS RIGHTS, INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY AND SALE OBLIGATIONS — PROTECT IDEAS AND BUYERS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nA business may create a logo, invent a machine, publish a training manual and sell products to customers. These activities raise different legal questions. JAMB tests intellectual-property categories, contract terms, consumer rights, ownership and the duties of buyers and sellers.\n\n1. LEGAL RIGHTS IN BUSINESS\nBusiness rights arise from contracts, property, legislation and other legal relationships. A right to use a brand is different from ownership of a building; a right to be paid is different from possession of goods.\n\nLegal protection depends on the facts and applicable Nigerian law. Commercial customs alone do not override every statutory rule.\n\n2. INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY: THE BIG DISTINCTIONS\nINTELLECTUAL PROPERTY (IP) protects specified creations, inventions, signs and other legally recognised intangible interests.\n\nCOPYRIGHT generally protects original expressive works such as books, music, films, software code and artistic works, subject to law. It does not automatically protect every idea merely because someone thought of it.\nTRADEMARK protects signs distinguishing goods or services, such as qualifying names and logos.\nPATENT protects qualifying inventions under applicable requirements and registration procedures.\nINDUSTRIAL DESIGN protects qualifying visual or ornamental features of products.\nTRADE SECRET concerns valuable confidential information protected through secrecy and relevant legal duties.\n\nEXAMPLE: A business logo may be protected as a trademark when legal requirements are met; an original illustration in the logo may also raise copyright issues. Different rights can overlap.\n\n3. COPYRIGHT VERSUS PATENT\nA written explanation of a new machine may attract copyright in its expression, but copyright does not automatically give exclusive rights over the machine's underlying technical invention. Patent protection requires meeting relevant patent-law conditions.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Copyright protects expression, not every general idea or scientific fact.\n\n4. TRADEMARK AND BRAND NAME\nA BRAND is a marketing identity. A TRADEMARK is a sign protected under relevant trademark law. A company name registration and trademark protection are not necessarily the same thing.\n\nEXAMPLE: Two businesses may dispute a confusingly similar product logo. The question involves distinguishing source and trademark rights, not merely whether both companies have been registered.\n\n5. PATENTS AND INDUSTRIAL DESIGNS\nPATENT: Commonly associated with new inventions meeting statutory conditions.\nINDUSTRIAL DESIGN: Commonly associated with a product's qualifying visual appearance rather than its technical operation.\n\nEXAMPLE: A novel water-purification mechanism may raise patent questions. A distinctive ornamental bottle shape may raise industrial-design questions. A bottle's name or symbol may raise trademark questions.\n\n6. CONTRACTS: FORMATION\nA legally enforceable agreement generally requires a valid offer and acceptance plus other relevant elements, including consideration where required, intention to create legal relations, capacity, certainty and lawful purpose.\n\nA displayed price is often an invitation to treat. A counter-offer proposes changed terms and is not unqualified acceptance of the original offer.\n\n7. SALE AND AGREEMENT TO SELL\nSALE: Ownership passes under the contract.\nAGREEMENT TO SELL: Ownership is to pass later or on fulfilment of specified conditions.\n\nPOSSESSION means physical control. TITLE means legal ownership. A delivery company may possess a parcel without owning it.\n\n8. CONDITIONS AND WARRANTIES\nIn traditional sale-of-goods terminology, a CONDITION is a major contractual term whose breach may justify rejection or termination in appropriate cases. A WARRANTY is a subsidiary term whose breach commonly gives rise to damages.\n\nModern consumer law and contract terms can affect available remedies. Do not assume every defect has the same legal consequence.\n\n9. SELLER'S DUTIES\nDepending on the agreement and law, sellers may be required to:\n• Deliver the agreed goods in the agreed quantity and time.\n• Transfer the title they are entitled to transfer.\n• Meet applicable description, quality and fitness requirements.\n• Supply relevant documents and information.\n• Avoid misleading claims and unsafe practices.\n\nEXAMPLE: A buyer orders 50 cartons of a specified brand. Delivering 40 cartons of a different brand may raise quantity and description issues.\n\n10. BUYER'S DUTIES\nBuyers commonly must pay the agreed price, accept conforming delivery as required and cooperate with agreed collection or delivery arrangements. A buyer may also have statutory rights to reject defective goods or seek remedies under qualifying circumstances.\n\nNeither party can assume that the other's breach automatically removes every remaining legal obligation.\n\n11. CONSUMER PROTECTION\nConsumer protection seeks to prevent unsafe goods, misleading advertising, unfair commercial practices and certain abusive terms. In Nigeria, the FEDERAL COMPETITION AND CONSUMER PROTECTION COMMISSION (FCCPC) is an important institution in this area. Other regulators have specialised mandates.\n\nA shop's 'goods once sold cannot be returned' sign does not automatically cancel statutory rights concerning defective or misdescribed products.\n\n12. PASSING OF RISK\nRisk concerns who bears loss or damage under a contract and applicable law. Ownership, possession and risk may pass at different times depending on the terms and legal rules.\n\nEXAMPLE: A buyer pays for goods still in a warehouse. The answer to who bears accidental damage depends on the contract and relevant law, not simply who physically holds the goods.\n\n13. BREACH AND REMEDIES\nPossible remedies include damages, rejection, repair, replacement, refund, specific performance or other statutory/contractual relief where legally available. The appropriate remedy depends on the breach and the applicable rules.\n\n14. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which IP right commonly protects an original book? Copyright.\nQ2. Which right distinguishes a company's product sign? Trademark.\nQ3. Which right may protect a qualifying invention? Patent.\nQ4. Which right may protect ornamental product appearance? Industrial design.\nQ5. Does copyright protect every abstract idea? No.\nQ6. Is a registered company name automatically the same as a registered trademark? No.\nQ7. What is the difference between sale and agreement to sell? Timing of ownership transfer.\nQ8. Can a warehouse possess goods without owning them? Yes.\nQ9. Which traditional contractual term is more fundamental: condition or warranty? Condition.\nQ10. Can a printed 'no returns' sign remove all statutory consumer rights? No.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Patent, trademark, copyright and design rights protect different subject matter.\n• Brand recognition does not itself establish every legal IP right.\n• Ownership, possession and risk are not interchangeable.\n• Conditions and warranties differ in traditional contract classification.\n• A seller must meet applicable description and quality obligations.\n• Consumer remedies depend on law and circumstances.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nCOPYRIGHT = EXPRESSION.\nTRADEMARK = IDENTIFYING SIGN.\nPATENT = QUALIFYING INVENTION.\nDESIGN = QUALIFYING APPEARANCE."}},{"id":"marketing-practice","old":{"title":"Marketing mix, packaging and personal selling","body":"Marketing identifies customer needs and organizes activities that satisfy them while achieving the firm's objectives. The marketing mix consists of controllable variables commonly grouped as the four Ps: product, price, place and promotion. Firms adjust these together rather than treating advertising alone as the whole marketing function.\n\nProduct includes the design, quality, brand, packaging and services attached to the offer. Packaging means developing and providing the container or wrapper for a product. Its graphics can identify the brand, explain use and attract attention; protection and convenience are also important. Branding identifies and distinguishes an offer. Changing the packet is not necessarily changing the physical product inside it.\n\nPrice includes the amount charged, discounts, credit terms and payment conditions. Place concerns channels and distribution arrangements that make the product available. Promotion communicates with customers and other relevant groups. The four Ps use place, not property, and promotion, not production. Producing a good is important, but it is not the missing fourth P in that standard list.\n\nAdvertising is paid, non-personal communication by an identified sponsor. Personal selling involves direct interaction with a prospective buyer. It permits questions, explanation and demonstrations, which are useful for complex industrial equipment. Sales promotion uses incentives such as free samples, gifts, competitions or temporary price reductions. Public relations builds understanding and goodwill between the organization and its publics. Advertising and public relations both communicate, but differ in purpose and degree of control over particular messages.\n\nCustomer service includes assistance before, during and after a sale. Reliable advice, prompt responses, installation, repairs and fair complaint handling can retain customers. A single gift cannot compensate indefinitely for unsafe goods or poor service. Giving credit can increase sales, but it also introduces collection costs and default risk; increased profit is not guaranteed.\n\nWorked example: a Kano machinery seller demonstrates a sewing machine, answers a tailor's questions and explains servicing. That is personal selling supported by customer service. A free starter pack offered for one week is sales promotion. A newspaper announcement paid for by the seller is advertising. The shop's location and delivery service belong to place.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A product demonstration can support more than one promotional activity; read the setting.\n• Promotion is wider than advertising.\n• Packaging combines protection and communication, not only decoration.\n• Higher sales from credit do not automatically mean higher net profit.\n• Customer service continues after the money has been collected."},"new":{"title":"Marketing mix, packaging and personal selling","body":"MARKETING MIX, PACKAGING AND PERSONAL SELLING — TURN CUSTOMER NEEDS INTO SALES\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB often asks students to identify which marketing activity is being performed in a real business situation. A product can be well advertised yet fail because it is wrongly priced, poorly packaged or unavailable. Learn the functions of each marketing tool and the distinctions between advertising, personal selling and sales promotion.\n\n1. MARKETING AND SELLING\nMarketing is the process of identifying customer needs, developing suitable offerings, setting prices, arranging distribution, communicating value and maintaining customer relationships. Selling is the activity of persuading customers and completing transactions. Marketing therefore begins before a product is manufactured and continues after it is sold.\n\nEXAMPLE: A Nigerian beverage producer surveys students, develops a smaller bottle, selects a price, supplies kiosks and responds to complaints. These are marketing activities. The salesperson who persuades a kiosk owner to stock the bottle is performing personal selling.\n\n2. THE FOUR Ps OF MARKETING\nPRODUCT refers to the good or service offered, including quality, design, features, packaging, branding and support.\nPRICE is what the buyer pays, including discounts and credit terms.\nPLACE refers to distribution channels, locations and availability.\nPROMOTION comprises activities that inform, persuade and remind buyers.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO\nA soap manufacturer offers a mild soap in a sealed 100-gram pack (product), charges ₦700 (price), sells through supermarkets and open-market traders (place), and uses radio adverts and product samples (promotion).\n\nJAMB TRAP: Place means how a product reaches buyers, not merely the factory address.\n\n3. PRODUCT AND PRODUCT MIX\nA product can be a physical good or service. Consumer goods are purchased mainly for personal use; industrial goods are purchased for business operations or further production.\n\nA PRODUCT LINE is a group of related products, such as several sizes of one detergent brand. A PRODUCT MIX is the complete assortment of products offered by a business.\n\nProducts may differ by quality, design, size, durability, warranty and after-sales support. A product that solves no real customer problem may fail despite promotion.\n\n4. BRANDING\nA brand is a name, term, sign, symbol or design used to identify and distinguish an offering. Branding can aid recognition, loyalty and product differentiation.\n\nA BRAND NAME is the pronounceable part of a brand; a BRAND MARK may be a symbol or visual element. A TRADEMARK is a sign protected under applicable intellectual-property law. Not every brand is automatically registered as a trademark.\n\nEXAMPLE: A buyer recognises a particular packaged rice by its name and distinctive symbol. Those identifiers support branding. The rice itself remains the product.\n\n5. PACKAGING\nPackaging is the design and provision of a container or wrapper for goods. Its functions include:\n• Protecting against contamination, moisture, breakage and tampering.\n• Making transport, handling and storage easier.\n• Identifying the brand and differentiating products.\n• Providing information on quantity, use and safety.\n• Supporting display and promotional appeal.\n• Facilitating convenient unit sizes.\n\nPRIMARY PACKAGING directly contains the product; SECONDARY PACKAGING groups or protects primary packages; TRANSPORT PACKAGING protects goods during distribution.\n\nEXAMPLE: A bottled drink's bottle is primary packaging; a carton holding 24 bottles is secondary or distribution packaging depending on use.\n\n6. LABELLING VERSUS PACKAGING\nA label supplies information attached to a product or its packaging. It may state brand, ingredients, quantity, directions, manufacture and expiry details or warnings as applicable.\n\nPackaging is the container or wrapper; labelling is the information displayed. A label can help sell a product, but it also supports informed and safe use.\n\n7. PRICING METHODS\nCOST-PLUS PRICING adds a markup to cost.\nCOMPETITION-BASED PRICING considers competitors' prices.\nPENETRATION PRICING uses a relatively low introductory price to gain customers.\nPRICE SKIMMING begins at a relatively high price and may reduce it later.\nPSYCHOLOGICAL PRICING uses price presentation to influence perception.\nPROMOTIONAL PRICING temporarily reduces prices or offers incentives.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A seller buys an item for ₦4,800 and applies 25% markup on cost. Markup = ₦1,200; selling price = ₦6,000. The profit margin on selling price is ₦1,200 ÷ ₦6,000 × 100 = 20%, not 25%.\n\n8. DISTRIBUTION CHANNELS\nDIRECT: Producer → Consumer.\nONE INTERMEDIARY: Producer → Retailer → Consumer.\nTWO INTERMEDIARIES: Producer → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer.\n\nWholesalers commonly buy in bulk and supply retailers. Retailers commonly sell in smaller quantities to final consumers. Some manufacturers sell online directly to customers, but intermediaries remain valuable where they provide reach, stockholding and delivery services.\n\n9. THE PROMOTIONAL MIX\nADVERTISING is paid, usually non-personal promotion through media.\nPERSONAL SELLING involves direct interaction with prospective buyers.\nSALES PROMOTION offers short-term incentives, such as coupons, free samples and temporary discounts.\nPUBLIC RELATIONS seeks understanding and goodwill among relevant publics.\nDIRECT MARKETING communicates directly with selected customers.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Giving out a free sample is usually sales promotion; a salesperson demonstrating the product face-to-face is personal selling.\n\n10. PERSONAL SELLING PROCESS\nA simplified selling process includes:\n1. PROSPECTING: Identify potential customers.\n2. PRE-APPROACH: Research their needs.\n3. APPROACH: Establish contact.\n4. PRESENTATION: Explain relevant benefits.\n5. HANDLING OBJECTIONS: Respond to genuine concerns.\n6. CLOSING: Seek agreement or order.\n7. FOLLOW-UP: Check satisfaction and support repeat business.\n\nEXAMPLE: A solar-equipment representative learns that a shopkeeper needs evening lighting, explains battery capacity, answers questions about maintenance and follows up after installation.\n\nGood personal selling requires truthful claims, listening and respect for customers—not pressure or deception.\n\n11. ADVERTISING MEDIA\nRADIO reaches listeners and can use local languages but cannot display products visually.\nTELEVISION combines sight and sound but may be expensive.\nOUTDOOR MEDIA offers repeated local exposure but limited space for detail.\nDIGITAL MEDIA can target audiences and measure responses but raises privacy and fraud concerns.\nPRINT MEDIA can carry detail but may have limited reach for some audiences.\n\nSelect a medium according to audience, cost, message and coverage.\n\n12. CUSTOMER SATISFACTION\nMarketing does not end at payment. Complaints, returns, warranties, repairs and reliable service affect loyalty. A dissatisfied customer may discourage others from buying.\n\n13. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which P concerns availability and distribution? Place.\nQ2. Which P includes discounts? Price.\nQ3. Is marketing the same as selling? No; marketing is broader.\nQ4. What protects and identifies goods? Packaging.\nQ5. What provides information on a pack? Labelling.\nQ6. Which promotion involves face-to-face persuasion? Personal selling.\nQ7. What is distributing free product samples? Sales promotion.\nQ8. Cost ₦2,000, markup 30% on cost. Selling price? ₦2,600.\nQ9. Producer → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer contains how many intermediaries? Two.\nQ10. Why is follow-up important in personal selling? To support satisfaction and repeat business.\n\nCOMMON EXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Advertising is only one element of promotion.\n• Packaging and labelling have related but distinct functions.\n• Markup on cost differs from margin on sales.\n• A brand is not automatically a registered trademark.\n• Personal selling differs from mass advertising.\n• Distribution is part of marketing, not a separate unrelated activity.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nPRODUCT → PRICE → PLACE → PROMOTION.\nSELLING PROCESS: PROSPECT → PRESENT → RESPOND → CLOSE → FOLLOW UP."}},{"id":"business-environment-institutions","old":{"title":"Business environment, product safety and public institutions","body":"The business environment consists of conditions that influence business decisions and performance. Internal conditions include staff, management, finance, resources and organizational culture. External conditions include customers, competitors, laws, economic trends, social patterns and technology. The macro-environment refers to broad outside forces rather than one firm's internal instructions.\n\nDemographic factors describe population characteristics such as age distribution, educational level, size and ethnic composition. Cultural factors concern values, beliefs, customs and accepted behaviour. Economic factors include income, inflation and interest rates. Technological factors include new production methods, communication systems and products. Technology may make an older product obsolete even when it still works physically. Obsolescence is loss of usefulness or market appeal, not necessarily complete mechanical failure.\n\nSocial responsibility means considering the effects of business on employees, consumers, the community and the natural environment. Businesses should supply safe goods, provide accurate information and manage waste responsibly. They cannot transfer every duty for product quality to a regulator. Meeting a contract is an obligation; maintaining a safe and tidy operating environment also expresses responsibility to workers and neighbours.\n\nThe Standards Organisation of Nigeria concerns relevant standards and product conformity. NAFDAC means National Agency for Food and Drug Administration and Control; its familiar slogan is “Safeguarding the health of the nation.” The Manufacturers Association of Nigeria represents manufacturers' interests. These organizations have different functions and should not be selected merely because all their names concern products or business.\n\nConsumerism is an organized effort to protect consumers' interests and secure fair treatment. It concerns harmful products, misleading claims and other exploitative practices, not simply discouraging one form of consumption. Quality, safety, information, choice and redress must work together. A customer who knows a product is unsafe has not thereby received adequate protection from its hazards.\n\nPollution can affect air, water and land; noise can also harm health and welfare. Gas flaring releases pollutants, while untreated industrial and domestic wastes create other hazards. Claims about the single “most dangerous” or “main” source need a defined place, period and measure. A short examination phrase should not become an unsupported universal ranking.\n\nWorked example: a Port Harcourt producer installs waste treatment, labels products accurately and investigates complaints. These actions address environmental and consumer responsibilities. Advertising its concern without changing harmful conduct would not provide the same protection.\n\nThe historical Consumer Protection Council had a broad consumer-protection role; modern Nigerian competition and consumer-protection institutions have different names and statutory arrangements.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A demographic description is not an internal management policy.\n• A trade association is not automatically a statutory regulator.\n• New technology can cause obsolescence without physical damage.\n• Pollution categories can overlap in one incident."},"new":{"title":"Business environment, product safety and public institutions","body":"BUSINESS ENVIRONMENT, PRODUCT SAFETY AND PUBLIC INSTITUTIONS — THE RULES A BUSINESS OPERATES UNDER\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nBusinesses respond to population changes, technology, inflation and law. Consumers expect safe products and truthful information. JAMB questions frequently ask whether a factor is internal or external and which Nigerian institution handles a particular type of product or market concern.\n\n1. INTERNAL AND EXTERNAL ENVIRONMENTS\nINTERNAL FACTORS are conditions mainly within the business: management, workers, financial resources, equipment, operating systems and organisational culture.\n\nEXTERNAL FACTORS arise outside the business: customers, suppliers, competitors, government policy, economic trends, technology, cultural practices and environmental conditions.\n\nEXAMPLE: A bakery's poorly maintained oven is an internal weakness. A rise in national fuel prices is an external economic challenge.\n\n2. MICRO AND MACRO ENVIRONMENTS\nThe MICRO ENVIRONMENT includes parties closely linked to operations: suppliers, customers, competitors, intermediaries and other immediate stakeholders.\nThe MACRO ENVIRONMENT comprises broad forces affecting many organisations: economic conditions, demographics, politics, law, technology, social values and the natural environment.\n\nA supplier's delayed delivery is a micro-environment issue. A nationwide change in interest rates is a macro-environment issue.\n\n3. DEMOGRAPHIC FACTORS\nDemography concerns population characteristics, such as size, age distribution, education, household structure, migration and location.\n\nEXAMPLE: A growing population of school-age children can increase demand for exercise books and school uniforms. An ageing population may change demand for healthcare and accessible services.\n\nBusinesses should not assume all members of a demographic group have identical preferences.\n\n4. CULTURAL AND SOCIAL FACTORS\nCulture includes shared values, beliefs, customs and accepted patterns of behaviour. Social factors also include lifestyles, education and attitudes.\n\nEXAMPLE: Food preferences can affect what a restaurant sells in a particular community. Businesses that ignore local expectations may lose customers even when prices are competitive.\n\n5. ECONOMIC FACTORS\nINFLATION increases the general price level over time, affecting input costs and purchasing power.\nINTEREST RATES influence borrowing costs and saving decisions.\nEXCHANGE RATES affect import costs and export receipts.\nINCOME AND EMPLOYMENT influence consumer demand.\nINFRASTRUCTURE affects transport, energy and communication costs.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A retailer imports an item priced at $100. At ₦1,400/$ it costs ₦140,000 before charges; at ₦1,600/$ it costs ₦160,000. The exchange-rate change raises the naira purchase cost by ₦20,000.\n\n6. POLITICAL, LEGAL AND TECHNOLOGICAL FACTORS\nGovernment policies and laws affect taxes, permits, safety standards, labour practices and competition. Technology can improve production and communication while making older products or skills less useful.\n\nEXAMPLE: A merchant adopts electronic inventory tracking to reduce stock errors. That is a technological response to a business problem.\n\n7. NATURAL AND ENVIRONMENTAL FACTORS\nWeather, flooding, resource availability, waste and pollution affect business operations. A flood can damage a warehouse and interrupt deliveries. Businesses may reduce exposure through safe siting, drainage, insurance and emergency plans.\n\n8. PESTLE AND SWOT\nPESTLE analyses POLITICAL, ECONOMIC, SOCIAL, TECHNOLOGICAL, LEGAL and ENVIRONMENTAL forces.\n\nSWOT identifies internal STRENGTHS and WEAKNESSES and external OPPORTUNITIES and THREATS.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO: A food producer has a trained quality-control team (strength), unreliable refrigeration (weakness), rising demand for packaged food (opportunity) and new competing imports (threat).\n\nJAMB TRAP: A strength is internal; a market opportunity is external.\n\n9. WHY PRODUCT SAFETY MATTERS\nUnsafe or falsely described goods can harm buyers, destroy trust and lead to legal consequences. Product safety involves appropriate ingredients, manufacturing processes, packaging, instructions, storage and compliance with applicable standards.\n\nEXAMPLE: A medicine exposed to excessive heat may become unsuitable for use even if its packaging looks attractive.\n\n10. NAFDAC\nThe NATIONAL AGENCY FOR FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION AND CONTROL (NAFDAC) regulates specified foods, drugs, cosmetics, medical devices and other covered products under its legal mandate.\n\nIts functions include relevant product regulation, inspections and enforcement. It is not a general stock exchange regulator or a commercial bank.\n\n11. STANDARDS ORGANISATION OF NIGERIA\nThe STANDARDS ORGANISATION OF NIGERIA (SON) develops and supports standards and conformity-assessment activities within its statutory remit. Product quality and standards can concern measurements, materials, manufacturing and safety.\n\nA standards mark should not be confused with a company's brand logo.\n\n12. FEDERAL COMPETITION AND CONSUMER PROTECTION COMMISSION\nThe FCCPC is a major Nigerian body for consumer protection and competition matters under applicable law. Its work relates to unfair market practices, consumer interests and competition concerns.\n\nEXAMPLE: Misleading pricing or unfair conduct may raise consumer-protection questions. Unsafe regulated medicines may also require engagement with a specialised product regulator such as NAFDAC.\n\n13. OTHER PUBLIC INSTITUTIONS\nThe CORPORATE AFFAIRS COMMISSION (CAC) handles company and business registration matters within its legal responsibilities.\nThe CENTRAL BANK OF NIGERIA (CBN) performs central-banking and monetary functions.\nThe SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION (SEC) regulates relevant securities-market activities.\nThe NIGERIA CUSTOMS SERVICE administers customs-related functions.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Registering a business with CAC does not mean its food products are automatically approved by NAFDAC.\n\n14. CONSUMER RIGHTS AND RESPONSIBILITIES\nCommon consumer rights include safety, information, choice, being heard and redress. Consumers should also follow instructions, keep receipts and report problems accurately.\n\nA printed 'no returns' notice does not automatically remove statutory remedies for defective or misdescribed goods.\n\n15. ETHICS AND SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY\nBusinesses should advertise truthfully, protect personal data, treat workers fairly, meet legal obligations and manage environmental effects. Corporate social responsibility extends beyond charitable donations to the way an enterprise operates.\n\n16. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Is staff training an internal or external factor? Internal.\nQ2. Is inflation a micro or macro factor? Macro.\nQ3. What does the D in demographic analysis concern? Population characteristics.\nQ4. Which institution regulates specified medicines? NAFDAC.\nQ5. Which institution handles business registration? CAC.\nQ6. Which body is central to consumer protection and competition? FCCPC.\nQ7. Which body is associated with standards and conformity? SON.\nQ8. A new competitor is a SWOT strength or threat? Threat.\nQ9. A firm's outdated machinery is a strength or weakness? Weakness.\nQ10. Does CAC registration alone constitute approval of a food product? No.\n\nCOMMON EXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Internal and external factors must not be mixed.\n• Micro and macro refer to scope of influence.\n• NAFDAC, SON, FCCPC, CAC, CBN and SEC have different mandates.\n• A brand logo is not automatically a standards certification.\n• Product safety includes storage and handling, not just manufacture.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nPESTLE = BROAD ENVIRONMENT.\nSWOT = INTERNAL STRENGTHS/WEAKNESSES + EXTERNAL OPPORTUNITIES/THREATS.\nCAC = REGISTRATION; NAFDAC = COVERED PRODUCT REGULATION; SON = STANDARDS."}},{"id":"financial-statements-capital","old":{"title":"Financial statements, equity and capital calculations","body":"A statement of financial position, traditionally called a balance sheet, shows assets, liabilities and owners’ equity at a stated date. Assets are resources controlled by the business. Liabilities are obligations owed to other parties. Owners’ equity is the residual claim after liabilities are deducted from assets. The accounting equation is assets equal equity plus liabilities.\n\nFixed or non-current assets support operations over longer periods, such as machinery and buildings. Current assets include stock, debtors, bank and cash expected to be realized within the relevant cycle. Current liabilities include amounts due within that cycle or the normal short-term period. Classification concerns the nature and timing of an item, not simply whether its number is large.\n\nOwners’ equity includes contributed share capital and relevant reserves. Long-term loans are not owners’ equity. Older school questions sometimes call capital a liability of the business to its proprietor because the business and owner are treated separately. Modern statements normally distinguish equity from liabilities. Explain that accounting-entity idea rather than claiming that an owner's ordinary investment is a bank debt.\n\nNet working capital is current assets minus current liabilities. It may be negative. A table showing ₦5 million of current assets and ₦11 million of current liabilities gives negative ₦6 million, not positive ₦6 million. Do not remove the minus sign merely because all offered options are positive. Check the table's totals when a printed number appears malformed, and disclose any inference needed.\n\nCapital employed commonly means total assets less current liabilities, or equivalently owners’ equity plus long-term debt. Fixed assets plus net working capital gives the same result when classifications are consistent. Some older questions use capital employed loosely for all fixed and current assets, meaning gross capital. The explanation must distinguish that gross usage from the usual net measure instead of teaching two contradictory formulas without context.\n\nWorked example: a business has fixed assets of ₦180,000, investments of ₦100,000 and current assets of ₦60,000. Current liabilities are ₦40,000. Total assets are ₦340,000; working capital is ₦20,000; capital employed is ₦300,000. The total assets and capital employed are different figures.\n\nHistorical balance sheets may show fictitious assets, such as unwritten-off expenses. For adjusted productive capital, deduct these balances from assets and equity before applying the capital-employed formula. State the convention: retaining them gives a different unadjusted figure.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A reserve can belong to equity without being a separate pile of cash.\n• A current liability is subtracted when finding net working capital.\n• Capital employed is not automatically the first row labelled capital.\n• Missing or inconsistent source figures should be explained, not silently invented."},"new":{"title":"Financial statements, equity and capital calculations","body":"FINANCIAL STATEMENTS, EQUITY AND CAPITAL — READ THE NUMBERS CORRECTLY\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB Commerce questions test accounting relationships rather than advanced bookkeeping. You must distinguish assets from liabilities, capital from profit, a balance sheet from an income statement, and liquidity from profitability. Small calculation errors usually come from classifying an item wrongly.\n\n1. ACCOUNTING AND FINANCIAL STATEMENTS\nAccounting records, classifies and summarises financial transactions so that owners, managers and other users can make decisions.\n\nA STATEMENT OF FINANCIAL POSITION, traditionally called a balance sheet, shows assets, liabilities and equity at a specified date.\nAn INCOME STATEMENT reports revenue, expenses and profit or loss over a period.\nA CASH FLOW STATEMENT reports cash inflows and outflows over a period.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A statement of financial position is a snapshot at a date; an income statement covers a period.\n\n2. ACCOUNTING EQUATION\nASSETS = LIABILITIES + OWNER'S EQUITY.\nTherefore:\nEQUITY = ASSETS − LIABILITIES.\nLIABILITIES = ASSETS − EQUITY.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A business has assets worth ₦850,000 and owes ₦320,000. Equity = ₦850,000 − ₦320,000 = ₦530,000.\n\n3. ASSETS\nAn asset is a resource controlled by an entity from which economic benefits are expected, under applicable accounting definitions.\n\nNON-CURRENT ASSETS are used over longer periods, such as buildings, machinery and equipment.\nCURRENT ASSETS include cash, inventory and trade receivables expected to be realised or used within the operating cycle or relevant short-term period.\n\nEXAMPLE: A delivery van used for several years is generally non-current. Goods held for resale are inventory and generally current.\n\n4. LIABILITIES\nA liability is a present obligation arising from past events that will require an outflow of economic resources under the relevant framework.\n\nCURRENT LIABILITIES include trade payables and short-term borrowings.\nNON-CURRENT LIABILITIES include longer-term obligations such as qualifying long-term loans.\n\nEXAMPLE: A trader owes a supplier ₦90,000 for goods received on credit. That amount is a liability, not an asset.\n\n5. OWNER'S EQUITY OR CAPITAL\nEquity is the residual interest after deducting liabilities from assets. In a sole proprietorship, the owner's capital account is affected by contributions, profits, losses and drawings.\n\nCLOSING CAPITAL = OPENING CAPITAL + ADDITIONAL CAPITAL + NET PROFIT − DRAWINGS, assuming no other adjustments.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Opening capital ₦400,000; additional capital ₦50,000; profit ₦120,000; drawings ₦30,000. Closing capital = ₦540,000.\n\n6. CAPITAL VERSUS REVENUE\nCAPITAL EXPENDITURE buys or improves resources expected to provide benefits over more than one period, such as machinery.\nREVENUE EXPENDITURE is incurred in ordinary operations, such as routine rent or repairs, subject to accounting rules.\n\nEXAMPLE: Buying a new delivery vehicle is normally capital expenditure. Paying for its ordinary fuel is revenue expenditure.\n\n7. GROSS PROFIT\nGROSS PROFIT = NET SALES − COST OF GOODS SOLD.\nNET SALES = SALES − SALES RETURNS.\nCOST OF GOODS SOLD = OPENING INVENTORY + NET PURCHASES + CARRIAGE INWARD − CLOSING INVENTORY, in a simplified trading calculation.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nOpening inventory ₦60,000.\nPurchases ₦300,000.\nPurchases returns ₦20,000.\nCarriage inward ₦10,000.\nClosing inventory ₦50,000.\nCost of goods sold = 60,000 + (300,000 − 20,000) + 10,000 − 50,000 = ₦300,000.\nIf net sales are ₦420,000, gross profit = ₦120,000.\n\n8. NET PROFIT\nNET PROFIT = GROSS PROFIT + OTHER INCOME − OPERATING EXPENSES and other relevant charges, under the question's simplified framework.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Gross profit ₦120,000; rent ₦25,000; salaries ₦45,000; utilities ₦10,000. Net profit = ₦40,000.\n\nA business may have a high gross profit but low net profit because of operating expenses.\n\n9. WORKING CAPITAL\nWORKING CAPITAL = CURRENT ASSETS − CURRENT LIABILITIES.\n\nEXAMPLE: Cash ₦80,000, receivables ₦70,000 and inventory ₦150,000 total current assets of ₦300,000. Payables ₦100,000 and short-term debt ₦50,000 total current liabilities of ₦150,000. Working capital = ₦150,000.\n\nPositive working capital is generally helpful but does not guarantee that bills can be paid on time; inventory may be slow-moving.\n\n10. LIQUIDITY RATIOS\nCURRENT RATIO = CURRENT ASSETS ÷ CURRENT LIABILITIES.\nQUICK RATIO = (CURRENT ASSETS − INVENTORY) ÷ CURRENT LIABILITIES in the common simplified textbook formula.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Current assets ₦400,000, including inventory ₦160,000; current liabilities ₦200,000.\nCurrent ratio = 2:1.\nQuick ratio = (400,000 − 160,000) ÷ 200,000 = 1.2:1.\n\nThe quick ratio removes inventory because stock may not be converted into cash immediately.\n\n11. PROFITABILITY RATIOS\nGROSS PROFIT MARGIN = GROSS PROFIT ÷ NET SALES × 100.\nNET PROFIT MARGIN = NET PROFIT ÷ NET SALES × 100.\n\nEXAMPLE: Gross profit ₦90,000, net profit ₦30,000, net sales ₦300,000. Gross margin = 30%; net margin = 10%.\n\nMARKUP uses COST as its denominator; MARGIN uses SELLING PRICE or SALES.\n\n12. DEBTORS AND CREDITORS\nTRADE RECEIVABLES (DEBTORS in traditional terminology) are amounts owed to the business by customers.\nTRADE PAYABLES (CREDITORS) are amounts the business owes suppliers.\n\nEXAMPLE: A retailer sells ₦25,000 of goods on credit to a customer. The retailer records a receivable, not cash received.\n\n13. DRAWINGS\nDRAWINGS are cash or goods taken by an owner for personal use. They reduce owner's equity but are not ordinary business operating expenses in a sole proprietor's profit calculation.\n\nEXAMPLE: The proprietor takes ₦15,000 from the till for household expenses. That is drawings, not wages paid to an employee.\n\n14. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Assets ₦700,000; liabilities ₦250,000. Equity? ₦450,000.\nQ2. Which statement shows financial position at a date? Statement of financial position.\nQ3. Is stock for resale current or non-current? Current.\nQ4. Is an unpaid supplier balance an asset? No, a liability.\nQ5. Opening capital ₦200,000, profit ₦50,000, drawings ₦20,000. Closing capital? ₦230,000.\nQ6. Net sales ₦500,000; COGS ₦350,000. Gross profit? ₦150,000.\nQ7. Gross profit ₦150,000; expenses ₦100,000. Net profit? ₦50,000.\nQ8. Current assets ₦180,000; current liabilities ₦120,000. Working capital? ₦60,000.\nQ9. Current assets ₦180,000; liabilities ₦120,000. Current ratio? 1.5:1.\nQ10. Is an owner's personal withdrawal a normal operating expense? No, drawings.\n\nCOMMON EXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Assets = liabilities + equity, not assets = profit + cash.\n• Drawings reduce capital but are not trading expenses.\n• Gross profit and net profit differ.\n• Credit sales can increase receivables without increasing cash.\n• Current and quick ratios are not identical.\n• Capital expenditure differs from ordinary running expenses.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nA = L + E.\nWORKING CAPITAL = CA − CL.\nGROSS PROFIT = SALES − COGS.\nNET PROFIT = GROSS PROFIT − EXPENSES (SIMPLIFIED)."}},{"id":"accounting-depreciation-inventory","old":{"title":"Depreciation, stock, drawings and profit calculations","body":"Accounting records should distinguish the nature of an item before calculations begin. Fixed assets, also called non-current assets, support business operations over longer periods. Current assets are expected to be realized or used within the operating cycle or relevant short-term period. Stock, debtors, prepayments and cash can be current assets; buildings, vehicles and fittings normally belong to fixed assets.\n\nDepreciation allocates a depreciable asset's cost over its useful life. It is not a cash payment into a repair fund and does not necessarily measure the exact fall in market value. Physical capital may lose service potential through use, age or obsolescence. Land itself is not normally depreciated in the same way as a machine, although land improvements can have limited lives.\n\nWhen a question specifies ten per cent depreciation of fixed assets, identify the assets included and the base to which that rate applies. Under a simple one-period cost-based calculation, subtract ten per cent from the relevant original figure. Do not depreciate cash or debtors merely because they appear on the same balance sheet. If the question asks for value before rather than after depreciation, the required answer changes.\n\nDrawings are money or goods taken by an owner for private use. They reduce the owner's capital and are not an ordinary business operating expense. Profit increases capital, while losses reduce it, subject to other capital movements. The proprietor and business are treated as separate accounting entities even when the business is not incorporated.\n\nCost of goods sold is opening stock plus net purchases and relevant carriage inwards, less closing stock. Closing stock is the unsold stock remaining at the end; it is not found by deducting selling expenses from sales. Carriage on sales is a distribution expense rather than part of the purchase cost of stock. Gross profit is sales less cost of goods sold.\n\nWorked example: a trader has opening stock of ₦1,800, purchases of ₦2,800 and closing stock of ₦350. Cost of goods sold is ₦4,250 before any other relevant adjustment. If carriage on sales is ₦500, that expense does not change the stated ₦350 closing stock.\n\nRate of stock turnover equals cost of goods sold divided by average stock. Rearranging, four turnovers and ₦49,600 average stock imply ₦198,400 cost of goods sold; sales revenue requires further margin information.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Drawings are not wages paid to employees.\n• Depreciation is not charged indiscriminately on every asset.\n• Working capital subtracts current liabilities from current assets.\n• Rate of stock turnover normally uses cost of goods sold, not sales revenue.\n• State whether an answer is before or after a requested depreciation adjustment."},"new":{"title":"Depreciation, stock, drawings and profit calculations","body":"DEPRECIATION, INVENTORY, DRAWINGS AND PROFIT — WORKED ACCOUNTING CALCULATIONS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nThis lesson turns accounting concepts into exam calculations. JAMB may ask for straight-line depreciation, reducing-balance depreciation, closing stock, gross profit, drawings or working capital. Write the formula first, substitute carefully and check whether an item is an expense, asset or owner's withdrawal.\n\n1. NON-CURRENT AND CURRENT ASSETS\nNON-CURRENT ASSETS provide service over longer periods: machinery, buildings, vehicles and fittings.\nCURRENT ASSETS are expected to be used, sold or realised in the operating cycle or short term: inventory, trade receivables, prepayments and cash.\n\nEXAMPLE: A bakery's oven is generally non-current; flour held for production is generally inventory.\n\n2. MEANING OF DEPRECIATION\nDepreciation is the systematic allocation of a depreciable asset's cost over its useful life. It reflects consumption of the asset's service potential through use, time, wear and technological change.\n\nDepreciation is not a cash payment into a special repair fund. Nor does the depreciation charge necessarily equal the asset's exact market-price decline.\n\nLAND is generally not depreciated where it has an unlimited useful life, although land improvements and other particular assets may be depreciable.\n\n3. STRAIGHT-LINE DEPRECIATION\nANNUAL DEPRECIATION = (COST − RESIDUAL VALUE) ÷ USEFUL LIFE.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A machine costs ₦600,000, is expected to have a ₦60,000 residual value and a six-year useful life.\nDepreciable amount = ₦540,000.\nAnnual depreciation = ₦540,000 ÷ 6 = ₦90,000.\nAnnual rate on original cost = ₦90,000 ÷ ₦600,000 × 100 = 15%.\n\nUnder the simplified straight-line method, annual depreciation is equal each full year.\n\n4. BOOK VALUE\nNET BOOK VALUE OR CARRYING AMOUNT = COST − ACCUMULATED DEPRECIATION, ignoring other adjustments.\n\nEXAMPLE: After two full years of ₦90,000 straight-line depreciation, accumulated depreciation is ₦180,000. Carrying amount = ₦600,000 − ₦180,000 = ₦420,000.\n\nBook value is an accounting measure, not necessarily the price the machine could fetch if sold.\n\n5. REDUCING-BALANCE DEPRECIATION\nUnder a fixed-percentage reducing-balance method, depreciation is charged on the opening carrying amount each year.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A machine costs ₦200,000; annual rate is 20%.\nYear 1 depreciation = 20% × ₦200,000 = ₦40,000; closing value ₦160,000.\nYear 2 depreciation = 20% × ₦160,000 = ₦32,000; closing value ₦128,000.\nYear 3 depreciation = 20% × ₦128,000 = ₦25,600; closing value ₦102,400.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Do not apply 20% to original cost every year when the question specifies reducing balance.\n\n6. STRAIGHT LINE VERSUS REDUCING BALANCE\nSTRAIGHT LINE: Equal annual charge under stable assumptions.\nREDUCING BALANCE: Declining annual charge as carrying amount falls.\nBoth allocate cost; neither guarantees an exact market valuation.\n\nThe appropriate method should reflect expected consumption of economic benefits, subject to accounting rules.\n\n7. INVENTORY AND COST OF GOODS SOLD\nINVENTORY is goods or materials held for sale or use in production.\n\nCOST OF GOODS SOLD = OPENING INVENTORY + NET PURCHASES + CARRIAGE INWARD − CLOSING INVENTORY in a simplified trading calculation.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE:\nOpening stock ₦50,000.\nPurchases ₦240,000.\nReturns outward ₦10,000.\nCarriage inward ₦5,000.\nClosing stock ₦35,000.\nCOGS = 50,000 + (240,000 − 10,000) + 5,000 − 35,000 = ₦250,000.\n\n8. GROSS AND NET PROFIT\nGROSS PROFIT = NET SALES − COGS.\nNET PROFIT = GROSS PROFIT + OTHER INCOME − OPERATING EXPENSES and other relevant charges under the stated framework.\n\nEXAMPLE: Net sales ₦350,000 and COGS ₦250,000 give gross profit ₦100,000. If expenses total ₦65,000, net profit is ₦35,000.\n\nCarriage inward generally contributes to purchase cost. Carriage outward is usually a selling or distribution expense in simplified trading questions.\n\n9. INVENTORY VALUATION\nFIFO (FIRST IN, FIRST OUT) assumes earlier inventory costs are assigned to goods sold first for costing purposes. WEIGHTED AVERAGE COST uses an average unit cost.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Buy 10 units at ₦100 each and 10 units at ₦120 each. Sell 12 units.\nUnder FIFO, COGS = (10 × ₦100) + (2 × ₦120) = ₦1,240.\nClosing inventory = 8 × ₦120 = ₦960.\n\nUnder periodic weighted average, average cost = (₦1,000 + ₦1,200) ÷ 20 = ₦110 per unit. COGS for 12 units = ₦1,320; closing inventory for 8 units = ₦880.\n\nBoth calculations use the same total available cost of ₦2,200 but allocate it differently.\n\n10. INVENTORY TURNOVER\nINVENTORY TURNOVER = COST OF GOODS SOLD ÷ AVERAGE INVENTORY.\nAVERAGE INVENTORY = (OPENING INVENTORY + CLOSING INVENTORY) ÷ 2.\n\nEXAMPLE: COGS ₦600,000; opening inventory ₦80,000; closing inventory ₦120,000. Average inventory = ₦100,000; turnover = 6 times.\n\nA high turnover may indicate strong sales or insufficient stock. Interpretation depends on the type of business.\n\n11. DRAWINGS AND CAPITAL\nDRAWINGS are money or goods taken by an owner for personal use. They reduce owner's equity; they are not the same as business wages.\n\nCLOSING CAPITAL = OPENING CAPITAL + ADDITIONAL CAPITAL + PROFIT − DRAWINGS, assuming no other changes.\n\nEXAMPLE: Opening capital ₦250,000, profit ₦70,000, drawings ₦20,000. Closing capital = ₦300,000.\n\n12. WORKING CAPITAL AND LIQUIDITY\nWORKING CAPITAL = CURRENT ASSETS − CURRENT LIABILITIES.\nCURRENT RATIO = CURRENT ASSETS ÷ CURRENT LIABILITIES.\nQUICK RATIO = (CURRENT ASSETS − INVENTORY) ÷ CURRENT LIABILITIES in the simplified textbook formula.\n\nEXAMPLE: Current assets ₦500,000, including inventory ₦200,000; current liabilities ₦250,000.\nWorking capital = ₦250,000.\nCurrent ratio = 2:1.\nQuick ratio = 1.2:1.\n\n13. CAPITAL VERSUS REVENUE EXPENDITURE\nBuying new equipment for long-term use is usually capital expenditure. Paying ordinary electricity or routine repairs is usually revenue expenditure.\n\nEXAMPLE: Replacing a small worn component in normal maintenance may be an expense; installing a major upgrade that increases capacity may qualify for capitalisation under applicable accounting rules.\n\n14. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Cost ₦300,000, residual ₦30,000, life 3 years. Annual straight-line depreciation? ₦90,000.\nQ2. Asset ₦100,000, reducing-balance rate 10%. First-year depreciation? ₦10,000.\nQ3. What is second-year depreciation in Q2? ₦9,000.\nQ4. Cost ₦300,000, accumulated depreciation ₦80,000. Carrying amount? ₦220,000.\nQ5. Opening stock ₦20,000, net purchases ₦100,000, closing stock ₦15,000. COGS without other adjustments? ₦105,000.\nQ6. Net sales ₦180,000; COGS ₦105,000. Gross profit? ₦75,000.\nQ7. Are drawings operating expenses? No.\nQ8. What method applies a constant rate to declining book value? Reducing balance.\nQ9. What does FIFO stand for? First in, first out.\nQ10. Current assets ₦120,000; current liabilities ₦80,000. Working capital? ₦40,000.\n\nCOMMON EXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Depreciation is an allocation, not cash set aside.\n• Straight line uses cost less residual value over life.\n• Reducing balance uses each year's opening carrying amount.\n• Carriage inward and outward have different accounting treatments.\n• Closing inventory is subtracted in COGS calculations.\n• Drawings reduce capital rather than profit.\n• FIFO and weighted average may produce different profit figures.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nSTRAIGHT LINE = (COST − RESIDUAL) ÷ LIFE.\nREDUCING BALANCE = RATE × OPENING BOOK VALUE.\nCOGS = OPENING STOCK + NET PURCHASES − CLOSING STOCK (PLUS RELEVANT INWARD COSTS)."}},{"id":"international-trade-economics","old":{"title":"Comparative advantage, trade barriers and external balances","body":"International trade involves exchange between residents of different countries. Differences in resources, climate, skills, technology and production costs encourage specialization and trade. Currency differences, transport costs, legal requirements and trade restrictions can make international transactions more difficult than a simple domestic sale.\n\nAbsolute advantage means producing more output from a given amount of resources, or using fewer resources for a given output. Comparative advantage means producing a good at a lower opportunity cost. Opportunity cost is the next-best alternative forgone. Comparative advantage explains why countries can benefit from specialization and exchange even when one country is more efficient in producing both goods. It is not the same as having the larger population or the strongest currency.\n\nA tariff is a tax on imports or exports under the applicable law. A quota limits quantity. Embargoes and sanctions restrict specified trade for policy reasons. Customs duties are artificial barriers because governments impose them; distance is a natural obstacle. Standards can protect safety but can also make compliance costly. Globalization increases international connections without removing every barrier.\n\nDumping commonly means selling an exported product below its normal value under the relevant trade-law test. Older school questions may describe sales below cost or at very low prices. Do not teach every foreign discount as legally established dumping: the comparison basis and evidence matter. Devaluation concerns a deliberate reduction in a currency's official value under the relevant exchange-rate arrangement, not a seller reducing a product's price.\n\nThe balance of trade compares merchandise exports with merchandise imports. The balance of payments records wider transactions between residents and non-residents. Traditional school presentations may use current, capital and monetary-movement accounts; modern statistical accounts distinguish financial transactions more explicitly. Current transactions include goods and services, while capital and financial transactions concern different kinds of transfers and claims.\n\nWorked example: Country A gives up two bags of maize to produce one unit of cloth, while Country B gives up four bags. A has the lower opportunity cost in cloth. This comparison, rather than the money price alone, identifies comparative advantage in the simplified example.\n\nTerms of trade compare export prices with import prices, commonly as an export-price index divided by an import-price index, multiplied by 100. A rise means a given quantity of exports purchases more imports; this differs from the value-based balance of trade.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A balance-of-payments problem is broader than a merchandise deficit.\n• A customs duty is not a physical transport distance.\n• Comparative advantage concerns opportunity cost, not simply absolute output.\n• Currency changes can help some traders and hurt others; their effect is not identical in every transaction.\n• Historical accounting headings must be explained rather than silently replaced."},"new":{"title":"Comparative advantage, trade barriers and external balances","body":"COMPARATIVE ADVANTAGE, TRADE BARRIERS AND EXTERNAL BALANCES — WHY COUNTRIES EXCHANGE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nCountries trade because their resources, skills and opportunity costs differ. JAMB questions often confuse absolute advantage with comparative advantage or the balance of trade with the balance of payments. This lesson teaches the reasoning and the calculations.\n\n1. INTERNATIONAL TRADE\nInternational trade is the exchange of goods and services between residents of different countries. Imports are purchases from abroad; exports are sales abroad.\n\nCountries trade to obtain goods they cannot efficiently produce, specialise in relatively efficient activities, access larger markets and obtain technology or inputs.\n\nInternational transactions may face exchange-rate risk, customs procedures, freight charges, documentation and trade restrictions.\n\n2. ABSOLUTE ADVANTAGE\nA country has an absolute advantage in producing a good if it can produce more of that good using the same resources or the same output using fewer resources.\n\nEXAMPLE: With equal labour resources, Country A produces 20 bags of rice while Country B produces 12. A has an absolute advantage in rice production under those assumptions.\n\nAbsolute advantage alone does not determine which good each country should specialise in when several goods are involved.\n\n3. OPPORTUNITY COST\nOpportunity cost is the value of the next-best alternative forgone when a choice is made.\n\nIf one unit of labour can produce either 4 shirts or 2 bags of rice, producing one additional bag of rice costs 2 shirts. The opportunity cost of one shirt is 0.5 bag of rice.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Opportunity cost is the alternative sacrificed, not automatically the amount of money spent.\n\n4. COMPARATIVE ADVANTAGE\nA country has comparative advantage in a good when it can produce that good at a LOWER OPPORTUNITY COST than another country.\n\nWORKED TABLE\nCountry A with equal resources can produce either 12 units of cocoa or 6 units of cloth.\nCountry B can produce either 8 units of cocoa or 8 units of cloth.\n\nFor A:\n1 cocoa costs 6 ÷ 12 = 0.5 cloth.\n1 cloth costs 12 ÷ 6 = 2 cocoa.\n\nFor B:\n1 cocoa costs 8 ÷ 8 = 1 cloth.\n1 cloth costs 8 ÷ 8 = 1 cocoa.\n\nA has comparative advantage in cocoa because it gives up only 0.5 cloth per cocoa, compared with B's 1 cloth.\nB has comparative advantage in cloth because it gives up only 1 cocoa per cloth, compared with A's 2 cocoa.\n\nA is absolutely more productive in cocoa but not cloth. Comparative advantage is determined by relative sacrifice, not merely output totals.\n\n5. GAINS FROM SPECIALISATION\nWhen countries specialise according to comparative advantage and exchange at mutually beneficial terms, total available consumption can increase under suitable assumptions.\n\nIn the example, a trade price of 1 cocoa for 0.75 cloth lies between the two countries' opportunity costs for cocoa: 0.5 and 1 cloth. Both may gain from trade at such a rate, ignoring transport and other costs.\n\nTrade benefits are not guaranteed for every person or industry. Adjustment costs, unequal bargaining power and barriers may affect outcomes.\n\n6. VISIBLE AND INVISIBLE TRADE\nVISIBLE TRADE covers physical goods such as machinery, cocoa and textiles.\nINVISIBLE TRADE covers services such as shipping, insurance, tourism and consultancy.\n\nA payment for a service is not merchandise trade simply because it involves money.\n\n7. BALANCE OF TRADE\nBALANCE OF VISIBLE TRADE = GOODS EXPORTS − GOODS IMPORTS.\nSURPLUS: Exports exceed imports.\nDEFICIT: Imports exceed exports.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Goods exports ₦1.4 trillion; goods imports ₦1.8 trillion. Balance = −₦0.4 trillion, a visible trade deficit.\n\n8. BALANCE OF PAYMENTS\nThe balance of payments records economic transactions between residents and non-residents over a period. It includes current, capital and financial accounts under standard accounting frameworks.\n\nThe CURRENT ACCOUNT includes goods, services, primary income and secondary income. The FINANCIAL ACCOUNT records cross-border investment and financing transactions.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A deficit in merchandise trade does not by itself establish the position of the entire balance of payments.\n\n9. EXCHANGE RATES\nAn exchange rate states the value of one currency in another.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A $1,000 import costs ₦1,500,000 at ₦1,500 per dollar. At ₦1,650 per dollar, it costs ₦1,650,000. The naira has depreciated relative to the dollar.\n\nDEPRECIATION refers to a fall in value under a market-rate system; DEVALUATION refers to an official downward adjustment under an administered or fixed-rate arrangement.\n\n10. TARIFFS\nA tariff is a tax on specified imports or exports, most commonly imports. Governments may use tariffs to raise revenue, support domestic producers or pursue trade-policy objectives.\n\nEXAMPLE: An imported item costs ₦100,000 before duty. A 20% ad valorem import tariff adds ₦20,000, producing ₦120,000 before other charges.\n\nA tariff is a tax, not a physical quantity restriction.\n\n11. QUOTAS, EMBARGOES AND LICENCES\nQUOTA: Quantitative limit on goods that may be imported or exported.\nEMBARGO: Official prohibition on specified trade.\nLICENCE: Authorisation required for specified transactions.\nEXCHANGE CONTROL: Regulation of foreign-currency access or transfers.\n\nTariffs raise the cost of imports directly; quotas limit quantity. Both can protect domestic producers but may increase prices and reduce consumer choice.\n\n12. FREE TRADE AND PROTECTIONISM\nFREE TRADE seeks to reduce barriers to international exchange.\nPROTECTIONISM uses measures to shelter selected domestic activities from foreign competition.\n\nArguments for protection may include infant-industry development, national security, employment or unfair-trade concerns. Arguments against excessive protection include higher consumer costs, inefficiency and retaliation.\n\n13. TERMS OF TRADE\nIn a simplified index approach:\nTERMS OF TRADE INDEX = EXPORT PRICE INDEX ÷ IMPORT PRICE INDEX × 100.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Export price index = 120; import price index = 150. Terms of trade = 120 ÷ 150 × 100 = 80.\n\nA decline in the index can indicate that more exports are required to purchase a given quantity of imports, all else equal. Interpret price indices rather than confusing terms of trade with the trade balance.\n\n14. ECONOMIC INTEGRATION\nFREE-TRADE AREA: Removes agreed internal barriers while members retain separate external tariff policies.\nCUSTOMS UNION: Adds a common external tariff.\nCOMMON MARKET: Adds freer movement of labour and capital.\nECONOMIC UNION: Involves deeper economic-policy coordination.\n\nTrade creation may replace expensive domestic production with cheaper member production. Trade diversion may shift purchases from a more efficient outside supplier to a less efficient member because of preferences.\n\n15. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What is absolute advantage? Producing more with the same resources or the same with fewer resources.\nQ2. What determines comparative advantage? Lower opportunity cost.\nQ3. If one cloth costs 3 cocoa in A and 2 cocoa in B, who has comparative advantage in cloth? B.\nQ4. What is a visible export? A physical good sold abroad.\nQ5. Exports ₦600m, imports ₦750m. Trade balance? ₦150m deficit.\nQ6. What is a tariff? Tax on specified international trade.\nQ7. What is a quota? Quantity limit.\nQ8. What is an embargo? Official prohibition.\nQ9. Export price index 150, import price index 125. Terms of trade? 120.\nQ10. Is the balance of trade identical to the balance of payments? No.\n\nCOMMON EXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Comparative advantage uses opportunity cost, not absolute output.\n• A country may benefit from trade even if it is more productive in several goods.\n• A tariff is a tax; a quota is a quantity limit.\n• A trade deficit is not the entire balance of payments.\n• Terms of trade measure relative export and import prices, not export minus import values.\n• Exchange-rate depreciation can raise the local-currency cost of imports.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE\nCOMPARATIVE ADVANTAGE = LOWEST OPPORTUNITY COST.\nTRADE BALANCE = GOODS EXPORTS − GOODS IMPORTS.\nTERMS OF TRADE = EXPORT PRICE INDEX ÷ IMPORT PRICE INDEX × 100."}},{"id":"shipping-orders-postal-documents","old":{"title":"Import orders, shipping receipts and delivery documents","body":"Trade documents serve different purposes at different stages. An enquiry asks about goods and terms. A quotation gives an offered price and conditions. An order states what the buyer wants to purchase. An invoice records goods supplied or charged for. A delivery note accompanies goods so that the receiver can check the consignment; it is not the same as a receipt for money paid.\n\nAn indent is an order for goods, commonly in foreign trade. A closed indent specifies the manufacturer or supplier from whom goods should be obtained. An open indent gives the agent discretion to select a suitable supplier within the order's instructions. “Open” does not mean that there are no limits on price, quality or accountability.\n\nA consignment note records goods handed to a carrier and the relevant delivery instructions. An advice note informs the buyer that goods have been dispatched and may arrive separately from them. A freight note concerns the carriage charge. An exporter should match the required document to its function rather than choosing whichever name sounds most international.\n\nA mate's receipt acknowledges cargo received on board or into the ship's custody under the relevant procedure. It can be exchanged for a bill of lading. A bill of lading acknowledges shipment, evidences the carriage contract and may operate as a document of title. It is not a customs invoice. A dock warrant acknowledges goods held by a dock or warehouse operator and can have a title function under its terms. A bill of sight permits provisional customs entry when full particulars are unavailable; it does not replace every storage receipt.\n\nA consular invoice is certified under the importing country's requirements where applicable. A certificate of origin states the country in which goods originate. These do not perform the same job as an indent ordering goods.\n\nFranco is a traditional delivered-price term under which the quotation includes the specified delivery to the buyer's destination. Compare its stated destination with FOB, where the buyer usually bears the main freight and insurance after the relevant on-board delivery. The parties' agreement controls the precise obligations.\n\nWorked example: a Nigerian importer sends an open indent through an agent, receives an invoice and uses shipping documents to arrange collection. The agent's freedom to choose a manufacturer does not make the invoice a purchase order.\n\nPoste restante holds letters for travellers to collect at a post office; courier services deliver urgent documents through dedicated arrangements.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Delivery of goods and payment of money need different evidence.\n• A mate's receipt and a consular invoice are not interchangeable.\n• A storage receipt is not automatically proof of customs-duty payment.\n• Read the destination named in a delivered-price quotation."},"new":{"title":"Import orders, shipping receipts and delivery documents","body":"IMPORT ORDERS, SHIPPING RECEIPTS AND DELIVERY DOCUMENTS\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS\nCommercial documents are evidence of different stages of a transaction. JAMB often describes a document's function without naming it. Identify who prepares it, when it is issued and what it proves. A document confirming delivery is not necessarily proof of payment.\n\n1. THE DOCUMENTARY CHAIN\nA typical transaction follows ENQUIRY → QUOTATION → ORDER → DELIVERY → INVOICE → PAYMENT → RECEIPT. The sequence can vary: an invoice may precede delivery, and advance payment is common in some transactions. The important point is the purpose of each document.\n\nENQUIRY: Buyer requests details of availability, price and terms.\nQUOTATION: Seller states proposed prices and terms, subject to its wording.\nORDER: Buyer requests specified goods or services.\nINVOICE: Seller's bill showing goods, quantities, prices and amount due.\nDELIVERY NOTE: Accompanies delivered goods and allows checking against the order.\nRECEIPT: Acknowledges money received.\nCREDIT NOTE: Commonly reduces an amount previously invoiced, such as for accepted returns.\nDEBIT NOTE: Records a debit adjustment or claim, depending on who issues it and the context.\n\nEXAMPLE: A school orders 30 desks. The supplier sends 30 desks and a delivery note. The school checks the quantity, then pays and receives a receipt. Signing the delivery note does not prove payment.\n\n2. THE INDENT\nAn INDENT is an order for goods, commonly associated with foreign trade and buying agents. An OPEN INDENT allows an agent discretion to select a supplier within specified instructions. A CLOSED INDENT identifies the particular manufacturer or supplier to use.\n\nEXAM TRAP: Open indent does not mean unlimited spending. The agent must follow agreed quality, quantity, price and other instructions.\n\n3. CONSIGNMENT NOTE\nA consignment note records goods accepted for carriage, together with information such as consignor, consignee, destination and nature of goods. It is useful for tracking responsibility during transport.\n\nCONSIGNOR: Person sending goods.\nCONSIGNEE: Person designated to receive goods.\nCARRIER: Person or business transporting goods.\n\nA consignment note is not automatically a negotiable document of title. Its legal effect depends on the transport arrangement.\n\n4. BILL OF LADING\nA BILL OF LADING is a sea-carriage document that may serve as evidence of the carrier's receipt or shipment of goods, evidence of the contract of carriage and, for qualifying forms, a document of title.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO: A Nigerian importer buys machinery shipped by sea. The exporter obtains a bill of lading after the carrier receives or loads the machinery as appropriate. The importer may need the relevant transport documents to claim delivery under the arrangement.\n\nJAMB TRAP: The bill of lading is associated with sea transport; an AIR WAYBILL is used in air freight and is generally not negotiable in the same way as an order bill of lading.\n\n5. AIR WAYBILL, RAIL AND ROAD DOCUMENTS\nAIR WAYBILL: Records air-carriage arrangements and shipment details.\nROAD CONSIGNMENT NOTE: Records goods carried by road and relevant parties.\nRAILWAY CONSIGNMENT NOTE: Records rail consignment details under applicable arrangements.\n\nThe transport mode helps identify the likely document. None of these documents should automatically be described as a cash receipt.\n\n6. SHIPPING AND INSURANCE DOCUMENTS\nPACKING LIST: Lists packages, marks, weights and contents.\nCOMMERCIAL INVOICE: States the seller's charge.\nCERTIFICATE OF ORIGIN: Indicates origin for relevant customs and trade purposes.\nINSURANCE CERTIFICATE: Evidence of specified insurance cover.\nCUSTOMS DECLARATION: Supplies required clearance information.\n\nEXAMPLE: Customs wants to know what is inside each numbered carton: packing list. A buyer wants evidence of marine cargo insurance: insurance certificate.\n\n7. POSTAL AND COURIER SERVICES\nPostal services carry letters, parcels and other permitted items. Registered mail provides additional recording and evidence of posting or delivery according to service terms. Courier services commonly provide collection, tracking and delivery of time-sensitive parcels.\n\nA TRACKING NUMBER helps locate a consignment's status; it is not by itself proof that the goods match the buyer's order.\n\n8. TERMS OF DELIVERY\nFOB (Free on Board) and CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight) allocate responsibilities under their applicable trade rules. Under CIF, the seller arranges specified freight and insurance to the named destination port, but risk does not necessarily remain with the seller until arrival.\n\nA DELIVERY NOTE concerns physical receipt or checking of goods. An INVOICE concerns the charge. A BILL OF LADING concerns sea carriage. Do not confuse their functions.\n\n9. DISCREPANCIES AND RETURNS\nIf a buyer orders 100 units but receives 92, the delivery note should help record the discrepancy. The buyer may notify the supplier, request the missing units or seek another contractual remedy. A CREDIT NOTE may adjust the invoice where the seller accepts a reduction.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: An invoice is ₦180,000. Goods worth ₦20,000 are accepted back and the seller issues a credit note. Revised amount due = ₦160,000, assuming no other adjustments.\n\n10. DIGITAL DOCUMENTS\nElectronic purchase orders, invoices and receipts can speed processing and create searchable records. Businesses should verify senders, maintain access controls and keep reliable audit trails. A forged email invoice can direct payment to a fraudster's account.\n\n11. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which document asks for prices? Enquiry.\nQ2. Which document states a seller's proposed terms? Quotation.\nQ3. Which document accompanies goods for quantity checking? Delivery note.\nQ4. Which document proves money received? Receipt.\nQ5. Which document commonly reduces an invoice? Credit note.\nQ6. What is an open indent? An order giving a buying agent limited supplier-selection discretion.\nQ7. Who sends goods? Consignor.\nQ8. Who receives goods? Consignee.\nQ9. Which transport document is associated with sea carriage? Bill of lading.\nQ10. Which document lists carton contents? Packing list.\n\nEXAMINATION TRAPS\n• Delivery note is not a payment receipt.\n• Invoice is a demand or record of a charge, not proof of settlement.\n• Open indent still has contractual limits.\n• Bill of lading and air waybill differ.\n• A credit note generally reduces the amount billed.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE: ENQUIRE → QUOTE → ORDER → DELIVER → INVOICE → PAY → RECEIPT."}},{"id":"employment-staffing-industrial-relations","old":{"title":"Staffing, worker relations and team leadership","body":"Staffing is the management function concerned with obtaining and developing suitable people for organizational work. Recruitment attracts candidates. Selection assesses applicants and chooses suitable people. Appointment engages the chosen person, while placement assigns that person to a position. Induction introduces the employee to the job and organization. Different textbooks group these stages slightly differently, so compare the actual activities rather than merely memorizing a three-word sequence.\n\nTraining develops knowledge and skills needed for work. A worker's education, training, experience and competence help distinguish skilled from unskilled work in classroom classifications. This does not mean that work described as unskilled requires no effort or learning. Remuneration includes wages, salaries and other agreed rewards for work. Fair pay, recognition and suitable conditions can help motivate employees.\n\nDirecting guides and encourages employees to carry out plans. Motivation is therefore commonly placed under directing, although staffing and reward systems also influence it. Planning establishes objectives; organizing assigns work and authority; controlling compares actual performance with standards and arranges correction. A manager should not call every action controlling simply because a superior is involved.\n\nA trade union represents workers' collective interests. An employers' association represents employers. A trade association represents members in a trade, and a chamber of commerce promotes business across different trades. Similar activities such as representation do not make these bodies identical. Classifying a named organization requires its actual membership and legal or institutional role.\n\nIndustrial relations concerns relationships between employers and workers, including negotiation and settlement of disputes. Collective bargaining seeks agreement on matters such as pay and conditions. Unresolved disputes may go through conciliation, arbitration or the appropriate industrial court under the applicable law. In historical Nigerian questions, the Industrial Arbitration Panel or similar arbitration terminology concerns labour disputes; it is not a general code-of-conduct bureau.\n\nEsprit de corps means team spirit: cooperation and a sense of shared belonging. Unity of command concerns one immediate reporting authority, while unity of direction concerns coordinated plans for related objectives. They support teamwork but are not alternative spellings of the same principle.\n\nWorked example: a supervisor explains a target, trains workers, recognizes good performance and helps settle a scheduling dispute. These actions combine direction, development and worker relations rather than replacing all management with recruitment.\n\nNURTW is formally a transport workers’ union; older papers may use trade association loosely.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Recruitment is wider than interviewing one applicant.\n• An appointment and the job placement are related but distinct stages.\n• Worker representation differs from a manufacturers' business association.\n• Narrow spans can underuse a capable supervisor; wide spans can overload one."},"new":{"title":"Staffing, worker relations and team leadership","body":"STAFFING, WORKER RELATIONS AND TEAM LEADERSHIP\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS\nBusinesses require people with appropriate skills and clear responsibilities. JAMB distinguishes recruitment from selection, training from induction, wages from salaries, and trade unions from employers' associations. Learn the actual activity rather than relying only on similar-sounding terms.\n\n1. HUMAN RESOURCE MANAGEMENT\nHuman resource management plans staffing needs, recruits and selects workers, supports training, evaluates performance, manages remuneration and helps maintain lawful and productive employment relations.\n\nSTAFFING is the management function concerned with obtaining, placing and developing suitable workers.\n\n2. RECRUITMENT AND SELECTION\nRECRUITMENT attracts a pool of candidates.\nSELECTION assesses candidates and chooses suitable applicants.\nAPPOINTMENT formally engages a chosen worker.\nPLACEMENT assigns the worker to a job or unit.\nINDUCTION introduces the new employee to colleagues, duties, rules and procedures.\n\nEXAMPLE: A supermarket advertises for cashiers (recruitment), interviews applicants (selection), offers one candidate a job (appointment) and introduces the worker to its till procedures (induction).\n\nJAMB TRAP: Advertising a vacancy is recruitment, not selection.\n\n3. INTERNAL AND EXTERNAL RECRUITMENT\nINTERNAL RECRUITMENT considers existing employees, possibly through promotion or transfer. It may be quicker and motivate workers but can limit fresh perspectives.\nEXTERNAL RECRUITMENT seeks candidates outside the organisation. It can widen the talent pool but may cost more and require longer adjustment.\n\nPROMOTION raises an employee to a higher position, commonly with greater responsibility. TRANSFER moves an employee between roles or locations, not necessarily to a higher grade.\n\n4. TRAINING AND DEVELOPMENT\nTRAINING improves skills for specific tasks. DEVELOPMENT prepares employees for broader future responsibilities. INDUCTION familiarises a new worker with the organisation.\n\nON-THE-JOB TRAINING includes coaching and supervised practical work.\nOFF-THE-JOB TRAINING includes external courses, workshops and simulations.\n\nEXAMPLE: A teller learns to reconcile the till under supervision: on-the-job training. A manager attends an external leadership course: off-the-job development.\n\n5. SKILLED AND UNSKILLED LABOUR\nTraditional textbook classifications distinguish labour by training and expertise. SKILLED WORK requires specialised competence; SEMI-SKILLED WORK requires some training; UNSKILLED WORK is a conventional term for jobs with limited formal entry requirements.\n\nThe label 'unskilled' does not mean the worker contributes no knowledge or effort. Real jobs can require substantial practical ability.\n\n6. REMUNERATION\nWAGES are often associated with payment by hour, day or output.\nSALARY is commonly a regular fixed payment for a period.\nCOMMISSION links earnings to specified sales or results.\nBONUS is an additional payment under agreed conditions.\nFRINGE BENEFITS include non-wage benefits such as qualifying transport, health or housing support.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A salesperson earns ₦70,000 basic pay plus 4% commission on ₦500,000 eligible sales. Commission = ₦20,000; total pay = ₦90,000 before deductions.\n\n7. TIME RATE AND PIECE RATE\nTIME RATE: Pay based on hours or days worked.\nPIECE RATE: Pay based on units completed, subject to applicable labour requirements.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A worker earns ₦600 per completed unit and makes 45 qualifying units. Gross piece-rate pay = ₦27,000.\n\nPiece rates may encourage output but can create quality or safety pressures if badly designed.\n\n8. MOTIVATION AND PRODUCTIVITY\nWorkers may be motivated by fair pay, recognition, safe conditions, opportunities for advancement, job security and meaningful responsibility.\n\nPRODUCTIVITY = OUTPUT ÷ INPUT under a specified measure.\n\nEXAMPLE: A workshop makes 240 chairs using 60 labour-hours: 4 chairs per labour-hour. After training it makes 300 chairs using 60 labour-hours: 5 chairs per labour-hour. Labour productivity increases by 25%.\n\nHigher productivity is not the same as simply working longer hours.\n\n9. LEADERSHIP STYLES\nAUTOCRATIC LEADERSHIP centralises decisions with the leader.\nDEMOCRATIC LEADERSHIP invites participation before decisions.\nLAISSEZ-FAIRE LEADERSHIP allows considerable worker autonomy.\n\nNo style is ideal for every situation. A safety emergency may require rapid direction; a creative planning session may benefit from participation.\n\n10. DELEGATION AND AUTHORITY\nDELEGATION assigns tasks and relevant authority to subordinates. RESPONSIBILITY concerns the duty to perform assigned work; ACCOUNTABILITY concerns answering for results.\n\nDelegating work does not automatically remove a manager's overall accountability.\n\n11. INDUSTRIAL RELATIONS\nIndustrial relations concern relationships among workers, employers and relevant institutions. Issues include wages, hours, safety, discipline, grievances and collective bargaining.\n\nTRADE UNION: Organisation representing workers' interests.\nEMPLOYERS' ASSOCIATION: Organisation representing employers' interests.\nCOLLECTIVE BARGAINING: Negotiation over employment terms between employers and worker representatives.\n\nA trade union is not the same as a chamber of commerce, though both are associations.\n\n12. INDUSTRIAL DISPUTES\nSTRIKE: Concerted withdrawal of labour by workers.\nLOCKOUT: Employer action excluding workers from work during a dispute under relevant conditions.\nPICKET: Demonstration or presence associated with an industrial dispute, subject to law.\nGRIEVANCE: Complaint about treatment or working conditions.\n\nDISPUTE RESOLUTION may involve negotiation, mediation, conciliation, arbitration or formal legal procedures. Their legal consequences and binding force differ.\n\n13. EMPLOYEE WELFARE AND SAFETY\nSafe equipment, training, protective measures, fair processes and reasonable working conditions help prevent injuries and improve morale. Businesses should observe applicable labour laws and contractual obligations.\n\n14. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which activity attracts job applicants? Recruitment.\nQ2. Which activity chooses among applicants? Selection.\nQ3. What introduces a new employee to the organisation? Induction.\nQ4. What is movement to a higher grade? Promotion.\nQ5. What is payment per unit produced? Piece rate.\nQ6. Sales ₦400,000 at 5% commission: commission? ₦20,000.\nQ7. What is output per unit of input called? Productivity.\nQ8. Which leadership style invites staff participation? Democratic.\nQ9. What organisation represents workers collectively? Trade union.\nQ10. What is negotiation between employers and worker representatives? Collective bargaining.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Recruitment ≠ selection.\n• Induction ≠ technical training.\n• Promotion ≠ transfer.\n• Commission ≠ fixed salary.\n• Strike is worker action; lockout is employer action.\n• Delegation does not remove all managerial accountability.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE: RECRUIT → SELECT → APPOINT → PLACE → INDUCT → TRAIN → APPRAISE."}},{"id":"pricing-promotion-product-strategy","old":{"title":"Pricing strategies, product mix and advertising media","body":"A pricing policy guides how a business sets and changes prices. It should consider costs, demand, competition, objectives and the customers being served. Price is one element of the marketing mix, alongside product, place and promotion. A policy that suits one product and season may be unsuitable for another.\n\nPrice skimming sets a relatively high initial price to obtain returns from buyers willing to pay more, commonly before wider competition or lower-priced versions develop. Penetration pricing uses a relatively low initial price to attract a large market quickly. Neither policy guarantees profit. Target-return pricing seeks a stated return on investment; bidding quotes a price for a particular contract.\n\nVariable pricing changes prices according to relevant conditions, such as season, demand or customer group, within legal and fair-dealing limits. Seasonal demand can make it relevant to warm clothing. Product-line pricing sets related prices across a range of products. A question should provide enough context before claiming that one strategy is universally best.\n\nA product mix is the full range of goods and services a firm offers. Branding gives an offer an identity and distinguishes it from competitors. A brand name can be protected as a trademark when legal requirements are met. A label may show the brand, ingredients or instructions; packaging is the container or wrapper. These functions overlap, but naming the branded word is different from naming the entire packet.\n\nPromotion includes advertising, personal selling, sales promotion and public relations. Informative advertising explains features or availability. Persuasive advertising encourages preference. Competitive advertising stresses a particular brand in relation to rivals. Publicity refers to public attention or information, often through media coverage rather than purchased advertisement space.\n\nBillboards and posters are outdoor media. Newspapers and magazines are print media. Radio communicates through sound, while television combines sound and moving images. Television can demonstrate use and attract several senses but is often costly and requires suitable audience access. Do not call it automatically cheap or universally the best channel.\n\nWorked example: a new phone launches at a high price, later falls in price and receives a competing budget model. The initial strategy may be skimming. A billboard identifies the brand; a demonstration advert shows how it works; a prize contest is sales promotion.\n\nA billboard reaches people near its display; its local visibility can be effective but geographically limited. No medium is universally most effective.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A product mix is not only a list of advertisements.\n• A brand and a patent protect different interests.\n• An advertising objective differs from its chosen medium.\n• A familiar slogan can persuade without changing the physical product."},"new":{"title":"Pricing strategies, product mix and advertising media","body":"PRICING STRATEGIES, PRODUCT MIX AND ADVERTISING MEDIA\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS\nJAMB presents marketing scenarios and asks which pricing or promotional strategy is used. A low launch price and a temporary discount are not necessarily the same strategy. A product line differs from the whole product mix. Learn the purpose, conditions and limitations of each method.\n\n1. PRICING AS A MARKETING DECISION\nPrice is what customers give up to obtain a product or service. Pricing should consider costs, customer demand, competitors, business objectives, perceived value, distribution and legal requirements.\n\nPrice affects both revenue and how customers perceive quality. A high price may suggest prestige but can reduce demand; a low price may attract customers but fail to cover costs.\n\n2. COST-PLUS PRICING\nA business calculates cost and adds a markup.\n\nSELLING PRICE = COST + MARKUP.\nMARKUP % = MARKUP ÷ COST × 100.\nGROSS MARGIN % = GROSS PROFIT ÷ SALES × 100.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Cost ₦8,000; markup 25% on cost = ₦2,000. Selling price = ₦10,000. Gross margin = ₦2,000 ÷ ₦10,000 × 100 = 20%.\n\nEXAM TRAP: A 25% markup on cost does not mean a 25% margin on selling price.\n\n3. PENETRATION PRICING\nPENETRATION PRICING introduces a product at a relatively low price to attract buyers, build market share or encourage trial. It may be useful when demand is price-sensitive and production can scale.\n\nEXAMPLE: A new streaming service offers a low introductory subscription to attract a large initial customer base.\n\nThe low price may be temporary or part of a longer strategy. It does not guarantee profitability.\n\n4. PRICE SKIMMING\nPRICE SKIMMING begins with a relatively high price aimed at customers willing to pay more, potentially reducing the price as competition or wider market demand develops.\n\nEXAMPLE: A new premium device is introduced at a high price and later offered at lower prices as alternatives become available.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Skimming starts high; penetration starts low.\n\n5. OTHER PRICING POLICIES\nCOMPETITION-BASED PRICING considers rivals' prices.\nVALUE-BASED PRICING considers perceived customer benefits.\nPSYCHOLOGICAL PRICING uses presentation such as ₦9,999 instead of ₦10,000.\nPRESTIGE PRICING maintains a high price to support a premium image.\nPROMOTIONAL PRICING offers temporary price incentives.\nSEASONAL OR VARIABLE PRICING adjusts for demand conditions.\nTENDER OR BID PRICING submits a proposed price for a specified contract.\nTARGET-RETURN PRICING aims for a specified return on investment under stated assumptions.\n\nEXAMPLE: A hotel raises room rates during a major festival: demand-related variable pricing. A supermarket gives 10% off for one weekend: promotional pricing.\n\n6. DISCOUNTS\nTRADE DISCOUNT: Reduction commonly offered to trade intermediaries.\nCASH DISCOUNT: Reduction for prompt payment under specified terms.\nQUANTITY DISCOUNT: Reduction for buying larger amounts.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: List price ₦50,000; trade discount 10%. Net invoice price = ₦45,000. If a further 2% cash discount applies to the ₦45,000 payable amount, prompt-payment amount = ₦44,100.\n\nDo not simply add successive percentages when they apply to different bases.\n\n7. PRICE ELASTICITY OF DEMAND\nPRICE ELASTICITY OF DEMAND measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to price changes:\nPED = % CHANGE IN QUANTITY DEMANDED ÷ % CHANGE IN PRICE.\n\nEXAMPLE: Price rises 10% and quantity demanded falls 20%. PED = −2; absolute elasticity is 2, indicating elastic demand in this simplified example.\n\nElasticity helps explain why raising price may increase or decrease total revenue, depending on demand response and other conditions.\n\n8. PRODUCT LINE AND PRODUCT MIX\nPRODUCT LINE: Related products, such as several sizes and variants of one detergent.\nPRODUCT MIX: All product lines and items a company offers.\nPRODUCT MIX WIDTH: Number of product lines.\nPRODUCT LINE DEPTH: Number of variants within a line.\n\nEXAMPLE: A business sells soap, toothpaste and shampoo. These may be three product lines. Offering four soap sizes increases the depth of its soap line, not necessarily the number of lines.\n\n9. PRODUCT LIFE CYCLE\nINTRODUCTION: Product enters the market; awareness and distribution develop.\nGROWTH: Sales may rise quickly as acceptance increases.\nMATURITY: Growth slows and competition may intensify.\nDECLINE: Sales fall as preferences or technologies change.\n\nNot every product follows a smooth predictable curve. Businesses can modify products, find new users or reposition brands.\n\n10. PRODUCT DIFFERENTIATION\nDifferentiation makes an offering meaningfully distinct through quality, design, service, packaging, location or brand identity.\n\nEXAMPLE: Two bottled-water brands may differ through container design, delivery reliability and customer service, even if the core product is similar.\n\n11. PROMOTIONAL MIX\nADVERTISING: Paid non-personal communication.\nPERSONAL SELLING: Direct persuasive interaction.\nSALES PROMOTION: Short-term incentives such as samples, coupons and contests.\nPUBLIC RELATIONS: Building understanding and goodwill.\nDIRECT MARKETING: Targeted contact with selected customers.\n\nA salesperson explaining a product in a store is personal selling; a radio message to thousands is advertising.\n\n12. ADVERTISING MEDIA\nRADIO: Useful for spoken messages and local-language audiences; no product image.\nTELEVISION: Combines sight and sound; production and placement can be costly.\nNEWSPAPERS: Can present detail and local announcements; readership varies.\nBILLBOARDS: Repeated visibility at fixed locations; limited detailed content.\nDIGITAL ADVERTISING: Can reach selected audiences and track responses, but needs attention to privacy, fraud and platform dependence.\nPOINT-OF-SALE DISPLAY: Reaches buyers close to purchase decisions.\n\nMEDIA SELECTION depends on audience, coverage, cost, urgency, product characteristics and message complexity.\n\n13. ETHICAL PROMOTION\nClaims should be truthful and supportable. Misleading health claims, hidden charges and fake endorsements can harm consumers and expose businesses to regulatory consequences.\n\n14. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which strategy begins with a low launch price? Penetration.\nQ2. Which strategy begins with a high launch price? Skimming.\nQ3. Cost ₦4,000, markup 20%. Selling price? ₦4,800.\nQ4. List price ₦20,000, 15% discount. Net price? ₦17,000.\nQ5. Which discount rewards early payment? Cash discount.\nQ6. What is the collection of all company products? Product mix.\nQ7. What is a group of related products? Product line.\nQ8. Which promotional method uses free samples? Sales promotion.\nQ9. Which medium combines sound and moving images? Television.\nQ10. Which promotional activity involves direct conversation with a buyer? Personal selling.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Penetration and skimming begin at opposite price levels.\n• A discount is not automatically penetration pricing.\n• Markup and margin use different denominators.\n• Product line is part of product mix.\n• Advertising is only one part of promotion.\n• Higher prices do not always produce higher revenue.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE: PENETRATION = LOW FIRST; SKIMMING = HIGH FIRST.\nPRODUCT LINE = RELATED GROUP; PRODUCT MIX = ALL OFFERINGS."}},{"id":"networks-security-digital-information","old":{"title":"Computer networks, security and digital information","body":"A computer network links devices so they can communicate and share resources. The Internet is a worldwide network of networks. An intranet is a private organizational network using related technologies. An extranet extends authorized access to selected outside parties, such as suppliers. An intranet is not normally open to every member of the public.\n\nA modem enables communication over a suitable connection by converting signals as required by the communication system. A web browser retrieves and displays web content, including text, images and video. A web server supplies resources to browsers. HTTP is a communication protocol; HTML is a markup language used to structure web pages. Neither is simply the name of a physical storage disk.\n\nIn an email address such as ada@example.com, the @ sign separates the local username from the domain identifying the receiving mail system. It is not a password. Historic telegram charges commonly depended on the number of chargeable words. Communication satellites can relay long-distance signals, including international telephone traffic. Modern links may also use terrestrial cables and other networks; satellite is not the only possible route.\n\nSystem software manages the computer and supports its operation. Operating systems such as Windows coordinate hardware and applications. Utility programs perform support tasks such as backup, security or file maintenance. Application software performs user tasks, such as word processing or preparing accounts. Registers are hardware storage locations, not application packages.\n\nA hard copy is printed output. A soft copy is information held or displayed electronically. Main memory supports current processing, while cache is small, fast memory that keeps frequently needed data near the processor. Historical classifications may treat cache within primary storage, but cache is not identical to all main memory. Secondary drives retain files; they do not replace the CPU's instruction-processing role.\n\nEncryption transforms data into coded form; decryption reverses the appropriate coding with the required method or key. Malware is malicious software, including viruses. Antivirus tools detect and remove or quarantine threats. Debugging finds and corrects program errors; it should not be taught as the precise synonym for virus removal.\n\nWorked example: an Ibadan firm lets employees use an intranet and gives a supplier limited extranet access. A browser displays its ordering page, and security utilities help protect the computers. Printing an order produces a hard copy.\n\nA management information system supplies organized information to support managerial decisions; it is not merely a file-transfer protocol.\n\nCybercafés face operating costs.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A browser and a web server have different roles.\n• HTML is a markup language; HTTP is a protocol.\n• Removing a virus is not merely correcting an ordinary programming mistake.\n• Downloading information requires a working connection, not only an empty storage disk."},"new":{"title":"Computer networks, security and digital information","body":"COMPUTER NETWORKS, SECURITY AND DIGITAL INFORMATION\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS\nCommerce now depends on networks for orders, inventory, banking and customer support. JAMB questions distinguish internet, intranet and extranet; browser and server; data and information; and security threats and protective controls.\n\n1. COMPUTER NETWORKS\nA COMPUTER NETWORK links devices so they can exchange information and share resources such as printers, files and applications.\n\nA LOCAL AREA NETWORK (LAN) connects devices within a limited area such as an office or school.\nA WIDE AREA NETWORK (WAN) connects devices across larger distances.\nThe INTERNET is a worldwide network of interconnected networks.\n\nEXAMPLE: Computers sharing a printer inside one branch form a LAN. A bank linking branches across several states uses wide-area networking.\n\n2. INTERNET, INTRANET AND EXTRANET\nINTERNET: Global public network infrastructure.\nINTRANET: Private organisational network using internet-related technologies.\nEXTRANET: Controlled extension of organisational resources to authorised outsiders, such as suppliers or distributors.\n\nEXAMPLE: Staff access an internal leave-request portal through an intranet. Approved suppliers access a restricted stock-ordering portal through an extranet.\n\nJAMB TRAP: An intranet is not normally open to every internet user.\n\n3. CLIENTS, SERVERS AND BROWSERS\nA CLIENT requests a service or resource.\nA SERVER supplies the requested service or resource.\nA WEB BROWSER retrieves and displays web content.\nA WEB SERVER responds to web requests.\n\nEXAMPLE: A student's browser requests a school's results page from a server. The browser is software on the user's device; the server is the system providing the resource.\n\n4. HTTP, HTTPS AND HTML\nHTTP is a protocol used for transferring web requests and responses.\nHTTPS uses HTTP over a secure encrypted transport connection, helping protect data in transit and authenticate the destination under the relevant certificate framework.\nHTML is a markup language for structuring web-page content.\n\nJAMB TRAP: HTML is not a network cable, and HTTP is not a programming language for writing ordinary web-page structure.\n\n5. EMAIL AND ADDRESSES\nEmail enables electronic messaging. In ada@example.com, 'ada' is the local part and 'example.com' is the domain.\n\nCC shows additional visible recipients; BCC can conceal recipients from other recipients in ordinary email use. Attachments carry files. SPAM is unsolicited or unwanted bulk messaging.\n\nA message appearing to come from a trusted sender can still be fraudulent.\n\n6. DATA AND INFORMATION\nDATA are raw facts or observations.\nINFORMATION is data processed and presented so it is meaningful for a purpose.\n\nEXAMPLE: '25, 30, 20' are sales quantities. 'Total sales were 75 units across three days' is processed information.\n\nThe common information-processing cycle is INPUT → PROCESSING → OUTPUT → STORAGE, with feedback or control as relevant.\n\n7. BUSINESS APPLICATIONS\nPOINT-OF-SALE SYSTEMS record sales.\nINVENTORY SYSTEMS track stock movement.\nACCOUNTING SOFTWARE records transactions.\nCUSTOMER RELATIONSHIP MANAGEMENT SYSTEMS organise customer interactions.\nENTERPRISE RESOURCE PLANNING SYSTEMS integrate selected business processes.\n\nEXAMPLE: A supermarket barcode scan updates a sale and may reduce recorded stock automatically. Incorrect initial inventory data can still produce incorrect reports.\n\n8. NETWORK HARDWARE\nROUTER forwards traffic between networks.\nSWITCH connects devices within a local network.\nMODEM handles signal conversion or access functions appropriate to a connection.\nWIRELESS ACCESS POINT provides wireless network connectivity.\nNETWORK INTERFACE enables a device to connect.\n\nA router is not the same thing as a web browser. Hardware and software play different roles.\n\n9. CYBERSECURITY THREATS\nMALWARE: Malicious software.\nVIRUS: Malware that can attach to or infect other files or programs.\nRANSOMWARE: Malware that can encrypt or otherwise deny access and demand payment.\nPHISHING: Fraudulent messages designed to obtain secrets or induce harmful actions.\nSOCIAL ENGINEERING: Manipulating people into revealing information or bypassing controls.\nUNAUTHORISED ACCESS: Entry without permission.\n\nEXAMPLE: A message claiming to be from a bank asks a customer to disclose a one-time code. This is a phishing attempt.\n\n10. SECURITY CONTROLS\nUse strong unique passwords, multi-factor authentication, updates, access permissions, reliable backups, device protection and staff awareness. Verify unusual payment instructions through a separate trusted channel.\n\nENCRYPTION helps protect data confidentiality. BACKUPS support recovery after loss or damage. ACCESS CONTROL restricts who may view or change information.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A backup is not the same as antivirus protection. Both address different risks.\n\n11. INFORMATION QUALITY\nUseful business information should be accurate, relevant, timely, sufficiently complete and understandable.\n\nEXAMPLE: A stock report showing yesterday's balance may be inaccurate for a customer ordering a fast-selling item today. Timeliness matters.\n\n12. E-COMMERCE\nE-COMMERCE is buying and selling through electronic networks.\nB2B: Business to business.\nB2C: Business to consumer.\nC2C: Consumer to consumer.\n\nA wholesaler supplying a retailer through an online portal is B2B. A retailer selling directly to a household through an app is B2C.\n\n13. DIGITAL ETHICS\nBusinesses should protect personal information, avoid misleading claims, respect intellectual-property rights and use data according to applicable requirements. Customers should verify sites and payment instructions.\n\n14. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What network commonly serves one office? LAN.\nQ2. What is the worldwide network of networks? Internet.\nQ3. What is a private staff network? Intranet.\nQ4. What allows selected outside partners controlled access? Extranet.\nQ5. Which software displays websites? Browser.\nQ6. What does HTML do? Structures web content.\nQ7. What does HTTPS help protect? Web communication in transit.\nQ8. What is fraudulent credential-seeking messaging? Phishing.\nQ9. What helps restore lost data? Backup.\nQ10. A wholesaler sells to a retailer online. Which e-commerce category? B2B.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Internet, intranet and extranet differ by access and scope.\n• Browser, server, router and modem are not interchangeable.\n• Data are raw facts; information is meaningful processed data.\n• Encryption, backups and access control serve different purposes.\n• HTTPS does not guarantee that every website or seller is trustworthy.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE: INTERNET = GLOBAL; INTRANET = PRIVATE STAFF; EXTRANET = AUTHORISED PARTNERS.\nINPUT → PROCESS → OUTPUT → STORE."}},{"id":"securities-trading-listing","old":{"title":"Capital-market instruments, listing and investment returns","body":"The capital market provides medium- and long-term finance. Its instruments include shares, stocks, bonds and debentures. The money market deals mainly in short-term instruments such as Treasury bills and commercial bills. A broad question offering both stocks and debentures as capital-market instruments may have more than one correct option; the maturity and legal nature of each instrument matter.\n\nA bond is a borrowing instrument with stated terms. Its coupon is the contractual interest rate or payment basis. An ordinary share represents ownership and may provide dividends and capital gains, but neither is guaranteed. A warrant gives a right to subscribe for specified securities under stated terms; it is not itself an ordinary interest-bearing loan.\n\nA stockbroker acts for clients in buying and selling admitted securities. A dealer or jobber trades on the dealer's own account. Investors supply funds and hold securities, while authorized market intermediaries conduct transactions under exchange and regulatory rules. A company being incorporated does not mean that anyone may trade on the exchange without authorization.\n\nThe primary market concerns new securities. A rights issue offers new shares to existing shareholders under specified entitlement terms. The secondary market permits trading existing securities. Daily exchange lists report prices and trading information, helping investors follow market movements. The price at which one investor sells an existing share does not automatically become new cash received by the company.\n\nHistorical Nigerian questions distinguish the main or first-tier market from the Second-tier Securities Market. The latter offered less demanding admission arrangements for smaller indigenous enterprises, including shorter records and particular public-shareholding conditions. Different papers may ask about a percentage offered to the public, shareholder numbers or years of trading. Use the historical rule actually requested rather than assuming that “less restricted” means unregulated. Both operate within a regulatory framework.\n\nWorked example: a firm raises cash by issuing new shares. Later, a shareholder sells through a broker. The first event provides primary-market finance; the second provides liquidity in the secondary market. A published price list helps another investor observe the latest market quotation.\n\nSome historical classroom rules specify at least 100 shareholders; check the exact era and market segment.\n\nHistorical school accounts of the Second-tier market also specify at least 10% offered publicly alongside a minimum of 100 shareholders; these are not permanent current listing requirements.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A share dividend differs from a bond coupon.\n• Treasury bills do not become long-term capital-market securities merely because government issues them.\n• The regulator and the exchange perform different functions.\n• Listing requirements and institutional names can change; old examination rules should not be presented as permanent current law.\n• A dealer's own-account trading differs from a broker's client agency."},"new":{"title":"Capital-market instruments, listing and investment returns","body":"CAPITAL-MARKET INSTRUMENTS, LISTING AND INVESTMENT RETURNS\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS\nJAMB questions may combine the capital market, money market, shares, debentures, stock exchanges, brokers and returns. The safest method is to classify an instrument by its legal nature and usual maturity, then calculate returns using the correct base.\n\n1. CAPITAL MARKET VERSUS MONEY MARKET\nThe CAPITAL MARKET facilitates medium- and long-term financing and investment, including shares and many bonds.\nThe MONEY MARKET deals mainly in short-term borrowing and lending instruments, such as Treasury bills and qualifying commercial paper.\n\nEXAMPLE: A company issues ordinary shares to finance expansion: capital-market financing. A government issues a short-term Treasury bill: money-market instrument.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Both shares and long-term debentures can be capital-market instruments. Do not assume only shares belong there.\n\n2. SHARES\nA SHARE represents an ownership interest in a company. Ordinary shareholders may receive dividends and benefit from price appreciation, but neither is guaranteed. They also bear the risk of losses.\n\nPREFERENCE SHARES carry specified preferential rights, often regarding dividends or capital repayment, subject to their terms.\n\n3. BONDS AND DEBENTURES\nA BOND or DEBENTURE is generally a debt security with stated terms. Investors lend to the issuer and may receive interest and principal repayment.\n\nCOUPON RATE refers to the contractual interest basis, commonly expressed as a percentage of face value.\nMATURITY is when principal is due under the instrument.\nSECURED DEBT has specified security; UNSECURED DEBT does not.\n\nEXAMPLE: A ₦100,000 bond with an 8% annual coupon pays ₦8,000 annually under simplified terms, regardless of whether an investor later buys it for ₦95,000 or ₦105,000.\n\n4. WARRANTS AND RIGHTS\nA WARRANT may grant the holder the right, but not the obligation, to subscribe for specified securities at agreed terms.\nA RIGHTS ISSUE offers existing shareholders the opportunity to purchase additional shares, commonly in proportion to holdings.\nA BONUS ISSUE allocates additional shares to existing holders from eligible reserves without itself bringing new cash into the company.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A warrant is not simply an ordinary interest-bearing loan.\n\n5. PRIMARY AND SECONDARY MARKETS\nPRIMARY MARKET: Issuer sells newly created securities and normally receives issue proceeds.\nSECONDARY MARKET: Existing investors trade previously issued securities; the issuer ordinarily does not receive the resale proceeds.\n\nEXAMPLE: A company offers new shares to the public: primary market. An investor later sells those shares on an exchange: secondary market.\n\n6. LISTING AND STOCK EXCHANGES\nLISTING means admitting eligible securities to trading on an exchange subject to its requirements. It can improve access to investors and marketability but creates disclosure and compliance obligations.\n\nA STOCK EXCHANGE provides an organised venue and rules for trading. It does not guarantee that every listed investment will increase in price.\n\nIn Nigeria, the SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION (SEC) is a major capital-market regulator. NGX is an example of a securities exchange. Their roles are distinct.\n\n7. MARKET PARTICIPANTS\nISSUER raises funds.\nINVESTOR buys securities.\nSTOCKBROKER executes transactions for clients under applicable licensing and rules.\nDEALER trades securities on its own account where permitted.\nREGISTRAR maintains specified shareholder and corporate-action records.\nCUSTODIAN holds and administers assets for clients.\nREGULATOR supervises market conduct under law.\n\nEXAMPLE: A person wishing to buy listed shares commonly uses an authorised intermediary; the broker is not the company issuing the shares.\n\n8. SHARE PRICE TERMS\nNOMINAL OR PAR VALUE: Stated face value where applicable.\nISSUE PRICE: Price paid for newly issued securities.\nMARKET PRICE: Price at which existing securities trade.\n\nThe issuer normally receives proceeds from a new issue, not from every later secondary-market transaction.\n\n9. DIVIDEND RETURN\nTOTAL DIVIDEND = NUMBER OF ELIGIBLE SHARES × DIVIDEND PER SHARE.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A shareholder owns 1,500 eligible shares and receives ₦0.60 per share. Dividend = ₦900 before any relevant deductions.\n\nDIVIDEND YIELD = ANNUAL DIVIDEND PER SHARE ÷ MARKET PRICE PER SHARE × 100.\n\nIf annual dividend is ₦1.20 and market price is ₦24, yield = 5%.\n\n10. CAPITAL GAIN\nCAPITAL GAIN = SELLING PROCEEDS − PURCHASE COST, ignoring charges in simplified questions.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Buy 300 shares at ₦12 each: cost ₦3,600. Sell at ₦15 each: proceeds ₦4,500. Capital gain = ₦900; percentage gain = 25%.\n\nActual net returns can be reduced by fees and taxes. A price fall produces a capital loss.\n\n11. TOTAL HOLDING-PERIOD RETURN\nSIMPLIFIED RETURN = (DIVIDENDS + CAPITAL GAIN) ÷ INITIAL PURCHASE COST × 100.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Buy 100 shares at ₦20 = ₦2,000. Receive dividends totalling ₦100. Sell all at ₦23 = ₦2,300. Capital gain = ₦300. Total simplified return = (₦100 + ₦300) ÷ ₦2,000 × 100 = 20%.\n\nDo not confuse dividend yield with total return.\n\n12. BOND CURRENT YIELD\nCURRENT YIELD = ANNUAL COUPON PAYMENT ÷ CURRENT MARKET PRICE × 100.\n\nEXAMPLE: A bond with face value ₦50,000 and 10% annual coupon pays ₦5,000. If its market price is ₦40,000, current yield = 12.5%. Coupon rate remains 10% of face value.\n\nCurrent yield excludes changes in market price and some other elements of total bond return.\n\n13. RIGHTS ISSUE CALCULATION\nA 1-for-4 rights issue offers one new share for every four existing shares.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: An investor owns 2,000 shares. Entitlement = 2,000 ÷ 4 = 500 new shares. At ₦6 per share, subscription cost = ₦3,000.\n\nA 1-for-5 bonus issue on 2,000 shares instead allocates 400 additional shares without a new subscription payment under the stated terms.\n\n14. INVESTMENT RISKS\nMARKET RISK: Prices fluctuate.\nCREDIT RISK: Issuer may fail to meet obligations.\nLIQUIDITY RISK: Difficulties selling promptly at a reasonable price.\nINFLATION RISK: Purchasing power of returns declines.\nCONCENTRATION RISK: Excessive exposure to one issuer or sector.\n\nDiversification may reduce some risks but cannot guarantee profit.\n\n15. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Which market deals mainly in short-term Treasury bills? Money market.\nQ2. Are ordinary shares capital-market instruments? Yes.\nQ3. Is a debenture usually ownership or debt? Debt.\nQ4. Who normally receives funds in a primary issue? Issuer.\nQ5. Who normally receives sale proceeds in a secondary trade? Selling investor.\nQ6. Dividend ₦0.50 on 800 shares: total? ₦400.\nQ7. Dividend ₦2, share price ₦40: yield? 5%.\nQ8. Buy for ₦5,000, sell for ₦6,200: capital gain? ₦1,200.\nQ9. Bond face value ₦10,000, coupon 6%: annual coupon? ₦600.\nQ10. Rights issue 1-for-5 on 1,000 shares: entitlement? 200 shares.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Capital market includes shares and many longer-term debt securities.\n• Broker, exchange, regulator and issuer have different functions.\n• Listing does not guarantee price increases.\n• Coupon rate differs from current yield.\n• Dividend yield differs from total investment return.\n• Rights issue may raise cash; bonus issue does not itself.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE: SHARES = OWNERSHIP; BONDS = DEBT.\nPRIMARY = NEW ISSUE; SECONDARY = INVESTOR RESALE.\nYIELD = INCOME ÷ RELEVANT PRICE × 100."}},{"id":"business-combinations-cooperation","old":{"title":"Business combinations, partnerships and restrictive arrangements","body":"Businesses may cooperate or combine to obtain finance, expertise, purchasing strength or wider markets. The precise arrangement determines ownership, control and legal obligations. A business combination is not automatically the same as a partnership, and cooperation does not always require the participating firms to surrender their separate identities.\n\nA partnership brings persons together to carry on business with a view to profit. Partners can combine complementary knowledge and skills so that one person's strength offsets another's weakness. An ordinary partnership commonly lacks the separate incorporated personality of a company. The partnership agreement should set out duties, profit sharing and arrangements for change or dissolution.\n\nA merger combines businesses under an agreed structure. A takeover gives one business control of another through the relevant transaction. A holding company controls subsidiary companies through sufficient voting interests. A trust, in historical industrial-organization language, places the affairs of participating businesses under centralized trustees or control. Do not confuse that commercial grouping with every personal trust in law.\n\nA cartel coordinates independent producers, often concerning prices, output or market shares. Participants may agree quotas, dividing a market while remaining separate businesses. OPEC illustrates cooperation among oil-producing countries, though the precise legal treatment differs from private firms' arrangements. Competition law may prohibit price-fixing and similar conduct; a classroom definition does not establish that every cooperative agreement is lawful.\n\nA ring is a historical term for a restrictive arrangement among traders or producers to control prices or terms, sometimes for a particular transaction or market. It is not the same as factoring, which concerns receivables finance. A consortium is a group of organizations cooperating on a project too large or demanding for one alone. A joint venture is a joint undertaking for a particular activity, with the exact duration and legal form determined by agreement.\n\nWorked example: three construction firms form a consortium to build a major bridge using their different equipment and expertise. If independent producers instead divide sales territories and fix prices, their arrangement is cartel-like rather than merely a bridge consortium. If one company buys control of another, that concerns acquisition and group ownership.\n\nForward integration combines a producer with a later distribution stage; backward integration moves toward its suppliers. A syndicate is a cooperative grouping for a common business purpose; distinguish its precise arrangement from a merger or project consortium.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A consortium need not erase each member's separate business.\n• A holding company and its subsidiary are different positions in a group.\n• A partnership combines people and resources, not only friendship.\n• A cartel quota is different from a government's import quota.\n• Check the actual arrangement before choosing a familiar label."},"new":{"title":"Business combinations, partnerships and restrictive arrangements","body":"BUSINESS COMBINATIONS, PARTNERSHIPS AND RESTRICTIVE ARRANGEMENTS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB tests the differences between a merger, takeover, holding company, cartel, trust, consortium and partnership. These arrangements may all involve businesses working together, but they differ in ownership, control, independence and competition.\n\n1. WHY BUSINESSES COMBINE\nFirms may combine to obtain capital, share expertise, reduce duplicated costs, secure raw materials, reach new customers or increase bargaining power. Some combinations improve efficiency; others may restrict competition and harm consumers.\n\nEXAMPLE: Two regional delivery companies combine their routes to serve more Nigerian cities. They may reduce duplicated transport costs, but their increased market power can raise competition concerns.\n\n2. PARTNERSHIP\nA partnership is an arrangement in which persons carry on business together with a view to profit, subject to applicable law. Partners may contribute money, property, labour or expertise. An agreement should address capital contributions, duties, profits, losses, admission and retirement.\n\nAn ordinary partnership traditionally differs from an incorporated company in legal personality and liability. Nigerian law also recognises particular registered partnership forms, so not every partnership has identical legal consequences.\n\nEXAMPLE: One partner contributes ₦2 million and another manages daily operations. The agreed profit-sharing ratio need not automatically equal the capital-contribution ratio.\n\n3. MERGER\nA MERGER combines two or more businesses under an agreed corporate structure. A horizontal merger involves businesses at a similar production or distribution stage; a vertical merger involves businesses at different stages; a conglomerate combination involves unrelated business lines.\n\nHORIZONTAL EXAMPLE: Two bread producers combine.\nVERTICAL EXAMPLE: A bread producer combines with a flour supplier.\nCONGLOMERATE EXAMPLE: A food producer combines with an unrelated software business.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Horizontal and vertical describe the relationship between activities, not whether the transaction is friendly or hostile.\n\n4. TAKEOVER AND ACQUISITION\nA TAKEOVER occurs when one business obtains control of another through a relevant transaction. An ACQUISITION involves purchasing a business, assets or an ownership interest. A takeover may be friendly or contested.\n\nA merger and a takeover can both change control, but they are not identical transaction structures.\n\n5. HOLDING COMPANY AND SUBSIDIARY\nA HOLDING COMPANY controls one or more subsidiary companies through relevant ownership or control rights. A SUBSIDIARY is the controlled company. Separate subsidiaries may retain their own legal identities despite group control.\n\nEXAMPLE: Company A controls Company B through sufficient voting rights. A is the holding or parent company and B is its subsidiary. That does not mean every contract signed by B automatically becomes A's contract.\n\n6. CARTEL\nA CARTEL is an arrangement among competing businesses to coordinate conduct, such as prices, output or market allocation, instead of competing independently.\n\nEXAMPLE: Several suppliers secretly agree not to sell below ₦15,000 and allocate customers among themselves. This is a classic price-fixing and market-sharing concern.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A cartel is not the same as a cooperative society formed for legitimate member benefits. Competition law can prohibit or regulate restrictive arrangements.\n\n7. TRUST AND COMBINE\nIn historical industrial-organisation terminology, a TRUST involves centralised control of participating enterprises, often through trustees. A COMBINE is a broad term for businesses joining or coordinating activities. The exact legal form matters.\n\nDo not assume every modern company group is a historical trust.\n\n8. CONSORTIUM AND JOINT VENTURE\nA CONSORTIUM is a group of organisations cooperating on a particular project or objective, often while retaining their separate identities.\nA JOINT VENTURE is a shared business undertaking in which participants contribute resources and share agreed risks and returns; it may be contractual or conducted through a separate entity.\n\nEXAMPLE: Three construction firms jointly bid to build a bridge. They may form a consortium without permanently merging their companies.\n\n9. TRADE ASSOCIATIONS\nA trade association represents firms in an industry and may provide training, information and advocacy. It is not automatically a cartel. However, coordination among competitors about prices or customers can raise competition-law issues even when undertaken through an association.\n\n10. BENEFITS AND DRAWBACKS\nPotential benefits: economies of scale, stronger distribution, access to technology, shared research and lower unit costs.\nPotential drawbacks: reduced competition, difficult integration, management conflicts, redundancies, excessive debt and concentration of power.\n\nEconomies of scale mean average cost may decline as output increases over a relevant range; they do not guarantee lower prices for customers.\n\n11. NIGERIAN REGULATORY CONTEXT\nCompetition and consumer-protection law can apply to mergers and restrictive arrangements. The Federal Competition and Consumer Protection Commission has relevant functions under Nigerian law. The legal treatment of a specific transaction depends on applicable requirements, not merely its business label.\n\n12. WORKED CLASSIFICATION\nA cocoa processor purchases a cocoa plantation: vertical combination.\nTwo cocoa processors unite: horizontal combination.\nA cocoa processor purchases an unrelated cinema chain: conglomerate combination.\nIndependent cocoa processors agree on a minimum selling price: cartel-type restrictive arrangement.\n\n13. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Two competing manufacturers combine. Type? Horizontal merger.\nQ2. Manufacturer combines with raw-material supplier. Type? Vertical merger.\nQ3. A firm gains control of another. Common term? Takeover.\nQ4. Company controlling subsidiaries? Holding company.\nQ5. Competitors fix prices together. Arrangement? Cartel.\nQ6. Independent firms collaborate on one major project. Arrangement? Consortium.\nQ7. Do subsidiaries necessarily lose separate legal identity? No.\nQ8. Does a trade association automatically constitute a cartel? No.\nQ9. What may reduce average unit cost as scale increases? Economies of scale.\nQ10. Is every business combination automatically lawful? No; applicable competition rules matter.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Merger and takeover are not interchangeable.\n• Horizontal means same stage; vertical means different stages.\n• A consortium need not end participants' independence.\n• Holding-company control does not automatically erase subsidiaries' separate identities.\n• Legitimate cooperation differs from price fixing.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE: HORIZONTAL = SAME LEVEL; VERTICAL = SUPPLY CHAIN; CARTEL = COMPETITORS COORDINATING RESTRICTIONS."}},{"id":"modern-retailing-channels","old":{"title":"Modern retailing, distribution channels and customer service","body":"Retailing is selling goods or services to final consumers for their own use. Wholesaling normally sells to retailers or other business users, commonly in larger quantities. A wholesaler connects producers with many retailers, while retailers provide convenient local access for consumers. The buyer's purpose helps identify the transaction; quantity alone is not always decisive.\n\nRetailers assemble a range of goods, break bulk into convenient quantities, explain products and may offer credit or after-sales service. They can report customers' preferences to suppliers. Wholesalers may also break bulk, hold stock, finance purchases and promote suppliers' goods. These shared functions do not make wholesaling and retailing identical.\n\nA multiple or chain store has branches under common ownership and control. A department store brings several product departments under one roof. A supermarket normally uses self-service, allowing customers to examine and select goods before payment. A vending machine accepts the required payment and dispenses the selected product automatically. An automated teller machine provides banking services and is not simply a shop vending snacks.\n\nModern retailing can combine branded products, attractive display, self-service, online or mail ordering and after-sales support. These are business practices rather than one universally fixed three-item list. A business may combine features of several store formats, but a chain is identified principally by common ownership across branches, not merely by having many departments inside one shop.\n\nManufacturers may sell directly to final consumers to retain the intermediary margin, learn about customers or control distribution. Eliminating a middleman does not eliminate the work of storage, delivery, finance and selling. The producer must perform those functions or pay another provider. Direct selling therefore does not automatically produce the lowest total cost in every case.\n\nAfter-sales service includes installation, repairs, maintenance, assistance and complaint handling after purchase. It can build loyalty and encourage repeat sales. Pre-sales service helps before the order, while the broader customer-service function covers the full relationship. Credit and hire purchase can widen demand but create collection and default risks for the seller.\n\nWorked example: a phone maker supplies a wholesaler, which supplies neighbourhood retailers. A retailer demonstrates a phone, sells it to a student and later helps with a warranty complaint. Buying, selling and servicing concern different stages of the same retail relationship.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A chain is not defined simply by one building's many departments.\n• Self-service and after-sales service happen at different stages.\n• A wholesaler is not normally the manufacturer.\n• Removing an intermediary does not remove every distribution expense."},"new":{"title":"Modern retailing, distribution channels and customer service","body":"MODERN RETAILING, DISTRIBUTION CHANNELS AND CUSTOMER SERVICE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nRetailing questions often describe a shop format or a distribution function. JAMB may ask whether a supermarket, department store, chain store, vending machine, wholesaler or online marketplace is involved. Focus on who sells to whom and how the business operates.\n\n1. RETAILING AND WHOLESALING\nRETAILING sells goods or services to final consumers for personal or household use.\nWHOLESALING sells mainly to retailers, institutions and other business buyers, often in larger quantities.\n\nEXAMPLE: A wholesaler supplies 200 cartons of noodles to several shops. A shop sells individual packs to households. The first activity is wholesaling; the second is retailing.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Quantity alone does not always decide the classification. The buyer's purpose and role in distribution matter.\n\n2. FUNCTIONS OF RETAILERS\nRetailers assemble assortments, break bulk, hold stock near customers, explain goods, offer convenient purchase quantities and may provide credit, delivery, installation or after-sales support. They communicate consumer preferences to suppliers.\n\n3. FUNCTIONS OF WHOLESALERS\nWholesalers buy in bulk, warehouse goods, finance inventory, transport stock, break bulk for retailers, bear some commercial risks and provide market information. Their services can reduce the number of direct transactions manufacturers must manage.\n\n4. DISTRIBUTION CHANNELS\nDIRECT: PRODUCER → CONSUMER.\nONE INTERMEDIARY: PRODUCER → RETAILER → CONSUMER.\nTWO INTERMEDIARIES: PRODUCER → WHOLESALER → RETAILER → CONSUMER.\n\nEXAMPLE: A tailor sells directly to a customer through WhatsApp: direct channel. A beverage producer supplies a distributor who supplies a corner shop: indirect channel.\n\nA longer channel may increase market coverage but also add handling costs and reduce producer control.\n\n5. SUPERMARKET\nA SUPERMARKET is a retail format commonly using self-service, fixed or displayed prices, shopping baskets or trolleys and checkout facilities. Customers select goods before paying.\n\nSelf-service does not mean staff are absent; staff still restock shelves, assist customers and handle payments.\n\n6. DEPARTMENT STORE\nA DEPARTMENT STORE contains different product departments under one roof or within a coordinated retail establishment, such as clothing, homeware and cosmetics.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Department store = product departments; chain store = multiple branches under common ownership.\n\n7. MULTIPLE OR CHAIN STORES\nCHAIN STORES operate several branches under common ownership and management. They may standardise branding, procurement and pricing while serving different locations.\n\nAdvantages include bulk purchasing and brand recognition. Drawbacks include coordination costs and reduced flexibility for local preferences.\n\n8. SPECIALITY AND CONVENIENCE STORES\nA SPECIALITY STORE concentrates on a narrow product category, such as sports equipment or electronics.\nA CONVENIENCE STORE prioritises easy access and quick purchases, often with limited assortment and extended hours.\n\nA small shop is not automatically a speciality store: classification depends on product focus.\n\n9. VENDING MACHINES AND AUTOMATED RETAIL\nA VENDING MACHINE accepts an authorised payment and dispenses selected goods automatically. It can operate without a salesperson during each transaction but requires restocking, maintenance and security.\n\nEXAMPLE: A machine dispenses bottled water after card payment. This is automated retailing, not wholesaling.\n\n10. E-COMMERCE AND OMNICHANNEL RETAIL\nONLINE RETAILING enables consumers to order through websites or apps.\nOMNICHANNEL RETAILING coordinates online and physical channels, for example ordering online and collecting from a store.\n\nMARKETPLACE: Platform connecting third-party sellers and buyers.\nDIRECT-TO-CONSUMER: Producer sells directly to end users.\n\nA marketplace platform may facilitate transactions without owning every product listed.\n\n11. FRANCHISING\nA FRANCHISOR grants a FRANCHISEE rights to operate under specified branding and business arrangements in exchange for agreed fees or other obligations.\n\nEXAMPLE: An independent operator runs a restaurant using a licensed brand and prescribed operating standards. The outlet may be independently owned rather than a branch directly owned by the brand.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Franchise outlet and company-owned chain branch can look similar to customers but differ in ownership.\n\n12. CUSTOMER SERVICE\nGood customer service includes accurate product information, courteous assistance, reliable delivery, fair complaint handling and after-sales support. Service quality affects customer retention.\n\nEXAMPLE: A customer receives a damaged blender. The retailer records the complaint, checks purchase details and follows applicable repair, replacement or refund obligations. Ignoring the complaint can damage reputation and create legal risk.\n\n13. STOCK CONTROL\nInventory systems help avoid STOCKOUTS (no stock when needed) and OVERSTOCKING (excess stock). Reorder levels indicate when new stock should be requested.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A shop sells an average of 20 packs per day and supplier lead time is 4 days. Expected demand during lead time = 80 packs. With safety stock of 30 packs, a simplified reorder point = 110 packs.\n\nThis calculation assumes stable average demand and lead time; actual stock planning may need variability adjustments.\n\n14. CHANNEL CHOICE\nConsider product perishability, delivery cost, market size, customer location, order frequency, storage needs and desired control.\n\nFresh bread may benefit from short, rapid channels. Packaged non-perishable goods may pass through wider wholesale networks.\n\n15. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. Selling to final consumers is what? Retailing.\nQ2. Who commonly supplies retailers in bulk? Wholesaler.\nQ3. Which format uses self-service? Supermarket.\nQ4. Which format groups products into departments? Department store.\nQ5. Which format has branches under common ownership? Chain store.\nQ6. What dispenses goods after automatic payment? Vending machine.\nQ7. What is a licensed branded outlet arrangement? Franchise.\nQ8. Producer → retailer → consumer has how many intermediaries? One.\nQ9. What is ordering online and collecting in store? Omnichannel retailing.\nQ10. Daily sales 10 units, lead time 5 days, safety stock 20. Reorder point? 70 units.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Retailing depends on selling to end users, not simply small quantity.\n• Department store differs from chain store.\n• Franchise outlet is not necessarily company-owned.\n• An online marketplace does not necessarily own all listed inventory.\n• Self-service still needs support and stock management.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE: RETAILER → FINAL CONSUMER; WHOLESALER → BUSINESS BUYERS; CHAIN = BRANCHES; DEPARTMENT = PRODUCT SECTIONS."}},{"id":"production-labour-distinctions","old":{"title":"Labour, land and production classifications","body":"Labour is human physical and mental effort used in production. Its reward is wages or salaries. Skilled labour requires relevant education, training or acquired expertise. Unskilled labour requires comparatively little specialized training for the particular task. These are classifications of skill, not statements about a worker's intelligence or worth.\n\nManual labour emphasizes physical effort. Blue-collar work commonly means manual, industrial or craft work. White-collar work commonly concerns office, administrative or professional duties. A mechanic or electrician can be both blue-collar and highly skilled. Therefore physical effort alone does not prove that a worker is unskilled. When a source question offers both descriptions without further detail, its explanation must identify that overlap.\n\nLand comprises natural resources used in production, including soil, water, minerals and natural sites. It is relatively fixed in total supply and differs in quality from place to place. This difference is heterogeneity. Land is traditionally called a passive factor because labour and enterprise organize its productive use. Remuneration can motivate workers, while land itself does not become willing to work because it receives rent.\n\nPrimary production obtains resources from nature. Fishing, crabbing, farming, forestry and mineral extraction are examples. Secondary production changes materials into useful products or constructs works. Manufacturing includes processing and factory assembly; construction combines materials into buildings, roads and similar works. A bare phrase such as assembling products needs context to distinguish factory manufacturing from the classroom construction definition.\n\nTertiary production supplies services. Direct services satisfy a particular person's wants, such as a doctor treating a patient. Public services benefit society collectively. Commerce includes trade and its supporting activities; it is not the name for every occupation.\n\nWorked example: a worker in Rivers State catches crabs, another operates a fish-processing machine, and a retailer sells the packaged fish. Catching is primary production, processing is secondary, and retailing is commerce. The machine operator may perform physical work while also needing considerable training.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Blue-collar does not mean unskilled.\n• Land's heterogeneous nature means differing qualities, not an unlimited total supply.\n• A finished iron product is different from iron ore extracted from nature.\n• Read the actual activity before choosing its occupational category."},"new":{"title":"Labour, land and production classifications","body":"LABOUR, LAND AND PRODUCTION CLASSIFICATIONS\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB tests the four factors of production, their rewards, occupational classifications and the difference between skilled work and physical work. A mechanic can be both skilled and blue-collar. Correct answers depend on the classification being asked about.\n\n1. MEANING OF PRODUCTION\nProduction creates or increases the utility of goods and services to satisfy human wants. It includes extracting resources, manufacturing goods and providing services.\n\nEXAMPLE: A farmer grows cassava, a factory turns it into flour and a transporter delivers it to markets. Each contributes to production or distribution of utility.\n\n2. FOUR FACTORS OF PRODUCTION\nLAND: Natural resources; reward traditionally called RENT.\nLABOUR: Human physical and mental effort; reward WAGES OR SALARIES.\nCAPITAL: Man-made productive resources; reward traditionally INTEREST.\nENTREPRENEURSHIP: Organising resources and bearing business risk; reward PROFIT.\n\nJAMB TRAP: In economics, capital means productive resources such as equipment, not merely any cash kept in a drawer. Money can finance the acquisition of capital goods.\n\n3. LAND\nLand includes soil, minerals, rivers, forests, climate-related natural resources and natural sites used in production.\n\nCHARACTERISTICS:\n• Total natural supply is relatively fixed.\n• Quality and location vary: HETEROGENEITY.\n• Land is geographically immobile.\n• It is traditionally described as a passive factor requiring other inputs for productive use.\n\nEXAMPLE: Two plots differ in fertility and access to roads. That difference illustrates heterogeneity, not mobility.\n\n4. LABOUR\nLabour is human effort directed toward production, whether physical or mental. It includes teachers, farmers, programmers, drivers, engineers and health workers.\n\nLabour is not homogeneous: workers differ in skills, training, productivity and experience. Workers are also people with rights and needs, not machines.\n\n5. SKILLED, SEMI-SKILLED AND UNSKILLED LABOUR\nSKILLED LABOUR requires specialised training or acquired competence.\nSEMI-SKILLED LABOUR involves some specific training or practice.\nUNSKILLED LABOUR is a traditional textbook term for work requiring comparatively little specialised formal preparation.\n\nThese categories describe job requirements, not a person's worth or intelligence.\n\nEXAMPLE: An electrician is generally skilled. A worker performing a simple repetitive task after brief instruction may be described as semi-skilled or unskilled in traditional exam classifications, depending on the task.\n\n6. MANUAL, BLUE-COLLAR AND WHITE-COLLAR WORK\nMANUAL LABOUR emphasises physical effort.\nBLUE-COLLAR WORK commonly refers to industrial, craft and manual occupations.\nWHITE-COLLAR WORK commonly refers to office, administrative and professional occupations.\n\nEXAMPLE: A trained welder is skilled and blue-collar. A professional accountant is skilled and commonly white-collar.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Blue-collar does not mean unskilled. The classifications overlap.\n\n7. DIVISION OF LABOUR\nDIVISION OF LABOUR divides a production process into specialised tasks. Workers concentrate on different operations rather than each producing an entire product.\n\nEXAMPLE: In a furniture workshop, one worker cuts wood, another assembles frames and another applies finishes.\n\nAdvantages include speed, skill development, efficient use of tools and potential cost reductions.\nDisadvantages include monotony, worker dependence on other stages and vulnerability when one stage stops.\n\n8. SPECIALISATION\nSPECIALISATION is concentration on a particular task, occupation or product. Division of labour is a form of specialisation within a production process.\n\nEXAMPLE: A town concentrates on pottery production; within a pottery workshop, workers divide clay preparation, shaping and firing.\n\nSpecialisation may improve efficiency but can increase dependence on markets and suppliers.\n\n9. PRODUCTIVITY\nPRODUCTIVITY measures output per unit of input.\n\nLABOUR PRODUCTIVITY = OUTPUT ÷ LABOUR INPUT.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A team makes 180 shirts in 60 labour-hours: 3 shirts per labour-hour. After better training it makes 240 shirts in the same hours: 4 shirts per labour-hour. Increase = (4 − 3) ÷ 3 × 100 = 33.33%.\n\nAn increase in total output does not always mean productivity increased: input use may have risen faster.\n\n10. CAPITAL\nFIXED CAPITAL includes durable productive resources such as factory machinery and equipment.\nWORKING OR CIRCULATING CAPITAL in traditional Commerce terminology includes resources used or turned over in daily operations, such as raw materials and cash needed for trading.\n\nEXAMPLE: A bakery oven is fixed capital. Flour consumed in baking is a circulating production input.\n\nAccounting 'working capital' is specifically current assets minus current liabilities; read the question's context carefully.\n\n11. ENTREPRENEURSHIP\nAn entrepreneur identifies opportunities, combines factors, makes decisions, innovates and bears relevant business risks.\n\nThe entrepreneur's reward is profit when the enterprise succeeds, but losses are possible. Management and entrepreneurship overlap but are not necessarily identical roles.\n\n12. PRIMARY, SECONDARY AND TERTIARY PRODUCTION\nPRIMARY: Extraction or cultivation of natural resources, such as fishing, farming and mining.\nSECONDARY: Manufacturing and construction that transform materials or create structures.\nTERTIARY: Services such as transport, banking, retailing, education and healthcare.\n\nEXAMPLE: Mining limestone is primary; manufacturing cement is secondary; delivering cement to a retailer is tertiary.\n\n13. DIRECT AND INDIRECT PRODUCTION\nDIRECT PRODUCTION refers to producing goods or services primarily for one's own consumption.\nINDIRECT PRODUCTION refers to producing for exchange or sale.\n\nEXAMPLE: A household grows vegetables solely for family meals: direct production. A farmer grows vegetables to sell in a market: indirect production.\n\n14. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What is the reward of land? Rent.\nQ2. What is the reward of labour? Wages or salaries.\nQ3. What is the reward of entrepreneurship? Profit.\nQ4. What does land heterogeneity mean? Differences in quality or characteristics.\nQ5. Can a mechanic be skilled and blue-collar? Yes.\nQ6. What divides work into specialised operations? Division of labour.\nQ7. Output 500 units, labour 100 hours. Productivity? 5 units per hour.\nQ8. Is fishing primary, secondary or tertiary production? Primary.\nQ9. Is manufacturing furniture primary, secondary or tertiary? Secondary.\nQ10. Is transport a tertiary activity? Yes.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Manual work is not necessarily unskilled.\n• Land includes more than soil.\n• Money and physical productive capital are not identical concepts.\n• Higher output does not automatically mean higher productivity.\n• Specialisation may create both efficiencies and dependence.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE: LAND–RENT; LABOUR–WAGES; CAPITAL–INTEREST; ENTREPRENEUR–PROFIT.\nPRIMARY = EXTRACT; SECONDARY = TRANSFORM; TERTIARY = SERVE."}},{"id":"insurance-average-benefits-claims","old":{"title":"Average clause, assurance benefits and marine sacrifice","body":"Indemnity aims to restore the insured's financial position after a covered loss, subject to the policy's conditions and limits. It does not permit the insured to profit from that loss. The sum insured is the agreed cover limit; the value at risk is the relevant value of the property. A premium buys cover and is not itself the compensation amount.\n\nAn average clause can reduce a property claim when the property is underinsured. The usual school formula is claim payable equals sum insured divided by value at risk, multiplied by the covered loss. This makes the insured bear the uninsured proportion. Check that the policy actually contains an average clause before applying the formula. Also distinguish partial loss from the entire value of the property.\n\nUtmost good faith, traditionally called uberrimae fidei, concerns honest disclosure of material facts under the applicable rules. Deliberately overstating a property's value can breach that duty. Indemnity still prevents a fraudulent profit: writing a high figure on an application does not guarantee an equally high claim payment.\n\nNon-indemnity life assurance pays an agreed benefit on the specified event rather than measuring a person's life as damaged merchandise. Whole-life cover concerns death; an endowment can pay on survival to maturity or earlier death under its conditions. Traditional classroom wording may call a life benefit a consolation payment, but it remains a contractual benefit, not necessarily an ex gratia gift. Ex gratia means payment without admitting a legal obligation.\n\nMarine hull cover concerns the vessel; cargo cover concerns the goods. Jettison is deliberate throwing of cargo overboard to lighten a vessel in danger. A qualifying intentional sacrifice for common safety can give rise to general average. Accidental partial damage does not automatically become general average merely because it occurs at sea.\n\nWorked example: goods worth ₦200,000 are insured for ₦100,000 under an average clause. A covered loss of ₦40,000 produces ₦100,000/₦200,000 × ₦40,000 = ₦20,000 before any other applicable adjustments. The uninsured half remains with the owner.\n\nThe traditional assurance distinction concerns certainty of an eventual event, especially death under whole-life cover; its timing remains uncertain. A printed option saying possibility does not mean certainty. A missing correct term must be explained.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sum insured, property value and actual loss are different figures.\n• A life-assurance benefit is not the resale value of a life.\n• Ex gratia and contractual life benefits are different.\n• Discarding cargo deliberately is jettison, not demurrage."},"new":{"title":"Average clause, assurance benefits and marine sacrifice","body":"INSURANCE AVERAGE CLAUSE, ASSURANCE BENEFITS AND MARINE SACRIFICE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nInsurance questions combine principles with calculations. JAMB may give a property's value, sum insured and partial loss, then ask what the insurer pays. Other questions distinguish indemnity from life assurance and general average from an ordinary cargo loss.\n\n1. INSURANCE AND RISK\nInsurance transfers specified financial risks to an insurer in return for a premium, subject to policy terms. The POLICY records the agreement; the PREMIUM is the amount paid for cover; the SUM INSURED is the stated cover amount or limit as applicable.\n\nEXAMPLE: A shop owner insures stock against specified fire risks. Paying the premium does not mean every kind of damage is automatically covered.\n\n2. PRINCIPLE OF INDEMNITY\nINDEMNITY aims to restore the insured to approximately the financial position before a covered loss, within policy conditions and limits, without allowing profit from the loss.\n\nEXAMPLE: Stock worth ₦500,000 suffers a covered loss of ₦80,000. A valid indemnity claim does not ordinarily entitle the owner to the full ₦500,000 merely because that is the stock's value.\n\n3. INSURABLE INTEREST\nINSURABLE INTEREST is a legally recognised financial or other interest in the subject matter of insurance under applicable law. It helps distinguish insurance from a wager.\n\nEXAMPLE: A shopkeeper has an interest in the shop's stock because damage would cause financial loss.\n\n4. UTMOST GOOD FAITH\nInsurance has traditionally emphasised honest disclosure of material facts, subject to modern legal rules and policy requirements. A material misrepresentation may affect cover or claims.\n\nEXAMPLE: Deliberately hiding a major known risk when the insurer asks about it can have serious consequences. The precise legal remedy depends on applicable law and circumstances.\n\n5. PROXIMATE CAUSE\nThe PROXIMATE CAUSE is the legally relevant dominant or effective cause of loss, not necessarily the last event in time.\n\nEXAMPLE: If a covered fire directly leads to smoke damage, the causal analysis differs from an unrelated excluded event. Policy wording still matters.\n\n6. SUBROGATION\nAfter indemnifying an insured, an insurer may acquire rights to pursue a responsible third party, subject to applicable rules, to prevent double recovery.\n\nEXAMPLE: A negligent driver damages an insured delivery van. After paying a covered claim, the insurer may seek recovery from the responsible party as permitted.\n\n7. CONTRIBUTION\nWhere more than one valid policy covers the same interest and loss under relevant conditions, insurers may share the claim. Contribution is different from subrogation: contribution concerns insurers sharing liability; subrogation concerns rights against third parties.\n\n8. AVERAGE CLAUSE\nAn AVERAGE CLAUSE may reduce payment for a partial property loss when the property is underinsured.\n\nSIMPLIFIED FORMULA:\nCLAIM PAYABLE = (SUM INSURED ÷ VALUE AT RISK) × COVERED LOSS.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A warehouse has goods worth ₦1,000,000 but insures them for ₦600,000. A covered partial fire loss is ₦200,000.\nClaim = (₦600,000 ÷ ₦1,000,000) × ₦200,000 = ₦120,000.\nThe insured bears the remaining ₦80,000 under this simplified average-clause calculation.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Do not pay the full ₦600,000 sum insured for a ₦200,000 partial loss.\n\n9. ANOTHER AVERAGE-CLAUSE EXAMPLE\nValue at risk ₦800,000; sum insured ₦400,000; covered loss ₦120,000.\nInsurance proportion = 400,000 ÷ 800,000 = 0.5.\nClaim = 0.5 × ₦120,000 = ₦60,000.\n\nIf the property is fully insured, the simplified ratio is 1 and the covered partial loss may be payable in full, subject to excesses, exclusions and other policy terms.\n\n10. EXCESS AND DEDUCTIBLE\nAn EXCESS or DEDUCTIBLE is the amount the insured must bear under a claim, subject to the policy's structure.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A covered claim before excess is ₦150,000. If a fixed ₦20,000 excess applies after relevant calculations, payment = ₦130,000.\n\nDo not apply an excess unless the question states one.\n\n11. LIFE ASSURANCE\nLife assurance typically pays a specified benefit when a covered event occurs, subject to the contract. It is not generally a strict indemnity calculation based on the exact financial loss at death.\n\nTERM ASSURANCE: Provides cover for a specified term under its conditions.\nWHOLE-LIFE ASSURANCE: Provides cover linked to death during life under its terms.\nENDOWMENT ASSURANCE: Traditionally combines a benefit payable on death within a term or on survival to a specified date, subject to policy wording.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Life assurance benefits are not ordinarily calculated using a warehouse-style average clause.\n\n12. MARINE INSURANCE\nMarine insurance may cover ships, cargo, freight and related interests against specified maritime risks.\n\nPARTICULAR AVERAGE concerns partial loss affecting a particular insured interest, subject to applicable rules.\nGENERAL AVERAGE arises when an extraordinary sacrifice or expenditure is intentionally and reasonably made for the common safety of property involved in a maritime adventure, with eligible interests contributing under the applicable principles.\n\n13. GENERAL AVERAGE SCENARIO\nA ship faces serious danger. To save ship and remaining cargo, the master reasonably orders some cargo thrown overboard. Under the qualifying rules, the loss may be shared among interests benefiting from the common sacrifice.\n\nJAMB TRAP: General average is not simply the mathematical average of damaged cargo values.\n\n14. CLAIMS PROCEDURE\nThe insured should notify the insurer as required, mitigate further loss where reasonable, preserve evidence and submit accurate supporting documents. The insurer examines cover, cause, value and policy terms.\n\n15. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What is the payment for insurance cover called? Premium.\nQ2. What principle prevents profit from a property loss? Indemnity.\nQ3. What is the right to recover from a responsible third party called? Subrogation.\nQ4. What allows insurers covering the same loss to share liability? Contribution.\nQ5. Property ₦500,000, sum insured ₦250,000, loss ₦100,000. Average-clause claim? ₦50,000.\nQ6. Property ₦900,000, insured ₦600,000, loss ₦150,000. Claim? ₦100,000.\nQ7. Does every property policy automatically contain an average clause? No.\nQ8. What is an excess? Insured's agreed share of a claim.\nQ9. Is life assurance ordinarily strict indemnity insurance? No.\nQ10. What is deliberate sacrifice for common maritime safety called? General average.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Premium is not claim payment.\n• Sum insured is not automatically payable after every loss.\n• Average clause applies only when relevant policy conditions require it.\n• Contribution differs from subrogation.\n• Life assurance differs from property indemnity.\n• General average concerns common maritime safety, not ordinary arithmetic averaging.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE: AVERAGE CLAIM = INSURED VALUE ÷ ACTUAL VALUE × COVERED LOSS.\nSUBROGATION = THIRD PARTY; CONTRIBUTION = OTHER INSURERS."}},{"id":"contracts-forms-remedies","old":{"title":"Forms of contract, discharge and the unpaid seller","body":"A contract is an agreement enforceable by law. A simple contract normally depends on its substantive requirements, including consideration where required, rather than a special sealed form. A specialty contract is traditionally executed as a deed under the relevant formal rules. A contract of record is an obligation formally recorded or acknowledged before a court, such as a recognizance in traditional classroom examples. These categories should not be confused merely because each has a written record.\n\nA valid contract has legal effect. A void contract has no legal effect as a contract from the outset under the relevant rule. A voidable contract remains effective unless the party entitled to avoid it lawfully does so. An unenforceable agreement may have substantive existence but cannot be enforced through the court in the circumstances. An unrelated third party cannot simply cancel another person's contract at will.\n\nDischarge means the ending of contractual obligations. Performance means carrying out what was promised. Agreement can end or replace an obligation by consent. Breach occurs when a party fails to perform as required; serious breach may entitle the other party to terminate and claim appropriate remedies. It is not successful performance merely because goods were delivered to somebody else.\n\nFrustration concerns an unforeseen event beyond the parties' control that makes the agreed performance impossible or fundamentally different. Whether destruction of a particular hired car frustrates a contract depends on the agreement and fault; a party cannot rely on its own breach as automatic frustration.\n\nA lien is a right to retain possession of goods until a relevant debt is paid. It does not by itself mean ownership of the goods. A factor is a mercantile agent entrusted with goods and authority to sell under the agency arrangement. A broker usually brings parties together without ordinary possession of the goods. An agent's authority, possession and right to remuneration must be distinguished.\n\nWorked example: a dealer promises to supply a specific car to Ada but instead supplies it to another buyer on the due date. Ada has not received performance. This illustrates breach, subject to the contract and available remedies, rather than frustration.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A lien is retention of possession, not automatic ownership.\n• Court records and specialty deeds are different categories.\n• Void and voidable have different effects.\n• Delivering to the wrong buyer does not fulfil the original promise."},"new":{"title":"Forms of contract, discharge and the unpaid seller","body":"FORMS OF CONTRACT, DISCHARGE AND THE UNPAID SELLER\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nContract questions use legal words that appear similar: void, voidable, unenforceable, condition, warranty, discharge and breach. JAMB also tests the rights of an unpaid seller. Understand the legal effect of each term rather than memorising a label alone.\n\n1. WHAT IS A CONTRACT?\nA contract is an agreement enforceable by law. Depending on the type of agreement and applicable law, relevant elements include offer, acceptance, consideration, intention to create legal relations, capacity, certainty and lawful purpose.\n\nEXAMPLE: A retailer agrees to supply 50 cartons for ₦250,000, and the buyer validly accepts. The parties may form a binding contract when the necessary elements are present.\n\n2. OFFER AND ACCEPTANCE\nAn OFFER expresses willingness to be bound on stated terms upon acceptance.\nACCEPTANCE is unqualified agreement to the offer's terms.\nA COUNTER-OFFER proposes changed terms and generally does not amount to acceptance of the original offer.\n\nEXAMPLE: Seller offers a machine for ₦300,000. Buyer says, 'I will pay ₦250,000.' That is ordinarily a counter-offer, not acceptance of ₦300,000.\n\n3. CONSIDERATION AND CAPACITY\nCONSIDERATION traditionally refers to something of legal value exchanged under relevant contract rules. CAPACITY concerns whether parties can enter the particular agreement with legal effect.\n\nSome agreements, such as properly executed deeds, can have different formal requirements. The legal rules depend on jurisdiction and circumstances.\n\n4. SIMPLE AND SPECIALTY CONTRACTS\nA SIMPLE CONTRACT is an ordinary contract that does not depend on execution as a deed, although some types must satisfy particular writing or other statutory requirements.\nA SPECIALTY CONTRACT is traditionally a contract made by deed under applicable formalities.\n\nA CONTRACT OF RECORD is a historical legal classification referring to obligations formally recorded by a court, such as certain recognisances in traditional textbooks. Not every document filed in court is a 'contract of record'.\n\nJAMB TRAP: 'Simple' does not necessarily mean oral; a simple contract may be written.\n\n5. EXPRESS AND IMPLIED TERMS\nEXPRESS TERMS are stated in words or writing.\nIMPLIED TERMS arise through law, the contract's context or other recognised grounds.\n\nEXAMPLE: A sales contract expressly specifies delivery on Friday. Applicable law may also imply certain rights concerning title or description.\n\n6. VALID, VOID, VOIDABLE AND UNENFORCEABLE\nVALID CONTRACT: Has legal effect.\nVOID CONTRACT: Has no contractual legal effect from the outset under the relevant rule.\nVOIDABLE CONTRACT: Operates unless the party entitled to avoid it validly does so.\nUNENFORCEABLE CONTRACT: May have substantive existence but cannot be enforced through legal proceedings in the relevant circumstances.\n\nEXAMPLE: A contract induced by a qualifying misrepresentation may be voidable rather than automatically void. The precise remedy depends on facts and law.\n\n7. CONDITIONS AND WARRANTIES\nA CONDITION is traditionally a major contractual term; breach may justify rejection or termination in appropriate cases.\nA WARRANTY is traditionally a subsidiary term; breach commonly leads to damages rather than automatic rejection.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A product manufacturer's commercial 'warranty card' is not exactly the same classification as a legal warranty term in sale-of-goods doctrine.\n\n8. DISCHARGE BY PERFORMANCE\nPERFORMANCE means parties carry out their obligations as required.\n\nEXAMPLE: A supplier delivers the agreed 100 bags and the buyer pays the contract price. The principal obligations may be discharged by performance.\n\n9. DISCHARGE BY AGREEMENT\nParties may agree to release, vary or replace obligations where the law permits. A NOVATION substitutes a new obligation or party under the necessary conditions; it is not merely an informal change in a delivery date.\n\n10. DISCHARGE BY BREACH\nA serious breach may entitle the innocent party to terminate further performance and claim remedies, subject to contract terms and law. Minor breaches do not necessarily justify termination.\n\nEXAMPLE: A supplier delivers defective goods. Whether the buyer may reject them depends on the contract, the nature of the defect and applicable consumer or sale-of-goods rules.\n\n11. DISCHARGE BY FRUSTRATION\nFRUSTRATION may discharge a contract when an unforeseen event, without relevant fault, makes performance legally or fundamentally different from what was agreed under applicable rules.\n\nEXAMPLE: A unique event venue is destroyed before the booked event. Frustration may be considered, depending on the agreement and circumstances.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A contract becoming more expensive or less profitable does not automatically establish frustration.\n\n12. REMEDIES FOR BREACH\nDAMAGES: Monetary compensation under legal rules.\nSPECIFIC PERFORMANCE: Court order requiring performance in appropriate cases.\nINJUNCTION: Court order restraining or requiring specified conduct where available.\nRESCISSION: Setting aside a contract in qualifying circumstances.\nRESTITUTION: Restoring benefits under applicable legal principles.\n\nNot every remedy is available for every breach. Specific performance is generally discretionary and subject to legal requirements.\n\n13. UNPAID SELLER\nAn UNPAID SELLER is a seller who has not received the full price or whose conditional payment has failed, under applicable sale-of-goods rules.\n\nTraditional rights may include:\nLIEN: Retaining possession of goods in qualifying circumstances until payment.\nSTOPPAGE IN TRANSITU: Reclaiming control of goods in transit under qualifying conditions, often where the buyer becomes insolvent.\nRESALE: Reselling goods when permitted by the relevant law and circumstances.\nACTION FOR PRICE OR DAMAGES: Seeking appropriate judicial relief.\n\n14. LIEN VERSUS STOPPAGE IN TRANSITU\nLIEN is associated with a seller retaining goods still in the seller's possession.\nSTOPPAGE IN TRANSITU concerns goods already handed to a carrier but not yet delivered to the buyer, where statutory conditions are met.\n\nWORKED SCENARIO: A seller has 20 cartons in its warehouse and the buyer fails to pay: consider lien. If cartons are already with a transporter and the buyer becomes insolvent before delivery: consider stoppage in transit.\n\n15. PASSING OF PROPERTY AND RISK\nOWNERSHIP, POSSESSION and RISK can be different questions. Contract terms and applicable law determine when property passes and who bears accidental loss.\n\nEXAMPLE: A carrier possesses goods without necessarily owning them. Do not equate physical custody with legal title.\n\n16. JAMB-STYLE PRACTICE\nQ1. What is unqualified agreement to an offer? Acceptance.\nQ2. What is a proposed change to an offer? Counter-offer.\nQ3. Can a simple contract be written? Yes.\nQ4. Which contract operates unless validly avoided? Voidable.\nQ5. Which contract has no contractual effect from outset? Void.\nQ6. What is discharge by completing obligations? Performance.\nQ7. Does higher cost alone always amount to frustration? No.\nQ8. What is the unpaid seller's right to retain possessed goods? Lien.\nQ9. What right may apply to qualifying goods still with a carrier? Stoppage in transit.\nQ10. What remedy awards money for a proven breach? Damages.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Void differs from voidable.\n• Unenforceable does not always mean void.\n• Simple contracts can be written.\n• Breach does not automatically justify termination.\n• Lien concerns possession; stoppage concerns goods in transit.\n• Ownership and possession are not interchangeable.\n\nMEMORY GUIDE: VOID = NO EFFECT; VOIDABLE = CAN BE AVOIDED; UNENFORCEABLE = CANNOT BE ENFORCED IN THE CIRCUMSTANCES.\nLIEN = KEEP GOODS; STOPPAGE = INTERCEPT GOODS IN TRANSIT."}},{"id":"product-benefits-segmentation","old":{"title":"Product benefits, satisfaction and market segmentation","body":"A product is an offer of goods, services or associated benefits that can satisfy a customer's want. Customers buy the usefulness of the offer, not only its physical materials. The core benefit is the basic need or problem being addressed. For a bus journey, the core benefit is movement to the required destination. For a torch, it is usable light.\n\nThe actual or formal product gives that benefit a particular form. Its features may include design, quality, brand, packaging and other characteristics. An augmented product adds supporting benefits such as installation, warranty or after-sales assistance. These layers are related, but a warranty is not the basic reason light is useful in a dark room.\n\nUtility means want-satisfying ability. Manufacturing can create form utility; transport creates place utility; warehousing creates time utility; exchange creates possession utility. Physical and psychological satisfaction can accompany a product, but product mix is a firm's range of offerings, not the exact definition of that satisfaction. Where a printed question omits utility or benefit from its options, the omission needs an explicit explanation.\n\nMarket segmentation divides a market into identifiable groups with similar needs or characteristics. A business can then choose target segments and prepare a suitable offer. Common bases include age, income, location, lifestyle and buying behaviour. Segmentation is not merely changing the price, although different segments may receive different legally appropriate offers.\n\nProduct differentiation makes an offer meaningfully different from competing offers through features, design, service or brand image. Positioning concerns the place the offer occupies in the intended customer's mind. Skimming and penetration are pricing strategies, not alternative names for dividing customers into groups.\n\nA marketing orientation starts with identifying and satisfying customer needs while achieving business objectives. A product orientation stresses product characteristics and quality. Knowing customers' desires distinguishes the marketing concept from simply assuming that a technically excellent product will sell itself.\n\nWorked example: a Nigerian shoe producer separates school pupils from office workers as customer groups. This is segmentation. Durable soles and an identifiable brand belong to the actual product; repair support adds an augmented benefit. The basic benefit remains suitable footwear.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Core benefit is not identical to brand name.\n• Product mix is a range, not a feeling of satisfaction.\n• Segmentation divides markets; differentiation distinguishes offers.\n• Market skimming sets an introductory price rather than creating customer groups."},"new":{"title":"Product benefits, satisfaction and market segmentation","body":"A product is an offer of goods, services or associated benefits that can satisfy a customer's want. Customers buy the usefulness of the offer, not only its physical materials. The core benefit is the basic need or problem being addressed. For a bus journey, the core benefit is movement to the required destination. For a torch, it is usable light.\n\nThe actual or formal product gives that benefit a particular form. Its features may include design, quality, brand, packaging and other characteristics. An augmented product adds supporting benefits such as installation, warranty or after-sales assistance. These layers are related, but a warranty is not the basic reason light is useful in a dark room.\n\nUtility means want-satisfying ability. Manufacturing can create form utility; transport creates place utility; warehousing creates time utility; exchange creates possession utility. Physical and psychological satisfaction can accompany a product, but product mix is a firm's range of offerings, not the exact definition of that satisfaction. Where a printed question omits utility or benefit from its options, the omission needs an explicit explanation.\n\nMarket segmentation divides a market into identifiable groups with similar needs or characteristics. A business can then choose target segments and prepare a suitable offer. Common bases include age, income, location, lifestyle and buying behaviour. Segmentation is not merely changing the price, although different segments may receive different legally appropriate offers.\n\nProduct differentiation makes an offer meaningfully different from competing offers through features, design, service or brand image. Positioning concerns the place the offer occupies in the intended customer's mind. Skimming and penetration are pricing strategies, not alternative names for dividing customers into groups.\n\nA marketing orientation starts with identifying and satisfying customer needs while achieving business objectives. A product orientation stresses product characteristics and quality. Knowing customers' desires distinguishes the marketing concept from simply assuming that a technically excellent product will sell itself.\n\nWorked example: a Nigerian shoe producer separates school pupils from office workers as customer groups. This is segmentation. Durable soles and an identifiable brand belong to the actual product; repair support adds an augmented benefit. The basic benefit remains suitable footwear.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Core benefit is not identical to brand name.\n• Product mix is a range, not a feeling of satisfaction.\n• Segmentation divides markets; differentiation distinguishes offers.\n• Market skimming sets an introductory price rather than creating customer groups."}},{"id":"securities-dividends-settlement","old":{"title":"Dividends, market settlement and government securities","body":"A share represents an ownership interest in a company. Its holder may receive dividends when lawfully declared and may gain or lose when the share price changes. A bond or debenture normally represents borrowing. Interest is a contractual borrowing cost, whereas an ordinary dividend is not guaranteed simply because a person owns shares.\n\nGilt-edged securities traditionally means government debt regarded as having high credit standing. The description does not mean that a security literally contains gold or cannot change in market value. An irredeemable bond or perpetual security has no ordinary fixed date for repayment of principal, while interest is payable according to its terms. Redeemable borrowing provides for repayment under stated conditions.\n\nCum dividend means that the buyer receives the entitlement to the dividend covered by the quotation. Ex dividend means that the share is sold without that entitlement, which remains with the eligible seller under the relevant record and settlement rules. Do not confuse entitlement to one dividend with transfer of every future ownership benefit.\n\nA broker acts for a client. A jobber or dealer traditionally trades on the dealer's own account. A contract note confirms details of a securities transaction carried out for a client, such as the security, quantity, price and charges. It is different from the transfer documentation used to register ownership or a delivery note for merchandise.\n\nOlder stock-exchange questions use contango and backwardation for settlement postponement charges. In that traditional classroom use, contango concerns a buyer postponing payment; backwardation concerns a seller postponing delivery of securities. Modern finance also uses these words for relationships between futures and spot prices. Identify the historical settlement context before applying a modern price-curve definition.\n\nThe Securities and Exchange Commission regulates the capital market under the applicable law. The exchange provides the organized market and its trading arrangements. The regulator is not the same organization as a commercial bank, deposit insurer or privatization bureau.\n\nWorked example: shares are bought ex dividend through a broker. The buyer obtains the shares, but not the current dividend excluded by that quotation. The broker supplies a contract note confirming the transaction; the note does not promise that the next market price will rise.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Ex dividend leaves the specified dividend with the eligible seller.\n• Perpetual borrowing still has contractual obligations.\n• A contract note is not an invitation to buy a new issue.\n• Historical settlement charges and modern futures terminology need separate explanations."},"new":{"title":"Dividends, market settlement and government securities","body":"A share represents an ownership interest in a company. Its holder may receive dividends when lawfully declared and may gain or lose when the share price changes. A bond or debenture normally represents borrowing. Interest is a contractual borrowing cost, whereas an ordinary dividend is not guaranteed simply because a person owns shares.\n\nGilt-edged securities traditionally means government debt regarded as having high credit standing. The description does not mean that a security literally contains gold or cannot change in market value. An irredeemable bond or perpetual security has no ordinary fixed date for repayment of principal, while interest is payable according to its terms. Redeemable borrowing provides for repayment under stated conditions.\n\nCum dividend means that the buyer receives the entitlement to the dividend covered by the quotation. Ex dividend means that the share is sold without that entitlement, which remains with the eligible seller under the relevant record and settlement rules. Do not confuse entitlement to one dividend with transfer of every future ownership benefit.\n\nA broker acts for a client. A jobber or dealer traditionally trades on the dealer's own account. A contract note confirms details of a securities transaction carried out for a client, such as the security, quantity, price and charges. It is different from the transfer documentation used to register ownership or a delivery note for merchandise.\n\nOlder stock-exchange questions use contango and backwardation for settlement postponement charges. In that traditional classroom use, contango concerns a buyer postponing payment; backwardation concerns a seller postponing delivery of securities. Modern finance also uses these words for relationships between futures and spot prices. Identify the historical settlement context before applying a modern price-curve definition.\n\nThe Securities and Exchange Commission regulates the capital market under the applicable law. The exchange provides the organized market and its trading arrangements. The regulator is not the same organization as a commercial bank, deposit insurer or privatization bureau.\n\nWorked example: shares are bought ex dividend through a broker. The buyer obtains the shares, but not the current dividend excluded by that quotation. The broker supplies a contract note confirming the transaction; the note does not promise that the next market price will rise.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Ex dividend leaves the specified dividend with the eligible seller.\n• Perpetual borrowing still has contractual obligations.\n• A contract note is not an invitation to buy a new issue.\n• Historical settlement charges and modern futures terminology need separate explanations."}},{"id":"office-trade-records-adjustments","old":{"title":"Statements of account, quotations and correcting invoices","body":"Trade documents record offers, orders, charges, delivery and payment. Each has a particular purpose. A letter of inquiry asks for information. A quotation answers with prices and relevant terms. An order asks the supplier to provide specified goods. An invoice records the seller's charge for the transaction. Reading the sequence helps identify which party sends each document.\n\nA statement of account summarizes dealings between a supplier and customer for a stated period. It normally shows opening balance, invoices or other debits, payments, credits and closing balance. It helps the parties see how much is owing. It is not identical to a statement of affairs, which concerns financial position in a different accounting context.\n\nA debit note increases or advises a debit in the relevant party's account; classroom invoice-correction questions commonly use a seller's debit note for an undercharge. A credit note reduces the customer's charge, such as for accepted returns or an overcharge. Always identify who issues the note and whose records are being discussed. The label alone should not replace checking the direction of the adjustment.\n\nAn advice note tells a buyer that goods have been dispatched. A delivery note accompanies or records delivery and can be acknowledged by the recipient. It is not usually the same document as the invoice requesting payment. A consignment note concerns goods entrusted to a carrier. A bill of lading has specific maritime receipt, carriage and possible title functions; it is not the name for every road-delivery slip.\n\nA document needed before departure, customs clearance or collection should be selected from the specific stage and authority in the question. A ship's manifest lists cargo; a dock or warehouse warrant concerns stored goods. A bill of sight supplies an initial declaration where the importer cannot yet provide the full description required for a detailed entry.\n\nWorked example: a carton correctly priced at ₦2,000 was invoiced at ₦200. The customer has been undercharged by ₦1,800. A seller's debit note for ₦1,800 corrects the additional charge; a new ₦2,000 charge without cancelling the old entry could overstate the amount due.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An undercharge requires the difference, not the whole price again.\n• A statement of account summarizes transactions rather than replacing every invoice.\n• Advice of dispatch and proof of delivery serve different stages.\n• Check issuer, recipient and timing before choosing a document."},"new":{"title":"Statements of account, quotations and correcting invoices","body":"Trade documents record offers, orders, charges, delivery and payment. Each has a particular purpose. A letter of inquiry asks for information. A quotation answers with prices and relevant terms. An order asks the supplier to provide specified goods. An invoice records the seller's charge for the transaction. Reading the sequence helps identify which party sends each document.\n\nA statement of account summarizes dealings between a supplier and customer for a stated period. It normally shows opening balance, invoices or other debits, payments, credits and closing balance. It helps the parties see how much is owing. It is not identical to a statement of affairs, which concerns financial position in a different accounting context.\n\nA debit note increases or advises a debit in the relevant party's account; classroom invoice-correction questions commonly use a seller's debit note for an undercharge. A credit note reduces the customer's charge, such as for accepted returns or an overcharge. Always identify who issues the note and whose records are being discussed. The label alone should not replace checking the direction of the adjustment.\n\nAn advice note tells a buyer that goods have been dispatched. A delivery note accompanies or records delivery and can be acknowledged by the recipient. It is not usually the same document as the invoice requesting payment. A consignment note concerns goods entrusted to a carrier. A bill of lading has specific maritime receipt, carriage and possible title functions; it is not the name for every road-delivery slip.\n\nA document needed before departure, customs clearance or collection should be selected from the specific stage and authority in the question. A ship's manifest lists cargo; a dock or warehouse warrant concerns stored goods. A bill of sight supplies an initial declaration where the importer cannot yet provide the full description required for a detailed entry.\n\nWorked example: a carton correctly priced at ₦2,000 was invoiced at ₦200. The customer has been undercharged by ₦1,800. A seller's debit note for ₦1,800 corrects the additional charge; a new ₦2,000 charge without cancelling the old entry could overstate the amount due.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An undercharge requires the difference, not the whole price again.\n• A statement of account summarizes transactions rather than replacing every invoice.\n• Advice of dispatch and proof of delivery serve different stages.\n• Check issuer, recipient and timing before choosing a document."}},{"id":"data-retrieval-business-applications","old":{"title":"Stored data, retrieval and business information software","body":"Data are recorded facts that can be processed into useful information. A computer accepts input, processes instructions and data, stores material and produces output. Hardware is the physical equipment; software consists of programs. An input device sends data into the system, while an output device presents results. The processor's control functions do not make it a long-term store for all business records.\n\nSecondary storage keeps files for later use. Hard disks, solid-state drives, optical discs and older floppy disks are examples. A DVD is an optical storage medium. A floppy disk is removable magnetic storage used in older systems. Both a hard disk and a suitable removable disk can hold information that can later be retrieved, so an unspecified question about retrieving information may not distinguish them sufficiently.\n\nRetrieval means locating and obtaining stored information. Downloading normally means receiving data from a remote system into a local system. Uploading sends local data to a remote system. Data transfer is the wider idea and can occur between two computers in several directions. Copying a file is not decryption, which concerns reversing encryption under the relevant method or key.\n\nA database organizes related records and supports searches, updates and reports. A business may store customers, stock and transactions in related tables. A database application can provide information useful to management, but a management information system includes people, procedures, data and processing arrangements rather than one program alone.\n\nA spreadsheet organizes cells and performs calculations; a word processor prepares text documents. Design applications help produce graphics or technical drawings. Select the application by the task. Do not call a drawing package the usual database merely because the completed drawing contains information.\n\nAccess security includes suitable passwords, permissions and other authentication arrangements. An email address identifies an account but is not by itself proof of authority to view confidential records. Sharing a password weakens the protection even if the storage device itself works correctly.\n\nWorked example: a Lagos wholesaler stores customer records in a database, retrieves unpaid invoices for a report and downloads a bank statement through an authorized account. The hard drive stores files; the database manages records; the download describes the direction of transfer.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A storage medium and an input device have different roles.\n• Retrieval is wider than downloading.\n• DVD is not an Internet protocol.\n• Possessing an email address does not automatically grant access to another person's data."},"new":{"title":"Stored data, retrieval and business information software","body":"Data are recorded facts that can be processed into useful information. A computer accepts input, processes instructions and data, stores material and produces output. Hardware is the physical equipment; software consists of programs. An input device sends data into the system, while an output device presents results. The processor's control functions do not make it a long-term store for all business records.\n\nSecondary storage keeps files for later use. Hard disks, solid-state drives, optical discs and older floppy disks are examples. A DVD is an optical storage medium. A floppy disk is removable magnetic storage used in older systems. Both a hard disk and a suitable removable disk can hold information that can later be retrieved, so an unspecified question about retrieving information may not distinguish them sufficiently.\n\nRetrieval means locating and obtaining stored information. Downloading normally means receiving data from a remote system into a local system. Uploading sends local data to a remote system. Data transfer is the wider idea and can occur between two computers in several directions. Copying a file is not decryption, which concerns reversing encryption under the relevant method or key.\n\nA database organizes related records and supports searches, updates and reports. A business may store customers, stock and transactions in related tables. A database application can provide information useful to management, but a management information system includes people, procedures, data and processing arrangements rather than one program alone.\n\nA spreadsheet organizes cells and performs calculations; a word processor prepares text documents. Design applications help produce graphics or technical drawings. Select the application by the task. Do not call a drawing package the usual database merely because the completed drawing contains information.\n\nAccess security includes suitable passwords, permissions and other authentication arrangements. An email address identifies an account but is not by itself proof of authority to view confidential records. Sharing a password weakens the protection even if the storage device itself works correctly.\n\nWorked example: a Lagos wholesaler stores customer records in a database, retrieves unpaid invoices for a report and downloads a bank statement through an authorized account. The hard drive stores files; the database manages records; the download describes the direction of transfer.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A storage medium and an input device have different roles.\n• Retrieval is wider than downloading.\n• DVD is not an Internet protocol.\n• Possessing an email address does not automatically grant access to another person's data."}},{"id":"professional-consumer-regulation","old":{"title":"Professional integrity, consumer dialogue and public duties","body":"A professional body organizes people practising a recognized occupation. It can promote training, ethical standards and the profession's interests. A trade association represents members in a particular trade or industry. A chamber of commerce has a broader business membership. These bodies should not be confused with every statutory regulator simply because they all protect standards in some way.\n\nProfessional integrity means acting honestly and maintaining the duties expected of the occupation. A lawyer who defrauds a client damages trust in legal services. Discipline is intended to protect the public and uphold professional standards, not merely protect a wrongdoer from the client. The legal authority to investigate, discipline or remove a practitioner must be checked under the applicable professional law; membership of an association alone does not describe every legal step.\n\nIn Nigerian legal questions, references to the Nigerian Bar Association may use a simplified classroom description of professional discipline. Explain that legal disciplinary institutions and procedures determine formal sanctions. Do not teach that any association can remove a person's legal right to practise solely by an informal vote.\n\nThe Nigerian Communications Commission regulates the communications sector under its legal framework. Consumer dialogue, complaints channels and public engagement can help users understand services and bring problems to the regulator's attention. A consumers' parliament is intended to protect and inform consumers and obtain feedback, not simply increase a service provider's profit or give government a commercial dividend.\n\nConsumer protection includes safety, accurate information, fair treatment and redress. Regulators, businesses, professional bodies and consumer organizations have different responsibilities. Providing a discussion forum does not transfer every duty of service quality from the provider to the customer.\n\nSocial responsibility also concerns effects on the host community and the natural environment. Safe waste disposal, support for community facilities and responsible conduct can overlap in their benefits. Environmental responsibility is a specific aspect of wider social responsibility; it is not automatically false merely because a question offers the wider label.\n\nWorked example: a consumer reports repeated unexplained charges at a regulator's engagement meeting. The complaint concerns consumer protection and service accountability. It is not a share subscription or a tax payment to government.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An association and a statutory regulator need not have identical powers.\n• Protecting professional integrity does not mean concealing misconduct.\n• Consumer dialogue is not merely advertising for providers.\n• Ethical responsibility can overlap with specific legal duties without replacing them."},"new":{"title":"Professional integrity, consumer dialogue and public duties","body":"A professional body organizes people practising a recognized occupation. It can promote training, ethical standards and the profession's interests. A trade association represents members in a particular trade or industry. A chamber of commerce has a broader business membership. These bodies should not be confused with every statutory regulator simply because they all protect standards in some way.\n\nProfessional integrity means acting honestly and maintaining the duties expected of the occupation. A lawyer who defrauds a client damages trust in legal services. Discipline is intended to protect the public and uphold professional standards, not merely protect a wrongdoer from the client. The legal authority to investigate, discipline or remove a practitioner must be checked under the applicable professional law; membership of an association alone does not describe every legal step.\n\nIn Nigerian legal questions, references to the Nigerian Bar Association may use a simplified classroom description of professional discipline. Explain that legal disciplinary institutions and procedures determine formal sanctions. Do not teach that any association can remove a person's legal right to practise solely by an informal vote.\n\nThe Nigerian Communications Commission regulates the communications sector under its legal framework. Consumer dialogue, complaints channels and public engagement can help users understand services and bring problems to the regulator's attention. A consumers' parliament is intended to protect and inform consumers and obtain feedback, not simply increase a service provider's profit or give government a commercial dividend.\n\nConsumer protection includes safety, accurate information, fair treatment and redress. Regulators, businesses, professional bodies and consumer organizations have different responsibilities. Providing a discussion forum does not transfer every duty of service quality from the provider to the customer.\n\nSocial responsibility also concerns effects on the host community and the natural environment. Safe waste disposal, support for community facilities and responsible conduct can overlap in their benefits. Environmental responsibility is a specific aspect of wider social responsibility; it is not automatically false merely because a question offers the wider label.\n\nWorked example: a consumer reports repeated unexplained charges at a regulator's engagement meeting. The complaint concerns consumer protection and service accountability. It is not a share subscription or a tax payment to government.\n\nCommon traps:\n• An association and a statutory regulator need not have identical powers.\n• Protecting professional integrity does not mean concealing misconduct.\n• Consumer dialogue is not merely advertising for providers.\n• Ethical responsibility can overlap with specific legal duties without replacing them."}},{"id":"money-acceptability-token-value","old":{"title":"Money, legal acceptance and token value","body":"Money is anything generally accepted as payment for goods and services and settlement of debts. General acceptability distinguishes money from an object that only one seller happens to desire. A medium of exchange passes between buyers and sellers. A unit of account or measure of value expresses prices in a common standard. A store of value carries purchasing power forward, while a standard of deferred payment states obligations due later.\n\nThese functions are related but different. A ₦5,000 price label uses the naira as a measure of value. Paying that amount uses money as a medium of exchange. Keeping money for a later purchase uses its store-of-value function, although inflation can reduce its purchasing power. Describing money simply as a means of settlement emphasizes the payment function rather than one physical material.\n\nUseful qualities include acceptability, portability, durability, divisibility, recognizability and reasonable stability of value. Relative scarcity means supply is not unlimited compared with demand. It does not mean that every rare object is money. Homogeneity means units of the same denomination and issue have the required uniform quality, not that every denomination has the same value.\n\nLegal tender has legal status for settling relevant debts under applicable conditions. Legal backing can support confidence, but market acceptance, institutional trust and reliable payment arrangements also matter. A governor's signature alone is not a complete economic explanation of why money is accepted. Forgery remains possible even where law prohibits it; security design and verification are also needed.\n\nToken money has a face value greater than the value of its material content. Commodity money derives substantial value from the commodity itself. A banknote's usefulness as money is not measured simply by the resale price of its paper. Different historical systems use different forms, so avoid assuming that every monetary instrument is a metal coin.\n\nWorked example: a coin accepted for ₦100 contains metal worth less than ₦100. It illustrates token money. A trader compares prices in naira, then receives payment by an approved banking channel. The currency unit and the payment mechanism are connected but different ideas.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Scarcity alone does not establish money.\n• Divisibility is a desirable quality; indivisibility is not.\n• Face value differs from metal value.\n• Legal acceptance does not make counterfeiting physically impossible."},"new":{"title":"Money, legal acceptance and token value","body":"Money is anything generally accepted as payment for goods and services and settlement of debts. General acceptability distinguishes money from an object that only one seller happens to desire. A medium of exchange passes between buyers and sellers. A unit of account or measure of value expresses prices in a common standard. A store of value carries purchasing power forward, while a standard of deferred payment states obligations due later.\n\nThese functions are related but different. A ₦5,000 price label uses the naira as a measure of value. Paying that amount uses money as a medium of exchange. Keeping money for a later purchase uses its store-of-value function, although inflation can reduce its purchasing power. Describing money simply as a means of settlement emphasizes the payment function rather than one physical material.\n\nUseful qualities include acceptability, portability, durability, divisibility, recognizability and reasonable stability of value. Relative scarcity means supply is not unlimited compared with demand. It does not mean that every rare object is money. Homogeneity means units of the same denomination and issue have the required uniform quality, not that every denomination has the same value.\n\nLegal tender has legal status for settling relevant debts under applicable conditions. Legal backing can support confidence, but market acceptance, institutional trust and reliable payment arrangements also matter. A governor's signature alone is not a complete economic explanation of why money is accepted. Forgery remains possible even where law prohibits it; security design and verification are also needed.\n\nToken money has a face value greater than the value of its material content. Commodity money derives substantial value from the commodity itself. A banknote's usefulness as money is not measured simply by the resale price of its paper. Different historical systems use different forms, so avoid assuming that every monetary instrument is a metal coin.\n\nWorked example: a coin accepted for ₦100 contains metal worth less than ₦100. It illustrates token money. A trader compares prices in naira, then receives payment by an approved banking channel. The currency unit and the payment mechanism are connected but different ideas.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Scarcity alone does not establish money.\n• Divisibility is a desirable quality; indivisibility is not.\n• Face value differs from metal value.\n• Legal acceptance does not make counterfeiting physically impossible."}},{"id":"company-documents-share-rights","old":{"title":"Company constitutions, share offers and shareholder rights","body":"A company is a legal person distinct from its shareholders once properly incorporated. Its constitutional documents explain its framework and internal arrangements under the law applying at the time. Historical Nigerian Commerce questions commonly distinguish the memorandum of association from the articles of association. Read the era of the question rather than assuming every older document requirement remains unchanged.\n\nThe memorandum traditionally states matters such as the company's name, objects, registered-office framework, liability and capital provisions. The articles concern internal management and the rights and procedures relevant to members and officers. A question about detailed shareholder rights is therefore usually testing articles rather than an objects clause. Neither document is simply a price list for goods sold by the company.\n\nA prospectus supplies information for a public investment invitation under the applicable rules. It can describe the securities offered and other material details. An invoice demands payment for a commercial transaction; an indent orders goods. They are not substitutes for an investment prospectus merely because each is a written business document.\n\nAuthorized or nominal capital is the historic maximum share capital permitted under the relevant constitutional framework. Issued capital is the nominal value of shares issued or offered and allotted under the terminology used. Called-up capital is the amount demanded on allotted shares. Paid-up capital is the amount actually paid. A part payment is not automatically the same thing as the amount called; a subscriber can owe an unpaid call.\n\nThe portion not issued is unissued capital. Reserved or reserve capital has a narrower traditional meaning associated with capital not callable except under specified winding-up conditions. Some papers use that term loosely, and the explanation must distinguish their intended calculation from the precise category.\n\nOrdinary shareholders own the company subject to its legal structure. Debenture holders are creditors. In a winding up, creditor and shareholder priorities depend on security, statutory rules and class rights. It is unsafe to claim that shareholders must always receive payment before every lender.\n\nWorked example: a company has historic authorized capital of ₦1 million, issues ₦800,000, calls ₦600,000 and receives ₦500,000. The four figures answer different questions. Cash received is ₦500,000, while the amount demanded is ₦600,000.\n\nIts separate legal personality permits a company to sue and be sued in its own name.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A memorandum's broad framework differs from internal articles.\n• A prospectus invites investment, not delivery of merchandise.\n• Called-up and paid-up amounts need not match.\n• Historical capital terminology needs its stated legal context."},"new":{"title":"Company constitutions, share offers and shareholder rights","body":"A company is a legal person distinct from its shareholders once properly incorporated. Its constitutional documents explain its framework and internal arrangements under the law applying at the time. Historical Nigerian Commerce questions commonly distinguish the memorandum of association from the articles of association. Read the era of the question rather than assuming every older document requirement remains unchanged.\n\nThe memorandum traditionally states matters such as the company's name, objects, registered-office framework, liability and capital provisions. The articles concern internal management and the rights and procedures relevant to members and officers. A question about detailed shareholder rights is therefore usually testing articles rather than an objects clause. Neither document is simply a price list for goods sold by the company.\n\nA prospectus supplies information for a public investment invitation under the applicable rules. It can describe the securities offered and other material details. An invoice demands payment for a commercial transaction; an indent orders goods. They are not substitutes for an investment prospectus merely because each is a written business document.\n\nAuthorized or nominal capital is the historic maximum share capital permitted under the relevant constitutional framework. Issued capital is the nominal value of shares issued or offered and allotted under the terminology used. Called-up capital is the amount demanded on allotted shares. Paid-up capital is the amount actually paid. A part payment is not automatically the same thing as the amount called; a subscriber can owe an unpaid call.\n\nThe portion not issued is unissued capital. Reserved or reserve capital has a narrower traditional meaning associated with capital not callable except under specified winding-up conditions. Some papers use that term loosely, and the explanation must distinguish their intended calculation from the precise category.\n\nOrdinary shareholders own the company subject to its legal structure. Debenture holders are creditors. In a winding up, creditor and shareholder priorities depend on security, statutory rules and class rights. It is unsafe to claim that shareholders must always receive payment before every lender.\n\nWorked example: a company has historic authorized capital of ₦1 million, issues ₦800,000, calls ₦600,000 and receives ₦500,000. The four figures answer different questions. Cash received is ₦500,000, while the amount demanded is ₦600,000.\n\nIts separate legal personality permits a company to sue and be sued in its own name.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A memorandum's broad framework differs from internal articles.\n• A prospectus invites investment, not delivery of merchandise.\n• Called-up and paid-up amounts need not match.\n• Historical capital terminology needs its stated legal context."}},{"id":"postal-delivery-services","old":{"title":"Postal services, proof of delivery and travellers’ mail","body":"Postal services collect, carry and deliver letters and other permitted items. Different services meet different needs. A sender should consider urgency, value, destination, proof of posting and delivery arrangements. A postal order transfers a specified value under its terms; it is not the service used to carry an urgent bundle of legal documents.\n\nOrdinary mail provides the standard collection and delivery arrangement. Registered mail adds recorded handling and other safeguards under the postal operator's conditions. It can provide evidence of posting and delivery or collection. Recorded delivery emphasizes an acknowledgment or record of delivery under the relevant service rules. Exact names and guarantees differ by operator and period, so read the historical question carefully.\n\nA certificate of posting establishes that an item was handed in for posting. It does not by itself prove that the addressee received the contents. Express services give priority or expedited handling. A courier provides a dedicated document or parcel delivery service, often with tracking and clear delivery arrangements. Speed alone does not establish the extent of insurance or compensation if an item is lost.\n\nPoste restante means mail held at a designated post office for collection by the addressee. It suits a traveller who has no fixed delivery address in the town. The recipient must follow the operator's identification and collection rules. A private mail bag or post-office box is a different continuing arrangement for receiving mail, not another name for every traveller's collection service.\n\nA parcel post service carries permitted parcels rather than defining every letter service. Surface and air refer to transport arrangements; registration or express handling concerns a service feature. An item can therefore be registered and carried by air without the words describing competing categories.\n\nWorked example: a visitor to Kano gives correspondents a suitable poste restante address and collects letters from the designated post office. A law firm sending an urgent signed agreement instead chooses a suitable tracked courier. A receipt proving posting helps the sender but does not itself prove delivery.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Poste restante concerns collection without a fixed local address.\n• A certificate of posting is not proof that the addressee received the item.\n• Courier service and a postal order perform different tasks.\n• Urgency, transport method and evidence of delivery are separate considerations."},"new":{"title":"Postal services, proof of delivery and travellers’ mail","body":"Postal services collect, carry and deliver letters and other permitted items. Different services meet different needs. A sender should consider urgency, value, destination, proof of posting and delivery arrangements. A postal order transfers a specified value under its terms; it is not the service used to carry an urgent bundle of legal documents.\n\nOrdinary mail provides the standard collection and delivery arrangement. Registered mail adds recorded handling and other safeguards under the postal operator's conditions. It can provide evidence of posting and delivery or collection. Recorded delivery emphasizes an acknowledgment or record of delivery under the relevant service rules. Exact names and guarantees differ by operator and period, so read the historical question carefully.\n\nA certificate of posting establishes that an item was handed in for posting. It does not by itself prove that the addressee received the contents. Express services give priority or expedited handling. A courier provides a dedicated document or parcel delivery service, often with tracking and clear delivery arrangements. Speed alone does not establish the extent of insurance or compensation if an item is lost.\n\nPoste restante means mail held at a designated post office for collection by the addressee. It suits a traveller who has no fixed delivery address in the town. The recipient must follow the operator's identification and collection rules. A private mail bag or post-office box is a different continuing arrangement for receiving mail, not another name for every traveller's collection service.\n\nA parcel post service carries permitted parcels rather than defining every letter service. Surface and air refer to transport arrangements; registration or express handling concerns a service feature. An item can therefore be registered and carried by air without the words describing competing categories.\n\nWorked example: a visitor to Kano gives correspondents a suitable poste restante address and collects letters from the designated post office. A law firm sending an urgent signed agreement instead chooses a suitable tracked courier. A receipt proving posting helps the sender but does not itself prove delivery.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Poste restante concerns collection without a fixed local address.\n• A certificate of posting is not proof that the addressee received the item.\n• Courier service and a postal order perform different tasks.\n• Urgency, transport method and evidence of delivery are separate considerations."}},{"id":"communication-forms-media","old":{"title":"Forms of communication and choosing business media","body":"Communication is the transmission of information and meaning from a sender to a receiver. Its success depends on the intended meaning being understood, not merely on a message leaving a machine. Business communication supports orders, instructions, payments, reports, complaints and coordination among people who may be in different places.\n\nVerbal communication uses words. It can be oral, such as speech in a meeting, or written, such as an email or letter. Non-verbal communication uses other signals, including gestures, facial expressions, diagrams or warning lights. A written message is still verbal in this classification because it uses words. Do not assume that verbal always means spoken when the options contrast verbal with non-verbal.\n\nA different classification divides messages into oral and written forms. Both are useful classroom categories, but they do not exclude every non-verbal sign. A question asking for the two main forms needs to identify the intended classification. Where the options supply both pairs, explain the level being classified instead of teaching that diagrams and gestures do not communicate.\n\nCommunication can reduce the expense and risk of travel by allowing instructions and information to move without every participant moving. It connects suppliers and customers and helps coordinate the delivery of goods. These are direct business benefits; communication does not by itself guarantee wealth or replace the actual transport of physical goods.\n\nThe medium should suit the task. Speech permits quick clarification; written messages can provide a lasting record. Radio reaches listeners through sound, while television combines sound and images. Print advertising ordinarily requires reading for its textual content, though pictures may also convey meaning. Confidential instructions and public advertising require different choices of audience and channel.\n\nWorked example: a Jos wholesaler emails a stock order and telephones to clarify the delivery time. Both messages are verbal: one is written and the other oral. A warehouse warning light is non-verbal. The exchange can save a journey while the goods themselves still need transport.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Written words can be verbal communication.\n• An email and a fax are channels or technologies, not the only two forms of communication.\n• A broad communication benefit does not remove the need for physical delivery.\n• Select the classification and medium from the precise question."},"new":{"title":"Forms of communication and choosing business media","body":"Communication is the transmission of information and meaning from a sender to a receiver. Its success depends on the intended meaning being understood, not merely on a message leaving a machine. Business communication supports orders, instructions, payments, reports, complaints and coordination among people who may be in different places.\n\nVerbal communication uses words. It can be oral, such as speech in a meeting, or written, such as an email or letter. Non-verbal communication uses other signals, including gestures, facial expressions, diagrams or warning lights. A written message is still verbal in this classification because it uses words. Do not assume that verbal always means spoken when the options contrast verbal with non-verbal.\n\nA different classification divides messages into oral and written forms. Both are useful classroom categories, but they do not exclude every non-verbal sign. A question asking for the two main forms needs to identify the intended classification. Where the options supply both pairs, explain the level being classified instead of teaching that diagrams and gestures do not communicate.\n\nCommunication can reduce the expense and risk of travel by allowing instructions and information to move without every participant moving. It connects suppliers and customers and helps coordinate the delivery of goods. These are direct business benefits; communication does not by itself guarantee wealth or replace the actual transport of physical goods.\n\nThe medium should suit the task. Speech permits quick clarification; written messages can provide a lasting record. Radio reaches listeners through sound, while television combines sound and images. Print advertising ordinarily requires reading for its textual content, though pictures may also convey meaning. Confidential instructions and public advertising require different choices of audience and channel.\n\nWorked example: a Jos wholesaler emails a stock order and telephones to clarify the delivery time. Both messages are verbal: one is written and the other oral. A warehouse warning light is non-verbal. The exchange can save a journey while the goods themselves still need transport.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Written words can be verbal communication.\n• An email and a fax are channels or technologies, not the only two forms of communication.\n• A broad communication benefit does not remove the need for physical delivery.\n• Select the classification and medium from the precise question."}}];
